(N'627c95ff-afec-5dfd-9015-a3a7d6c34098',N'a1215685-9168-55fc-9b4f-d80a0f204d91',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>- Đáp án là towels - “bring your own towels” → cần mang khăn.</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ towel (n): khăn </p><p>+ provide (v): cung cấp</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1981),
(N'8c39c305-9cde-57bc-8073-92e9a4fc4f1f',N'7bf8814a-29fe-5c88-8b45-6f223fd8417a',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 6] Test 9 - Section 2</h2><p>You will hear a talk given by a wildlife tour guide to a group of tourists who are on their way to see turtles nesting on a beach in Australia. First you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 14. Welcome to the Indigo Beach Turtle Center tour. Now, turtles are easily recognized by their hard shell which protects their body as they swim through the water. We''re on our way to Indigo Beach to see some of these majestic marine reptiles as they come to lay their eggs on the beach. </p><p>Let me tell you about the Indigo Beach Turtle Center. Australia is an important nesting ground for a variety of turtle species including flatbacks, leatherbacks and loggerheads. The turtles at Indigo Beach are the endangered loggerhead turtles, the second largest species in the world. All the Australian turtles lay their eggs on beaches and the turtles we are going to see are no exception. What is remarkable about the Indigo Beach Turtle Center is the hundreds of loggerhead turtles that nest there. People often ask me when the best time to see the turtles is. The turtle nesting season goes for about five months from November until March. Although you can see them over this whole time, my favorite time is between November and January. From January to March I find it gets very hot and the weather is quite unsettled. The turtles we are going to see are sadly under threat. In the open ocean, fishing is one of the biggest threats to turtles, but this was banned around Indigo Beach in 1978. Another threat to the turtles is people building homes on their natural habitat along beaches and the coastline. But luckily Indigo Beach is a no development zone. In fact, it is foxes, crabs and birds who pose the greatest danger to baby turtles in this area. Upon arrival at the center, you can, if you wish, hire an audio guide which gives you an informative commentary recorded by the rangers to enhance your experience. This is to replace the personal tours, which we found hard to staff. When we get down to the beach, you''ll see that the center is in the process of building a viewing platform to see the nesting turtles from, and this will hopefully be ready for next season. Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 15 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 15 to 20. OK, before we get to the Indigo Beach Turtle Center, I''m going to show you a map to help you find your w' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ay around. Can you all see the entrance at the bottom of the map? The first thing I''d like to point out is the new turtle research zone. There are lots of interesting displays to see here. To find it, from the main entrance, go along Turtle Avenue to the end until it splits into two. Take the right hand fork and it''s the building in amongst the trees. We''re very proud of our new information kiosk where you can find out about turtles through our interactive displays. To reach this, go straight from the entrance and it''s the L-shaped building on your left across the road from the amphitheater. We''ve added a new children''s activity area to keep the young ones happy and informed. To find this, starting from the entrance, walk along Turtle Avenue and take the fourth path on your left. The centre is the building just next to the toilets there. We''ve also built baby turtle pools to help with research and conservation. The pools are in the oval shaped area at the opposite end of the centre from the entrance. </p><p>The area is surrounded by trees, so it''s a lovely shady area to watch baby turtles interacting with each other. If you''re looking for a souvenir or a memento of your experience, then we hope you''ll like our new gift shop. It used to be on the left of the entrance as you came in, in that circular shaped building, but as we expanded, we had to move it. To find it, from the entrance, take the first right off Turtle Avenue and then turn right again. It''s not far from the meeting area. Some of you may be interested in our brand new conference hall, which has state-of-the-art facilities. It''s the second building you come to on your right from the entrance. The square one, just before the amphitheater. It also has a deck with views of the beach and ocean. That is the end of Part 2. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 2</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>You will hear a talk given by a wildlife tour guide to a group of tourists who are on their way to see turtles nesting on a beach in Australia. First you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 14. Welcome to the Indigo Beach Turtle Center tour. Now, turtles are easily recognized by their hard shell which protects their body as they swim through the water. We''re on our way to Indigo Beach to see some of these majestic marine reptiles as they come to lay their eggs on the beach. </p><p>Let me tell you about the Indigo Beach Turtle Center. Australia is an important nesting ground for a variety of turtle species including flatbacks, leatherbacks and loggerheads. The turtles at Indigo Beach are the endangered loggerhead turtles, the second largest species in the world. All the Australian turtles lay their eggs on beaches and the turtles we are going to see are no exception. What is remarkable about the Indigo Beach Turtle Center is the hundreds of loggerhead turtles that nest there. People often ask me when the best time to see the turtles is. The turtle nesting season goes for about five months from November until March. Although you can see them over this whole time, my favorite time is between November and January. From January to March I find it gets very hot and the weather is quite unsettled. The turtles we are going to see are sadly under threat. In the open ocean, fishing is one of the biggest threats t