(N'e50e215f-d3b9-53b2-b6d3-69c0df9fc809',N'f83ce549-2263-532e-8dfd-d5cfdca72abd',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>Campgrounds at Desert Highlands National Park</h2><p>Speaker A: Desert Highlands National Park.<br>
Speaker B: Hello, my friends and I are interested in camping at Desert Highlands, and I have a few questions.<br>
Speaker A: Well, we have nine campgrounds to choose from and they are open all year long.<br>
The number of campgrounds is nine, so nine has been written in the space.<br>
Now we shall begin.<br>
You should answer the questions as you listen because you will not hear the recording a second time.<br>
Listen carefully and answer questions one to six.<br>
Speaker A: Desert Highlands National Park.<br>
Speaker B: Hello, my friends and I are interested in camping at Desert Highlands, and I have a few questions.<br>
Speaker A: Well, we have nine campgrounds to choose from and they are open all year long.<br>
Speaker B: Are there any campsites where we can make reservations?<br>
Speaker A: Yes, but only one. That''s Red Rock Campsite. It''s the biggest site.<br>
Speaker B: Okay. Now, my friend told me it costs just ten dollars to camp in the National Park. Is that right?<br>
Speaker A: Well, we''ve had to increase the fees because of cuts in government support. So most of the sites are fifteen dollars per night.<br>
Speaker A: At Red Rock, you have to pay more, but you get running water there. Those sites are twenty-five dollars.<br>
Speaker B: That''s okay. And how do we pay?<br>
Speaker A: Well, if there''s a member of staff on duty, just pay them directly.<br>
Speaker A: Otherwise, there''s a self-registration process.<br>
Speaker A: You fill out a form and then you deposit that with your fee in the iron box.<br>
Speaker A: There''s one located at the entrance of each campground.<br>
Speaker A: They''ve got big pictures of park rangers on the front.<br>
Speaker B: I take it that most of the campgrounds'' facilities are rather primitive.<br>
Speaker A: Well, there aren''t any laundry areas in the park, and the only campground where you''ll get showers is Red Rock.<br>
Speaker A: But all of the sites have picnic tables and fire grates.<br>
Speaker B: Okay. Well, that sounds fine.<br>
Speaker B: What else? What''s the situation as regards drinking water?<br>
Speaker A: There''s none at the campsites, but you can fill up your bottles at the visitor centers.<br>
Speaker A: There''s one at each of the entrances to the park.<br>
Speaker B: Fine. And I assume there''s no electricity at the campsites?<br>
Speaker A: That''s right. Although if you want to bring your own g' AS nvarchar(max))+N'enerator, that''s fine.<br>
Speaker A: Quite a lot of people do.<br>
Speaker B: I don''t think we''ll bother.<br>
Speaker B: I''m glad there''s a fire grate, though. Sitting around the campfire at night is one of the best things about camping.<br>
Speaker A: Well, do keep in mind that according to the regulations, you''re not allowed to cut or gather any vegetation while you''re in the park, nor can you bring your own wood for the fire with you.<br>
Speaker A: It has to be purchased in the office, which is right where you enter the park.<br>
Speaker A: And while we''re talking about regulations, keep in mind that quiet hours are from 10 p.m. to 6 a.m.<br>
Speaker B: All right. I guess we''ll just enjoy lots of stargazing.<br>
Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 7 to 10.<br>
Now listen and answer questions 7 to 10.<br>
Speaker B: We all like mountain biking, and we''re hoping that there are some nice trails.<br>
Speaker A: Yes, you can pick up trail maps at the visitor centers.<br>
Speaker A: But let me tell you about some special features.<br>
Speaker B: Thanks.<br>
Speaker A: First, there''s the K-Stone Trail.<br>
Speaker B: Sorry?<br>
Speaker A: Kai Stone. That''s K-A-I-S-T-O-N-E.<br>
Speaker A: It''s the longest trail, and it''s probably one of the most difficult, too.<br>
Speaker B: Why is that? Is there an incline?<br>
Speaker A: No, but for the first part, you have to go through a lot of sand, which is really hard work.<br>
Speaker A: After that, there is a plain, which is rough going because it''s full of rocks.<br>
Speaker A: If you''re looking for a really challenging workout, that''s the one for you.<br>
Speaker A: Then there''s the Old Glen Road.<br>
Speaker A: That''s a good trail for an afternoon, and it ends up in a really interesting location.<br>
Speaker A: It starts off quite level because you''re going along what was once a lake, though it''s dried up now for most of the time.<br>
Speaker A: Then you leave that and climb up a hill to get to your destination.<br>
Speaker B: What''s there?<br>
Speaker A: I''ve heard that there''s some interesting abandoned towns in the desert.<br>
Speaker A: Not a town, but something else abandoned. A mine.<br>
Speaker A: You can go in, but we do advise extreme caution.<br>
Speaker B: Thank you very much.<br>
Speaker B: Well, I''m really looking forward to visiting the park now.<br>
Speaker A: Glad to have been of help.<br>
Speaker A: Goodbye.<br>
Speaker B: Goodbye.<br>
