(N'5cb93754-0b19-556b-9c50-b132119b6efa',N'f83ce549-2263-532e-8dfd-d5cfdca72abd',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 6] Test 8 - Section 3</h2><p>Speaker A: Have you had a chance to look at our research on Australian native bees?<br>
Speaker B: Yes, it''s very relevant to my research on European bees.<br>
Speaker A: What aspect do you think is most relevant?<br>
Speaker B: Well, as you know, my main research interest is the European honeybee and the problems facing it.<br>
Speaker A: Yes, I''ve heard about the Varroa mite, but I don''t know much about it.<br>
Speaker B: Well, it almost wiped out the European honeybee population in North America back in the 1980s.<br>
Speaker A: Thankfully we don''t have it here in Australia yet. It sounds like a nasty little creature.<br>
Speaker B: Certainly is. It''s like a little hitchhiker really. It hops onto a bee. Then when the bee lands on a flower, the mite gets off and waits for another bee to come along.<br>
Speaker A: And from there, I guess the mite climbs onto another bee.<br>
Speaker B: Right. The mite then hitches a ride back to the hive where the bee lives with the rest of the colony.<br>
