(N'487d04ac-ffad-576e-b052-49ad569bfe5d',N'f83ce549-2263-532e-8dfd-d5cfdca72abd',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>The Restoration of Paintings</h2><p>Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. Welcome to today''s lecture about restoration of paintings. We all know that no matter how carefully a painting is stored or handled, it can be damaged. It''s the job of a restorer to fix this damage. But to do so and return the painting to the original appearance, we need to know about the material that the artist used when creating it. So the first stage to a restorer is to determine what kind of paint applied to what kind of surface. The restoration process also involves the determinants of what kind of damages have been done to the artwork. </p><p>There are several damages included. When the artist is preparing to hang on a display, he or she has to stretch the canvas to fit it over a frame. And this can result in the ripping of the canvas. A restorer needs to be able to fix this kind of problem and hopefully make preparation from the beginning. And another thing is time. This can be excessive damage to the art. Over a year, a painting can change from being quite bright to appearing dull. This is often due to the collection of dirt on the surface of the work. This all needs to be removed. And was almost the common task the restorers performed. Another problem is the paintings. It''s fading with the colors. If a painting is hung in a round place, particularly in the tanning of the sun, this fades the paint and its appearance. This happens not just hanging the painting, also in storing them. When artists want to store the painting, it''s common for them to roll it up for storage. This process can result in a crack in the paint. Some artists such as Picasso said he might disagree. But it''s possible that the paint could cut off from the canvas entirely. Something that a short-broke artist wouldn''t like. It''s important that when we''re restoring the paintings to consider how they react to things in the environment. For example, the paint can become sticky. This can be a very serious problem, which can be caused by even the minimum change of temperature. This is something else to watch for. A lot of damage to artworks because of the restorers themselves. Restorers used to try to make canvas stronger, and they put something onto the work. Part of its processing involved putting wax on the back of the canvas. But this leaves visible marks to the surface of the paintings. Hopefully, nowadays, we can prove scientific knowledge restoration can avoid making similar errors. Resto' AS nvarchar(max))+N'rers have several tools of exposures. Some are high-tech, others, surprisingly, low-tech. It''s not that common to see a restorer examine an ink painting to use a certain tool to eliminate the mud living under the surface of the paint. But it''s important to analyze what kind of paint or materials that artists used. Restorers often use microscopes so they can analyze the materials thoroughly. For restoration involves cleaning the surface of the painting, restorers use sponges made from cotton to gently clean the work. </p><p>You might be curious about what is used to clean the paintings. Because some very old paintings were covered with varnish, chemicals cannot be used for cleaning due to the fact that they can remove or damage the varnish layer. But restorers have to use something to clean the paintings. This is where the low-tech tools come in. The most common liquid used in cleaning is human saliva. It does a great job of cleaning the painting. It doesn''t cause damage</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. Welcome to today''s lecture about restoration of paintings. We all know that no matter how carefully a painting is stored or handled, it can be damaged. It''s the job of a restorer to fix this damage. But to do so and return the painting to the original appearance, we need to know about the material that the artist 