(N'bbe33dbd-eb2b-55d3-9981-dd6bdecea3c9',N'f069109c-0f4a-53b5-8e8e-e33909145dde',0,3,19,N'<p>local government</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là E - “large glass building” = greenhouse. - “allow… to use” → use of a greenhouse.</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ glass building (n): nhà kính</p><p> + seedlings (n): cây con</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1979),
(N'4003fdcc-a7b8-5499-8323-67c6c75271da',N'f069109c-0f4a-53b5-8e8e-e33909145dde',0,3,19,N'<p>university</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là C - “tests on soil… indicate what plants… fertilizer” → advice on soil.</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ soil (n): đất </p><p>+ tests (n): kiểm tra</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1979),
(N'28d17a9c-5752-5915-b226-dbb020ecd6fe',N'f069109c-0f4a-53b5-8e8e-e33909145dde',0,3,19,N'<p>supermarket</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là D - “lose weight and stay healthy” → eat-to-keep-fit course.</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ lose weight (v): giảm cân </p><p>+ stay healthy (v): giữ sức khỏe</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1979),
(N'7c161539-8256-5bbe-ac72-6813637e9fb2',N'aa02d32d-bbc9-5ad6-8257-9b45d44aac0e',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Company Analysis</h2><p></p><p>Speaker A: Hi Frances, Sam. So, how did you go with applying the different theoretical business tools for the report I asked you to write?<br>
Speaker B: It was interesting and made us really focus on which tool was best to use when analysing a business.<br>
Speaker A: Good. So, tell me what you found out.<br>
Speaker B: We liked the theory behind pest analysis. That''s political, economic, social and technological, although we''re not sure if it was very applicable to our case.<br>
Speaker B: Some of the other groups had studies for which it worked really well and they said it was easy to use, but I felt it focused too much on the big picture and was unsuitable for our company.<br>
Speaker C: I agree. On the other hand, I enjoyed using the drill-down method.<br>
Speaker C: It was painstaking to do and we seemed to be working on it for ages, but the results were worth waiting for.<br>
Speaker B: Yes, I liked the way it eventually broke down complex problems.<br>
Speaker C: Yes, I much preferred doing the PMI analysis.<br>
Speaker C: It was so straightforward to break down into its three components, plus, minus and interesting implications.<br>
Speaker C: You just needed to brainstorm these components and then write them up.<br>
Speaker C: Fast and effective. And it didn''t make your brain hurt doing it.<br>
Speaker B: I agree. And you don''t need any special training at all. Anyone can use it.<br>
Speaker A: Hmm. So, was there any tool that you thought was superior to the others?<br>
Speaker B: In my opinion, the one tool that is critical to business, especially for larger companies, is Pareto analysis.<br>
Speaker B: If they applied Pareto analysis, they would see that by focusing on the critical 20% of their problems, they could generate 80% of the benefits.<br>
Speaker B: This would allow them to work faster and it also reduces their workload.<br>
Speaker B: It''s all about finding out what their basic problems are.<br>
Speaker C: I agree with Frances about Pareto, but my favourite was the SWOT analysis.<br>
Speaker C: You know, looking at the company''s strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats, as it can be used in so many different situations.<br>
Speaker C: It works in large and small companies and even down to departmental level.<br>
Speaker C: It was great for our case study.<br>
Speaker B: Yes, that''s true.<br>
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 26 to 30 on page ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'6.<br>
Now listen and answer questions 26 to 30.<br>
Speaker A: OK. I''ve read your draft report, but can you tell me a bit more about that company you focused on?<br>
Speaker B: Well, the company we used in our study was a manufacturing company who have relied on distributors to take their product to the end user.<br>
Speaker B: We did some analysis examining the possibility of them distributing their own products.<br>
Speaker B: We initially looked at their strengths.<br>
Speaker B: The company, although it is small, is well known and has a very good reputation.<br>
Speaker B: The management are very committed to increasing the company profits and they are confident this move would be successful.<br>
Speaker C: Right. And the company has quite a lot of staff who have previously worked for distribution companies, so the knowledge is already there.<br>
Speaker C: That was something we didn''t expect to find and is probably going to help them the most to achieve their goals.<br>
Speaker A: I was quite impressed by some of the opportunities you detailed.<br>
Speaker A: I did think you missed something quite pertinent to their future growth, however.<br>
Speaker B: What was that?<br>
Speaker A: You didn''t consider the potential for establishing an offshore division.<br>
Speaker A: You talked about the company negotiating better terms with its suppliers.<br>
Speaker A: Most of these are based abroad and so if the company had a way of actually distributing the product there, this could be a tremendous opportunity and give them an edge over their competitors.<br>
Speaker B: That''s a good point.<br>
Speaker B: So what did you think about the threats we identified?<br>
Speaker A: I thought you did a good job.<br>
Speaker A: I was really pleased to see that you considered how the government''s planned environmental policy could really affect the viability of this venture.<br>
Speaker A: With this law being introduced next year, they might need to find new ways of using their existing technology.<br>
Speaker A: It''s easy to identify what your competitors are doing, but it''s the bigger picture that often gets ignored.<br>
Speaker A: So, Sam, what did you learn from this assignment?<br>
Speaker C: Heaps. I guess I hadn''t realised how much time it takes to ensure your company survives and profits.<br>
Speaker C: It was great to study the different tools in books, but applying them in the real world was much harder than I thought.<br>
Speaker C: It was a great learning exp'+N'erience.<br>
Speaker A: I''m glad I could make your assignment relevant.<br>
Speaker A: I''m basically happy with your report.<br>
Speaker A: You''ve got the format correct with the necessary headings, etc.<br>
Speaker A: And you''ve got sufficient details under the different headings.<br>
Speaker A: Just remember to state whether or not you think the company should go ahead with the new venture.<br>
Speaker A: But you''ve done a good job so far.<br>
