ter, I found this work was a bit tough. </p><p>Mona: Every night when I got off work, I was nearly exhausted. </p><p>Mona: What I wanted was just a good rest and sleep. </p><p>Tom: Doesn''t sound like an easy job. </p><p>Mona: It definitely wasn''t. </p><p>Mona: Then, there was the personnel office. </p><p>Mona: I went there on the first day when I arrived at the hotel, as I was not unfamiliar with eve' AS nvarchar(max))+N'rything. </p><p>Mona: I asked the staff several questions, but the employee of the personnel office seemed a bit annoyed and started complaining, which gave me a very bad impression. </p><p>Mona: Lastly, as regards cleaning, it wasn''t too bad compared with other parts of the hotel, except the vacuum cleaners, which were probably bought such a long time ago that the sound seemed too loud when they were working. </p><p>Mona: But fortunately, the hotel hasn''t received any complaint from the customers so far. </p><p>Narrator: Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 27 to 30. </p><p>Narrator: Now, listen and answer questions 27 to 30. </p><p>Tom: I think you''ve benefited a lot from the internship. </p><p>Mona: Yeah, absolutely. </p><p>Mona: In the beginning, I just wanted to complete the essay for the course, but in the end, I found it has given me some practical examples which are helpful to what I intend to do after graduation. </p><p>Mona: As you know, I''ve always wanted to do something related to hotel management. </p><p>Mona: Due to the time limitation, I still haven''t got a clear idea of some specific work positions. </p><p>Tom: Well, I heard you''re going to give us a presentation of your essay next time in class. </p><p>Mona: Yes. Actually, I''m not so good at giving a presentation, but the tutor says that''s just what he wants me to do. </p><p>Mona: To know what is important when giving a presentation, like how to attract the audience, make eye contact, and make gestures, etc. </p><p>Mona: And other students can learn from my presentation. </p><p>Tom: I''m looking forward to it. </p><p>Tom: You know, now I''m only in the second year of the course, and I have to write an essay next semester, but I have no idea about where I can do an internship.</p><p>Tom: There are several hotels which I''m interested in. Do you think I should call their managers? </p><p>Mona: Well, I suggest you''d better not, because you''re more likely to be rejected by them. </p><p>Mona: There is a career office on campus that has got a list of companies offering internship opportunities to students. </p><p>Mona: The office will help you contact where you want to go. </p><p>Mona: You just have to prepare your personal information, like the CV.</p><p>Mona: Oh, don''t forget to provide a reference from the tutor. </p><p>Tom: Great. I''ve got it. </p><p>Tom: So, have you got any plans for your essay? </p><p>Tom: I''ve noticed that the hotel industry is'+N' developing at a high speed. </p><p>Tom: People are more and more concerned about the quality of accommodation. </p><p>Tom: What I''m struck by is the numerous statistics showing the ever-increasing number of five-star hotels. </p><p>Tom: So, I intend to carry out extensive research on this aspect. </p><p>Tom: I''m thinking about using videos as a research method, but I haven''t decided yet. </p><p>Mona: I think it''s an interesting topic. </p><p>Mona: Anyway, if you have any questions later, I will be happy to help. </p><p>Tom: Thanks a lot, Mona. </p><p>Mona: You''re welcome. </p><p>Narrator: That is the end of part three. </p><p>Narrator: You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part three</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-6/iL.VOL6.5.S1977.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1977),
(N'6a80afa5-72c1-5f0c-8c32-538a0613e437',N'7c4e6c2e-b798-512c-981e-bc29964f70f3',0,2,18,N'<p>What problem does Mona point out about each part of the hotel where she interned?
Choose the correct letter, <span>A-F</span>, next to Question 21-26.
</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,1,NULL,0,1977),
(N'9aebc993-d1c8-52a8-894f-a632f379dc59',N'6a80afa5-72c1-5f0c-8c32-538a0613e437',0,3,19,N'<p>reception </p>',N'<p>Đáp án là C </p><p>- Mona mô tả công việc ở quầy lễ tân lặp đi lặp lại mỗi ngày, không có gì mới mẻ, thể hiện rõ tính chất routine hàng ngày</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì không đề cập đến sự nghiêm khắc, ồn ào hay thiếu kiên nhẫn ở bộ phận này</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ routine (noun) thói quen hằng ngày</p><p>+ repetitive (adjective) lặp lại</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1977),
(N'e1d150a3-7027-505d-bb8c-cb43d81eceb2',N'6a80afa5-72c1-5f0c-8c32-538a0613e437',0,3,19,N'<p>restaurant </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là B</p><p>- Nhà hàng được mô tả có quy định rất chặt chẽ, vi phạm nhỏ cũng bị trừ lương, thể hiện sự strict</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì không nói đến tiếng ồn, mệt mỏi hay thiếu nhân lực</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ obey: (verb) tuân thủ; </p><p>+ deduct: (verb) khấu trừ</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1977),
(N'53ca444a-bed2-574c-9f44-69ebc68e0d13',N'6a80afa5-72c1-5f0c-8c32-538a0613e437',0,3,19,N'<p>coffee bar </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là D</p><p>- Mona nêu rõ coffee bar thường xuyên thiếu nhân lực, khiến khách phải chờ đợi lâu</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì không nhấn mạnh sự mệt mỏi cá nhân hay tiếng ồn</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ shortage (noun) sự thiếu hụt</p><p>+ staff (noun) nhân viên</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1977),
(N'78f6369c-a6a1-5291-8650-de862ba48354',N'6a80afa5-72c1-5f0c-8c32-538a0613e437',0,3,19,N'<p>shop </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là F</p><p>- Công việc tại shop khiến Mona kiệt sức mỗi ngày, phản ánh rõ tính chất rất mệt mỏi</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì không nói đến tiếng ồn hay quy định nghiêm ngặt</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ exhausted (adjective) kiệt sức</p><p>+ tough (adjective) vất vả</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1977),
(N'453c345d-6b24-5902-895d-e79fdc4ffd5d',N'6a80afa5-72c1-5f0c-8c32-538a0613e437',0,3,19,N'<p>personal office </p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A</p><p>- Nhân viên phòng nhân sự tỏ ra khó chịu, thiếu kiên nhẫn khi được hỏi, để lại ấn tượng xấu</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì không liên quan đến tiếng ồn hay cường độ công việc</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ annoyed (adjective) khó chịu</p><p>+ patience (noun) sự kiên nhẫn</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1977),
(N'7eb36d2b-e037-5374-b886-f41c585bf6c5',N'6a80afa5-72c1-5f0c-8c32-538a0613e437',0,3,19,N'<p>cleaning </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là E</p><p>- Bộ phận vệ sinh gặp vấn đề về tiếng ồn do máy hút bụi quá cũ và kêu to</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì Mona nói công việc này không quá tệ</p><p>- Key word:</p><p>+ vacuum cleaner: (noun) máy hút bụi</p><p>+ noisy: (adjective) ồn ào</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1977),
(N'ab31fe15-4f0a-5f69-a8b9-ada847b2720b',N'7c4e6c2e-b798-512c-981e-bc29964f70f3',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,2,NULL,0,1977),
(N'514939be-2b1a-5f9c-bed0-ac35efffa966',N'ab31fe15-4f0a-5f69-a8b9-ada847b2720b',0,3,19,N'<p> How does the experience benefit Mona?</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là A</p><p>- Trải nghiệm thực tập giúp Mona có ví dụ thực tế cho định hướng nghề nghiệp sau khi tốt nghiệp</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì cô không nhấn mạnh đến luận văn hay một vị trí cụ thể</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ practical (adjective) thực tế</p><p>+ graduation (noun) tốt nghiệp</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1977),
(N'01f22697-e818-5a6e-9bb0-62caab12a6f4',N'ab31fe15-4f0a-5f69-a8b9-ada847b2720b',0,3,19,N'<p>Why does the tutor want Mona to do the presentation? </p>',N'<p>Đáp án là C</p><p>- Tutor muốn Mona trình bày để các sinh viên khác học hỏi kỹ năng thuyết trình</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì không phải do cô làm tốt hay muốn thể hiện bản thân</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ presentation: (noun) bài thuyết trình</p><p>+ learn from: (verb phrase) học hỏi từ</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1977),
(N'50a28100-8c05-5d21-95fb-c04ed1b4567f',N'ab31fe15-4f0a-5f69-a8b9-ada847b2720b',0,3,19,N'<p>What does Mona suggest Tom do?</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là A</p><p>- Mona khuyên Tom nên liên hệ trung tâm hướng nghiệp của trường để tìm chỗ thực tập</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì cô khuyên không nên liên hệ trực tiếp quản lý khách sạn</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ career office: (noun) trung tâm hướng nghiệp</p><p>+ internship: (noun) thực tập</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1977),
(N'f1f5c76b-3e0f-59e0-b176-77b424799832',N'ab31fe15-4f0a-5f69-a8b9-ada847b2720b',0,3,19,N'<p>What is Tom’s plan for his own essay?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A</p><p>- Tom dự định nghiên cứu sự gia tăng của các khách sạn năm sao cho bài luận của mình</p><p>- Các đáp án còn lại sai vì video chỉ là phương pháp dự kiến, chưa quyết định</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ five-star hotels: (noun) khách sạn năm sao</p><p>+ research: (noun) nghiên cứu</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1977),
(N'e6cb0538-83f9-52a9-b5f5-171385c89472',N'481b0680-a0d8-5650-aaa5-1cf3b8c93550',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>The Red Sea Urchin</h2><p>Part 4. You will hear part of a lecture about a marine creature called the Red Sea Urchin. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now, listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. Good morning. Last week, I talked about various research methods in the natural sciences, and you did some background reading on that topic. Today, I want to take up that theme again and look at a specific example of how data gained using newer techniques has really pushed our knowledge forward, more or less overturning existing theories.</p><p>So, I''m going to look at the case of the Red Sea Urchin. First of all, some background. As the name suggests, this is a marine animal, and it''s found in shallow coastal waters. It grazes on plants. It''s an herbivore, in other words. Here in North America, we can find it all along our west coast, from Alaska down to California, so many of you will be familiar with it. And it survives very well because it''s covered in spines, which are very sharp, and these deter most predators. Now, in the 1960s, in this country, the Sea Urchin was thought to be partly responsible for the sudden decline in kelp, a plant which supports an extensive ecosystem. So, in fact, it was considered to be a pest. Incidentally, since the 1960s, the thinking on that has changed, and humans are now thought to be to blame. But anyway, Red Sea Urchins weren''t popular, and people even tried to poison them to get rid of them in any way possible. But then, in the 1970s, attitudes changed. Interestingly, this had nothing to do with science. There were no new insights into the biology of the Sea Urchin. It was, in fact, a purely economic development. It became apparent that there was a demand for the Red Sea Urchin in Asia, where certain parts of the animal were considered to be a great delicacy. And so, a commercial fishery was established based on Sea Urchins. They brought high prices. In fact, at one point during the 1990s, they were one of the most valuable marine resources in the state of California. So, people began to look at the Sea Urchin in a different light. And just at this time, people noticed an apparent decline in the numbers of Sea Urchins. This prompted a wave of scientific studies with the purpose of getting more information about the species—its life cycle, its biology, survival rate, growth patterns, and so on. So, what did research uncover? Well, the most significant findings came from research based on bio' AS nvarchar(max))+N'chemical techniques. Basically, individual Sea Urchins were tagged and injected with tetracycline, which allowed researchers to track their growth rates. And it quickly became apparent that the Sea Urchins grew a lot more slowly and lived a lot longer than had previously been thought. Astonishingly, they can have a vast lifespan, greater than that of almost any other terrestrial or marine species. They can live to be 100 years old, and some may even reach 200. Just imagine, an individual Red Sea Urchin that hatched early in the 19th century may still be thriving and even breeding. It was found that this animal does grow fairly quickly when it''s young. At the age of 2, it can grow from 2 to 4 centimeters in just one year. But even then, according to scientists who conducted the research, it still takes 6 or 7 years before it''s big enough to be harvested. By the time the Red Sea Urchin is a teenager, its growth slows quite dramatically. And from the age of 22, when it''s about 12 centimeters long, it grows at just 0.1 of a centimetre a year. But somehow, the animal never really stops growing. It just grows incredibly slowly. Some of the largest and probably the oldest Sea Urchins were found off the coast of British Columbia, between Vancouver Island and the mainland. And now, another very recent study, one using an entirely different technique, has produced independent evidence for these findings. The study measured levels of radiocarbon, and it has shown that even very old Sea Urchins show almost no sign of aging when something finally kills them.</p><p>Unlike when someone looks at you or me, you can''t look at a Sea Urchin and say whether it''s young or middle-aged. And in addition to that, even when they''re incredibly ancient, they are still able to breed. So by now, you may be wondering how, with this phenomenal lifespan, there aren''t a lot more Sea Urchins around than there actually are. Well, there is a suggestion that reproduction rates for the species are low, because not many Red Sea Urchins are able to survive the first year of life. The study also confirmed the finding that in older Sea Urchins, there is a very steady, very consistent rate of growth. And this growth is independent of ocean conditions and other variables. So, what are the implications of these findings</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Part 4. You will hear part of a lecture about a marine creature called the Red Sea Urchin. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now, listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. Good morning. Last week, I talked about various research methods in the natural sciences, and you did some background reading on that topic. Today, I want to take up that theme again and look at a specific example of how data gained using newer techniques has really pushed our knowledge forward, more or less overturning existing theories.</p><p>So, I''m going to look at the case of the Red Sea Urchin. First of all, some background. As the name suggests, this is a marine animal, and it''s found in shallow coastal waters. It grazes on plants. It''s an herbivore, in other words. Here in North America, we can find it all along our west coast, from Alaska down to California, so many of you will be familiar with it. And it survives very well because it''s covered in spines, which are very sharp, and these deter most predators. Now,