ntury may still be thriving and even breeding. It was found that this animal does grow fairly quickly when it''s young. At the age of 2, it can grow from 2 to 4 centimeters in just one year. But even then, according to scientists who conducted the research, it still takes 6 or 7 years before it''s big enough to be harvested. By the time the Red Sea Urchin is a teenager, its growth slows quite dramatically. And from the age of 22, when it''s about 12 centimeters long, it grows at just 0.1 of a centimetre a year. But somehow, the animal never really stops growing. It just grows incredibly slowly. Some of the largest and probably the oldest Sea Urchins were found off the coast of British Columbia, between Vancouver Island and the mainland. And now, another very recent study, one using an entirely different technique, has produced independent evidence for these findings. The study measured levels of radiocarbon, and it has shown that even very old Sea Urchins show almost no sign of aging when something finally kills them.</p><p>Unlike when someone looks at you or me, you can''t look at a Sea Urchin and say whether it''s young or middle-aged. And in addition to that, even when they''re incredibly ancient, they are still able to breed. So by now, you may be wondering how, with this phenomenal lifespan, there aren''t a lot more Sea Urchins around than there actually are. Well, there is a suggestion that reproduction rates for the species are low, because not many Red Sea Urchins are able to survive the first year of life. The study also confirmed the finding that in older Sea Urchins, there is a very steady, very consistent rate of growth. And this growth is independent of ocean conditions and other variables. So, what are the implications of these findings</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-6/iL.VOL6.5.S1977.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1977),
