(N'0ce75653-539a-571e-808e-9354f8564d78',N'fdd6e4b5-5fff-5bde-950e-4f2ff76f208e',0,3,19,N'Question number 30',N'<p>- Đáp án là D (nutrients) - “add balanced nutrients” → cung cấp dưỡng chất</p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ nutrients (n): chất dinh dưỡng </p><p>+ balanced (adj): cân bằng</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1881),
(N'2aaf4624-b109-5709-8641-15a87f4cdf8f',N'b614f55b-5505-54fe-b28e-0a43c89f4a9a',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Canadian maple syrup</h2><p>You will hear two environmental science students called Dominic and Ella discussing some ways of solving environmental problems. First you have some time to look at questions 21 to 25 on page 5. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 25. Speaker A: Okay, everyone get into pairs and look at the innovative environmental solutions we''ve been reading about this week. Discuss some of the pros and cons of each one. Speaker A: You want to work together Ella? Speaker B: Sure Dominic. Which solution do you want to look at first? Speaker A: I think the supermarket garden project''s interesting. Speaker B: That''s where the supermarket grows fruit and vegetables in the store and sells them. </p><p>Speaker A: Yes. Understandably only a few shops have decided to adopt this idea. It''s hard to set up. Speaker B: What''s the reaction from customers? Speaker A: Well, of course they love the idea. One thing I didn''t expect though. Speaker B: What was that? Speaker A: The idea is hugely popular with local chefs and many of them are finding new ways to use the plants and all their different stages of growth. Speaker B: I see. I found the solar car park idea to be really practical. Speaker A: I know what you mean. Putting solar panels on the roofs of car parks to generate electricity might be a cost-effective way to power a whole shopping centre. Speaker B: The difficulty could be finding space to put all the panels. Some smaller shopping centres probably don''t have covered car parks. Speaker A: That''s right. It''s a shame because the technology seems to be all there and ready to go if the conditions are right. Speaker B: What do you think of the wildlife crossing solution? Speaker B: You know, when a bridge or a tunnel is built over or under a major roadway so that animals can cross. Speaker A: That idea has become very popular and has so many benefits. A lot of research has been done hasn''t it? Speaker B: Yes. It''s not surprising that the data reveals a huge drop in the number of road accidents in areas where there are wildlife crossings. Speaker A: That''s one of the predicted outcomes. Speaker B: Something I read about in the research, which I''d never considered before, was how the animal populations in these areas are affected. Speaker B: Naturally more of them survive, but because their habitat is not interrupted too much by the roads, the genetic diversity of local species has improved. Speaker A: That''s true. It''s worth the huge cost' AS nvarchar(max))+N' of building them if the result is a win-win for people and animals. Speaker A: I found the 100% renewable energy campaign, like solar and wind energy, to be really impressive. Speaker B: Yes, it''s not so much the size of the movement, I mean only a few countries are involved, as the amount of support offered once a community joins. Speaker B: Considering the sheer amount of time and planning it takes for a city to convert completely to renewables, the organisers of the movement would need to do that. Speaker A: Did you read about carrot mobbing as an environmental movement? Speaker B: What''s that? Speaker A: It''s where huge numbers of people learn through the internet about where to shop at stores that use the profits to support environmental projects. Speaker A: So they stop buying products that aren''t eco-friendly, like a boycott? Speaker B: No, actually, it''s the opposite of that. It allows businesses to fund initiatives to help protect the environment by boosting their profiles. Speaker A: It''s a pity that most participants don''t get to know each other personally. It''s all through online notices. Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 26 to 30 on page 6. Now listen and answer questions 26 to 30. Speaker A: Now everyone, let''s look at one particular environmental innovation, aerial reforestation. With your partner, discuss the process involved in this idea. Speaker B: So, aerial reforestation is where they drop mixtures from plains to sow vegetables, grains or trees and so on in areas that might be hard to reach. Speaker A: Let''s see. What are the steps? Speaker B: Well, the first step is choosing the right area. Whatever size land you''re working on, ensuring there''s enough light is crucial. Speaker B: The next step, whatever you''re planting, is to make sure you have the right seeds. To give them a better chance against the competition from other plants, you need to use a lot. Speaker B: So then you make a mixture with decent quality, durable compost for nourishment. Speaker A: Then do they infuse it with insecticide? Speaker B: That''s not necessary in this model. Speaker B: The next step is pressing the whole thing into a small chunk of clay to make a ball. This may then be covered in an outer material such as paper. Speaker A: Right. That way, when it lands, it will add balanced nutrients to the mixture. Speaker B: Yes. And it may also protect it from insects and other plants. Speaker B: So a few days befo'+N're they release it from the plain onto the land below, the final step is dipping it in water, just enough to get the germination process started. Speaker A: Yes. And after that... That is the end of Section 3. You now have half a minute to check your answers. Now turn to Section 4 on page 7. P4 You will hear a lecture on a very popular Canadian food product called maple syrup. First you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40 on page 7. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. Maple syrup can be described as very sweet. Canadians pour it generously over things like pancakes for breakfast. The syrup is produced from the maple tree, and it actually comes from the tree''s sap, which is the watery substance that comes out of the trunk of the tree when this is cut. In order for enough sap to be produced, very special climate conditions are needed. In spring, the temperatures need to drop to well below zero at night, but then they need to rise sharply during the day, so they''re hugely different over a 24-hour period. And that''s exactly what happens in northeastern Canada every spring. The indigenous peoples of North America, known as the First Nations, discovered the maple secret long before the Europeans arrived. Early folk stories say it was first discovered by a chief who threw his tomahawk, which is similar to an axe, into the trunk of a tree. Clear sap started to run from the cut. His wife used this sap to boil meat, and the heat turned it into a sweet syrup, which they found delicious. Whether or not that story is true, we do know that the maple tree was very important to First Nations peoples for hundreds of years. Collecting the sap each spring became a tradition among them. Whole families would migrate to the areas where the maple trees grew and set up camp there. They collected the sap from the trees using buckets. Then they needed to concentrate the sap, removing some of the water from it to create syrup. They had two ways of doing this. One way was to drop hot stones from a fire into the buckets to create steam. If repeated often enough, this would eventually concentrate the syrup. Another method that they used to concentrate the syrup was to leave the sap outside overnight, exposed to freezing temperatures. This would mean that a thick layer of ice would form on top, which could be discarded, leaving the sugary sap below. Again, this process could be repeated over several days, and eventually a sweet brown syrup was produced. The Firs'+N't Nations people had various uses for the sap and syrup. They drank the sap fresh from the tree as a tonic, knowing that it was an important source of energy. But for the most part, they reduced the syrup further and then cooled it so that it crystallized into sugar. This was easy to carry and kept indefinitely. European settlers who arrived in Canada began to learn about the syrup from First Nations peoples and further expanded its uses. For example, they incorporated it into medicine of different kinds. They also discovered that by adding yeast to the sap, they could make a maple drink. And by throwing the hot syrup into the snow, they could make a toffee-like candy. But, like the First Nations, they turned most of their sap into maple sugar. </p><p>For most people, it was the only type of sugar they could afford, since the only alternative was the sugar imported from the Caribbean, which was made from sugar cane, and this was very expensive. However, after 1880, the Canadian government decided to stop charging tax on Caribbean sugar, and this made it much cheaper. That meant it was no longer cost-effective to produce maple sugar, and so syrup-making became the only viable industry remaining for Canadians, and that''s what we''ve been doing ever since. At present, maple syrup is enjoying a boom. In the last two decades, production has doubled in Canada. However, serious threats remain. Like any agricultural product, the harvest is subject to such things as major storms or the arrival of invasive insects, both of which have happened, and these could destroy everything. It is predicted with some certainty that, in the longer term, climate change will also have an adverse effect on the sapran. Then there are the social factors. Producing maple syrup is a very labour-intensive process, making it an expensive purchase. And of equal concern is the fact that nowadays there''s a lot of competition from cheaper, artificially flavoured brands. Furthermore, we''re at a time when obesity is a major issue, making a product that is 67% sugar increasingly difficult to sell. Nevertheless, there are reasons to be optimistic. Producers are developing markets abroad. Our syrup is currently being exported to over 50 countries. Back here at home, chefs use it enthusiastically. It''s certainly high in calories, but it also contains proteins, minerals and antioxidants. As such, it is still relatively healthy when you compare it with sugar or corn syrup. Canadians are proud of maple'+N' syrup and its deep roots in our history and geography. However, the primary reason for maple syrup''s success around the world remains its taste. There''s nothing quite like it. That is the end of the listening test. You now have 10 minutes to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet. You have 2 minutes left. You have 1 minute left. Please stop writing and wait for your question booklet to be collected</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>You will hear two environmental science students called Dominic and Ella discussing some ways of solving environmental problems. First you have some time to look at questions 21 to 25 on page 5. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 25. Speaker A: Okay, everyone get into pairs and look at the innovative environmental solutions we''ve been reading about this week. Discuss some of the pros and cons of each one. Speaker A: You want to work together Ella? Speaker B: Sure Dominic. Which solution do you want to look at first? Speaker A: I think the supermarket garden project''s interesting. Speaker B: That''s where the supermarket grows fruit and vegetables in the store and sells them. </p><p>Speaker A: Yes. Understandably only a few shops have decided to adopt this idea. It''s hard to set up. Speaker B: What''s the reaction from customers? Speaker A: Well, of course they love the idea. One thing I didn''t expect though. Speaker B: What was that? Speaker A: The idea is hugely popular with local chefs and many of them are finding new ways to use the plants and all their different stages of growth. Speaker B: I see. I found the solar car park idea to be really practical. Speaker A: I know what you mean. Putting solar panels on the roofs of car parks to generate electricity might be a cost-effective way to power a whole shopping centre. Speaker B: The difficulty could be fin