(N'176afe94-9a89-537d-8d67-79a2d1e784ac',N'8f86099a-e67f-5978-83a5-459adf85108e',0,3,19,N'<p>Which <span>TWO</span> of the following statements are true of the workshop on ''Periodicity of Eating and  Human Health''?   </p>',N'<p>- Synonyms: from around the world (khắp nơi trên thế giới) = international (quốc tế)</p><p>- Interpretation: Các chuyên gia về thực phẩm từ khắp nơi trên thế giới tham gia hội thảo.</p><p>- Answer: Hội thảo có sự tham gia của hội đồng chuyên gia dinh dưỡng quốc tế.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là C</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1837),
(N'8bd84391-26d2-5a36-a9a8-519936112ba8',N'8f86099a-e67f-5978-83a5-459adf85108e',0,3,19,N'<p>Which <span>TWO</span> of the following statements are true of the workshop on ''Periodicity of Eating and  Human Health''?   </p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Hội thảo nhận được hỗ trợ tài chính từ một nhà sản xuất thực phẩm lớn. </p><p>- Answer: Hội thảo được tài trợ bởi một công ty thực phẩm đa quốc gia hàng đầu </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là E   </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1837),
(N'54cd3a8f-6276-5d76-ac86-35a450e518b7',N'44b4b942-bf09-5429-afe0-8eb30bc9ab8b',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the summary below. </p><p>Choose <span>ONE WORD ONLY</span> from the passage for each answer. </p><p>Write your answers in boxes 23-26 on your answer sheet. </p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Carbohydrates and athletes',11,0,3,NULL,0,1837),
(N'9c65bb10-604e-5f4b-9af8-29fd0bf09e47',N'54cd3a8f-6276-5d76-ac86-35a450e518b7',0,3,19,N'<h3>Carbohydrates and athletes</h3><p>A key   }{SPACE}{ practised by athletes who take part in long-distance cycling competitions</p><p>and }{SPACE}{ is the consumption of carbohydrates during exercise.</p><p>The advice that many follow is to eat as much as possible during exercise rather than delay until they experience }{SPACE}{ .</p><p>Carbohydrate intake is also important after exercise in the }{SPACE}{ stage. It is at this point that an athlete will replace the energy retained in the muscles.</p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Một ____ quan trọng được các vận động viên thực hiện... là tiêu thụ carbohydrate. </p><p>- Answer: Việc tiêu thụ carbohydrate khi luyện tập là một chiến thuật quan trọng của các vận động viên </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là strategy  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1837),
(N'8cbf292c-ad32-5dc5-8311-e387466d0b7d',N'54cd3a8f-6276-5d76-ac86-35a450e518b7',0,3,19,N'Question number 24',N'<p>- Interpretation: Các vận động viên tham gia các cuộc đua xe đạp đường dài và ____. </p><p>- Answer: Các sự kiện sức bền bao gồm marathons (chạy marathon) và đua xe đạp đường dài.  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1837),
(N'23cabf77-b3a5-5ecd-8091-1ae695c66a33',N'54cd3a8f-6276-5d76-ac86-35a450e518b7',0,3,19,N'Question number 25',N'<p>- Synonyms: "delay until they experience" = "wait for the onset of fatigue".</p><p>- Interpretation: thay vì trì hoãn cho đến khi cảm thấy ____.</p><p>- Answer: "Fatigue" (mệt mỏi) là từ chỉ trạng thái cần tránh trước khi nạp carbohydrate.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1837),
(N'c7011909-f7d8-5cc1-b77d-4722a9671705',N'54cd3a8f-6276-5d76-ac86-35a450e518b7',0,3,19,N'Question number 26',N'<p>- Interpretation: cũng quan trọng trong giai đoạn ____ sau khi tập.</p><p>- Answer: "Recovery" (hồi phục) là giai đoạn sau tập luyện cần nạp carbohydrate.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1837),
(N'36792004-a239-547a-a47f-e32182af2f70',N'37b54f52-687a-52de-9f05-31712e509713',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<p><span><span>Risk taking</span></span></p><p></p><p><span><span>A </span></span>We run risks. Most of the time we ignore them; sometimes we stop and decide how to deal with them. That’s life. Risks arise in sports, in business, in personal relationships; they are present in the laboratory, in political calculations, everywhere. Acting individually, we often prefer to gain some benefit by accepting some danger. Acting collectively, we make similar trade-offs.</p><p></p><p><span><span>B</span></span> When society must assess risks, and those risks can be clarified, at least in part, by science, technology or mathematics, the responsibility of the research community is to be clear and complete. But that is not always so easy. Furthermore, too few people pay full attention to the best evidence, much less modify their behavior as a result. Even when the evidence of high risk is clear – about smoking, for instance – many will deny the risk, or dispute it, or accept that it exists and still go ahead. As a former cigarette smoker, I understand denial.</p><p></p><p><span><span>C</span></span> Perhaps more important than the basic facts of risk are the varied perceptions of it. Hearing the prediction of 60% chance of rain, or recognizing the minuscule likelihood of becoming ill from a flu shot, most people understand the right responses: take your umbrella and get your flu shot. But some, fearfully but irrationally, go to the extreme and stay at home and avoid all contact with other people. Irrational decisions occasionally affect us all. When it comes to risks the best odds do not always influence people to make the correct decision.</p><p></p><p><span><span>D</span></span> In her book, The Universe and the Teacup, the science writer K. C. Cole reminds us that the risks of losing something and gaining something appear to be calculated in our minds according to quite different scales. People will risk a lot to prevent a loss, she notes; some people will rush into a burning building to save a pet. But they will usually risk very little if the only perceived outcome is a possible gain; many people won’t even risk pursuing an exciting job, if the current job, though unfulfilling, remains comfortable. That psychological mechanism, a desire to maintain the status quo, helps explain the high hurdles that any rational risk assessment must confront.</p><p></p><p><span><span>E</span></span> Organizations face the same hurdles whenever they must cope with major shifts in po' AS nvarchar(max))+N'licy. In his classic examination of the character of high-tech industry, Only the Paranoid Survive, Andrew S. Grove gives a perceptive account of a crisis that took place during his tenure as Chairman of the Intel Corporation. The business environment for Intel and its competitors was transformed by a flood of low-cost memory chips from Japan, and Intel made the difficult decision to abandon its chip-making business. Such a period of change in the outside world is filled with danger, Grove notes, precisely because it is so easy for executives to try to disregard it or misinterpret the mounting evidence showing the profound risks of not making change at the very core. The distinction Cole cites is instructive here: one reason management and staff may not act is that they tend to focus on the potential gains of risking change (which they characterize as small), rather than on the catastrophic losses that risking change could prevent. The period becomes critical for a company, according to Grove, and deadly when unattended to.</p><p></p><p><span><span>F</span></span> Likewise, at the national level, and even the global one, institutions tend to be more willing to take a risk if it means avoiding a loss, than they are if it might confer a gain. Almost everyone, for instance, sees the signs of ecological decay. Reid J. Lifset’s article, ‘Full Accounting’, describes newly developed techniques for clarifying which critical decision would have the highest pay-offs for the environment. Once these ‘leverage points’, as he calls them, are located, many environmental organizations will be willing to risk the resources needed to prevent further deterioration.</p><p></p><p><span><span>G</span></span> More complicated are decisions by nations to invest broadly in educational research. Some governmental leaders are oblivious to the risks posed by social under-investment, because the urgent needs of the present take precedence over actions that would give rise to even more substantial gains in the future. Their attitude is that today’s risks must be manageable, and that tomorrow’s gains are at the very least difficult to measure. Better consideration and decision making for what lies ahead is essential.</p><p></p><p><span><span>H</span></span> Nevertheless, I confess to being an optimist. My glass is generally half full – though that may not help me handle risk. Whether one’s temperament is optimistic or pessimistic, everyone in our scientific and engineering community has'+N' a social responsibility: measure with care and portray with honesty the level of risk involved in any action or decision.</p>'),NULL,NULL,N'',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1837),
(N'e1e8fb64-cc50-5cd1-9a23-902199e1319a',N'36792004-a239-547a-a47f-e32182af2f70',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete each sentence with the correct ending, <span><span>A–G</span></span>, below. </p><p>Write the correct letter, <span><span>A–G</span></span>, in boxes 27–30 on your answer sheet.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',10,0,1,NULL,0,1837),
(N'8687a740-9efc-5a43-8096-27d51245e9b9',N'e1e8fb64-cc50-5cd1-9a23-902199e1319a',0,3,19,N'<p> K. C. Cole points out that avoiding a loss seems more important to people than</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: K. C. Cole chỉ ra rằng tránh mất mát dường như quan trọng hơn đối với mọi người so với ____.</p><p>+ Answer: Mọi người sẵn sàng chấp nhận rủi ro lớn để ngăn ngừa mất mát, nhưng rất ít rủi ro để đạt được lợi ích</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là E: đạt được lợi ích</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1837),
(N'13d42c39-678b-5dfe-b1dd-61fd9153e8c4',N'e1e8fb64-cc50-5cd1-9a23-902199e1319a',0,3,19,N'<p>According to Andrew S. Grove, it can be very dangerous for companies to ignore </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Theo Andrew S. Grove, có thể rất nguy hiểm cho các công ty nếu bỏ qua ____.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn đưa ra ví dụ rằng môi trường kinh doanh của Intel và các đối thủ cạnh tranh đã được biến đổi bởi chip giá rẻ từ Nhật Bản</p><p>-&gt; thời kỳ này nguy hiểm vì các nhà quản lý thường phớt lờ hoặc hiểu sai bằng chứng của những rủi ro từ việc không có sự thay đổi từ gốc (at the very core)</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là F: thay đổi nội tại</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1837),
(N'17579344-c164-5d43-8b22-a8786c3fc08f',N'e1e8fb64-cc50-5cd1-9a23-902199e1319a',0,3,19,N'<p>Reid J. Lifset argues that environmental agencies can benefit from </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Reid J. Lifset lập luận rằng các cơ quan môi trường có thể hưởng lợi từ ____.</p><p>+ Answer: Bài báo của Lifset đã miêu tả phương pháp mới: xác định các điểm đòn bẩy (giải pháp hiệu quả nhất) để ngăn chặn suy thoái môi trường</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là D: phát hiện gần đây về cách giải quyết vấn đề hiệu quả nhất</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1837),
(N'61df9570-94d4-5a19-92e8-4607f0c9bb5e',N'e1e8fb64-cc50-5cd1-9a23-902199e1319a',0,3,19,N'<p>The writer suggests that government leaders need to focus more on   </p>',N'<p>+ Synonym: what lies ahead (khoảng thời gian sắp tới) = future (tương lai)</p><p>+ Interpretation: Tác giả gợi ý rằng các nhà lãnh đạo chính phủ cần tập trung nhiều hơn vào ____.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng các cuộc khủng hoảng phải được quản lý và lợi ích tương lai phải dễ đo lường, qua đó đưa ra kết luận rằng việc ra quyết định cho tương lai là cần thiết</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A: lên kế hoạch cho tương lai</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1837),
(N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',N'36792004-a239-547a-a47f-e32182af2f70',0,2,18,N'<p>Reading Passage 3 has eight paragraphs, <span><span>A–H</span></span>.<br>Which paragraph contains the following information?<br>Write the correct letter, <span><span>A–H</span></span>, in boxes 31–39 on your answer sheet.<br><span><span>NB</span></span> <em>You may use any letter more than once.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',12,0,2,NULL,0,1837),
(N'd7c9db67-5d24-56f4-8d08-6d7338f77085',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p>the areas of study that help us understand risk  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: các lĩnh vực nghiên cứu giúp chúng ta hiểu về rủi ro.</p><p>+ Answer: Các rủi ro có thể được làm rõ (ít nhất một phần) bởi khoa học, công nghệ và toán học</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1837),
(N'c3d9dd99-c6bb-5c74-9245-498d4a3e6576',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p>reference to key actions that might benefit the world </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: đề cập đến những hành động then chốt có thể mang lại lợi ích cho thế giới.</p><p>+ Answer: Một khi những “leverage points” của Lifset đã được định vị, các tổ chức môi trường sẵn sàng đầu tư nguồn lực để ngăn chặn suy thoái môi trường</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là F</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1837),
(N'b3061095-9fac-58a2-b6bb-e7756c532d0b',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p>examples of statistical predictions that the majority of the public react to in the  same way </p>',N'<p>+ Synonyms: dự đoán xác suất mà hầu hết phản ứng giống nhau. Interpretation: ví dụ về dự đoán thống kê mà phần lớn công chúng phản ứng theo cùng một cách.</p><p>+ Answer: Dự báo 60% khả năng mưa hoặc nguy cơ rất nhỏ từ tiêm phòng cúm khiến hầu hết mọi người hành động hợp lý (mang ô, tiêm phòng)</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là C</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1837),
(N'0f7cabdd-9599-5ed8-ad42-04414f4129ed',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p>the variety of contexts in which risk occurs  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation (Vietnamese): sự đa dạng của các bối cảnh mà rủi ro xuất hiện.</p><p>+ Answer: Rủi ro xuất hiện ở khắp mọi nơi trong cuộc sống.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1837),
(N'0fd2ba04-e9c2-58f2-b319-c8ac7475e8d0',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p> mention of a habit that has been proved to be very risky </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: đề cập đến một thói quen đã được chứng minh là rất rủi ro.</p><p>+ Answer: Hút thuốc là thói quen có bằng chứng rõ ràng về rủi ro cao</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1837),
(N'1c61ad6d-a824-59d6-b76f-59845baf5794',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p>reference to market forces motivating change  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: đề cập đến các lực lượng thị trường thúc đẩy sự thay đổi.</p><p>+ Answer: Chip giá rẻ từ Nhật Bản đã buộc Intel phải thay đổi chiến lược kinh doanh</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là E</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1837),
(N'5474e906-0adb-54af-8fdd-8ddcc4743348',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p>the writer’s current attitude towards risk  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: thái độ hiện tại của tác giả đối với rủi ro.</p><p>+ Answer: Tác giả thừa nhận mình là người lạc quan, nhìn nhận mọi việc theo hướng tích cực</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là H</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1837),
(N'08be68bf-1bd8-5926-94a7-d4bf56d0269b',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p>a reason why some individuals avoid making a career change  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation”Lý do tại sao một số cá nhân tránh thay đổi nghề nghiệp.</p><p>+ Answer: Họ thích duy trì hiện trạng hơn là chấp nhận rủi ro để có công việc thú vị hơn, miễn sao là công việc chiện tại vẫn còn thoải mái</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1837),
(N'44074347-fe3d-5ffc-95c8-a7c5cbafd513',N'c2298ee3-7bdd-51f8-9589-6a0eca0fa262',0,3,19,N'<p> how some people over-react to information about risk </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: cách một số người phản ứng thái quá với thông tin về rủi ro.</p><p>+ Answer: Một số người sợ hãi một cách phi lý, ở nhà và tránh tiếp xúc với người khác.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là C</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1837),
(N'acd0ce32-e58f-5136-9e07-6e66804007a6',N'36792004-a239-547a-a47f-e32182af2f70',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A</span>, <span>B</span>, <span>C</span> or <span>D</span>.<br>Write the correct letter in box 40 on your answer sheet. </p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,3,NULL,0,1837),
(N'7d59bba9-f482-5f9b-b099-45dbf0213ba1',N'acd0ce32-e58f-5136-9e07-6e66804007a6',0,3,19,N'<p> At the end of the passage the writer concludes that </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Ở cuối bài đọc, tác giả kết luận rằng ____.</p><p>+ Answer: Tất cả mọi người trong cộng đồng khoa học và kĩ sư có một trách nhiệm: đo lường một cách</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B: các chuyên gia nên trung thực với công chúng về các rủi ro</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1837),
(N'aae99f01-0b6f-52ff-9c6d-7003bcedf26c',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.10.S1678.mp3',N'Vol 4 LISTENING FULL TEST 10',0,7,1,NULL,1,1678),
(N'45f9cb5e-fcf6-581f-b056-17e76f601167',N'aae99f01-0b6f-52ff-9c6d-7003bcedf26c',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>Wedding arrangements - Clarence House Hotel</h2><p>Hello, Clarence House. Oh hello. Um, I''m getting married next year, and I understand you''re licensed to hold weddings at the hotel. Yes, we are. Can I ask what date you were thinking of? Either the 1st or the 7th of June... Right, let me see. Oh, I''m afraid we already have a booking for the 7th, but we still have availability on June the 1st. That would be fine. So how many guests are you going to be? About 80. So for that number, we''d recommend the Oxford Suite. I don''t know if you''ve been to the hotel. </p><p>Yes. In fact, I went to a wedding there a few years ago, and I was really impressed. Very good! I think we were in the Oxford Suite then. Um, the room where we were eating had big windows with really good views. You could see right across the city. That''s it! And you''d have exclusive access to the garden. It''ll be at its best in June, so we could have the photos there. Sure! In fact, you can even have the ceremony there if you want. It makes a lovely background. Oh, I hadn''t thought of that! Yes, that''s certainly an idea. Um, is there somewhere for dancing? Yes, there''s plenty of space in the middle of the dining room. Oh yeah, I remember now.  So just to give you an idea, would you like me to tell you about our special wedding package? Sure, right. So for 80 guests, you''d have the exclusive use of the Oxford Suite, as I said, and then a reception so that would be in the dining room, and that would be a sit-down meal with three courses. And then, towards the end of the evening, when everyone''s beginning to run out of energy, we bring in a buffet. Nothing too complicated, just a selection of sandwiches together with tea or coffee. That''s always very popular. Right. And we''ll want some music. Is that included in the package? No, but we could probably get you a DJ. Or we know a very good band if you''d prefer that. Yes, that''d be better actually. Something to get everyone on their feet. Good. So, apart from the music, you said that''s an all-inclusive package. So, how much does that package come to? £5,995. </p><p>That''s a bit more than the package they quoted for me at the Stanley Hotel. Theirs was £4,500. Yes, but I think if you look at what you''re getting for the money, you''ll find we''re the best value. And it is a big day after all. Yes, you''re probably right. Oh, and I nearly forgot. I''m looking for someone who can make me a really special cake. Can you suggest one? Certainly. There''s a new comp' AS nvarchar(max))+N'any that we''ve started using. They''re very good.  In fact, our wedding coordinator will be able to give you all sorts of advice. We can offer you a free consultation and a tour of the venue. Yes, well, I''d definitely be interested. Right. Well, the coordinator''s name is Sarah Izard. That''s I Z A R D. Now, when would you like to meet her? She''s available from 9 to 5 most weekdays or later if that''s more convenient. I''m free on the 12th of October in the morning sometime or the 15th of October. That would be better, thanks. Should we say 11:30? Fine. And can I just take your name and a contact number? Sure, it''s Debbie.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Hello, Clarence House. Oh hello. Um, I''m getting married next year, and I understand you''re licensed to hold weddings at the hotel. Yes, we are. Can I ask what date you were thinking of? Either the 1st or the 7th of June... Right, let me see. Oh, I''m afraid we already have a booking for the 7th, but we still have availability on June the 1st. That would be fine. So how many guests are you going to be? About 80. So for that number, we''d recommend the Oxford Suite. I don''t know if you''ve been to the hotel. </p><p>Yes. In fact, I went to a wedding there a few years ago, and I was really impressed. Very good! I think we were in the Oxford Suite then. Um, the room where we were eating had big windows with really good views. You could see right across the city. That''s it! And you''d have exclusive access to the garden. It''ll be at its best in June, so we could have the photos there. Sure! In fact, you can even have the ceremony there if you want. It makes a lovely background. Oh, I hadn''t thought of that! Yes, that''s certainly an idea. Um, is there somewhere for dancing? Yes, there''s plenty of space in the middle of the dining room. Oh yeah, I remember now.  So just to give you an idea, would you like me to tell you about our special wedding package? Sure, right. So for 80 guests, you''d have the exclusive use of the Oxford Suite, as I said, and then a reception so that would be in the dining room, and that would be a sit-down meal with three courses. And then, towards the end of the evening, when everyone''s beginning to run out of energy, we bring in a buffet. Nothing too complicated, just a selection of sandwiches together with tea or coffee. That''s always very popular. Right. And we''ll want some music. Is that included in the package? No, but we could probably get you a DJ. Or we know a very good band if you''d prefer that. Yes, that''d be better actually. Something to get everyone on their feet. Good. So, apart from the music, you said that''s an all-inclusive package. So, how much does that package come to? £5,995. </p><p>That''s a bit more than the package they quoted for me at the Stanley Hotel. Theirs was £4,500. Yes, but I think if you look at what you''re getting for the money, you''ll find we''re the best value. And it is a big day after all. Yes, you''re probably right. Oh, and I nearly forgot. I''m looking for someone who can make me a really special cake. Can you suggest one? Certainly. There''s a new company that we''ve started using. They''re very good.  In' AS nvarchar(max))+N' fact, our wedding coordinator will be able to give you all sorts of advice. We can offer you a free consultation and a tour of the venue. Yes, well, I''d definitely be interested. Right. Well, the coordinator''s name is Sarah Izard. That''s I Z A R D. Now, when would you like to meet her? She''s available from 9 to 5 most weekdays or later if that''s more convenient. I''m free on the 12th of October in the morning sometime or the 15th of October. That would be better, thanks. Should we say 11:30? Fine. And can I just take your name and a contact number? Sure, it''s Debbie.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.10.S1678.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1678),
(N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',N'45f9cb5e-fcf6-581f-b056-17e76f601167',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.
Write <span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER </span>for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Wedding arrangements - Clarence House Hotel',11,0,1,NULL,0,1678),
(N'763b7f6f-7ac5-5253-8cda-022210ea067f',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'<h3>Wedding arrangements - Clarence House Hotel</h3><p>Available date: 1st June</p><p>The Oxford Suite has:</p><p>• a dining room with a good view of the }{SPACE}{ </p><p>• a private garden for }{SPACE}{ and for the wedding ceremony</p><p>• an area suitable for dancing</p><p>The wedding package includes:</p><p>• use of the Oxford Suite</p><p>• a three-course meal</p><p>- room arranged with }{SPACE}{ tables</p><p>- table }{SPACE}{ included</p><p>- white tablecloths and chairs</p><p>- personalised printed }{SPACE}{ </p><p>• a late-evening buffet with a choice of }{SPACE}{ and hot drinks</p><p>Music: hotel will organise a band (not included in price)</p><p>Total cost of package: £ }{SPACE}{ </p><p>Can also recommend a }{SPACE}{ supplier (new company)</p><p>Wedding coordinator Sarah }{SPACE}{ </p><p>Date arranged for visit: }{SPACE}{ at 11.30 a.m</p>',N'<p>Question: “a dining room with a good view of the ___.” → Cần danh từ chỉ cảnh quan. Answer: Từ script, đáp án là city. Key vocab: “view = cảnh nhìn thấy”, “across the city = nhìn khắp thành phố”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1678),
(N'3db3c2da-db6d-5019-a46b-f3f92d71e67f',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 2',N'<p>Question: “a private garden for ___ and for the wedding ceremony.” → Cần danh từ hoạt động. Answer: Script nêu rõ photos. Key vocab: “exclusive access = quyền sử dụng riêng”, “ceremony = lễ cưới”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1678),
(N'0e389a4a-567b-5e98-a115-4cbf301fde25',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 3',N'<p>Question: “room arranged with ___ tables.” → Cần tính từ mô tả. Answer: Script khẳng định round tables. Key vocab: “arranged = bố trí”, “round vs long tables”.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1678),
(N'5ecfffa4-9119-58f0-8776-1fdfe8449dd7',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 4',N'<p>Question: “table ___ included.” → Cần danh từ liên quan. Answer: Đáp án là decorations. Key vocab: “included = bao gồm”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,4,NULL,0,1678),
(N'710f55c2-fa15-5440-8c94-83303aeef30a',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 5',N'<p>Question: “personalised printed ___.” → Cần danh từ. Answer: menu. Key vocab: “personalised = cá nhân hóa”, “printed = in ấn”.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,5,NULL,0,1678),
(N'0954a366-483e-55bd-b478-7c1e13b523c8',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 6',N'<p>Question: “a late-evening buffet with a choice of ___ and hot drinks.” → Cần danh từ thức ăn. Answer: sandwiches. Key vocab: “buffet = tiệc đứng”, “selection = lựa chọn”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,6,NULL,0,1678),
(N'a6406215-02aa-5890-8282-10e303e6c3e6',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 7',N'<p>Question: “Total cost of package: £___.” → Cần con số. Answer: 5,995. Key vocab: “package = gói dịch vụ”, “cost = chi phí”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,7,NULL,0,1678),
(N'2aeaedea-ea92-5044-9663-acdccc76b836',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 8',N'<p>Question: “Can also recommend a ___ supplier.” → Cần danh từ chỉ loại dịch vụ. Answer: cake. Key vocab: “supplier = nhà cung cấp”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,8,NULL,0,1678),
(N'cc19e576-5494-50d2-8971-1dde84e5ec5e',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 9',N'<p>Question: “Wedding coordinator Sarah ___.” → Cần họ tên. Answer: Izard. Key vocab: “coordinator = điều phối viên”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,1678),
(N'f6374fb5-6dff-58c1-86ee-a0ad952ced14',N'755ccc78-8f97-5b69-a820-e5189ed7e2c8',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>Question: “Date arranged for visit: ___ at 11.30 a.m.” → Cần ngày tháng. Người trả lời bảo ngày 15 sẽ tốt hơn và hẹn vào 11:30
Answer: 15th October. Key vocab: “arranged = sắp xếp”, “visit = buổi gặp”.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1678),
(N'7f8272c3-ae94-5d12-b6ad-ff72f462abbd',N'aae99f01-0b6f-52ff-9c6d-7003bcedf26c',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>Swimming Lessons</h2><p>Now here''s Ian Hemming with a special report on learning. Two times a week I swim at my local pool for 35 minutes at a time. It''s the only regular exercise I take. There are a couple of things that I''m not happy about, though. I''m fairly achy after swimming, which is odd given that I''ve always assumed it was the perfect impact-free exercise. </p><p>And why is it the men and women who regularly overtake me as I plough up and down the pool appear to be moving so effortlessly? Time to visit Ed Cray, the founder of the Easy Swim School. Ed watched me swim a few lengths. Then, while I caught my breath, he pointed out that although I had a good horizontal position in the water, I was wasting precious energy by slapping my hands into the water. He showed me how to do the stroke without splashing, and after a few practice lengths, I could see the sense of what he was saying.  Ed first qualified as a swimming instructor 10 years ago and started teaching straight away, but it''s just five years since he introduced a new way of teaching swimming, which he called the Cray Method. This was so successful that two years ago, he won an award for innovation in swimming instruction. Ed believes that many people put off traditional swimming lessons at school because, instead of focusing on enjoyment and building confidence, the instructors only seem interested in speed and racing. And swimming, according to Ed, is about much more than that. He also thinks they start children on the wrong strokes. Now Ed and his team run courses for all ages and all levels. Although he''s more than happy to correct the technique of competent swimmers like me, he specialises in teaching adult non-swimmers. He recommends starting with a series of six lessons taken over one week and then following this up, if necessary, with another short weekend course or weekly lessons; this way, people make more progress, he says. Most adult beginners choose to take private lessons as they tend to be lacking in confidence. This works out at £40 for a 35-minute lesson or £60 for a couple sharing.  Ed also has special programmes for teaching beginners from different age groups, such as young children from 3 to 5 years old, babies, and adults. The programmes all have some features in common, but obviously, teaching a 60-year-old and a 6-month-old are two entirely different things. Breathing in the water is something that comes naturally to babies, for example. They spend their first few' AS nvarchar(max))+N' minutes in the water blowing bubbles. This is a skill they''re very good at, and they manage not to swallow any water. </p><p>It''s a similar thing with young children. Adults, on the other hand, are taught through this process on dry land first, so they understand the mechanics of it all. Ed doesn''t like to predict how long it will take to learn to swim independently. He says it''s almost impossible to generalise with adults, who can take anything from four lessons to four months getting the hang of it. Children are generally quicker and can generally manage to swim across the pool without any support after their first four lessons. Ed says it''s possible for babies to float independently after a few lessons, but it depends on the individual.  Easy Swim''s lessons are quite different from anything I''d witnessed before. Although Ed stayed on the side of the pool during my lesson, he actually swims with adult beginners. He also encourages parents to swim with their children during their first few lessons while he demonstrates the activities from the side. Many adults are afraid to put their heads under the water, and this can result in bad posture and bad style. But Ed says that he never puts pressure on adults to put their heads under, although he encourages babies to do this straight away. Older children can be more fearful, so it can take longer for them to gain the confidence to swim underwater. Now, if you''re interested in finding out more about Easy Swim''s lessons, go to their website at www.easyswim.co.uk.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Now here''s Ian Hemming with a special report on learning. Two times a week I swim at my local pool for 35 minutes at a time. It''s the only regular exercise I take. There are a couple of things that I''m not happy about, though. I''m fairly achy after swimming, which is odd given that I''ve always assumed it was the perfect impact-free exercise. </p><p>And why is it the men and women who regularly overtake me as I plough up and down the pool appear to be moving so effortlessly? Time to visit Ed Cray, the founder of the Easy Swim School. Ed watched me swim a few lengths. Then, while I caught my breath, he pointed out that although I had a good horizontal position in the water, I was wasting precious energy by slapping my hands into the water. He showed me how to do the stroke without splashing, and after a few practice lengths, I could see the sense of what he was saying.  Ed first qualified as a swimming instructor 10 years ago and started teaching straight away, but it''s just five years since he introduced a new way of teaching swimming, which he called the Cray Method. This was so successful that two years ago, he won an award for innovation in swimming instruction. Ed believes that many people put off traditional swimming lessons at school because, instead of focusing on enjoyment and building confidence, the instructors only seem interested in speed and racing. And swimming, according to Ed, is about much more than that. He also thinks they start children on the wrong strokes. Now Ed and his team run courses for all ages and all levels. Although he''s more than happy to correct the technique of competent swimmers like me, he specialises in teaching adult non-swimmers. He recommends starting with a series of six lessons taken over one week and then following this up, if necessary, with another short weekend course or weekly lessons; this way, people make more progress, he says. Most adult beginners choose to take private lessons as they tend to be lacking in confidence. This works out at £40 for a 35-minute lesson or £60 for a couple sharing.  Ed also has special programmes for teaching beginners from different age groups, such as young children from 3 to 5 years old, babies, and adults. The programmes all have some features in common, but obviously, teaching a 60-year-old and a 6-month-old are two entirely different things. Breathing in the water is something that comes naturally to babies, for example. They spend their first few minutes in the water blo' AS nvarchar(max))+N'wing bubbles. This is a skill they''re very good at, and they manage not to swallow any water. </p><p>It''s a similar thing with young children. Adults, on the other hand, are taught through this process on dry land first, so they understand the mechanics of it all. Ed doesn''t like to predict how long it will take to learn to swim independently. He says it''s almost impossible to generalise with adults, who can take anything from four lessons to four months getting the hang of it. Children are generally quicker and can generally manage to swim across the pool without any support after their first four lessons. Ed says it''s possible for babies to float independently after a few lessons, but it depends on the individual.  Easy Swim''s lessons are quite different from anything I''d witnessed before. Although Ed stayed on the side of the pool during my lesson, he actually swims with adult beginners. He also encourages parents to swim with their children during their first few lessons while he demonstrates the activities from the side. Many adults are afraid to put their heads under the water, and this can result in bad posture and bad style. But Ed says that he never puts pressure on adults to put their heads under, although he encourages babies to do this straight away. Older children can be more fearful, so it can take longer for them to gain the confidence to swim underwater. Now, if you''re interested in finding out more about Easy Swim''s lessons, go to their website at www.easyswim.co.uk.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.10.S1678.PART2.mp3',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1678),
(N'46ba7c8b-9ab2-5929-8175-728743d7fd34',N'7f8272c3-ae94-5d12-b6ad-ff72f462abbd',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A, B or C.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Swimming Lessons',1,0,1,NULL,0,1678),
(N'a4a7c60c-13d2-59de-8ca1-aec0e2ead475',N'46ba7c8b-9ab2-5929-8175-728743d7fd34',0,3,19,N'<p>What problem about his swimming does lan mention?</p>',N'<p>Đúng vì Ian nói rõ bị đau nhức sau khi bơi.
B sai vì Ian không nói về việc bơi xa, mà chỉ nhắc người khác vượt qua mình dễ dàng.
 C sai vì Ian không hề nhắc tới sợ lặn.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1678),
(N'223af980-d7fc-5ecc-b87a-1d01d158a020',N'46ba7c8b-9ab2-5929-8175-728743d7fd34',0,3,19,N'<p>What was the first thing that Ed Cray corrected?</p>',N'<p>Đúng vì Ed chỉnh động tác tay trước tiên.
 A sai vì không hề đề cập chỉnh động tác chân.
 C sai vì Ed khen vị trí Ian trong nước là tốt, không cần sửa.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1678),
(N'8def8d87-4110-59b0-936e-cf64b260708a',N'46ba7c8b-9ab2-5929-8175-728743d7fd34',0,3,19,N'<p>When was the Cray method first used?</p>',N'<p>Đúng vì Cray Method bắt đầu 5 năm trước.
 A sai vì 2 năm trước chỉ là lúc ông ấy được trao giải, không phải lúc bắt đầu.
 C sai vì 10 năm trước Ed mới đủ chuẩn dạy bơi, chưa có phương pháp này.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1678),
(N'8d9d2dc9-9909-5290-9ea4-ca827e8e5689',N'46ba7c8b-9ab2-5929-8175-728743d7fd34',0,3,19,N'<p>Ed says the problem with school swimming lessons is the focus on</p>',N'<p>Đúng vì Ed phê phán thái độ quá chú trọng thi đua.
 A sai vì ông ấy nói thiếu sự tập trung vào enjoyment, tức là enjoyment không được coi trọng.
 B sai vì không phải thiếu đa dạng kiểu bơi, mà là thiếu tập trung vào niềm vui.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1678),
(N'6421720c-86a3-5a7f-a81e-833ef878d981',N'46ba7c8b-9ab2-5929-8175-728743d7fd34',0,3,19,N'<p>What type of course does Ed advise adult beginners to do first?</p>',N'<p>Đúng vì Ed khuyên người lớn nên bắt đầu với khóa sáu buổi trong 1 tuần.
 B sai vì khóa cuối tuần chỉ được gợi ý sau đó, không phải đầu tiên.
 C sai vì weekly lessons cũng chỉ là tiếp theo, không phải bước khởi đầu.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1678),
(N'9d4b6d45-dbea-5c22-bf03-0d97acb921cd',N'46ba7c8b-9ab2-5929-8175-728743d7fd34',0,3,19,N'<p>What''s the cost of a private lesson for one adult?</p>',N'<p>B Đúng vì giá cho một người là £40. A sai vì không có mức £35 nào được nhắc tới. C sai vì £60 là giá cho hai người cùng học, không phải cho một người.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1678),
(N'389a492d-8d14-5b5d-8cc6-42197bbaccf2',N'7f8272c3-ae94-5d12-b6ad-ff72f462abbd',0,2,18,N'<p>What does Ian say about beginner''s lessons for the following groups of people?
Write the correct letter, <span>A, B or C</span>, next to questions 17-20.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,2,NULL,0,1678),
(N'ec9d69ac-1a4e-5837-adb5-fa660644098e',N'389a492d-8d14-5b5d-8cc6-42197bbaccf2',0,3,19,N'<p>Learners are taught breathing techniques before getting in the water.</p>',N'<p>Ques: “Learners are taught breathing techniques before getting in the water.” - Học viên được dạy kĩ thuật thở trước khi xuống nước </p><p>Cần nghe xem ai được dạy kĩ thuật thở trước khi xuống nưc èAdults, on the other hand, are taught through this process on dry land first – Người lớn, mặt khác được dạy kĩ thuật này trên cạn trước... </p><p>Người lớn (adults) được dạy kỹ thuật thở trên cạn trước khi xuống nước.  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1678),
(N'83fb1453-befc-5294-bece-31a5ab785f85',N'389a492d-8d14-5b5d-8cc6-42197bbaccf2',0,3,19,N'<p>Learners can usually swim independently after four lessons.</p>',N'<p>Learners can usually swim independently after four lessons.”
 → Học viên thường có thể bơi độc lập sau bốn buổi học.
·        Cần nghe xem nhóm nào có thể bơi độc lập sau 4 buổi học.
·        Script: “Children are generally quicker and can generally manage to swim across the pool without any support after their first four lessons.”
·        → Trẻ 3–5 tuổi có thể tự bơi qua bể sau 4 buổi học đầu tiên, không cần hỗ trợ.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1678),
(N'a902e62e-5723-5129-bc61-c3ba75416f90',N'389a492d-8d14-5b5d-8cc6-42197bbaccf2',0,3,19,N'<p>Learners swim with the instructor in the water.</p>',N'<p>Cần nghe xem nhóm nào được giáo viên cùng xuống bơi trực tiếp.
·  Script: “Although Ed stayed on the side of the pool during my lesson, he actually swims with adult beginners.”
·  → Giáo viên trực tiếp xuống bơi cùng với người lớn mới học.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1678),
(N'8a04a880-09c0-5750-9f69-a7b9db7bd398',N'389a492d-8d14-5b5d-8cc6-42197bbaccf2',0,3,19,N'<p>Learners put their heads under the water in the first lesson.</p>',N'<p>Learners put their heads under the water in the first lesson.”
 → Học viên cho đầu xuống nước ngay từ buổi học đầu tiên.
·        Cần nghe xem nhóm nào được khuyến khích cho đầu xuống nước ngay từ đầu.
·        Script: “He never puts pressure on adults to put their heads under, although he encourages babies to do this straight away.”
·        → Người lớn không bị ép cho đầu xuống nước, nhưng em bé được khuyến khích làm điều này ngay lập tức.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1678),
(N'96abce82-2522-5603-8123-bc061f4e974d',N'aae99f01-0b6f-52ff-9c6d-7003bcedf26c',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Group Project</h2><p>What''s up Jen? How are you? Oh, hey Bill. Where''s everybody else? I don''t know. You''re the first to get here. Well, I hope they don''t take too long. I have a class at noon. Why don''t we at least discuss what we''ve completed and what''s left to do? Sounds like a plan. Have you heard anything from Irene? Yeah, Irene told me the experiment is just about done. She''s been practically living in the lab the past week. </p><p>Wow, we owe her a favour, don''t we? You can say that again. Has Kim been helping her? I thought that lab work was not her style. Well, not so much in the lab, but she has started compiling the data for the analysis. Kim is terrific. I hate doing stats. I''ve always been bad at math. I always thought there wasn''t anything to that boys-are-good-at-math theory. Yes, and I''m proof.  Anyway, Kim hasn''t actually run the stats for the project yet, since she still has to wait for the rest of the experiment data. But she''s ready to get started when we get the experiment done. Seriously, without Irene and Kim, we''d be in trouble. Yeah, you''re right about that. So, what about Linda? What''s she been up to? Who knows? All she ever talks about is her fiancé. I haven''t seen or heard from her all week. Well, I hope she turns up soon. We''ll need everyone if we''re going to get this done on time. Yeah, and she knows that Professor Cooper is a hard grader of projects. I''m worried that Professor Cooper is not going to give us a passing grade.  Anyway, we haven''t talked about the introduction yet. Have you finished reading those articles Professor Cooper gave us? Bill, she gave us those two weeks ago. Hey, I''ve been busy. And I haven''t. Come on, Bill. Jen, you know we all depend on you for that. You''re the only person I know that reads every assignment from beginning to end. And you and Professor Cooper often have private discussions about readings in the middle of class. But hey, I''m not complaining. That''s why all the rest of us asked you to be on our team. Oh, so that''s why. </p><p>So it wasn''t because I''m popular, huh? Hey, you''re the best. No one''s going to argue that. Hey, I''m thirsty. You want something to drink? No, I''m good. Okay, I''ll be right back.  They''re still not here? Nope. And I''ve got a class I''ve got to get to. Well, let''s divide up responsibilities, like who''s responsible for each section. It only makes sense that Irene writes up all the materials and methods since she spent the most time with that. Aw, that''s easy. Hey, ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'didn''t you just say we owe her one? Yeah, yeah, you''re right. Irene can do the methodology section. Do you want to do the results? Not at all. I thought Kim would do that. Just because she is good at stats doesn''t mean she''ll be good at writing it up. And besides, you are the one good at copy editing. Well, all the same, I think it''ll be easiest if she does the results section. I''ll edit the whole report.  Come on. That''s asking a lot of you. Well, what part are you going to do, Jen? Isn''t that obvious? Since I''m the one who does all the reading. I''m sure you''ll give us the best literature review, Jen. We''re all counting on you for that. I guess that leaves the discussion section and the bibliography. Yeah. Can you make suggestions on these? </p><p>Okay. But seriously, wouldn''t it make more sense if you do the list of references? There are enough commas, capital letters, and parentheses to keep you happy in there. Yeah, you got a point. It sure does make sense that I do the bibliography. I guess Linda could handle the discussion section, even if she hasn''t been around much. Yeah, but isn''t that like the most important section? Well, what else is she going to do? The whole paper. It''ll serve her right. Then we''d all fail. Okay. But I think we all should start messaging her to make sure she gets busy. The discussion section can be long. What about the abstract? You really don''t want me to do the abstract, do you? That''s not the point. I just think that the abstract should be for whoever finishes first. Right. That is the end of Part 3.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>What''s up Jen? How are you? Oh, hey Bill. Where''s everybody else? I don''t know. You''re the first to get here. Well, I hope they don''t take too long. I have a class at noon. Why don''t we at least discuss what we''ve completed and what''s left to do? Sounds like a plan. Have you heard anything from Irene? Yeah, Irene told me the experiment is just about done. She''s been practically living in the lab the past week. </p><p>Wow, we owe her a favour, don''t we? You can say that again. Has Kim been helping her? I thought that lab work was not her style. Well, not so much in the lab, but she has started compiling the data for the analysis. Kim is terrific. I hate doing stats. I''ve always been bad at math. I always thought there wasn''t anything to that boys-are-good-at-math theory. Yes, and I''m proof.  Anyway, Kim hasn''t actually run the stats for the project yet, since she still has to wait for the rest of the experiment data. But she''s ready to get started when we get the experiment done. Seriously, without Irene and Kim, we''d be in trouble. Yeah, you''re right about that. So, what about Linda? What''s she been up to? Who knows? All she ever talks about is her fiancé. I haven''t seen or heard from her all week. Well, I hope she turns up soon. We''ll need everyone if we''re going to get this done on time. Yeah, and she knows that Professor Cooper is a hard grader of projects. I''m worried that Professor Cooper is not going to give us a passing grade.  Anyway, we haven''t talked about the introduction yet. Have you finished reading those articles Professor Cooper gave us? Bill, she gave us those two weeks ago. Hey, I''ve been busy. And I haven''t. Come on, Bill. Jen, you know we all depend on you for that. You''re the only person I know that reads every assignment from beginning to end. And you and Professor Cooper often have private discussions about readings in the middle of class. But hey, I''m not complaining. That''s why all the rest of us asked you to be on our team. Oh, so that''s why. </p><p>So it wasn''t because I''m popular, huh? Hey, you''re the best. No one''s going to argue that. Hey, I''m thirsty. You want something to drink? No, I''m good. Okay, I''ll be right back.  They''re still not here? Nope. And I''ve got a class I''ve got to get to. Well, let''s divide up responsibilities, like who''s responsible for each section. It only makes sense that Irene writes up all the materials and methods since she spent the most time with that. Aw, that''s easy. Hey, didn''t you just say we' AS nvarchar(max))+N' owe her one? Yeah, yeah, you''re right. Irene can do the methodology section. Do you want to do the results? Not at all. I thought Kim would do that. Just because she is good at stats doesn''t mean she''ll be good at writing it up. And besides, you are the one good at copy editing. Well, all the same, I think it''ll be easiest if she does the results section. I''ll edit the whole report.  Come on. That''s asking a lot of you. Well, what part are you going to do, Jen? Isn''t that obvious? Since I''m the one who does all the reading. I''m sure you''ll give us the best literature review, Jen. We''re all counting on you for that. I guess that leaves the discussion section and the bibliography. Yeah. Can you make suggestions on these? </p><p>Okay. But seriously, wouldn''t it make more sense if you do the list of references? There are enough commas, capital letters, and parentheses to keep you happy in there. Yeah, you got a point. It sure does make sense that I do the bibliography. I guess Linda could handle the discussion section, even if she hasn''t been around much. Yeah, but isn''t that like the most important section? Well, what else is she going to do? The whole paper. It''ll serve her right. Then we''d all fail. Okay. But I think we all should start messaging her to make sure she gets busy. The discussion section can be long. What about the abstract? You really don''t want me to do the abstract, do you? That''s not the point. I just think that the abstract should be for whoever finishes first. Right. That is the end of Part 3.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.10.S1678.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1678),
(N'f227cc1e-aab1-56d8-bb32-5bf7a8a8e130',N'96abce82-2522-5603-8123-bc061f4e974d',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A, B or C.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,1,NULL,0,1678),
(N'4bf327bd-54df-5b0a-b191-4fa36e14aecc',N'f227cc1e-aab1-56d8-bb32-5bf7a8a8e130',0,3,19,N'<p>Jen states that Irene has recently</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Jen states that Irene has recently... ? → Jen nói Irene gần đây đã dành nhiều thời gian ở phòng thí nghiệm. Giải thích: Jen nói “she''s been practically living in the lab” → rõ ràng là đã ở nhiều giờ/ngày trong lab (spent a lot of time).
 A sai (finished planting the experiment): không có câu nào nói Irene mới hoàn tất trồng gì cả.
 B sai (taken an extended break): ngược lại, cô ấy ở lại lab nhiều, không phải nghỉ dài.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1678),
(N'77833657-08d2-5890-af9d-0ffc0fb939ee',N'f227cc1e-aab1-56d8-bb32-5bf7a8a8e130',0,3,19,N'<p>What is Bill''s attitude toward Kim?</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: What is Bill''s attitude toward Kim? → Bill tỏ ra biết ơn/đánh giá cao đóng góp của Kim. Giải thích: Bill mô tả Kim làm việc chuẩn bị dữ liệu và nói “Kim is terrific!” → thái độ tích cực, cảm kích.
 B sai (He thinks she is stylish): không có bình luận liên quan đến phong cách/đẹp.
 C sai (He resents her avoiding lab work): không hề thấy ghét hay oán; Bill còn khen cô ấy.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1678),
(N'd5ed6a20-c232-5a82-a916-e870d33f0485',N'f227cc1e-aab1-56d8-bb32-5bf7a8a8e130',0,3,19,N'<p>Jen believes that their project</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Jen believes that their project ... ? → Jen cho rằng dự án sẽ gặp rắc rối / thất bại nếu thiếu đóng góp của Kim và Irene. Giải thích: câu “without Irene and Kim, we''d be in trouble” thể hiện Jen nghĩ họ rất cần hai người đó.
 A sai (would be easier for boys good at maths): không có ý nói giới tính hay dễ hơn cho những bạn giỏi toán.
 C sai (is almost done because Kim has finished the data analysis): Kim chưa chạy số liệu (script nói cô chưa chạy stats vì chờ dữ liệu), nên không thể kết luận dự án “hầu như xong vì Kim đã hoàn tất phân tích”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1678),
(N'd8d331d9-10bc-550e-a942-96e30f71f637',N'f227cc1e-aab1-56d8-bb32-5bf7a8a8e130',0,3,19,N'<p>Bill and Jen are irritated with Linda because</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Bill and Jen are irritated with Linda because ... ? → Họ bực vì Linda không giữ liên lạc / không xuất hiện. Giải thích: câu “I haven''t seen or heard from her all week” cho thấy Linda không liên lạc/ vắng mặt, đó là lý do khó chịu.
 A sai (she is always late with her work): không có thông tin nói cô ấy thường nộp muộn.
 C sai (she thinks the project is an easy grade): không thấy Linda nói dự án dễ; thậm chí họ lo vì giáo sư Cooper khó cho điểm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1678),
(N'4fe972b5-8f47-57e9-a81b-f76729aaf751',N'f227cc1e-aab1-56d8-bb32-5bf7a8a8e130',0,3,19,N'<p>Jen was asked to join the team because she</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Jen was asked to join the team because she ... ? → Vì Jen đọc kỹ mọi bài tập từ đầu tới cuối (cẩn thận, chịu đọc tài liệu) → nên được mời vào đội. Giải thích: Bill nói rõ “reads every assignment from beginning to end” là lý do họ muốn có Jen.
 A sai (is popular with everyone on the team): dù mọi người có quý Jen, nhưng script nêu rõ lý do thực tế là cô đọc kỹ tài liệu, không phải “chỉ vì nổi tiếng”.
 C sai (enjoys talking to Professor Cooper): có đề cập Jen “often has private discussions” với Prof Cooper, nhưng đó là một đặc điểm — lý do mời là vì cô đọc kỹ và làm bài đầy đủ, không chỉ vì nói chuyện với giảng viên.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1678),
(N'4be81674-593f-58fd-88d9-a5a881acb4cb',N'96abce82-2522-5603-8123-bc061f4e974d',0,2,18,N'<p>Which section will each of the following group members write up?
