Yes, of course. No problem.
Great! So I''ll send you…

</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.1.S1676.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1676),
(N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',N'b5eab2c9-e52e-5b12-a8b8-288826265b4b',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the form below.
Write <span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Sailing Courses',11,0,1,NULL,0,1676),
(N'7d30bc57-1301-56ee-9185-82043ccf563b',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'<h3>Sailing Courses</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p>Example: There will be a Taster Day on <span>22nd June</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><span>Two-day course</span></p> <p>• This covers basic techniques.</p> <p>• Children are always accompanied by their }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Cost: £ }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Five-day course</span></p> <p>• Cost: £125</p> <p>• Children can sail alone from the third day onwards.</p> <p>• Children learn how to sail in different kinds of weather.</p> <p>• Children receive a }{SPACE}{ on the last day.</p> <p><span>Clothing</span></p> <p>• Children should wear warm clothes.</p> <p>• Children must wear }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Children are recommended to bring extra trousers which are }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Children are not allowed to wear }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Additional information</span></p> <p>• Visitors can buy a }{SPACE}{ in the Marina Stores.</p> <p>• There is a free car park next to the }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Send the registration form two weeks before the course.</p> <p>• Remember to include the name of Jacob’s }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• There is an optional form giving permission to take }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Question: “Bọn trẻ luôn được đi cùng với ai trong khóa học 2 ngày?” → Cần danh từ chỉ người. </p><p>Answer: Người nói khẳng định “The kids always sail with the instructor they’ve been allocated”, bọn trẻ luôn được chèo thuyền với người hướng dẫn đã chỉ định → đáp án là instructor. </p><p>Key vocab: accompanied by = đi cùng với; allocated = được phân công.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1676),
(N'5442d358-99f5-5b1a-ad52-6fb8599a4fe0',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 2',N'<p>Question: “Chi phí của khóa học là bao nhiêu?” → Cần một con số (số tiền). </p><p>Answer: Người nói khẳng định “the cost is £65”, phần £125 chỉ để gây nhiễu cho khóa 5 ngày → đáp án là 65. </p><p>Key vocab: cost = giá; heard = nghe nhầm/thông tin gây nhiễu.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1676),
(N'35f7e654-4959-5c9a-90a2-3a832da905b6',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 3',N'<p>Question: “Trẻ em sẽ nhận được gì vào ngày cuối cùng của khóa học?” → Cần danh từ chỉ vật/giấy tờ. </p><p>Answer: Người hỏi trực tiếp “certificate”: chứng chỉ/ bằng cấp và người trả lời xác nhận “Absolutely! We give them out...” → đáp án là certificate. </p><p>Key vocab: certificate = chứng chỉ; final day = ngày cuối.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1676),
(N'504d6e8c-b127-53aa-867c-6f6eac298854',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 4',N'<p>Question: “Trẻ em bắt buộc phải đi gì?” → Cần danh từ chỉ đồ vật (giày dép). </p><p>Answer: Người nói nhấn mạnh “they’ve got to have shoes”: lũ trẻ cần có giày và “mustn’t have bare feet”: và không nên đi chân trần → đáp án là shoes. </p><p>Key vocab: shoes = giày; bare feet = chân trần.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,4,NULL,0,1676),
(N'c4ca26ab-a1d6-5ad7-b7dc-2b76ceedbe69',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 5',N'<p>Question: “Trẻ em được khuyên mang theo loại quần dự phòng nào?” → Cần tính từ bổ nghĩa cho trousers. </p><p>Answer: Người nói nêu rõ “extra pair of trousers, waterproof ones”: cần thêm một cái quần, loại chống thấm nước → đáp án là waterproof. </p><p>Key vocab: waterproof = chống thấm nước; extra pair = thêm một đôi.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,5,NULL,0,1676),
(N'bd05b677-6135-53f7-a545-1cab0aba2a29',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 6',N'<p>Question: “Trẻ em không được phép mặc gì?” → Cần danh từ chỉ loại quần áo. </p><p>Answer: Người nói nhấn mạnh “mustn’t wear jeans because they get extremely heavy when they’re wet”: quần jeans rất nặng khi bị ướt nên trẻ em không được khuyến khích mặc → đáp án là jeans. </p><p>Key vocab: mustn’t = không được phép; heavy when wet = nặng khi bị ướt.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,6,NULL,0,1676),
(N'4f9d3ea0-bdef-526c-9d98-0ba329680808',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 7',N'<p>Question: “Khách tham quan có thể mua gì ở Marina Stores?” → Cần danh từ chỉ đồ ăn nhẹ. </p><p>Answer: “he can get a snack” có thể mua bim bim → đáp án là snack. </p><p>Key vocab: meal = bữa ăn; snack = đồ ăn nhẹ.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,7,NULL,0,1676),
(N'e0248aa8-780b-504a-8dd7-995e3de14454',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 8',N'<p>Question: “Có một bãi đỗ xe miễn phí bên cạnh cái gì?” → Cần danh từ chỉ địa điểm. </p><p>Answer: Người nói nhấn mạnh “no charge for the car park just by the bridge” có một chỗ đỗ xe miễn phí gần cây cầu → đáp án là bridge. </p><p>Key vocab: car park = bãi đỗ xe; by = bên cạnh.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,8,NULL,0,1676),
(N'88a87e79-5659-57ed-aaef-0ed4e257e3f9',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 9',N'<p>Question: “Cần ghi tên của ai vào mẫu đăng ký của Jacob?” → Cần danh từ chỉ người. </p><p>Answer: Người hỏi xác nhận “Will you need to know who his doctor is?” Bạn cần thông tin về bác sĩ của con tôi chứ? → đáp án là doctor. </p><p>Key vocab: surgery = phòng khám; make a note = ghi chú.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,1676),
(N'e3cb4277-2f12-50a3-850d-e7067066c1c0',N'f6534e6a-29da-56d1-83f9-700fb48e419b',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>Question: “Có một mẫu đơn xin cho phép ___ trong khóa học?” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Answer: Script nhấn mạnh "you''re willing for photographs to be taken”: mẫu đơn hỏi về việc hình ảnh của bạn được sử dụng và chụp hoàn toàn tự nguyện trong khóa học → đáp án là photographs. </p><p>Key vocab: optional = tùy chọn; permission = sự cho phép.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1676),
(N'a73c2a99-fe1a-5bf6-82f3-4e994986fef4',N'17964a92-ef3f-532c-a00e-8933bae1af3b',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Working for Johnson Jones</h2><p>(11-15) Good morning! My name''s Mark Bergin. I''m a Human Resources Manager at Johnson Jones, and I''m here to tell you about what it''s like working for one of the country''s best employers. The company has 225 department stores and owns a further 150 supermarkets. At our latest store opening, we had over 9, 000 applicants for 750 posts. So, why do so many people want to work for us? Well, the first thing that attracts them isn''t so much the salary, although that is a little above average. It''s the idea of getting a massive 25% off everything from washing machines to designed handbags. People are prepared to put in long hours to get those kinds of plus. Johnson Jones has been going strong for nearly 150 years and has outlived many of its strongest competitors. Of course, this has something to do with the high-quality products that we sell and our motos better for less, which means customers know they are getting good value for money. But we''re not alone in that. Where we are different is in our approach to making a profit. Other companies think in the short term, whereas we always take a more strategic view looking three or five years ahead. The company isn''t owned by a family or shareholders. It''s a partnership, which means it''s owned by its full-time employees, who are all partners and have a share in the company. In return for the very generous benefits they get, they''re expected to put in extra effort and take an active role in running the company. This means they''re involved in things like making decisions about pension contributions, for example. 00:03:11 Another very attractive reason for working for us is the annual bonus, which we get as a percentage of our salary. Last year, it was 15% about two months'' salary, which is pretty standard. It can go down to 8%, but that''s unusual, and a couple of times it''s even been above 20%. Some people use it to pay off loans; others save it for a deposit on a house. The most popular benefit for partners is the subsidised holiday centres, which are owned by the company. Once a year, partners can book in for up to two weeks. It''s for anyone from cleaners to directors, with a minimum of three months'' service. After six months'' service, partners are entitled to a further subsidy on the cost of their stay. (16-20) 00:04:44 Let me just give you an idea of what''s on offer at the Holiday Centres. The Grange, which is the largest of the Holiday Centres, is used for conferences b' AS nvarchar(max))+N'y the company, and the Board of Directors always have their annual meeting there. It''s all very informal, not like a normal conference centre. Meadow House is located in Kent, which is famous for its apples and other fruit, and it''s a large country house. What''s unusual about it is that, unlike other hotels, you have access to the kitchen, so it feels more like staying with a friend. Coombe Manor is a working farm. There are loads of cows and sheep, none of which actually end up on our supermarket shelves. It''s a paradise for kids as there''s plenty of space to run wild and also an indoor pool. 00:05:40 You can choose to stay in cottages or camp by the lake. The Barn is where I recently celebrated my 25th wedding anniversary. You can rent the whole place out for a large group of up to 40 friends. We got all our food delivered from the nearest Johnson Jones supermarket, so there was no cooking to be done, perfect. All that was missing was the spa, which should be opening next year. The Stable is often hard to get into because it''s so popular. That''s partly because it''s right on the beach with beautiful sea views and only an hour by train from London. Ideal for the weekend. the company. The Sailing Club is located there, which was started by the company''s founder in 1885. So I''m sure you''ll all agree...</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>(11-15) Good morning! My name''s Mark Bergin. I''m a Human Resources Manager at Johnson Jones, and I''m here to tell you about what it''s like working for one of the country''s best employers. The company has 225 department stores and owns a further 150 supermarkets. At our latest store opening, we had over 9, 000 applicants for 750 posts. So, why do so many people want to work for us? Well, the first thing that attracts them isn''t so much the salary, although that is a little above average. It''s the idea of getting a massive 25% off everything from washing machines to designed handbags. People are prepared to put in long hours to get those kinds of plus. Johnson Jones has been going strong for nearly 150 years and has outlived many of its strongest competitors. Of course, this has something to do with the high-quality products that we sell and our motos better for less, which means customers know they are getting good value for money. But we''re not alone in that. Where we are different is in our approach to making a profit. Other companies think in the short term, whereas we always take a more strategic view looking three or five years ahead. The company isn''t owned by a family or shareholders. It''s a partnership, which means it''s owned by its full-time employees, who are all partners and have a share in the company. In return for the very generous benefits they get, they''re expected to put in extra effort and take an active role in running the company. This means they''re involved in things like making decisions about pension contributions, for example. 00:03:11 Another very attractive reason for working for us is the annual bonus, which we get as a percentage of our salary. Last year, it was 15% about two months'' salary, which is pretty standard. It can go down to 8%, but that''s unusual, and a couple of times it''s even been above 20%. Some people use it to pay off loans; others save it for a deposit on a house. The most popular benefit for partners is the subsidised holiday centres, which are owned by the company. Once a year, partners can book in for up to two weeks. It''s for anyone from cleaners to directors, with a minimum of three months'' service. After six months'' service, partners are entitled to a further subsidy on the cost of their stay. (16-20) 00:04:44 Let me just give you an idea of what''s on offer at the Holiday Centres. The Grange, which is the largest of the Holiday Centres, is used for conferences by the company, and the Board of Directors ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'always have their annual meeting there. It''s all very informal, not like a normal conference centre. Meadow House is located in Kent, which is famous for its apples and other fruit, and it''s a large country house. What''s unusual about it is that, unlike other hotels, you have access to the kitchen, so it feels more like staying with a friend. Coombe Manor is a working farm. There are loads of cows and sheep, none of which actually end up on our supermarket shelves. It''s a paradise for kids as there''s plenty of space to run wild and also an indoor pool. 00:05:40 You can choose to stay in cottages or camp by the lake. The Barn is where I recently celebrated my 25th wedding anniversary. You can rent the whole place out for a large group of up to 40 friends. We got all our food delivered from the nearest Johnson Jones supermarket, so there was no cooking to be done, perfect. All that was missing was the spa, which should be opening next year. The Stable is often hard to get into because it''s so popular. That''s partly because it''s right on the beach with beautiful sea views and only an hour by train from London. Ideal for the weekend. the company. The Sailing Club is located there, which was started by the company''s founder in 1885. So I''m sure you''ll all agree...</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.1.S1676.PART2.mp3',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1676),
(N'3776f93e-2c79-5942-a35a-cbcedd534703',N'a73c2a99-fe1a-5bf6-82f3-4e994986fef4',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter <span>A</span>, <span>B</span> or <span>C</span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Working for Johnson Jones',1,0,1,NULL,0,1676),
(N'5ea75442-dad1-589f-9513-d0171be373a3',N'3776f93e-2c79-5942-a35a-cbcedd534703',0,3,19,N'<p>What does Mark say first attracts people to working for Johnson Jones?</p>',N'<p>Question: “Điều đầu tiên thu hút mọi người đến làm việc cho Johnson Jones là gì?” → Cần chọn ý chính được nhấn mạnh. Script: “Vậy tại sao nhiều người muốn làm cho chúng tôi? Điều đầu tiên thu hút họ không hẳn là mức lương, mặc dù hơi trên trung bình; mà là được giảm giá 25% cho mọi thứ, từ máy giặt đến túi xách hàng hiệu.” </p><p>Answer: Người nói phủ nhận yếu tố “pay”-mức lương, khẳng định yếu tố chính là staff discount-ưu đãi cho nhân viên → đáp án B.</p><p>Key vocab: attracts = thu hút; discount
= giảm giá.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1676),
(N'6b1f2f22-aaab-5ce8-baae-95d6190cf2e5',N'3776f93e-2c79-5942-a35a-cbcedd534703',0,3,19,N'<p>Mark says the company is one of the country’s most successful retailers because it</p>',N'<p>Question: “Công ty của là một trongnhững nhà bán lẻ thành công nhất vì điều gì?” → Cần chọn nguyên nhân. </p><p>Script: “Điều làm chúng tôi khác biệt là cách tiếp cận lợi nhuận. Các công ty khác nghĩ ngắn hạn, trong khi chúng tôi luôn có tầm nhìn chiến lược ba đến năm năm phía trước.” </p><p>Answer: Nội dung nhấn mạnh sự khác biệt do “long term planning” - lên kế hoạch dài hạn → đáp án A. </p><p>Key vocab: short term = ngắn hạn; strategic view = tầm nhìn chiến lược.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1676),
(N'528ff9bf-9a51-57bc-9011-7b8451ee4b8d',N'3776f93e-2c79-5942-a35a-cbcedd534703',0,3,19,N'<p>Mark says that because Johnson Jones is a partnership, the staff</p>',N'<p>Question: “Do Johnson Jones là một công ty hợp danh (là loại hình doanh nghiệp có ít nhất hai thành viên hợp danh-chủ sở hữu chung, cùng kinh doanh dưới một tên chung), nhân viên có được điều gì?” → Cần chọn quyền lợi/trách nhiệm của nhân viên. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Công ty không thuộc sở hữu của gia đình hay cổ đông; nó là công ty hợp danh, nghĩa là thuộc về nhân viên toàn thời gian, tất cả đều là đối tác và có cổ phần. Đổi lại cho những lợi ích hào phóng, họ được kỳ vọng phải nỗ lực thêm và tham gia tích cực vào việc điều hành công ty. Điều này có nghĩa là họ tham gia vào các quyết định như đóng góp hưu trí.” </p><p>Answer: Nhấn mạnh rằng nhân viên có thêm trách nhiệm và quyền quyết định (decision-making) → đáp án C. </p><p>Key vocab: active role = vai trò tích cực; responsibility = trách nhiệm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1676),
(N'166b2257-0e88-56f0-83c9-f304f6ab42ae',N'3776f93e-2c79-5942-a35a-cbcedd534703',0,3,19,N'<p>What is the typical annual bonus that partners receive?</p>',N'<p>Question: “Mức thưởng hằng năm điển hình mà đối tác nhận được là bao nhiêu?” → Cần chọn tỉ lệ phần trăm. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Một lý do rất hấp dẫn khác là tiền thưởng hàng năm, được tính theo phần trăm lương. Năm ngoái là 15%, khoảng hai tháng lương, đây là mức khá chuẩn. Nó có thể xuống 8% (hiếm) và đôi khi vượt 20%.” </p><p>Answer: Mức “chuẩn” được nhấn mạnh là 15% → đáp án B. </p><p>Key vocab: bonus = tiền thưởng; standard = chuẩn/thường thấy.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1676),
(N'9b57dc28-737e-54ea-bcb1-052651b0392b',N'3776f93e-2c79-5942-a35a-cbcedd534703',0,3,19,N'<p>To qualify for the leisure and holiday facilities, partners must have worked for
the company for</p>',N'<p>Question: “Để đủ điều kiện sử dụng cơ sở nghỉ dưỡng và giải trí, nhân viên cần làm việc trong bao lâu?” → Cần chọn thời gian cụ thể. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Lợi ích phổ biến nhất là các trung tâm nghỉ dưỡng được công ty tài trợ. Một năm một lần, nhân viên có thể đặt tối đa hai tuần. Nó dành cho bất kỳ ai, từ lao công đến giám đốc, với điều kiện tối thiểu ba tháng làm việc. Sau sáu tháng, họ còn được trợ cấp thêm cho chi phí lưu trú.” </p><p>Answer: Thời gian tối thiểu là three months-ba tháng → đáp án C. </p><p>Key vocab: minimum = tối thiểu; service = thời gian làm việc.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1676),
(N'0767e1c5-3db2-5124-8493-30a604f3559c',N'a73c2a99-fe1a-5bf6-82f3-4e994986fef4',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose <span>FIVE</span> answers from the box and write the correct letter <span>A–G</span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'What information is given about each of the following staff holiday centres?',15,0,2,NULL,0,1676),
(N'83821ed5-bdc5-581a-8ce0-02941ddbfa3a',N'0767e1c5-3db2-5124-8493-30a604f3559c',0,3,19,N'<p>The Grange</p>',N'<p>Script: “The Grange, trung tâm nghỉ dưỡng lớn nhất, được công ty dùng để tổ chức hội nghị, và Hội đồng Giám đốc luôn họp thường niên ở đó. Tất cả đều rất thoải mái, không giống trung tâm hội nghị bình thường.”

Answer: Nhấn mạnh rằng nơi này thường xuyên được ban giám đốc (senior management) sử dụng → đáp án C.

Key vocab: Board of Directors = ban giám đốc; conference = hội nghị.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1676);
GO

Answer: Nhấn mạnh “feels more like staying with a friend” → giống như ở nhà người quen → đáp án F.

Key vocab: access to the kitchen = dùng bếp; staying with a friend = như ở nhà bạn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1676),
(N'799f78ec-9e03-50ad-812d-bc6e7f9bed66',N'0767e1c5-3db2-5124-8493-30a604f3559c',0,3,19,N'<p>Coombe Manor</p>',N'<p>Script: “Coombe Manor là một trang trại đang hoạt động. Có rất nhiều bò và cừu... Đây là thiên đường cho trẻ em vì có nhiều không gian chơi đùa và cả hồ bơi trong nhà.”

Answer: Nhấn mạnh sự phù hợp cho trẻ em → thích hợp cho các gia đình có trẻ nhỏ → đáp án B.

Key vocab: paradise for kids = thiên đường cho trẻ em; plenty of space = nhiều không gian.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1676),
(N'1e3c48b4-0179-55aa-8b93-c29ddccfb852',N'0767e1c5-3db2-5124-8493-30a604f3559c',0,3,19,N'<p>The Barn</p><p>Script: “The Barn là nơi tôi vừa tổ chức kỷ niệm 25 năm ngày cưới. Bạn có thể thuê toàn bộ nơi này cho nhóm lớn tới 40 người. Chúng tôi đặt đồ ăn từ siêu thị Johnson Jones gần nhất.”

Answer: Rõ ràng nơi này dùng để tổ chức tiệc/kỷ niệm → đáp án A.

Key vocab: celebrate anniversary = tổ chức kỷ niệm; rent the whole place = thuê cả chỗ.</p>',N'',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1676),
(N'8bb9e12c-6315-5c54-b79f-2b92cadd0fb9',N'0767e1c5-3db2-5124-8493-30a604f3559c',0,3,19,N'<p>The Stable</p><p></p>',N'<p>Script: “The Stable thường khó đặt chỗ vì quá phổ biến. Một phần vì nó nằm ngay bãi biển, có view biển đẹp và chỉ cách London một giờ tàu. Lý tưởng cho cuối tuần.”

Answer: Nhấn mạnh “ideal for weekend” → phù hợp cho kỳ nghỉ ngắn → đáp án G.

Key vocab: ideal for weekend = lý tưởng cho cuối tuần; popular = được ưa chuộng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1676),
(N'b54c4ae6-e822-5934-a965-9dd7710495c7',N'17964a92-ef3f-532c-a00e-8933bae1af3b',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Organising a debate</h2><p>Right now, you wanted to organise a formal discussion of some sort in the class, is that right? Daniel: Yes, we thought it would be really good to end our psychology course with a formal debate, you know, where there''s a statement and people argue for or against it. Good idea. Where did the enthusiasm come from? Have either of you organised debates before? Fiona: No, but one of my friends did in his last year at secondary school, and he says he got a lot out of doing it, and I know they have debates in lots of other departments here at the university. I see. So what do you think the students and the others would get out of this. 00:01:09 Well, a debate will encourage people to study all sides of the subject matter, and I also think listening to other people''s ideas can have a focusing effect on your own. Sometimes you kind of don''t know what you truly believe until you hear something you disagree with. OK, what about the topic for the debate? We''ve got three to choose from. We''ll show you in a minute to check they''re OK. At first, we thought we''d ask the other students to suggest something, but we were afraid we''d get 30 different topics. So instead, we brainstormed some, just the two of us, then made a shortlist, and we''ll get them to vote for the one they like best. Right? But the thing is, if people don''t know how debates work, they could feel at a disadvantage. I mean, it wouldn''t affect their grades or anything, but it could put them off. So we thought we should have a meeting beforehand to make sure everyone''s clear about what''s involved in a debate and how to prepare for it. I think that''s a good idea. And we thought we could establish some key points of a successful debate there, like that debates are effective methods for considering an issue and that you need to understand both sides of an argument in order to support one side effectively. We think it''ll be best if we can use an issue that the students already have strong opinions about. Then some people will be happy to speak in support of it and others to oppose it. Right. What else do you want to do at the meeting? 00:02:41 Well, we''ve got to think about how people will do the background research for the debate. We could organise everyone into two teams. That should save time for the main speakers; they won''t have to do it on their own. And we''ll need to decide on some rules, like how long each person speaks for, and to select a chair for the debate, some' AS nvarchar(max))+N'one who''s really confident. Fine. Now I think you should have some sort of follow-up to the debate. After it''s over, I''d like you to get everyone to write a short essay focusing on what they gained from participating in it. And in turn, that can feed into the work we''ve been doing on learning and motivation. But would you be willing to read them and report back? I mean, give some feedback on them. I wouldn''t have made the suggestion if I wasn''t. Thanks. OK, now let''s think a bit more about the meeting. You need to decide exactly how you''ll introduce the students to different features of a debate. Well, first of all we need to inform everyone about what actually happens at a debate, the procedures, and so on. We could talk about that and write the main information on the board. Or instead of that, we could make a summary of the main stages and hand that out. Then talk it through. Yes, OK, that''s better. OK, but we do need to talk about body language as well. Yes, that''s a very important tool. I''ve got an interesting article on that with some good diagrams of techniques for things like eye contact. I''ll make some copies for you to give out. Great. Then we need to think about where people will find the information on the debate topic. So they''ll need to know where to look for the information. We could compile a list of sources, textbooks, and articles, and so on. We could, but couldn''t people offer their own suggestions and advice on that at the meeting instead? Yes, get everyone involved. Right. What about preparation for asking and answering questions? We could try to demonstrate that, but I''m not sure how. Maybe we could put up a list of points on the board, then discuss them. Yes, that sounds better. OK, well let''s meet up again.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Right now, you wanted to organise a formal discussion of some sort in the class, is that right? Daniel: Yes, we thought it would be really good to end our psychology course with a formal debate, you know, where there''s a statement and people argue for or against it. Good idea. Where did the enthusiasm come from? Have either of you organised debates before? Fiona: No, but one of my friends did in his last year at secondary school, and he says he got a lot out of doing it, and I know they have debates in lots of other departments here at the university. I see. So what do you think the students and the others would get out of this. 00:01:09 Well, a debate will encourage people to study all sides of the subject matter, and I also think listening to other people''s ideas can have a focusing effect on your own. Sometimes you kind of don''t know what you truly believe until you hear something you disagree with. OK, what about the topic for the debate? We''ve got three to choose from. We''ll show you in a minute to check they''re OK. At first, we thought we''d ask the other students to suggest something, but we were afraid we''d get 30 different topics. So instead, we brainstormed some, just the two of us, then made a shortlist, and we''ll get them to vote for the one they like best. Right? But the thing is, if people don''t know how debates work, they could feel at a disadvantage. I mean, it wouldn''t affect their grades or anything, but it could put them off. So we thought we should have a meeting beforehand to make sure everyone''s clear about what''s involved in a debate and how to prepare for it. I think that''s a good idea. And we thought we could establish some key points of a successful debate there, like that debates are effective methods for considering an issue and that you need to understand both sides of an argument in order to support one side effectively. We think it''ll be best if we can use an issue that the students already have strong opinions about. Then some people will be happy to speak in support of it and others to oppose it. Right. What else do you want to do at the meeting? 00:02:41 Well, we''ve got to think about how people will do the background research for the debate. We could organise everyone into two teams. That should save time for the main speakers; they won''t have to do it on their own. And we''ll need to decide on some rules, like how long each person speaks for, and to select a chair for the debate, someone who''s really confident. Fine. No' AS nvarchar(max))+N'w I think you should have some sort of follow-up to the debate. After it''s over, I''d like you to get everyone to write a short essay focusing on what they gained from participating in it. And in turn, that can feed into the work we''ve been doing on learning and motivation. But would you be willing to read them and report back? I mean, give some feedback on them. I wouldn''t have made the suggestion if I wasn''t. Thanks. OK, now let''s think a bit more about the meeting. You need to decide exactly how you''ll introduce the students to different features of a debate. Well, first of all we need to inform everyone about what actually happens at a debate, the procedures, and so on. We could talk about that and write the main information on the board. Or instead of that, we could make a summary of the main stages and hand that out. Then talk it through. Yes, OK, that''s better. OK, but we do need to talk about body language as well. Yes, that''s a very important tool. I''ve got an interesting article on that with some good diagrams of techniques for things like eye contact. I''ll make some copies for you to give out. Great. Then we need to think about where people will find the information on the debate topic. So they''ll need to know where to look for the information. We could compile a list of sources, textbooks, and articles, and so on. We could, but couldn''t people offer their own suggestions and advice on that at the meeting instead? Yes, get everyone involved. Right. What about preparation for asking and answering questions? We could try to demonstrate that, but I''m not sure how. Maybe we could put up a list of points on the board, then discuss them. Yes, that sounds better. OK, well let''s meet up again.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.1.S1676.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1676),
(N'd8f53b15-884c-5023-92a2-630b4b191159',N'b54c4ae6-e822-5934-a965-9dd7710495c7',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter <span>A</span>, <span>B</span> or <span>C</span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Organising a debate',1,0,1,NULL,0,1676),
(N'649b54fd-23c4-5b19-ae2f-94b94572c2a3',N'd8f53b15-884c-5023-92a2-630b4b191159',0,3,19,N'<p>Why is Fiona keen to organise a debate?</p>',N'<p>Question: “Tại sao Fiona muốn tổ chức một cuộc tranh luận?” → Cần chọn lý do cá nhân. </p><p>Script: “Fiona: Không, nhưng một người bạn của tôi đã tổ chức khi còn học phổ thông, và cậu ấy nói đã học hỏi được nhiều điều, và tôi cũng biết ở nhiều khoa trong trường đại học này cũng có tổ chức tranh luận.” </p><p>Answer: Fiona nói cô nghe bạn kể lại và biết nhiều khoa khác có, chứ bản thân chưa từng tổ chức → đáp án C. </p><p>Key vocab: friend = bạn; have debates = có tổ chức tranh luận.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1676),
(N'265182a1-94ae-5d6f-8fe8-a87d08945b6d',N'd8f53b15-884c-5023-92a2-630b4b191159',0,3,19,N'<p>Fiona says that an advantage of debating is that it can</p>',N'<p>Question: “Fiona nói lợi ích của tranh luận là gì?” → Cần chọn kỹ năng. </p><p>Script: “Một cuộc tranh luận sẽ khuyến khích mọi người nghiên cứu các mặt của vấn đề, và tôi cũng nghĩ rằng lắng nghe ý kiến người khác sẽ giúp bạn tập trung hơn vào quan điểm của mình.” </p><p>Answer: Nhấn mạnh lợi ích của listening skills- lắng nghe → đáp án B. </p><p>Key vocab: listening = lắng nghe; focusing effect = tác dụng tập trung.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1676),
(N'5fd79edf-fff6-5e5b-bd3f-b0eebe5e4595',N'd8f53b15-884c-5023-92a2-630b4b191159',0,3,19,N'<p>How will the debate topic be selected?</p>',N'<p>Question: “Chủ đề tranh luận sẽ được chọn như thế nào?”</p><p>Script: “Ban đầu, chúng tôi định nhờ các sinh viên khác gợi ý, nhưng sợ sẽ có tới 30 chủ đề khác nhau. Vì vậy, chỉ có hai chúng tôi cùng động não, cùng lập danh sách ngắn, và sẽ để họ bỏ phiếu chọn chủ đề họ thích nhất.” </p><p>Answer: Sinh viên khác chọn từ đề xuất của Daniel và Fiona → đáp án B. </p><p>Key vocab: brainstorm = động não; vote = bỏ phiếu.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1676),
(N'78494bc6-1ee7-56ce-beab-b5c9a52c7aae',N'd8f53b15-884c-5023-92a2-630b4b191159',0,3,19,N'<p>Daniel and Fiona think one key to a successful debate is for students to</p>',N'<p>Question: “Daniel và Fiona nghĩ chìa khóa của một cuộc tranh luận thành công là gì?” </p><p>Script: “Chúng tôi nghĩ sẽ tốt nhất nếu dùng một chủ đề mà sinh viên đã có ý kiến mạnh. Khi đó một số sinh viên sẽ vui vẻ ủng hộ, và có số khác phản đối.” </p><p>Answer: Nhấn mạnh tầm quan trọng của một chủ đề thực sự liên quan tới sinh viên → đáp án A. </p><p>Key vocab: strong opinions = ý kiến mạnh mẽ; oppose = phản đối.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1676),
(N'ba59902e-f04a-539d-b00c-4c208a47b593',N'd8f53b15-884c-5023-92a2-630b4b191159',0,3,19,N'<p>Daniel thinks it would be good for the students to be in two teams because it
would</p>',N'<p>Question: “Daniel nghĩ việc chia thành hai nhóm có lợi ở điểm nào?” </p><p>Script: “Chúng tôi phải nghĩ cách để mọi người làm nghiên cứu nền. Có thể chia thành hai nhóm. Điều này sẽ tiết kiệm thời gian cho người phát biểu vì họ không phải làm một mình.” </p><p>Answer: Ý chính là chia sẻ công việc → đáp án C. </p><p>Key vocab: save time = tiết kiệm thời gian; on their own = một mình.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1676),
(N'b518571b-a5b9-537e-a751-78e3ba7de19d',N'd8f53b15-884c-5023-92a2-630b4b191159',0,3,19,N'<p>What does the lecturer agree to do after the debate?</p>',N'<p>Question: “Giảng viên gợi ý làm gì sau cuộc tranh luận?” </p><p>Script: “Bây giờ tôi nghĩ các bạn nên có phần tiếp nối sau tranh luận. Tôi muốn mọi người viết một bài luận ngắn về điều đã học... Nhưng các bạn có sẵn lòng đọc và viết báo cáo lại cho tôi không? Ý tôi là phản hồi về chúng.” </p><p>Answer: Giảng viên đề nghị Daniel và Fiona viết báo cáo phản hồi → đáp án B. </p><p>Key vocab: report back = báo cáo lại; feedback = phản hồi.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1676),
(N'd4c77162-5e39-536b-a427-55f4aa6e1843',N'b54c4ae6-e822-5934-a965-9dd7710495c7',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose <span>FOUR</span> answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span>A–F</span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'What will Daniel and Fiona do at the meeting to help students with debate?',15,0,2,NULL,0,1676),
(N'27001066-e0d7-5ddf-b0c3-19d0f2180ab4',N'd4c77162-5e39-536b-a427-55f4aa6e1843',0,3,19,N'<p>understanding debating procedures</p>',N'<p>Question: “Làm thế nào để giúp sinh viên hiểu quy trình tranh luận?” → Cần chọn cách thức truyền đạt.
Script (dịch): “Trước hết, chúng ta cần cho mọi người biết điều gì thực sự diễn ra trong một cuộc tranh luận, quy trình, v.v. Chúng ta có thể nói và viết lên bảng. Hoặc thay vào đó, ta có thể tóm tắt các bước chính và phát cho họ. Sau đó thảo luận.”
Answer: Fiona chọn phương án làm bản tóm tắt → đáp án B.
Key vocab: hand out = phát; summary = tóm tắt.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1676),
(N'3c88618a-897c-5d06-821e-dfd01801c77c',N'd4c77162-5e39-536b-a427-55f4aa6e1843',0,3,19,N'<p>using body language</p>',N'<p>Question: “Làm thế nào để sinh viên học cách sử dụng ngôn ngữ cơ thể?”
Script (dịch): “Chúng ta cũng cần nói về ngôn ngữ cơ thể. Vâng, đó là một công cụ rất quan trọng. Cô có một bài viết thú vị về chủ đề này với một số sơ đồ kỹ thuật như giao tiếp bằng mắt. Cô sẽ photo và phát cho các bạn.”
Answer: Sẽ phát bản photo bài viết → đáp án D.
Key vocab: article = bài viết; copies = bản photo.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1676),
(N'fba2ef11-2747-5972-9791-26669e5dd743',N'd4c77162-5e39-536b-a427-55f4aa6e1843',0,3,19,N'<p>accessing resources</p>',N'<p>Question: “Làm thế nào để sinh viên biết cách tìm tài liệu cho chủ đề tranh luận?”
Script (dịch): “Sau đó chúng ta cần nghĩ xem mọi người sẽ tìm thông tin ở đâu. … Chúng ta có thể lập danh sách nguồn, sách, bài báo. Hoặc để mọi người tự đề xuất và chia sẻ ý kiến trong buổi họp. Ừ, như vậy mọi người sẽ cùng tham gia.”
Answer: Quyết định cho sinh viên đưa ra đề xuất → đáp án A.
Key vocab: suggestions = đề xuất; advice = lời khuyên
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1676),
(N'0d9052aa-6def-5f77-b774-90bad5c0b447',N'd4c77162-5e39-536b-a427-55f4aa6e1843',0,3,19,N'<p>asking and answering questions</p>',N'<p>Question: “Làm thế nào để chuẩn bị cho việc hỏi và trả lời câu hỏi?”
Script (dịch): “Thế còn việc chuẩn bị cho hỏi và trả lời câu hỏi? Chúng ta có thể thử diễn minh họa, nhưng không chắc cách nào. Có lẽ ta nên viết một danh sách điểm chính lên bảng rồi thảo luận. Ừ, nghe có vẻ tốt hơn.”
Answer: Chọn cách viết lên bảng → đáp án C.
Key vocab: list of points = danh sách điểm chính; board = bảng.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1676),
(N'969319c7-a10a-50e3-8d4e-050b0bf69be9',N'17964a92-ef3f-532c-a00e-8933bae1af3b',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Crisis Communication Theory</h2><p>Today, I want to talk about how organisations can respond to a crisis, which happens when a serious problem occurs suddenly and must be dealt with quickly. I will talk about a theory that tells crisis managers how to communicate in different kinds of crisis. Some people ask why we need theory in crisis communication. Isn''t logic the answer? Yes, it is sometimes, but organisations often handle crisis communication badly. What we find is that, in reality, much of the advice that organisations receive on how to respond to a crisis is just a guess, theory, and research allows us to take those suggestions and test them. Then managers can be given advice based on actual evidence of what works and what doesn''t. The crisis theory has categorised three general types of crisis. One type is a crisis where the organisation is a victim and has very little responsibility. For example, this kind of crisis could be caused by a rumor. The second kind is accidental. There isn''t much responsibility because there are other outside factors that contributed to it and the third one and the most difficult to actually manage is a preventable crisis. In this kind of crisis, those affected believe strongly that a mistake was made within the organisation. The type of crisis is important because the more people think the organisation is responsible for the crisis, the angrier they become at the organisation. The theory begins by suggesting a two-part basic response that can be used in all kinds of crises. The first part is to provide information to those affected so that they can protect themselves from further harm. Many crises pose a risk to people. For example, if there is a chemical release. People might have to evacuate an area, or if there''s a product which could be harmful, people need to be told about it so they don''t buy it or, so if they have bought it, they can return it. The second part of the basic response is to communicate the action the organisation is planning in order to prevent another crisis. This is important because those affected by a crisis are often concerned that it is going to happen again. Beyond this, crisis managers also have other tools to shape how people think about the crisis. Crisis managers can use several strategies to do this. One strategy is to diminish the crisis. If crisis managers have proof which is convincing they can argue that a crisis is not as bad as people think. If they can argue this succ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'essfully; the harmful effects are reduced. Another way to shape thinking is to give excuses, which emphasize a lack of intents. Therefore, people will see the crisis as an accident. This is important because an accidental crisis is much easier to manage than an intentional crisis. Crisis managers can also protect the organization''s reputation when there is a major crisis. Their first goal is to reduce the negative opinions generated by the crisis. This can be done by presenting new positive facts and figures about the organization or by reminding people of past good works done by the organization. Rebuild strategies are useful for preventable crises or when there has been a series of accidental crises, as these crises present a severe threat to the perceived reliability of the organization. For example, a crisis where the organization is clearly at fault or broke a law can be very damaging; it can result in loss of business. Rebuild strategies can also be used to reduce the impact of the crisis. To do this, crisis managers say and do things to benefit those affected by the crisis. The reputation of the organization can also be protected in practical ways; the problems can sometimes. be addressed by offering an apology to those affected by the crisis. This may be sufficient action when the problem is relatively minor. However, if the crisis is very severe, the most common strategy is to offer compensation to those affected. So to summarise, crisis communication theory is interesting to managers as it gives positive guidance on how to respond. It can help them figure out which strategy is.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Today, I want to talk about how organisations can respond to a crisis, which happens when a serious problem occurs suddenly and must be dealt with quickly. I will talk about a theory that tells crisis managers how to communicate in different kinds of crisis. Some people ask why we need theory in crisis communication. Isn''t logic the answer? Yes, it is sometimes, but organisations often handle crisis communication badly. What we find is that, in reality, much of the advice that organisations receive on how to respond to a crisis is just a guess, theory, and research allows us to take those suggestions and test them. Then managers can be given advice based on actual evidence of what works and what doesn''t. The crisis theory has categorised three general types of crisis. One type is a crisis where the organisation is a victim and has very little responsibility. For example, this kind of crisis could be caused by a rumor. The second kind is accidental. There isn''t much responsibility because there are other outside factors that contributed to it and the third one and the most difficult to actually manage is a preventable crisis. In this kind of crisis, those affected believe strongly that a mistake was made within the organisation. The type of crisis is important because the more people think the organisation is responsible for the crisis, the angrier they become at the organisation. The theory begins by suggesting a two-part basic response that can be used in all kinds of crises. The first part is to provide information to those affected so that they can protect themselves from further harm. Many crises pose a risk to people. For example, if there is a chemical release. People might have to evacuate an area, or if there''s a product which could be harmful, people need to be told about it so they don''t buy it or, so if they have bought it, they can return it. The second part of the basic response is to communicate the action the organisation is planning in order to prevent another crisis. This is important because those affected by a crisis are often concerned that it is going to happen again. Beyond this, crisis managers also have other tools to shape how people think about the crisis. Crisis managers can use several strategies to do this. One strategy is to diminish the crisis. If crisis managers have proof which is convincing they can argue that a crisis is not as bad as people think. If they can argue this successfully; the harmful effects are reduced. A' AS nvarchar(max))+N'nother way to shape thinking is to give excuses, which emphasize a lack of intents. Therefore, people will see the crisis as an accident. This is important because an accidental crisis is much easier to manage than an intentional crisis. Crisis managers can also protect the organization''s reputation when there is a major crisis. Their first goal is to reduce the negative opinions generated by the crisis. This can be done by presenting new positive facts and figures about the organization or by reminding people of past good works done by the organization. Rebuild strategies are useful for preventable crises or when there has been a series of accidental crises, as these crises present a severe threat to the perceived reliability of the organization. For example, a crisis where the organization is clearly at fault or broke a law can be very damaging; it can result in loss of business. Rebuild strategies can also be used to reduce the impact of the crisis. To do this, crisis managers say and do things to benefit those affected by the crisis. The reputation of the organization can also be protected in practical ways; the problems can sometimes. be addressed by offering an apology to those affected by the crisis. This may be sufficient action when the problem is relatively minor. However, if the crisis is very severe, the most common strategy is to offer compensation to those affected. So to summarise, crisis communication theory is interesting to managers as it gives positive guidance on how to respond. It can help them figure out which strategy is.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.1.S1676.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1676),
(N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',N'969319c7-a10a-50e3-8d4e-050b0bf69be9',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the table below.</p><p>Write <span>ONE WORD ONLY</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Crisis Communication Theory',11,0,1,NULL,0,1676),
(N'f1fe0f65-a48a-573e-ab26-d9a1e9972e06',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'<h3>Crisis Communication Theory</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>Why do we need theory?</span></p> <p>• Some people think using }{SPACE}{ is better than theory.</p> <p>• But a lot of advice is simply a }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Types of crisis</span></p> <p>• The organisation can be a victim – e.g. due to a }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Crisis can be accidental – caused by external factors</p> <p>• Crisis can be preventable – resulting from a }{SPACE}{ in the organisation.</p> <p><span>Suggested response for all crises</span></p> <p>• Give information to prevent any more damage</p> <p>• Communicate what }{SPACE}{ is to be taken</p> <p><span>Strategies to influence people’s thinking</span></p> <p>• Diminish the crisis</p> <p>– provide }{SPACE}{ that the crisis is not so bad</p> <p>• Give excuses for the crisis</p> <p>– highlight that it was not intentional</p> <p><span>Protecting the organisation’s reputation</span></p> <p>• Initial objective is to lessen }{SPACE}{ opinions</p> <p>• Rebuild strategies are important when:</p> <p>– there is a serious }{SPACE}{ to the organisation’s reputation</p> <p>– the impact of the crisis needs lessening</p> <p>• Things can be improved by providing an }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• In serious cases, }{SPACE}{ is usually offered</p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Question: “Một số người nghĩ dùng ... tốt hơn lý thuyết.” → Cần danh từ chỉ phương pháp. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Một số người hỏi vì sao cần lý thuyết trong khủng hoảng truyền thông. Logic có phải câu trả lời không? Đôi khi đúng, nhưng các tổ chức thường xử lý kém.” </p><p>Answer: Cụm hỏi–đáp nhấn mạnh “logic” → logic. </p><p>Key vocab: logic = lý lẽ; theory = lý thuyết.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1676),
(N'0d001912-2540-5e3e-9b05-aedfe679d98c',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>Question: “Nhưng nhiều lời khuyên thực chất chỉ là ...” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Thực tế nhiều lời khuyên chỉ là phỏng đoán, còn lý thuyết và nghiên cứu cho phép kiểm chứng.” </p><p>Answer: Từ khóa “just a guess” → guess. Key vocab: guess = phỏng đoán; evidence = bằng chứng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1676),
(N'483aae60-0025-5d24-a5db-28ebe9c5e196',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 33',N'<p>Question: “Tổ chức có thể là nạn nhân, ví dụ do ...” → Cần danh từ nguyên nhân. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Một loại là khủng hoảng khi tổ chức là nạn nhân... ví dụ do tin đồn gây ra.” </p><p>Answer: rumour (script dùng rumor). </p><p>Key vocab: rumour/rumor = tin đồn; victim = nạn nhân.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1676),
(N'a4ebdb26-2e95-5e02-ae9a-bf4c349dfa1b',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>Question: “Khủng hoảng có thể phòng ngừa, do một ... trong tổ chức.” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Khó nhất là khủng hoảng có thể phòng ngừa; người bị ảnh hưởng tin rằng đã có sai lầm trong tổ chức.” </p><p>Answer: mistake. </p><p>Key vocab: preventable = có thể phòng ngừa; mistake = sai lầm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1676),
(N'e0478ad4-3192-5e06-b761-282ab0de6f02',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>Question: “Truyền đạt .... sẽ được thực hiện.” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Phần thứ hai là thông báo hành động mà tổ chức dự định làm để ngăn khủng hoảng tái diễn. Điều này quan trọng vì người bị ảnh hưởng lo nó xảy ra lần nữa.” </p><p>Answer: action. </p><p>Key vocab: communicate = truyền đạt; prevent = ngăn chặn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1676),
(N'7c3e8b85-ab2b-5780-8a84-22fa8d42c45d',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>Question: “Giảm mức độ khủng hoảng – đưa ra ... rằng khủng hoảng không quá tệ.” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Một chiến lược là giảm nhẹ khủng hoảng. Nếu có bằng chứng thuyết phục, họ có thể lập luận khủng hoảng không tệ như nghĩ. Nếu thuyết phục, tác hại sẽ giảm.” </p><p>Answer: proof. </p><p>Key vocab: proof = bằng chứng; diminish = làm giảm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1676),
(N'54f954a0-2fc2-5ad7-8e42-f2649ff0ac70',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>Question: “Mục tiêu ban đầu là giảm ý kiến ...” → Cần tính từ. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Mục tiêu đầu tiên là giảm các ý kiến tiêu cực do khủng hoảng tạo ra. Có thể làm bằng cách đưa số liệu tích cực.” </p><p>Answer: negative. </p><p>Key vocab: reduce = giảm; opinions = ý kiến.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1676),
(N'f3d213a0-9994-51b7-9942-6e6ce37a78b9',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 38',N'<p>Question: “Có mối ... nghiêm trọng đối với danh tiếng.” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Chiến lược phục hồi hữu ích... vì các khủng hoảng này là mối đe dọa nghiêm trọng tới độ tin cậy cảm nhận. Ví dụ, khủng hoảng do tổ chức rõ ràng sai phạm...” </p><p>Answer: threat. </p><p>Key vocab: severe = nghiêm trọng; reliability = độ tin cậy.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1676),
(N'7f52acc9-db55-53bc-91ad-d9d20da0d5ff',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>Question: “Có thể cải thiện bằng cách đưa ra một ...” → Cần danh từ bắt đầu bằng nguyên âm (an ...). </p><p>Script (dịch): “Danh tiếng cũng có thể bảo vệ theo cách thực tế: đôi khi giải quyết bằng cách đưa ra lời xin lỗi. Có thể đã đủ nếu vấn đề nhỏ.” </p><p>Answer: apology. </p><p>Key vocab: apology = lời xin lỗi; sufficient = đủ.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1676),
(N'a7e4ea93-36f5-5f4f-8841-80caeeefce37',N'338e7218-8c49-57ef-99b6-10e66e7b513f',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>Question: “Trong trường hợp nghiêm trọng, thường đưa ...” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Script (dịch): “Tuy nhiên, nếu khủng hoảng rất nghiêm trọng, chiến lược phổ biến nhất là cung cấp bồi thường cho người bị ảnh hưởng.” </p><p>Answer: compensation. </p><p>Key vocab: compensation = bồi thường; severe = nghiêm trọng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1676),
(N'72667ae6-6505-5fa4-88e9-6c5d172ea855',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.5.S1673.mp3',N'Vol 3 LISTENING FULL TEST 5',0,7,1,NULL,1,1673),
(N'265fa874-04c3-5143-9ee5-2e4fa9ecb9f8',N'72667ae6-6505-5fa4-88e9-6c5d172ea855',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>Rented Properties Information About a House</h2><p><span>Part One</span><br>You will hear a student calling a landlord to rent a house.</p><p>First, you have some time to look at Questions 1 to 6.</p><p><span>Now listen carefully and answer Questions 1 to 6.</span></p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Hello.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Oh hello.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>I wanted to inquire about the house you''re going to let out. Is it still available?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Yes. May I ask where you heard about it? Was it in an advert?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Right, it was.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Okay. What do you want to know?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Well, I''m going to share the house with two friends. We''ve been staying on campus but we''re going to graduate, so we need to find a house as soon as possible. Anyway, could you tell me the exact date when we can move in?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Well, the contract with my current tenant is due next week on May the 3rd, and I need a day to clean and check the things in the house. So I think the house will be ready on the 5th of May. Is that okay?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Yes, that will be fine. And what we''re most concerned about is the rent. We''d like something around $500 a month for each of us, but we could go up to $600 if we have to. But we can''t go beyond that.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>You are lucky. The rent will be $1,700 in total, which is within your budget. You''ll need to pay monthly to me by cash or credit card, I don''t mind. And you''ll have to pay me $1,500 as a deposit before you move in. The deposit is as insurance in case you break something.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Okay, that''s reasonable.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Oh, I nearly forgot. One important thing is that you''ll be required to provide a credit check to guarantee your ability to pay the rent every month. This will charge you $15.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>No problem. How about the facilities?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>First, there are three bedrooms combined with two bathrooms. Then there is a shared kitchen, which was refurbished several months ago. By the way, do you cook yourselves?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Yeah, we like cooking. Do we have to buy our own cookers?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Basic facilities for cooking are available, but if you want a dishwasher, I''m sorry it needs to be added later.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>I think t' AS nvarchar(max))+N'he basic facilities are enough for us. Is there any place we can park, as one of my friends has a private car?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Yes, there is a garage which not only provides space for your car but also has a special section where you can do some of your work. It''s very quiet for you to concentrate.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>It sounds so special.</p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at Questions 7 to 10.</span></p><p><span>Now listen and answer Questions 7 to 10.</span></p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>There are some other utilities I have to tell you. There is a garden in front of the house, in which you will find some interesting plants and beautiful flowers.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Do we have to take care of these plants and flowers?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Well, you don''t have to worry about that. There will be a professional gardener to do the landscaping in the garden regularly. But you do need to help water the grass once a week. Is it a problem for you?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Of course not. We''d like to do that.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Another thing is, in this district every household is arranged with a cleaner responsible for cleaning up the rubbish and recycling. But this service will cost you $15 every month.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>What other bills do we have to pay?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Bills for electricity, water, and gas will also be covered by yourselves.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Okay. One more question — is air conditioning available in the house?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>If you mean central air conditioning, I''m afraid there isn''t at the moment. But each room has a conditioning unit which is installed on the window.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>That will be fine. Anyway, I think it''s a good place to live in. Can we go and see the house, please?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Yes, of course. May I have your name and phone number?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Yes, I''m Sam Dresler. That''s S-A-M D-R-E-S-L-E-R.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Okay, Sam Dresler, I''ve got that down. And my phone number is 6753 4571.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Thank you very much. I''ll contact you after discussing it with my two friends.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>No problem. Bye.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Bye.</p><p>That is the end of Part One.</p><p>You now have 1 minute to check your answers to Part One.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span>Part One</span><br>You will hear a student calling a landlord to rent a house.</p><p>First, you have some time to look at Questions 1 to 6.</p><p><span>Now listen carefully and answer Questions 1 to 6.</span></p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Hello.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Oh hello.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>I wanted to inquire about the house you''re going to let out. Is it still available?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Yes. May I ask where you heard about it? Was it in an advert?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Right, it was.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Okay. What do you want to know?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Well, I''m going to share the house with two friends. We''ve been staying on campus but we''re going to graduate, so we need to find a house as soon as possible. Anyway, could you tell me the exact date when we can move in?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Well, the contract with my current tenant is due next week on May the 3rd, and I need a day to clean and check the things in the house. So I think the house will be ready on the 5th of May. Is that okay?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Yes, that will be fine. And what we''re most concerned about is the rent. We''d like something around $500 a month for each of us, but we could go up to $600 if we have to. But we can''t go beyond that.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>You are lucky. The rent will be $1,700 in total, which is within your budget. You''ll need to pay monthly to me by cash or credit card, I don''t mind. And you''ll have to pay me $1,500 as a deposit before you move in. The deposit is as insurance in case you break something.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Okay, that''s reasonable.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Oh, I nearly forgot. One important thing is that you''ll be required to provide a credit check to guarantee your ability to pay the rent every month. This will charge you $15.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>No problem. How about the facilities?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>First, there are three bedrooms combined with two bathrooms. Then there is a shared kitchen, which was refurbished several months ago. By the way, do you cook yourselves?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Yeah, we like cooking. Do we have to buy our own cookers?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Basic facilities for cooking are available, but if you want a dishwasher, I''m sorry it needs to be added later.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>I think the basic facilities are enough for us. Is there any ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'place we can park, as one of my friends has a private car?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Yes, there is a garage which not only provides space for your car but also has a special section where you can do some of your work. It''s very quiet for you to concentrate.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>It sounds so special.</p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at Questions 7 to 10.</span></p><p><span>Now listen and answer Questions 7 to 10.</span></p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>There are some other utilities I have to tell you. There is a garden in front of the house, in which you will find some interesting plants and beautiful flowers.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Do we have to take care of these plants and flowers?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Well, you don''t have to worry about that. There will be a professional gardener to do the landscaping in the garden regularly. But you do need to help water the grass once a week. Is it a problem for you?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Of course not. We''d like to do that.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Another thing is, in this district every household is arranged with a cleaner responsible for cleaning up the rubbish and recycling. But this service will cost you $15 every month.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>What other bills do we have to pay?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Bills for electricity, water, and gas will also be covered by yourselves.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Okay. One more question — is air conditioning available in the house?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>If you mean central air conditioning, I''m afraid there isn''t at the moment. But each room has a conditioning unit which is installed on the window.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>That will be fine. Anyway, I think it''s a good place to live in. Can we go and see the house, please?</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Yes, of course. May I have your name and phone number?</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Yes, I''m Sam Dresler. That''s S-A-M D-R-E-S-L-E-R.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>Okay, Sam Dresler, I''ve got that down. And my phone number is 6753 4571.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Thank you very much. I''ll contact you after discussing it with my two friends.</p><p><span>Landlord:</span><br>No problem. Bye.</p><p><span>Student:</span><br>Bye.</p><p>That is the end of Part One.</p><p>You now have 1 minute to check your answers to Part One.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.5.S1673.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1673),
(N'f2c3ce61-b40f-5029-82ff-5ac7fd4983d5',N'265fa874-04c3-5143-9ee5-2e4fa9ecb9f8',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the table below.<br>Write <span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Rented Properties Information About a House',11,0,1,NULL,0,1673),
