(N'09f7190f-da2f-5a9d-b9a9-0293b6727c60',N'a37ffc43-c1c9-578e-b9ac-3e62ce0fe308',0,3,19,N'Question number 28',N'<p>“Where the money comes from” = sources of funding → B. sources of funding.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1670),
(N'13d06de1-4c72-5414-8ce0-33d413212bbd',N'a37ffc43-c1c9-578e-b9ac-3e62ce0fe308',0,3,19,N'Question number 29',N'<p>Nói về “staff” = nhân lực → A. workforce.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1670),
(N'c7a23a46-a75b-5195-be99-26c355710eef',N'a37ffc43-c1c9-578e-b9ac-3e62ce0fe308',0,3,19,N'Question number 30',N'<p>Thông tin sản lượng = production figures.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1670),
(N'bc94e3a8-70cf-5c16-81b9-01754c2d6887',N'ed404198-880d-541d-a2fc-93e915a4b45f',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>The question of dolphin intelligence</h2><p><span>You will hear a marine biologist giving a lecture about the intelligence of the dolphin. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.  </span></p><p>There is considerable controversy over the question of the intelligence of the marine mammal, the dolphin. Although many marine biologists, including myself, believe that it is a highly intelligent animal, I''d like to look at both sides of the question.  Critics who say dolphins are not intelligent raise objections in several areas. As to the evidence of the dolphin''s large brain size, the critics claim that this is not a sign of intelligence, but simply due to the presence of fatty glial cells. They argue that these cells are not used to think, which might imply intelligence, but rather they are used as insulation for the brain in the cold oceanic habitat. That is, I admit, one of the functions of glial cells.  The critics also point out certain evidence of behaviour which they say shows that dolphins are rather dumb. </p><p>For example, South African scientist Paul Manger says that dolphins are too stupid to jump over even a slight barrier, for example when they are trapped in a net used to catch fish like tuna. Again, I concede that tragically this failure to escape often happens and leads to the unnecessary injury or death of dolphins.  Finally, the critics resort to what I call the dumb bird argument. They downplay the possibility that certain animals are intelligent by pointing to seemingly remarkable feats in obviously small-brained species. </p><p>If pigeons can do such things, or rats, the critics suggest, surely the fact that a dolphin or other large-brained animal might do similarly remarkable things isn''t really a mark of intelligence.  For example, say these critics, some pigeons have learned to select a key marked “thank you” when they are delivered bits of food. Pigeons will also peck at their own reflections, indicating, some insist, that they are aware of their own bodies. And once more I concede that some birds do display these actions.  Let me address these arguments with what I see as the counter evidence. First, as to brain size, the glial cells are not simply protective. </p><p>They also function as important connections within the brain, which seem to be important for problem solving and idea creation, although scientists do not yet agree exactly how.  As to behaviour, le' AS nvarchar(max))+N't me begin by saying that I certainly don''t think we should believe everything positive that people claim about dolphins. For example, the notion that they smile when they are happy or pleased. This is certainly not true, because they don''t have the facial muscles necessary for such physical expressions.  But many aspects of their behaviour do indicate to me that they are highly intelligent. In captivity, dolphins are known to watch their keepers'' particular manner of walking, for instance, and copy this. I am always impressed by how adept they are as mimics when I witness such behaviour.  </p><p>And dolphins have been known to outsmart their keepers. One female dolphin, who was rewarded with a fish for every piece of rubbish she collected from her tank, managed to trick her trainers into a bounty of food. They discovered that she had been keeping a newspaper on the bottom of the tank and then ripping off small pieces of it to bring to the trainers one by one, thus maximising her snacks. I''d call that smart.  For hundreds of years, we have known that dolphins exhibit social behaviour. To elaborate on this interaction among dolphins, let me cite several examples. Dolphins have been known to come to the aid of sick or wounded companions, supporting the injured or ailing near the surface of the water to help them breathe.  Each dolphin produces unique sounds similar to a whistle, by which they recognise each other. They use these to signal one another over very long distances. I''ll give you another instance. </p><p>Dolphins touch and stroke each other to make up following a fight. Surely a sign of rather sophisticated intelligence.  As for the dumb bird argument, yes, clearly even small-brained animals like pigeons can be trained to perform tricks. But these behaviours are usually no more than conditioned responses, learned in response to specific rewards like food. Or they are reactions which are instinctive.  </p><p>Are these truly comparable to the actions of Presley, a dolphin at the New York Aquarium, who, without any prompting whatsoever, reacted to being marked with paint by taking off at high speed to a distant part of his tank where a mirror was mounted? There he spun round and round, the way humans do in a dressing room, appearing to check himself out.  There are many such observations of dolphin behaviour, which is why many animal researchers believe in dolphin intelligence.  </p><p><span>That is the end of Part 4. </span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span>You will hear a marine biologist giving a lecture about the intelligence of the dolphin. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.  </span></p><p>There is considerable controversy over the question of the intelligence of the marine mammal, the dolphin. Although many marine biologists, including myself, believe that it is a highly intelligent animal, I''d like to look at both sides of the question.  Critics who say dolphins are not intelligent raise objections in several areas. As to the evidence of the dolphin''s large brain size, the critics claim that this is not a sign of intelligence, but simply due to the presence of fatty glial cells. They argue that these cells are not used to think, which might imply intelligence, but rather they are used as insulation for the brain in the cold oceanic habitat. That is, I admit, one of the functions of glial cells.  The critics also point out certain evidence of behaviour which they say shows that dolphins are rather dumb. </p><p>For example, South African scientist Paul Manger says that dolphins are too stupid to jump over even a slight barrier, for example when they are trapped in a net used to catch fish like tuna. Again, I concede that tragically this failure to escape often happens and leads to the unnecessary injury or death of dolphins.  Finally, the critics resort to what I call the dumb bird argument. They downplay the possibility that certain animals are intelligent by pointing to seemingly remarkable feats in obviously small-brained species. </p><p>If pigeons can do such things, or rats, the critics suggest, surely the fact that a dolphin or other large-brained animal might do similarly remarkable things isn''t really a mark of intelligence.  For example, say these critics, some pigeons have learned to select a key marked “thank you” when they are delivered bits of food. Pigeons will also peck at their own reflections, indicating, some insist, that they are aware of their own bodies. And once more I concede that some birds do display these actions.  Let me address these arguments with what I see as the counter evidence. First, as to brain size, the glial cells are not simply protective. </p><p>They also function as important connections within the brain, which seem to be important for problem solving and idea creation, although scientists do not yet agree exactly how.  As to behaviour, let me begin by saying that I certainly don''t t' AS nvarchar(max))+N'hink we should believe everything positive that people claim about dolphins. For example, the notion that they smile when they are happy or pleased. This is certainly not true, because they don''t have the facial muscles necessary for such physical expressions.  But many aspects of their behaviour do indicate to me that they are highly intelligent. In captivity, dolphins are known to watch their keepers'' particular manner of walking, for instance, and copy this. I am always impressed by how adept they are as mimics when I witness such behaviour.  </p><p>And dolphins have been known to outsmart their keepers. One female dolphin, who was rewarded with a fish for every piece of rubbish she collected from her tank, managed to trick her trainers into a bounty of food. They discovered that she had been keeping a newspaper on the bottom of the tank and then ripping off small pieces of it to bring to the trainers one by one, thus maximising her snacks. I''d call that smart.  For hundreds of years, we have known that dolphins exhibit social behaviour. To elaborate on this interaction among dolphins, let me cite several examples. Dolphins have been known to come to the aid of sick or wounded companions, supporting the injured or ailing near the surface of the water to help them breathe.  Each dolphin produces unique sounds similar to a whistle, by which they recognise each other. They use these to signal one another over very long distances. I''ll give you another instance. </p><p>Dolphins touch and stroke each other to make up following a fight. Surely a sign of rather sophisticated intelligence.  As for the dumb bird argument, yes, clearly even small-brained animals like pigeons can be trained to perform tricks. But these behaviours are usually no more than conditioned responses, learned in response to specific rewards like food. Or they are reactions which are instinctive.  </p><p>Are these truly comparable to the actions of Presley, a dolphin at the New York Aquarium, who, without any prompting whatsoever, reacted to being marked with paint by taking off at high speed to a distant part of his tank where a mirror was mounted? There he spun round and round, the way humans do in a dressing room, appearing to check himself out.  There are many such observations of dolphin behaviour, which is why many animal researchers believe