(N'428144e4-1803-586c-9525-38a7bdb76c0e',N'd9ffed14-c529-5f94-8427-df23ac2d8492',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>Hỏi: Some positive claims are untrue, e.g. that dolphins ___. → Dẫn chứng “they smile when they are happy” bị bác bỏ. → Đáp án: smile.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1670),
(N'584c4890-efe4-5a94-9e01-aafb8412ff71',N'd9ffed14-c529-5f94-8427-df23ac2d8492',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>Hỏi: Dolphins imitate people’s movements, such as their way of ___. → “manner of walking” = cách đi. → Đáp án: walking.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1670),
(N'ed51c14a-6e23-5382-bb89-7a7f1a9be7a8',N'd9ffed14-c529-5f94-8427-df23ac2d8492',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>Hỏi: She hid a ___ in her tank. → “She had been keeping a newspaper” → đúng từ. → Đáp án: newspaper.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1670),
(N'5fb2f421-5f0c-5a54-a502-46b1d487da95',N'd9ffed14-c529-5f94-8427-df23ac2d8492',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>Hỏi: Dolphins have been known to be ___ animals. → “social behaviour” → “social animals”. → Đáp án: social.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1670),
(N'b46f67dc-9c86-5e2e-9b0e-e5c76382bfc0',N'd9ffed14-c529-5f94-8427-df23ac2d8492',0,3,19,N'Question number 38',N'<p>Hỏi: They use a sound like a ___ to signal to each other. → Từ được nhấn mạnh là “whistle”. → Đáp án: whistle.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1670),
(N'dd22f6dd-54dc-5390-a3e5-8f2d98cea9bb',N'd9ffed14-c529-5f94-8427-df23ac2d8492',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>Hỏi: They touch and stroke each other after a ___. → “after a fight” → đúng nguyên văn. → Đáp án: fight.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1670),
(N'd693eaa8-5e71-5d72-8c2b-4b3c10276d26',N'd9ffed14-c529-5f94-8427-df23ac2d8492',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>Hỏi: Tricks displayed by small-brained animals usually are only conditioned responses or ___ reactions. → Diễn giả nói rõ: “conditioned responses ... or instinctive reactions.” → Đáp án: instinctive.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1670),
(N'ed55ec76-3a82-5ebc-8dd0-1b2d1ec239d1',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.2.S1668.mp3',N'Vol 3 LISTENING FULL TEST 2',0,7,1,NULL,1,1668),
(N'effc36a7-e15a-5678-af00-f81317010d0d',N'ed55ec76-3a82-5ebc-8dd0-1b2d1ec239d1',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Summerton - Activity Centre</h2><p>Morning. You''ve reached Somerton Child Care Centre. This is Sarah speaking. How can I assist you today? Hi Sarah, I''m planning to visit your centre with my children next weekend, and I''d like to get some information about your facilities and activities. Course, I''d be happy to help. First, let me give you our location. We''re on North Hill, right opposite the supermarket. Oh wait, sorry about that. That''s our old location. We''re now opposite the toy factory, just down the road. Got it. Is parking available there? Absolutely There''s plenty of parking available both in front of the centre and in the overflow lot around the corner. You''ll find it much easier than the old location which had limited space. That''s a relief. What about entry fees? Entry is actually free for everyone, and to make it even better, adults receive a complimentary drink. Some people think it''s only coffee, but we offer tea, water, and even juice as options. That''s generous. Are there any new features or attractions at your centre? Oh yes, we''ve added a lot recently. There''s a large slide which we call the Wildcat. It''s quite popular. It''s bigger and steeper than our older slides so it''s a real hit. Then we''ve introduced a mini golf area for children, complete with five themed courses. Wow, that sounds amazing! Are there any other play areas? Well, we still have the Jungle Gym and the Arts and Crafts Corner, but those aren''t new, there is also a sand pit, but it''s currently under renovation. </p><p>The slide and the golf courses are the main attractions we''re highlighting this year. Are there any special requirements for kids who want to participate? Yes, there are a few; First all children must wear trousers. This is mainly for safety during activities like sliding or climbing. Shorts just aren''t suitable. Okay, that''s good. to know. Oh, and every child must also wear socks. This is particularly important for the indoor play zones, as we don''t allow barefoot play for hygiene reasons. Got it. Anything else we need to prepare? Yes, you''ll need to bring a label with each child''s name on it. We can provide labels at the center if you forget, but it''s easier if you come prepared. Sure, I can manage that. By the way, I''m also thinking of organizing a birthday party for my son. Do you have any packages for that? Absolutely! Our birthday party packages are very popular. To book a party, you''ll need at least eight children. That''s the minimum number' AS nvarchar(max))+N' of participants. And what''s included in the package? The Basic package includes food, drinks, and decorations. You can also add optional extras like a cake or entertainment, say a magician, or a clown for an additional fee. That sounds nice. Do you cater for dietary restrictions? Yes, we do. We have vegetarian and gluten-free options, and we can customize the menu further if needed. Just let us know in advance so we can make the necessary arrangements. Perfect! Are there any discounts if we book early? Actually, yes. If you book at least two weeks in advance, you''ll get a 10% discount on the total package price. That applies to all parties booked for weekend. That''s fantastic! Thanks so much for the detailed information. You''re welcome. Feel free to call back if you have any more questions, or you can visit our website for more details. Will do. Thanks again.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Morning. You''ve reached Somerton Child Care Centre. This is Sarah speaking. How can I assist you today? Hi Sarah, I''m planning to visit your centre with my children next weekend, and I''d like to get some information about your facilities and activities. Course, I''d be happy to help. First, let me give you our location. We''re on North Hill, right opposite the supermarket. Oh wait, sorry about that. That''s our old location. We''re now opposite the toy factory, just down the road. Got it. Is parking available there? Absolutely There''s plenty of parking available both in front of the centre and in the overflow lot around the corner. You''ll find it much easier than the old location which had limited space. That''s a relief. What about entry fees? Entry is actually free for everyone, and to make it even better, adults receive a complimentary drink. Some people think it''s only coffee, but we offer tea, water, and even juice as options. That''s generous. Are there any new features or attractions at your centre? Oh yes, we''ve added a lot recently. There''s a large slide which we call the Wildcat. It''s quite popular. It''s bigger and steeper than our older slides so it''s a real hit. Then we''ve introduced a mini golf area for children, complete with five themed courses. Wow, that sounds amazing! Are there any other play areas? Well, we still have the Jungle Gym and the Arts and Crafts Corner, but those aren''t new, there is also a sand pit, but it''s currently under renovation. </p><p>The slide and the golf courses are the main attractions we''re highlighting this year. Are there any special requirements for kids who want to participate? Yes, there are a few; First all children must wear trousers. This is mainly for safety during activities like sliding or climbing. Shorts just aren''t suitable. Okay, that''s good. to know. Oh, and every child must also wear socks. This is particularly important for the indoor play zones, as we don''t allow barefoot play for hygiene reasons. Got it. Anything else we need to prepare? Yes, you''ll need to bring a label with each child''s name on it. We can provide labels at the center if you forget, but it''s easier if you come prepared. Sure, I can manage that. By the way, I''m also thinking of organizing a birthday party for my son. Do you have any packages for that? Absolutely! Our birthday party packages are very popular. To book a party, you''ll need at least eight children. That''s the minimum number of participants. And what''s included in the' AS nvarchar(max))+N' package? The Basic package includes food, drinks, and decorations. You can also add optional extras like a cake or entertainment, say a magician, or a clown for an additional fee. That sounds nice. Do you cater for dietary restrictions? Yes, we do. We have vegetarian and gluten-free options, and we can customize the menu further if needed. Just let us know in advance so we can make the necessary arrangements. Perfect! Are there any discounts if we book early? Actually, yes. If you book at least two weeks in advance, you''ll get a 10% discount on the total package price. That applies to all parties booked for weekend. That''s fantastic! Thanks so much for the detailed information. You''re welcome. Feel free to call back if you have any more questions, or you can visit our website for more details. Will do. Thanks again.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.2.S1668.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1668),
(N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',N'effc36a7-e15a-5678-af00-f81317010d0d',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the note below.
Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Sumerton - Activity Centre',11,0,1,NULL,0,1668),
(N'5e7a40a8-3156-5517-aafa-90be252acbf8',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'<h3>Sumerton - Activity Centre</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p>• Location: North Hill, opposite the }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• A lot of areas for }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Free entry includes a free }{SPACE}{ to adults.</p> <p>• New attractions include a large }{SPACE}{ for the "wildcat"</p> <p>• Children can play in new }{SPACE}{ courses.</p> <p><span>Requirements:</span></p> <p>• Children must wear }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Wear a pair of }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Give a label for their }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Minimum number of participants: }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Birthday parties include: food, drink, }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Trung tâm nằm đối diện với cái gì?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ địa điểm. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói sửa lại vị trí chính xác: “We’re now opposite the toy factory.” → Đáp án là toy factory. </p><p>Key vocab: opposite = đối diện; location = địa điểm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1668),
(N'd9804c84-bc25-58a0-ba15-0016535eb8a2',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 2',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Có nhiều khu vực dành cho cái gì?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ phương tiện/dịch vụ. </p><p>Trả lời: Người hỏi và trả lời đều nhắc “parking available... plenty of parking” → Đáp án là parking. </p><p>Key vocab: available = có sẵn; plenty of = nhiều.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1668),
(N'7924d768-7249-56f2-875f-f05470458931',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 3',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Vé miễn phí cho người lớn bao gồm gì?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ đồ uống. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói khẳng định: “adults receive a complimentary drink.” → Đáp án là drink. </p><p>Key vocab: complimentary = miễn phí; receive = nhận được.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1668),
(N'c348e41d-fc90-5d02-8ef1-9f84132629ce',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 4',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Điểm thu hút mới bao gồm một cái gì lớn (Wildcat)?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ trò chơi. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói: “a large slide ... we call the Wildcat.” → Đáp án là slide. Key vocab: attraction = điểm thu hút; large = lớn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,4,NULL,0,1668),
(N'd3f924f1-7d81-59e4-8483-2abec2bb7ee6',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 5',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Trẻ em có thể chơi ở bao nhiêu sân gì mới?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ môn thể thao. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói: “a mini golf area... five themed courses.” → Đáp án là golf. </p><p>Key vocab: courses = sân/bài tập; themed = theo chủ đề.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,5,NULL,0,1668),
(N'43b9b61b-11af-59de-ba5a-6672b9876582',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 6',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Trẻ em phải mặc gì?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ trang phục. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói nhấn mạnh: “must wear trousers.” → Đáp án là trousers. </p><p>Key vocab: must wear = bắt buộc mặc; safety = an toàn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,6,NULL,0,1668),
(N'6d78da26-6e11-5827-88d9-97c85ea1c5c8',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 7',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Trẻ em phải đi thêm gì?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ đồ mặc chân. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói: “must also wear socks.” → Đáp án là socks. </p><p>Key vocab: indoor = trong nhà; hygiene = vệ sinh.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,7,NULL,0,1668),
(N'b32d4a26-9354-54c9-afbe-80b9597709e3',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 8',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Phải có nhãn ghi gì cho mỗi trẻ?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ thông tin cá nhân. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói rõ: “label with each child’s name.” → Đáp án là name. </p><p>Key vocab: label = nhãn; bring = mang theo.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,8,NULL,0,1668),
(N'4d1cd2b8-f0cc-5880-b519-748ff98f3d1f',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 9',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Số lượng tối thiểu của người tham gia là bao nhiêu?” → Cần một số đếm. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói: “at least eight children... minimum number.” → Đáp án là eight. </p><p>Key vocab: minimum = tối thiểu; participants = người tham gia.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,1668),
(N'b356946a-83f3-5d64-81c2-caf61f30024f',N'7ba27de9-7068-5837-adaa-8e51fa547c23',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Gói sinh nhật bao gồm thức ăn, đồ uống và gì nữa?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ vật trang trí. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói: “includes food, drinks, and decorations.” → Đáp án là decorations. </p><p>Key vocab: include = bao gồm; package = gói dịch vụ.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1668),
(N'b94b7557-ac7c-5172-a680-f853a11a4cc5',N'ed55ec76-3a82-5ebc-8dd0-1b2d1ec239d1',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 3] Mangrovetree Resort</h2><p>Now turn to section 2. Section 2 You will hear a tour guide introducing a resort called Mangrove Tree. </p><p><span>First, you''ll have some time to look at questions 11 to 17. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 17.  </span></p><p>Welcome to all of you. Can everybody see and hear me? Good, I''m David, your guide for this tour of the Mangrove Tree Resort. I''ll start today with some general background information. As there have been a number of changes made, I printed the map of the new complex for each of you, and I''d just like to point out where everything is, and then you can take a look at it for yourself.  Basically, the best feature of our new location is that we have our own private beach, which is on the left and accessible directly from the hotel. Now we''re standing at the information desk and we''re going to look at the views in each direction.  </p><p>Out to the west, there''s a gift shop where guests can shop for small trinkets made by local villagers from shells and starfish. If you look carefully up there on the other side of the restaurant, there''s a state-of-the-art spa facility just next to the grove of palm trees where guests can have treatments including massage or detoxify their body in the steam room.  </p><p>A major drawback of our previous hotel complex was that there was no room for an on-site helicopter port, which meant that our most important guests were forced to commute from the local airport. As we were unable to construct a suitable facility on the eastern side, we have constructed a large heliport to the north.  </p><p>Guests wishing to stretch their legs after a long flight can simply head south to the tennis courts for a match on our splendidly manicured courts. Now if you look in front of you, there''s a swimming pool in the centre equipped with slides and a diving board, which is the main feature of the new complex, and is overlooked by all of the other buildings.  We have decided to maintain the family-friendly status of our original hotel. However, from guestserv, we have come to realise that parents also enjoy time to relax without having to look after their children.  </p><p>Therefore, we have constructed a fun play centre where parents can leave their children with a trained child minder and take some time for themselves. This facility is located just to the north of the swimming pool and is covered with a small canopy to protect the children from the sun.  Many of our wo' AS nvarchar(max))+N'rking guests will require access to a quiet space for work. If you direct them just south of the swimming pool, they will discover our conference centre which comes complete with meeting rooms, offices and Wi-Fi access.  In order to access the Wi-Fi network, they should simply report to the information desk where an assistant will provide them with a personal password. This desk is situated just between the conference centre and the beach.  </p><p>Finally, the complex also contains a large sports centre where guests can enjoy our gym facilities and take part in sessions for yoga and pilots. Overlooking the swimming pool from the southeast, the centre offers stunning views of the entire complex and across the ocean.  </p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 18 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 18 to 20. </span></p><p>Next, I would like to discuss our new restaurant, which is run by the award-winning chef Alberto Brava.  Every week he will offer a new menu of fresh seafood, such as crab and sea bass, which has been caught that day by local fishermen. The daily menu will include a range of organic meats and vegetables freshly grilled on the barbecue, served with a selection of specially prepared sauces and spices.  </p><p>There is no menu specifically for vegetarians, however there will be a large range of grilled vegetables for them to choose. The hotel now has a selection of suites to cater to our more discerning guests and provide for special occasions.  As these suites are finely decorated with furniture and paintings, they are not suitable for families with children. Our tikki suite is the finest of them all. It is slightly too ornate for business people, but would be perfect for couples on their honeymoon who may even have been married in the hotel.  Finally, I would like to share some exciting news. </p><p>Our hotel has won awards in the past for its fantastic service. However, we have just won the prestigious Green Award for our efforts in reducing our carbon footprint.  The award will be proudly displayed in the restaurant facility, where it can be admired by our guests and staff alike. Well, here we are back at our starting point, the Information Desk. </p><p><span>That is the end of section 2.  You now have half a minute to check your answers.  </span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Now turn to section 2. Section 2 You will hear a tour guide introducing a resort called Mangrove Tree. </p><p><span>First, you''ll have some time to look at questions 11 to 17. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 17.  </span></p><p>Welcome to all of you. Can everybody see and hear me? Good, I''m David, your guide for this tour of the Mangrove Tree Resort. I''ll start today with some general background information. As there have been a number of changes made, I printed the map of the new complex for each of you, and I''d just like to point out where everything is, and then you can take a look at it for yourself.  Basically, the best feature of our new location is that we have our own private beach, which is on the left and accessible directly from the hotel. Now we''re standing at the information desk and we''re going to look at the views in each direction.  </p><p>Out to the west, there''s a gift shop where guests can shop for small trinkets made by local villagers from shells and starfish. If you look carefully up there on the other side of the restaurant, there''s a state-of-the-art spa facility just next to the grove of palm trees where guests can have treatments including massage or detoxify their body in the steam room.  </p><p>A major drawback of our previous hotel complex was that there was no room for an on-site helicopter port, which meant that our most important guests were forced to commute from the local airport. As we were unable to construct a suitable facility on the eastern side, we have constructed a large heliport to the north.  </p><p>Guests wishing to stretch their legs after a long flight can simply head south to the tennis courts for a match on our splendidly manicured courts. Now if you look in front of you, there''s a swimming pool in the centre equipped with slides and a diving board, which is the main feature of the new complex, and is overlooked by all of the other buildings.  We have decided to maintain the family-friendly status of our original hotel. However, from guestserv, we have come to realise that parents also enjoy time to relax without having to look after their children.  </p><p>Therefore, we have constructed a fun play centre where parents can leave their children with a trained child minder and take some time for themselves. This facility is located just to the north of the swimming pool and is covered with a small canopy to protect the children from the sun.  Many of our working guests will require access to ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'a quiet space for work. If you direct them just south of the swimming pool, they will discover our conference centre which comes complete with meeting rooms, offices and Wi-Fi access.  In order to access the Wi-Fi network, they should simply report to the information desk where an assistant will provide them with a personal password. This desk is situated just between the conference centre and the beach.  </p><p>Finally, the complex also contains a large sports centre where guests can enjoy our gym facilities and take part in sessions for yoga and pilots. Overlooking the swimming pool from the southeast, the centre offers stunning views of the entire complex and across the ocean.  </p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 18 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 18 to 20. </span></p><p>Next, I would like to discuss our new restaurant, which is run by the award-winning chef Alberto Brava.  Every week he will offer a new menu of fresh seafood, such as crab and sea bass, which has been caught that day by local fishermen. The daily menu will include a range of organic meats and vegetables freshly grilled on the barbecue, served with a selection of specially prepared sauces and spices.  </p><p>There is no menu specifically for vegetarians, however there will be a large range of grilled vegetables for them to choose. The hotel now has a selection of suites to cater to our more discerning guests and provide for special occasions.  As these suites are finely decorated with furniture and paintings, they are not suitable for families with children. Our tikki suite is the finest of them all. It is slightly too ornate for business people, but would be perfect for couples on their honeymoon who may even have been married in the hotel.  Finally, I would like to share some exciting news. </p><p>Our hotel has won awards in the past for its fantastic service. However, we have just won the prestigious Green Award for our efforts in reducing our carbon footprint.  The award will be proudly displayed in the restaurant facility, where it can be admired by our guests and staff alike. Well, here we are back at our starting point, the Information Desk. </p><p><span>That is the end of section 2.  You now have half a minute to check your answers.  </span></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.2.S1668.PART2.mp3',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1668),
(N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',N'b94b7557-ac7c-5172-a680-f853a11a4cc5',0,2,18,N'<p>Label the plan below</p><p>Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</span> for each answers</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Mangrovetree Resort',11,0,1,NULL,0,1668),
(N'98017b69-f20c-5c52-8fe9-e3c3a3b4b782',N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',0,3,19,N'<h3>Mangrovetree Resort</h3><p><img src="https://ieltsroom.com/upload/images/ieltsadvancedtest/6aca3c5ddec64cacb7a2.jpg" alt="Question diagram"></p> }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{ ',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Điền nhãn bản đồ – ô 11 nằm đối diện Gift Shop → là restaurant. Cụm “on the other side of the restaurant” giúp xác định rõ vị trí. restaurant = nhà hàng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1668),
(N'7739718e-ec4f-5ebb-935f-0233894c4141',N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',0,3,19,N'Question number 12',N'<p>Nằm cạnh hàng cây cọ (grove of palm trees) ở góc Tây Bắc, gần bãi biển. spa facility = khu spa hiện đại.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1668),
(N'7eac3686-0221-5e37-997b-b33cb5cf0d86',N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',0,3,19,N'Question number 13',N'<p>Từ “head south” → khu này nằm phía Nam của heliport, đúng vị trí ô 13. tennis court = sân tennis.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1668),
(N'7d7019fb-7c34-592e-b94e-25f09c3742d2',N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',0,3,19,N'Question number 14',N'<p>“In the centre” → ở giữa bản đồ, là bể bơi trung tâm. swimming pool = hồ bơi.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1668),
(N'1c7ed917-30a6-5185-a0c0-fccab8257229',N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',0,3,19,N'Question number 15',N'<p>“To the north of the swimming pool” → ô 15 ở phía Bắc bể bơi. play centre = khu vui chơi trẻ em.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1668),
(N'63308569-920d-5f7d-8d5f-fafe9175b67e',N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',0,3,19,N'Question number 16',N'<p>“South of the swimming pool” → ô 16 ngay dưới hồ bơi, sát Information Desk. conference centre = trung tâm hội nghị.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1668),
(N'ea87475e-8700-593d-993a-b4521564765f',N'028390d4-040c-56cf-a9cc-9af8821cefe9',0,3,19,N'Question number 17',N'<p>“From the southeast” → vị trí góc Đông Nam, đúng ô 17. sports centre = trung tâm thể thao.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1668),
(N'78df8e78-ef60-524d-8f20-387bc3d2c41d',N'b94b7557-ac7c-5172-a680-f853a11a4cc5',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A</span>, <span>B </span>or <span>C</span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,2,NULL,0,1668),
(N'ed87e797-8d6d-5816-85f8-84645b131a43',N'78df8e78-ef60-524d-8f20-387bc3d2c41d',0,3,19,N'<p>What kind of food does the restaurant serve every day?</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Nhà hàng phục vụ món ăn gì hàng ngày?” → Từ khóa “daily menu” nhấn mạnh món barbecue.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1668),
(N'b3a3642d-80bd-5ab4-873f-dcac9dab16f1',N'78df8e78-ef60-524d-8f20-387bc3d2c41d',0,3,19,N'<p>What is the feature of the Tiki room?</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Đặc điểm của phòng Tiki?” → Dành cho cặp đôi mới cưới (honeymoon).</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1668),
(N'0a21c5d8-29d5-5d55-a832-5d1028025289',N'78df8e78-ef60-524d-8f20-387bc3d2c41d',0,3,19,N'<p>What did the hotel win an award for recently?</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Khách sạn đạt giải vì điều gì?” → Green Award = giải thưởng về bảo vệ môi trường.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1668),
(N'4437664d-014c-547d-8454-c1e933ab6059',N'ed55ec76-3a82-5ebc-8dd0-1b2d1ec239d1',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 3] Test 2 - Section 3</h2><p><span>Part 3. You will hear a discussion between two students about taking a course to become a personal support worker. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24. </span></p><p> Becky. Hi. What are you doing here at the college? Hi, Jason. Just using the library. What about you? I''m taking the PSW program. You know? Personal support worker. You''re kidding. I''ve just qualified as a PSW.  Really? I''d love to talk to you about the program. Okay. Well, first off, the main duties they taught me here were exactly what I needed on the job. You mean like bathing and dressing people? Yes, young and old need this.  You''ll work with people of all ages, from young people with disabilities to seniors. But you know, nothing really prepares you for how alone many people are. They crave conversation. </p><p>And I don''t mind chatting with them while I work, though that''s not officially in my job description.  I think I''d like to focus on seniors care. And with the country''s older population growing, maybe I''ll get a job fast. Do we have to be responsible for any medications? No, that''s not for us to do. And while you will prepare meals, you won''t feed people.  But working to keep clients healthy and able to live on their own without risk as long as possible is always the goal. And we''re recognizing that with lifespans increasing, long-term care facilities won''t be able to keep up with the number of people who will need care.  I almost didn''t meet the requirements for entry into the PSW program. I was one course short getting my high school diploma, so I had to go back to summer school. </p><p>I took science because I''m no good at math.  Oh, and speaking of not getting in, having my first aid certificate was a real piece of luck. I would have been turned down without that, too. And I would have had a problem with immunizations, except that I traveled the year before, and when the doctor checked my records, they were up to date.  I also had to hand in a written essay on why I wanted to be a PSW.  </p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30. </span></p><p> So Becky, what did you think about the courses you had to take in the Personal Support Worker program? Well, I used to think I got my points across pretty well with people until I took the understanding is essential co' AS nvarchar(max))+N'urse.  For starters, it taught me how to express myself much more effectively. I also learned a lot about preparing clear reports from my supervisors and dealing in a better way with people in general. You know, like giving facts without personal views.  You''ll find that you need those skills every single day. I''m enjoying the foundations course. I''m taking it now and I''m getting a lot out of it. Because support workers are a newer level of care in the province.  I think we absolutely have to understand how we fit into the overall system. It''s a great place to voice your opinions freely. </p><p>Some are quite personal. Did you have to take a course called The Way We Work?  I didn''t know psychology was part of the program. Hmm, that course was a favorite of mine, but it''s probably not what you''re thinking, Jason. It''s about what happens on the inside of the body and covers the functions that keep our bodies operating well.  Even if you''re not a PSW, the course is really interesting. I learned so much. What do you think about residence care? I know the thinking now is that long-term care facilities aren''t the answer for our aging population.  Right. And so this course zeroes in on the changes people can make to their homes so they can stay there, like installing higher toilets, for example. You''ll also learn about specialized equipment people can get to help themselves, safety bars for the shower, and so on.  Do any of the courses focus on the families of our clients? After all, any kind of illness or disability affects everybody. So true. And yes, there is one. It''s called dynamics. You usually get that in your last term.  </p><p>You''ll learn different strategies to help relieve some of the anxiety and strain that happens within the family. I got a lot out of that course. It''s the only one that focuses on emotions rather than facts.  I think giving people this kind of help is just as valuable as any kind of expensive equipment they buy to help themselves. The course I''m really looking forward to is The Power Within. Oh, it''s terrific.  Getting old has quite an impact on the mind, as you know, but you''ll be taught ways to help clients keep the mind active so they''re not so forgetful. You''d be surprised how much improvement you can see in clients when you apply these methods.   </p><p><span>That is the end of part three. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part three.</span>
</p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span>Part 3. You will hear a discussion between two students about taking a course to become a personal support worker. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24. </span></p><p> Becky. Hi. What are you doing here at the college? Hi, Jason. Just using the library. What about you? I''m taking the PSW program. You know? Personal support worker. You''re kidding. I''ve just qualified as a PSW.  Really? I''d love to talk to you about the program. Okay. Well, first off, the main duties they taught me here were exactly what I needed on the job. You mean like bathing and dressing people? Yes, young and old need this.  You''ll work with people of all ages, from young people with disabilities to seniors. But you know, nothing really prepares you for how alone many people are. They crave conversation. </p><p>And I don''t mind chatting with them while I work, though that''s not officially in my job description.  I think I''d like to focus on seniors care. And with the country''s older population growing, maybe I''ll get a job fast. Do we have to be responsible for any medications? No, that''s not for us to do. And while you will prepare meals, you won''t feed people.  But working to keep clients healthy and able to live on their own without risk as long as possible is always the goal. And we''re recognizing that with lifespans increasing, long-term care facilities won''t be able to keep up with the number of people who will need care.  I almost didn''t meet the requirements for entry into the PSW program. I was one course short getting my high school diploma, so I had to go back to summer school. </p><p>I took science because I''m no good at math.  Oh, and speaking of not getting in, having my first aid certificate was a real piece of luck. I would have been turned down without that, too. And I would have had a problem with immunizations, except that I traveled the year before, and when the doctor checked my records, they were up to date.  I also had to hand in a written essay on why I wanted to be a PSW.  </p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30. </span></p><p> So Becky, what did you think about the courses you had to take in the Personal Support Worker program? Well, I used to think I got my points across pretty well with people until I took the understanding is essential course.  For starters, it taught me h' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ow to express myself much more effectively. I also learned a lot about preparing clear reports from my supervisors and dealing in a better way with people in general. You know, like giving facts without personal views.  You''ll find that you need those skills every single day. I''m enjoying the foundations course. I''m taking it now and I''m getting a lot out of it. Because support workers are a newer level of care in the province.  I think we absolutely have to understand how we fit into the overall system. It''s a great place to voice your opinions freely. </p><p>Some are quite personal. Did you have to take a course called The Way We Work?  I didn''t know psychology was part of the program. Hmm, that course was a favorite of mine, but it''s probably not what you''re thinking, Jason. It''s about what happens on the inside of the body and covers the functions that keep our bodies operating well.  Even if you''re not a PSW, the course is really interesting. I learned so much. What do you think about residence care? I know the thinking now is that long-term care facilities aren''t the answer for our aging population.  Right. And so this course zeroes in on the changes people can make to their homes so they can stay there, like installing higher toilets, for example. You''ll also learn about specialized equipment people can get to help themselves, safety bars for the shower, and so on.  Do any of the courses focus on the families of our clients? After all, any kind of illness or disability affects everybody. So true. And yes, there is one. It''s called dynamics. You usually get that in your last term.  </p><p>You''ll learn different strategies to help relieve some of the anxiety and strain that happens within the family. I got a lot out of that course. It''s the only one that focuses on emotions rather than facts.  I think giving people this kind of help is just as valuable as any kind of expensive equipment they buy to help themselves. The course I''m really looking forward to is The Power Within. Oh, it''s terrific.  Getting old has quite an impact on the mind, as you know, but you''ll be taught ways to help clients keep the mind active so they''re not so forgetful. You''d be surprised how much improvement you can see in clients when you apply these methods.   </p><p><span>That is the end of part three. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part three.</span>
</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.2.S1668.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1668),
(N'd8a27aea-a1b4-5813-bedc-fae94c5cf077',N'4437664d-014c-547d-8454-c1e933ab6059',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose <span>TWO</span> letters. <span>A-E.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',5,0,1,NULL,0,1668),
(N'd9332247-b1fb-5469-9f93-8b2d76dff7aa',N'd8a27aea-a1b4-5813-bedc-fae94c5cf077',0,3,19,N'<p>Which are the TWO main duties for Personal Support Workers (PSWs)?</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: What are the two main duties for PSWs? </p><p>→ PSWs giúp khách hàng với các nhu cầu thể chất (physical needs). </p><p>Từ vựng: bathing and dressing = tắm rửa và mặc quần áo </p><p>→ biểu hiện cho helping with physical needs.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1668),
(N'3d305a49-d98f-55f5-b823-6815d25cecaf',N'd8a27aea-a1b4-5813-bedc-fae94c5cf077',0,3,19,N'<p>Which are the TWO main duties for Personal Support Workers (PSWs)?</p>',N'<p>PSW phải đảm bảo an toàn và sức khỏe cho khách hàng </p><p>→ tương ứng ensuring clients are well and safe. </p><p>Từ vựng: without risk = không rủi ro, an toàn. </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1668),
(N'ac948b4e-eb56-5185-89d6-a0758348bb07',N'4437664d-014c-547d-8454-c1e933ab6059',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose <span>TWO</span> letters. <span>A-E.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',5,0,2,NULL,0,1668),
(N'fd2a80f5-3a65-5105-8169-2903710e3cd1',N'ac948b4e-eb56-5185-89d6-a0758348bb07',0,3,19,N'<p>Which are the <span>TWO</span> requirements to do the PSW programme?</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Which are the two requirements to do the PSW programme? </p><p>→ Cần tốt nghiệp trung học. </p><p>Từ vựng: high school diploma = bằng tốt nghiệp phổ thông.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1668),
(N'b561d97f-878c-53c2-b54e-5d261038e25f',N'ac948b4e-eb56-5185-89d6-a0758348bb07',0,3,19,N'<p>Which are the <span>TWO</span> requirements to do the PSW programme?</p>',N'<p>Yêu cầu thứ hai là First Aid training (chứng chỉ sơ cứu). </p><p>Từ vựng: turned down = bị từ chối.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1668),
(N'ee62edf7-9b9f-59ca-9746-4cf022ba383c',N'4437664d-014c-547d-8454-c1e933ab6059',0,2,18,N'<p>What topic do students learn about in each of the following courses?
Choose <span>SIX</span> answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span>A-G</span>, next to questions 25-30.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,3,NULL,0,1668),
(N'9723bf40-c728-59c9-b3bf-470250c7df58',N'ee62edf7-9b9f-59ca-9746-4cf022ba383c',0,3,19,N'<p>Understanding is Essential</p>',N'<p>Course: Understanding is Essential </p><p>→ học kỹ năng giao tiếp trong công việc (workplace communication). </p><p>Từ vựng: express myself = diễn đạt bản thân.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1668),
(N'7d261391-1dfa-596b-b7d2-d0121a6c1471',N'ee62edf7-9b9f-59ca-9746-4cf022ba383c',0,3,19,N'<p>Foundations</p>',N'<p>Course: Foundations </p><p>→ học vai trò của PSW trong hệ thống y tế. </p><p>Từ vựng: fit into the system = hòa nhập vào hệ thống.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1668),
(N'69d34f3a-58fc-581e-862c-66a026b005c6',N'ee62edf7-9b9f-59ca-9746-4cf022ba383c',0,3,19,N'<p>The Way We Work</p>',N'<p>Course: The Way We Work </p><p>→ về hệ thống sinh học (biological systems). </p><p>Từ vựng: functions = chức năng sinh học.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1668),
(N'8b0818a2-b84f-559e-96d0-55063e4ac759',N'ee62edf7-9b9f-59ca-9746-4cf022ba383c',0,3,19,N'<p>Residence Care</p>',N'<p>Course: Residence Care </p><p>→ về independent living (sống tự lập). </p><p>Từ vựng: safety bars = thanh vịn an toàn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1668),
(N'3184f4db-42a2-56ac-8980-aec713a8dfc4',N'ee62edf7-9b9f-59ca-9746-4cf022ba383c',0,3,19,N'<p>Dynamics</p>',N'<p>Course: Dynamics </p><p>→ học xử lý căng thẳng (handling stress), đặc biệt trong gia đình. </p><p>Từ vựng: relieve anxiety = giảm lo âu.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1668),
(N'14187c6c-7413-5e36-8955-f36e57999b0a',N'ee62edf7-9b9f-59ca-9746-4cf022ba383c',0,3,19,N'<p>The Power Within</p>',N'<p>Course: The Power Within </p><p>→ về memory stimulation activities (hoạt động kích thích trí nhớ). </p><p>Từ vựng: keep the mind active = giữ đầu óc minh mẫn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1668),
(N'426aa1bb-c6a3-5926-a5d1-52953dc05eb1',N'ed55ec76-3a82-5ebc-8dd0-1b2d1ec239d1',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>History of the area</h2><p><span>Part 4. You will hear part of a lecture for history students about a relic in Turin, a town in southern France. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.  </span></p><p>Okay, in this lecture we are going to learn the history of Turin, which is a county in southern part of France. We will also be looking at some archaeological findings as they are the basis of what we know today.  The history of this area can date back to 2,500 years ago. In the earliest years, there were just small settlements where people lived a very simple life. Slowly, the place grew into a town and became livelier, and business activities began to emerge.  According to Peter Rocher, a French archaeologist, this place was an important agricultural county since a long time ago. In his findings, there were buried pieces of grain and broken utensils, which were the evidence of ancient Romans who traded their pottery for grains with people in Turin.  </p><p>This also proves that the business activities were quite active in this area back then. However, it is something else that gave us a much better idea of what life was like in Turin about 2,000 years ago.  In the 1500s, a French man found what seemed to be the remains of a castle when he was digging a well. There were bricks and stones that looked like part of a building. In the 17th century, a group of British archaeologists came to Turin to study the relics.  Later, Swedish government also sent a team of archaeologists. The two teams did a thorough survey of the whole area together. It was hard work, which took them several months to dig out the ruins of old buildings and recover the area to what it originally looked like.  The outcome of their work was surprising for all of them. It was a very big area of buildings and facilities for different purposes, which was a very good proof that a civilization existed from so long ago.  In the northeastern part of the area, archaeologists found signs of a local market. There were stone stands that sellers used to place their commodities, and they even found silver coins, which were the currency back then.  </p><p>It further proved that the business was flourishing in ancient Turin. About one mile to the east, in the eastern part of that region, archaeologists found the remains of a building. From the words on a stone, they knew the building was called cast.  It looked like an old workshop, an' AS nvarchar(max))+N'd archaeologists found tools for making textiles. It was a very important evidence that this industry was established about more than 2,000 years ago, which was situated in the central area was a big round building, where there was something like a fountain.  The team found buckets near it. This showed that it had been the place where people collected water. We could imagine the scene that dozens of people were standing by this fountain with buckets in their hands.  What surprised the archaeologists most was that there was an ancient theatre in the northeastern part. There was a stage and several stone seats uncovered. </p><p>Right next to the theatre, archaeologists unearthed a long stone.  Then more stones were found, and they extended across a deeper area. From this, we could conclude that these stones were from an old bridge on the river. Architects were very skilled back then. They used their wisdom to conquer technical obstacles.  But people use their wisdom much more than millie for practical purposes. In the southwestern area, what seemed to be like a garden was uncovered. There were pots and wooden basins. It must have been a very beautiful place, full of plants and flowers of different colours.  </p><p>More importantly, there were two statues of lions just beside the gate. It means French people admired the power of this beast at that time, so that they used stones to make these statues. Not far from Dolby House, also in the central area, there was an elevated area, but its lower area in the centre.  Through a lot of careful research, archaeologists came to the conclusion that this was a Roman bath. It''s where rich people went to enjoy a comfortable and relaxing hot bath. Next to the bath, there was a sculpture of a woman who was like pouring water into the bath.  Across from it, on the other side of the bath pool, people can see the sculpture of the Roman Emperor who ruled this area for many years. These discoveries are good material for us to study the history of Turin and southern France.  </p><p><span>What we''re going to do now is that is the end of part four. You now have half a minute to check your answers.  </span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span>Part 4. You will hear part of a lecture for history students about a relic in Turin, a town in southern France. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.  </span></p><p>Okay, in this lecture we are going to learn the history of Turin, which is a county in southern part of France. We will also be looking at some archaeological findings as they are the basis of what we know today.  The history of this area can date back to 2,500 years ago. In the earliest years, there were just small settlements where people lived a very simple life. Slowly, the place grew into a town and became livelier, and business activities began to emerge.  According to Peter Rocher, a French archaeologist, this place was an important agricultural county since a long time ago. In his findings, there were buried pieces of grain and broken utensils, which were the evidence of ancient Romans who traded their pottery for grains with people in Turin.  </p><p>This also proves that the business activities were quite active in this area back then. However, it is something else that gave us a much better idea of what life was like in Turin about 2,000 years ago.  In the 1500s, a French man found what seemed to be the remains of a castle when he was digging a well. There were bricks and stones that looked like part of a building. In the 17th century, a group of British archaeologists came to Turin to study the relics.  Later, Swedish government also sent a team of archaeologists. The two teams did a thorough survey of the whole area together. It was hard work, which took them several months to dig out the ruins of old buildings and recover the area to what it originally looked like.  The outcome of their work was surprising for all of them. It was a very big area of buildings and facilities for different purposes, which was a very good proof that a civilization existed from so long ago.  In the northeastern part of the area, archaeologists found signs of a local market. There were stone stands that sellers used to place their commodities, and they even found silver coins, which were the currency back then.  </p><p>It further proved that the business was flourishing in ancient Turin. About one mile to the east, in the eastern part of that region, archaeologists found the remains of a building. From the words on a stone, they knew the building was called cast.  It looked like an old workshop, and archaeologists found tools' AS nvarchar(max))+N' for making textiles. It was a very important evidence that this industry was established about more than 2,000 years ago, which was situated in the central area was a big round building, where there was something like a fountain.  The team found buckets near it. This showed that it had been the place where people collected water. We could imagine the scene that dozens of people were standing by this fountain with buckets in their hands.  What surprised the archaeologists most was that there was an ancient theatre in the northeastern part. There was a stage and several stone seats uncovered. </p><p>Right next to the theatre, archaeologists unearthed a long stone.  Then more stones were found, and they extended across a deeper area. From this, we could conclude that these stones were from an old bridge on the river. Architects were very skilled back then. They used their wisdom to conquer technical obstacles.  But people use their wisdom much more than millie for practical purposes. In the southwestern area, what seemed to be like a garden was uncovered. There were pots and wooden basins. It must have been a very beautiful place, full of plants and flowers of different colours.  </p><p>More importantly, there were two statues of lions just beside the gate. It means French people admired the power of this beast at that time, so that they used stones to make these statues. Not far from Dolby House, also in the central area, there was an elevated area, but its lower area in the centre.  Through a lot of careful research, archaeologists came to the conclusion that this was a Roman bath. It''s where rich people went to enjoy a comfortable and relaxing hot bath. Next to the bath, there was a sculpture of a woman who was like pouring water into the bath.  Across from it, on the other side of the bath pool, people can see the sculpture of the Roman Emperor who ruled this area for many years. These discoveries are good material for us to study the history of Turin and southern France.  </p><p><span>What we''re going to do now is that is the end of part four. You now have half a minute to check your answers.  </span></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.2.S1668.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1668),
