(N'1b9ef9c1-3a58-5ab4-ac20-9f2713a6d18f',N'158fe2dc-0fe1-5193-b851-ace43f30cb56',0,3,19,N'<p>Joan says the TWO ways the RDA needs to improve are by</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Joan cho rằng hoạt động gây quỹ hiện tại chưa hiệu quả và cần được cải thiện. Key words: fundraising efforts: nỗ lực gây quỹ, inefficient: kém hiệu quả.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1662),
(N'7a4dc7b6-3c6c-5f92-9436-874df9c4f3a0',N'158fe2dc-0fe1-5193-b851-ace43f30cb56',0,3,19,N'<p>Joan says the TWO ways the RDA needs to improve are by</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là E. Joan nhấn mạnh các trung tâm chưa nhận thức đầy đủ về khó khăn của người khuyết tật. Key words: difficulties: khó khăn, disabled: người khuyết tật. </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1662),
(N'a3c2a920-68c8-5429-8612-304150879132',N'1ef71a32-973c-58d7-b32b-b7ff2006fba9',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 2] Test 9 - Section 3</h2><p>Right. Ravi, tell me how the research for your assignment about children''s outdoor play is going. Yes. Well, I''ve been reading about why children spend less time playing outside now than 20 or 30 years ago. So is it just because of the amount of online time that children have now, which obviously happens indoors? Well, if that was the case, there''d be a correlation between high Internet use and low levels of outdoor play.</p><p>And it''s nothing to do with whether there are adequate facilities outside, playgrounds for example, as any open space will do. There''s perhaps no single more important factor than parental worries about the dangers of vehicles on the streets. But it could also be that children today prefer playing indoors to outdoors? Well, a study in Tokyo suggests it''s the other way around. But children are spending more time in structured activities and out-of-school clubs than before. And this has a knock-on effect on what''s left for outdoor play.</p><p>Right. Your premise is that outdoor play is good. What exactly do you want to focus on in your assignment? Well, not so much on things that apply to exercise in general, like children''s muscles getting stronger because they run around. And not on what seems, for instance, self-evident, that outdoor play helps children to concentrate on things other than lessons. Sure. I''d like to investigate some of the more unexpected benefits, like to children''s digestion. This is because fresh air tends to stimulate hunger. Perhaps leaving to one side the question of whether climbing trees helps educate children about the natural world. No, not really relevant. What sort of decisions do outdoor games and activities force children to make? Well, for example, how they decide how dangerous something might be. And I''d like to outline the main findings on that. Okay. And as you''re arguing that there''s been a shift in play patterns over the last generation or so, how are you going to substantiate this? Yes. Surveys have compared parents'' recollections of their childhood play 30 years ago with what their children do now.</p><p>And the nature of what children do when they are playing outdoors has changed. How exactly? Well, surprisingly, it''s not so much how often children play outside or preferences for large or small groups, but the fact that the play sessions don''t go on for as long. And games involving one child running after another, who tries to get away, these are still as ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'popular as ever. But one thing that has declined is improvised games. And I''d like to examine why this might be. Interesting. And what about safety? Well, from my reading, serious accidents are still rare. More kids hurt themselves falling out of bed than out of trees.</p><p><span><span>Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30.</span></span></p><p>I think the decline of outdoor play is a problem which should worry parents. I mean, perhaps the children themselves don''t miss it. They may be happy to spend lots of time indoors. But the links between inactivity and physical problems when they''re adults are so well established that I think kids should be encouraged to spend more time outdoors with their brothers and sisters and friends. What about schools? Well, what I think they should do differently regarding outdoor play, and it wouldn''t actually require a great deal of expenditure, is base their policy on different outcomes, like developing children''s confidence, not just thinking about exam results. Although I don''t know how it would go down with the parents.</p><p>And do children get more contact with nature in rural areas? There''s research on this by Smith and Barker. Intuitively, you''d expect children in rural areas to spend more time out in the open. But in fact, they are generally told to keep off agricultural land. Plus, there are difficulties with supervision outside towns and cities. Right. And Smith and Barker''s paper was published in... 2001. Not the most up-to-date, but still valid to use. But you''ll need to stipulate very precisely that their focus was, if I remember correctly, just on rural areas. So you''d need to consider how far their restricted findings might be applicable to urban areas as well. Okay. Right. So now I''d suggest you produce an overview...</p><p><span><span>That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.</span></span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Right. Ravi, tell me how the research for your assignment about children''s outdoor play is going. Yes. Well, I''ve been reading about why children spend less time playing outside now than 20 or 30 years ago. So is it just because of the amount of online time that children have now, which obviously happens indoors? Well, if that was the case, there''d be a correlation between high Internet use and low levels of outdoor play.</p><p>And it''s nothing to do with whether there are adequate facilities outside, playgrounds for example, as any open space will do. There''s perhaps no single more important factor than parental worries about the dangers of vehicles on the streets. But it could also be that children today prefer playing indoors to outdoors? Well, a study in Tokyo suggests it''s the other way around. But children are spending more time in structured activities and out-of-school clubs than before. And this has a knock-on effect on what''s left for outdoor play.</p><p>Right. Your premise is that outdoor play is good. What exactly do you want to focus on in your assignment? Well, not so much on things that apply to exercise in general, like children''s muscles getting stronger because they run around. And not on what seems, for instance, self-evident, that outdoor play helps children to concentrate on things other than lessons. Sure. I''d like to investigate some of the more unexpected benefits, like to children''s digestion. This is because fresh air tends to stimulate hunger. Perhaps leaving to one side the question of whether climbing trees helps educate children about the natural world. No, not really relevant. What sort of decisions do outdoor games and activities force children to make? Well, for example, how they decide how dangerous something might be. And I''d like to outline the main findings on that. Okay. And as you''re arguing that there''s been a shift in play patterns over the last generation or so, how are you going to substantiate this? Yes. Surveys have compared parents'' recollections of their childhood play 30 years ago with what their children do now.</p><p>And the nature of what children do when they are playing outdoors has changed. How exactly? Well, surprisingly, it''s not so much how often children play outside or preferences for large or small groups, but the fact that the play sessions don''t go on for as long. And games involving one child running after another, who tries to get away, these are still as popular as ever. But one thing that' AS nvarchar(max))+N' has declined is improvised games. And I''d like to examine why this might be. Interesting. And what about safety? Well, from my reading, serious accidents are still rare. More kids hurt themselves falling out of bed than out of trees.</p><p><span><span>Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30.</span></span></p><p>I think the decline of outdoor play is a problem which should worry parents. I mean, perhaps the children themselves don''t miss it. They may be happy to spend lots of time indoors. But the links between inactivity and physical problems when they''re adults are so well established that I think kids should be encouraged to spend more time outdoors with their brothers and sisters and friends. What about schools? Well, what I think they should do differently regarding outdoor play, and it wouldn''t actually require a great deal of expenditure, is base their policy on different outcomes, like developing children''s confidence, not just thinking about exam results. Although I don''t know how it would go down with the parents.</p><p>And do children get more contact with nature in rural areas? There''s research on this by Smith and Barker. Intuitively, you''d expect children in rural areas to spend more time out in the open. But in fact, they are generally told to keep off agricultural land. Plus, there are difficulties with supervision outside towns and cities. Right. And Smith and Barker''s paper was published in... 2001. Not the most up-to-date, but still valid to use. But you''ll need to stipulate very precisely that their focus was, if I remember correctly, just on rural areas. So you''d need to consider how far their restricted findings might be applicable to urban areas as well. Okay. Right. So now I''d suggest you produce an overview...</p><p><span><span>That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.</span></span></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-2/iL.VOL2.9.S1662.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1662),
