(N'9ff047fd-86b1-574e-ac2d-c71aa0edb6d6',N'90ad41b3-801e-59b1-80b6-e527052273ab',0,3,19,N'<p>Why does Brad say he can help Jeff anytime?</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là A vì Brad nói rõ rằng tuần sau anh ấy không có bài kiểm tra. 
- Đây là lý do trực tiếp khiến anh ấy có thể giúp Jeff bất cứ lúc nào. 
- Từ khóa quan trọng là no tests this next week.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1651),
(N'1ba8ee11-4b17-5353-938b-cb492e4f62d3',N'd02558ec-1a09-5a73-b19b-bdd1030df360',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Case study on LCP technologies</h2><p><span>Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. </span></p><p>The basis of my work has been a case study of a company in India called LCP Technologies. You may have heard about it as it has been in the news lately. India, as you know, is famous for its high quality software companies. LCP Technologies is a software company specializing in outsourcing, designing solutions to specific problems other companies have. And it''s one of the biggest by size in the country with over 10,000 employees. The new CEO, Manjeet Khanna, has built a reputation for innovation that has attracted attention from every continent. His basic idea is not to reduce levels of management but to make the workplace more democratic. By that I don''t mean that everyone has equal power and equal responsibility, which most people would agree would make a company impossible to manage. But everyone is encouraged to be a critical and active part of the company. </p><p>Let me give you an example. Many companies have a system of rating and grading employees and managers are also graded, but normally by superiors as this sort of information generally goes upwards and it''s used for reviews and performance assessments. In LCP Technologies, everyone, including the CEO, is graded. And what''s more, these grades are published on the internal staff website, accessible for everyone in the company. The initial reaction from managers not used to such a concept of transparency was understandably negative, as you can imagine, but it did work. </p><p>According to Khanna, one benefit in particular is that the culture has changed. It''s now one of openness, much more so than in the past. What''s more, managers and in fact the whole workforce now feel much more comfortable commenting on and also correcting any management faults. So, the philosophy has encouraged good communication at all levels of the company and that works to the benefit of everyone. In fact, the motto Khanna goes by, which may sound unusual in an era where we are all used to thinking of the customer as king, is employee first. The benefits of this style are obvious. One of LCP''s problems was high staff turnover, with trained software engineers leaving to join another company, sometimes after just a few months'' service. This is one of the typical problems of industries that are in demand. However, the company now manages to keep its staff. And according to statistics, considerably fewer key engine' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ers are leaving than the industry average, indicating a high level of job satisfaction. </p><p>This has been an important part of enabling the terrific growth of the company and that, in turn, has seen income increase by 32% in two years, which is pretty astonishing. What''s more, according to Khanna, his philosophy reinforces the message that the solution to a problem can come from any part of the company, not simply from the top. The system depends on winning the trust of everyone involved, of course. Khanna again broke with tradition by making it clear that grading would not be used to determine promotion. Two other tools that form part of his management style are his personal touch. He answers all emails from anyone in the company in person and is on record as saying that this can take up to 10 hours a week of his time. And finally, he introduced a novel approach to dealing with staff issues. Khanna developed an online forum for complaints. </p><p>Each one is called a ticket, since that avoids the overtly unpleasant image of a complaint. Any employee or manager can post one online about absolutely anything, whether about faulty air conditioning, food choices in the cafeteria, or even much more sensitive issues such as the length of vacation you''re allowed. When the new system for dealing with complaints was introduced, people were unsure about criticizing the company and so sent their complaints in anonymously. But then it was impossible to check if they were happy with the action taken. So, a ban was placed on this, and now everyone has to take responsibility for their complaint. Another interesting aspect of the company. </p><p></p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span>Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. </span></p><p>The basis of my work has been a case study of a company in India called LCP Technologies. You may have heard about it as it has been in the news lately. India, as you know, is famous for its high quality software companies. LCP Technologies is a software company specializing in outsourcing, designing solutions to specific problems other companies have. And it''s one of the biggest by size in the country with over 10,000 employees. The new CEO, Manjeet Khanna, has built a reputation for innovation that has attracted attention from every continent. His basic idea is not to reduce levels of management but to make the workplace more democratic. By that I don''t mean that everyone has equal power and equal responsibility, which most people would agree would make a company impossible to manage. But everyone is encouraged to be a critical and active part of the company. </p><p>Let me give you an example. Many companies have a system of rating and grading employees and managers are also graded, but normally by superiors as this sort of information generally goes upwards and it''s used for reviews and performance assessments. In LCP Technologies, everyone, including the CEO, is graded. And what''s more, these grades are published on the internal staff website, accessible for everyone in the company. The initial reaction from managers not used to such a concept of transparency was understandably negative, as you can imagine, but it did work. </p><p>According to Khanna, one benefit in particular is that the culture has changed. It''s now one of openness, much more so than in the past. What''s more, managers and in fact the whole workforce now feel much more comfortable commenting on and also correcting any management faults. So, the philosophy has encouraged good communication at all levels of the company and that works to the benefit of everyone. In fact, the motto Khanna goes by, which may sound unusual in an era where we are all used to thinking of the customer as king, is employee first. The benefits of this style are obvious. One of LCP''s problems was high staff turnover, with trained software engineers leaving to join another company, sometimes after just a few months'' service. This is one of the typical problems of industries that are in demand. However, the company now manages to keep its staff. And according to statistics, considerably fewer key engineers are leaving than the industry avera' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ge, indicating a high level of job satisfaction. </p><p>This has been an important part of enabling the terrific growth of the company and that, in turn, has seen income increase by 32% in two years, which is pretty astonishing. What''s more, according to Khanna, his philosophy reinforces the message that the solution to a problem can come from any part of the company, not simply from the top. The system depends on winning the trust of everyone involved, of course. Khanna again broke with tradition by making it clear that grading would not be used to determine promotion. Two other tools that form part of his management style are his personal touch. He answers all emails from anyone in the company in person and is on record as saying that this can take up to 10 hours a week of his time. And finally, he introduced a novel approach to dealing with staff issues. Khanna developed an online forum for complaints. </p><p>Each one is called a ticket, since that avoids the overtly unpleasant image of a complaint. Any employee or manager can post one online about absolutely anything, whether about faulty air conditioning, food choices in the cafeteria, or even much more sensitive issues such as the length of vacation you''re allowed. When the new system for dealing with complaints was introduced, people were unsure about criticizing the company and so sent their complaints in anonymously. But then it was impossible to check if they were happy with the action taken. So, a ban was placed on this, and now everyone has to take responsibility for their complaint. Another interesting aspect of the company. </p><p></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-5/iL.VOL5.9.S1651.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1651),
(N'8cbc9db4-2474-5e0a-898b-72fde33f1c26',N'1ba8ee11-4b17-5353-938b-cb492e4f62d3',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.</p><p>Write <span><span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS </span></span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,1,NULL,0,1651),
