(N'eb4ac460-70d9-5e87-97af-8a9ebbb01367',N'bfd8c313-4dcf-537e-b1de-e6270f140462',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose<span><span> SIX</span></span> answers from the box and write correct letter <span><span>A-I.</span></span> </p><p>Where can each of the following items be found?</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,1,NULL,0,1646),
(N'd77cf08b-48c2-5f57-abe3-5c3c7faf89bf',N'eb4ac460-70d9-5e87-97af-8a9ebbb01367',0,3,19,N'<p>Alarm</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là D. Chuông báo động được đặt ngay cạnh cửa sau, gần công tắc đèn trong bếp, nên khi vào nhà bằng cửa sau sẽ gặp ngay hệ thống báo động. Key words: alarm: báo động, back door: cửa sau. Scripts: “that is where the alarm is situated. Right next to the light switch, just beside the door.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1646),
(N'b96638c7-fa75-5f3b-a7b4-9d461fc675e7',N'eb4ac460-70d9-5e87-97af-8a9ebbb01367',0,3,19,N'<p>Garage key</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Chìa khóa garage được treo trên móc bên trong tủ ở hành lang, cạnh tủ nước nóng. Key words: garage key: chìa khóa garage, hallway cupboard: tủ hành lang. Scripts: “You’ll find them hanging on a hook inside the cupboard in the hallway.”

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1646),
(N'85b3f0bb-4fc0-5fd2-aca8-d015eb540aa7',N'eb4ac460-70d9-5e87-97af-8a9ebbb01367',0,3,19,N'<p>Laundry detergent</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là H. Bột giặt được để cùng chỗ với nước rửa bát, bên dưới bồn rửa trong bếp. Key words: laundry detergent: bột giặt, kitchen sink: bồn rửa bếp. Scripts: “next to the dishwashing liquid under the kitchen sink.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1646),
(N'd62c8c08-2c26-5006-875c-c1287766ddb4',N'eb4ac460-70d9-5e87-97af-8a9ebbb01367',0,3,19,N'<p>Beach </p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A. Khăn tắm biển được để trong giỏ đặt trên máy giặt và có thể mang ra bãi biển sử dụng. Key words: beach towels: khăn biển, washing machine: máy giặt. Scripts: “a pile of beach towels in a basket on the washing machine.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1646),
(N'dacdb71b-a70f-550b-8e46-a7e502f86105',N'eb4ac460-70d9-5e87-97af-8a9ebbb01367',0,3,19,N'<p>Bath towels</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là C. Khăn tắm dùng trong phòng tắm được để trong tủ nước nóng, trên kệ phía trên bình nước. Key words: bath towels: khăn phòng tắm, hot water cupboard: tủ nước nóng. Scripts: “newer towels for use in the bathroom only… in the hot water cupboard.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1646),
(N'b6f23ee4-73c7-57d6-be83-e278c9ec20dd',N'eb4ac460-70d9-5e87-97af-8a9ebbb01367',0,3,19,N'<p>Light bulbs</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là G. Bóng đèn dự phòng được để trong hộp giày đặt trên tủ ngăn kéo trong phòng ngủ. Key words: light bulbs: bóng đèn, bedroom: phòng ngủ. Scripts: “spare light bulbs in a shoebox… in the bedroom.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1646),
(N'19554e17-4d8b-5340-b401-e8e6f1f2f161',N'bfd8c313-4dcf-537e-b1de-e6270f140462',0,2,18,N'<p>Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,2,NULL,0,1646),
(N'18e3ed0f-8c8c-5bbe-8454-a8d30e47b50e',N'19554e17-4d8b-5340-b401-e8e6f1f2f161',0,3,19,N'<p>Difficult parking in town at the weekend because of so many }{SPACE}{ </p><p>Museum is closed on }{SPACE}{ </p><p><span>Recommended places to eat: 19</span></p> }{SPACE}{ for Chinese food</p><p>Pizzeria for Italian food</p><p>Phone number for takeaway pizza: }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>Đáp án là tourists. Việc đỗ xe trong thị trấn trở nên khó khăn vào cuối tuần vì có quá nhiều khách du lịch đổ về. Key words: parking: đỗ xe, tourists: khách du lịch, weekend: cuối tuần. Scripts: “except for weekends when the place is swamped with tourists.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1646),
(N'0aa56add-a473-588b-a185-693f75629a08',N'19554e17-4d8b-5340-b401-e8e6f1f2f161',0,3,19,N'Question number 18',N'<p>Đáp án là Mondays. Bảo tàng Lịch sử Cổ đại không mở cửa vào thứ Hai, nên không thể kết hợp mua sắm với tham quan vào ngày này. Key words: museum: bảo tàng, closed: đóng cửa, Monday: thứ Hai. Scripts: “you should know that it’s not open on Mondays.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1646),
(N'25a0c3f5-428f-5c38-b636-be08294f5aac',N'19554e17-4d8b-5340-b401-e8e6f1f2f161',0,3,19,N'Question number 19',N'<p>Đáp án là Happy Dragon. Khi nói về đồ ăn Trung Quốc, người nói khuyên nên chọn nhà hàng Happy Dragon vì chất lượng tốt và rất được người dân địa phương ưa chuộng. Key words: Chinese food: đồ Trung Quốc, restaurant: nhà hàng, locals: người địa phương. Scripts: “If you like Chinese, the Happy Dragon has excellent food.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1646),
(N'073c9383-c84c-5660-93c5-5564c08e1467',N'19554e17-4d8b-5340-b401-e8e6f1f2f161',0,3,19,N'Question number 20',N'<p>Đáp án là 3231190. Đây là số điện thoại mới để gọi pizza mang đi, vì số trên thực đơn cũ đã thay đổi. Key words: phone number: số điện thoại, takeaway pizza: pizza mang đi. Scripts: “Phone 3231190 for pizza.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1646),
(N'32d7116e-0cff-5752-8802-d8f6474babf0',N'3ac15c67-ae56-540f-b03a-2f455a0530f4',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 2] Test 8 - Section 3</h2><p>Thanks for coming in today to discuss your internship experience. Completing some sort of work experience like an internship is a core part of our Master''s program and we want to make sure students are able to make the most of it. That''s fine. So, as I understand, you were offered an internship by Gregory Associates. Is that correct? Actually, I got offers from a few companies but Gregory Associates was the only one I seriously considered. Was there any reason for that? Yeah, they didn''t offer the best conditions. Some of the other companies were offering to cover transportation and other living costs, that kind of thing. But I knew Gregory Associates was a widely recognized leader in the industry and that was the big factor for me. And were you happy with your choice? </p><p>Well, yes and no. Mixed feelings? Yeah, I mean, don''t get me wrong. Everyone in the office was great. They didn''t talk down to me and I was happy to assist if I wasn''t sure about something. Was it the work, then? Some students do find internships a little tedious and boring. It''s not that it was boring. I was doing new things every day and I loved that. They really kept me on my toes. It''s just that I''m studying economics, you know? But most of the projects I was assigned to involved more administrative stuff. It just wasn''t relevant to what I''ve been studying.  I''m sorry to hear that. I''ll make a note of it. Let''s talk about the good things. What did you particularly enjoy about the experience? Well, as an intern, the managers do tend to keep you at arm''s length. They just don''t trust you enough to let you take on a lot of responsibility for the big projects. That''s understandable. Yes, but in a way, I liked that because I got to stand back and watch and get a real sense of how a company like that runs on a day-to-day basis. And that was the highlight for me. That''s great to hear. How did you find managing the internship alongside your study commitments? Well, because my internship was over the summer break, that wasn''t an issue for me at all. OK. What would you say was the biggest struggle, then? In the beginning, I might have said the hours. Those 6 a.m. starts were tough. But I quickly got used to that. In retrospect, it was worth it. As I wasn''t earning anything, the whole experience really drained my savings account. Yeah, that can be tough. And my last question. What was the outcome of this internship for you? Some of our students are lucky' AS nvarchar(max))+N' enough to get offers of employment before finishing. At first, I was hoping I would be one of them. In the end, I wasn''t, but I''m happy about that now. It''s good for me, after all. I''m going to finish my degree, because I''m only a semester away from graduation now. But then, after that, I''ve decided to pursue a different line of work. Well, I do hope you''re successful with that. Now, we''re putting together a process for students who want to apply for internships, and we were wondering if you could help us with it. Sure. What would you say is the first step? Before they do anything else, students need to get their documents sorted. Companies need to see all sorts of things, such as reference letters and verified copies of academic transcripts. It can take time to do all of that, number one. And then students should begin researching companies? Absolutely. They should look at a wide range of companies and the internships they offer. They really shouldn''t limit themselves at that stage. It would be time-consuming to apply to them all, though. Yeah, so I think they need to weed out those positions they are not qualified for and then? Well, the next part is the applications, of course. </p><p>I think the big mistake here is that some students just send the same cover letter and the same CV to each company, when in reality every position is a little bit different. They really need to alter their applications so that they refer to the individual needs of each position. I''ll make a note of that. And should students get an interview, it''s best to call each company. An email is too easy to ignore or delete. And not too soon, either. A week after the applications have been submitted is probably ideal. So if they get an interview, what''s next? Obviously, they need to prepare. For me, this included all sorts of things, like practicing my body language in front of the mirror. Most students are so obsessed with having the right answers. But I think the most important thing is actually to ask questions. It shows the employer that you are genuinely interested in their company and in the position. That''s really helpful advice. Thanks for coming by today. No problem.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Thanks for coming in today to discuss your internship experience. Completing some sort of work experience like an internship is a core part of our Master''s program and we want to make sure students are able to make the most of it. That''s fine. So, as I understand, you were offered an internship by Gregory Associates. Is that correct? Actually, I got offers from a few companies but Gregory Associates was the only one I seriously considered. Was there any reason for that? Yeah, they didn''t offer the best conditions. Some of the other companies were offering to cover transportation and other living costs, that kind of thing. But I knew Gregory Associates was a widely recognized leader in the industry and that was the big factor for me. And were you happy with your choice? </p><p>Well, yes and no. Mixed feelings? Yeah, I mean, don''t get me wrong. Everyone in the office was great. They didn''t talk down to me and I was happy to assist if I wasn''t sure about something. Was it the work, then? Some students do find internships a little tedious and boring. It''s not that it was boring. I was doing new things every day and I loved that. They really kept me on my toes. It''s just that I''m studying economics, you know? But most of the projects I was assigned to involved more administrative stuff. It just wasn''t relevant to what I''ve been studying.  I''m sorry to hear that. I''ll make a note of it. Let''s talk about the good things. What did you particularly enjoy about the experience? Well, as an intern, the managers do tend to keep you at arm''s length. They just don''t trust you enough to let you take on a lot of responsibility for the big projects. That''s understandable. Yes, but in a way, I liked that because I got to stand back and watch and get a real sense of how a company like that runs on a day-to-day basis. And that was the highlight for me. That''s great to hear. How did you find managing the internship alongside your study commitments? Well, because my internship was over the summer break, that wasn''t an issue for me at all. OK. What would you say was the biggest struggle, then? In the beginning, I might have said the hours. Those 6 a.m. starts were tough. But I quickly got used to that. In retrospect, it was worth it. As I wasn''t earning anything, the whole experience really drained my savings account. Yeah, that can be tough. And my last question. What was the outcome of this internship for you? Some of our students are lucky enough to get offers of employment' AS nvarchar(max))+N' before finishing. At first, I was hoping I would be one of them. In the end, I wasn''t, but I''m happy about that now. It''s good for me, after all. I''m going to finish my degree, because I''m only a semester away from graduation now. But then, after that, I''ve decided to pursue a different line of work. Well, I do hope you''re successful with that. Now, we''re putting together a process for students who want to apply for internships, and we were wondering if you could help us with it. Sure. What would you say is the first step? Before they do anything else, students need to get their documents sorted. Companies need to see all sorts of things, such as reference letters and verified copies of academic transcripts. It can take time to do all of that, number one. And then students should begin researching companies? Absolutely. They should look at a wide range of companies and the internships they offer. They really shouldn''t limit themselves at that stage. It would be time-consuming to apply to them all, though. Yeah, so I think they need to weed out those positions they are not qualified for and then? Well, the next part is the applications, of course. </p><p>I think the big mistake here is that some students just send the same cover letter and the same CV to each company, when in reality every position is a little bit different. They really need to alter their applications so that they refer to the individual needs of each position. I''ll make a note of that. And should students get an interview, it''s best to call each company. An email is too easy to ignore or delete. And not too soon, either. A week after the applications have been submitted is probably ideal. So if they get an interview, what''s next? Obviously, they need to prepare. For me, this included all sorts of things, like practicing my body language in front of the mirror. Most students are so obsessed with having the right answers. But I think the most important thing is actually to ask questions. It shows the employer that you are genuinely interested in their company and in the position. That''s really helpful advice. Thanks for coming by today. No problem.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-2/iL.VOL2.8.S1646.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1646),
(N'23b7a601-4dff-5f75-add1-7d158794e8ba',N'32d7116e-0cff-5752-8802-d8f6474babf0',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter<span> A, B or C.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,1,NULL,0,1646),
(N'eab97bbb-92be-5a85-87cf-d25f78682817',N'23b7a601-4dff-5f75-add1-7d158794e8ba',0,3,19,N'<p>Why did Joanne accept the offer from Gregory Associates?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Joanne nói rõ cô không chọn Gregory Associates vì quyền lợi tài chính, mà vì đây là một công ty có tên tuổi lớn trong ngành và được công nhận rộng rãi. Key words: well known: nổi tiếng, industry leader: công ty hàng đầu. Scripts: “Gregory Associates was a widely recognized leader in the industry.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1646),
(N'407f84fd-61ec-5ad5-8984-f5ce96853aea',N'23b7a601-4dff-5f75-add1-7d158794e8ba',0,3,19,N'<p>Joanne was disappointed because</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là C. Joanne thất vọng vì phần lớn công việc trong kỳ thực tập mang tính hành chính và không liên quan trực tiếp đến chuyên ngành kinh tế mà cô đang theo học. Key words: not relevant: không liên quan, studies: ngành học. Scripts: “most of the projects I was assigned to involved more administrative stuff… It just wasn’t relevant to what I’ve been studying.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1646),
(N'3ff8155a-0d4e-56da-a784-5f4648f84462',N'23b7a601-4dff-5f75-add1-7d158794e8ba',0,3,19,N'<p>What did Joanne like best about her internship?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A. Điều Joanne thích nhất là được quan sát cách một công ty lớn vận hành hàng ngày, dù không được giao nhiều trách nhiệm trực tiếp cho các dự án lớn. Key words: observe: quan sát, how a company runs: cách công ty vận hành. Scripts: “I got to stand back and watch and get a real sense of how a company like that runs.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1646),
(N'eb162d72-3cf3-5825-a688-7194ecc62a93',N'23b7a601-4dff-5f75-add1-7d158794e8ba',0,3,19,N'<p>What was the hardest part of the internship?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Phần khó khăn nhất của kỳ thực tập là vấn đề tài chính vì Joanne không có thu nhập, khiến khoản tiết kiệm của cô bị hao hụt đáng kể. Key words: no income: không có thu nhập, savings: tiền tiết kiệm. Scripts: “As I wasn’t earning anything, the whole experience really drained my savings account.”

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1646),
(N'6cfd7b7d-9644-5a3e-813e-0d5c7bdbb1aa',N'23b7a601-4dff-5f75-add1-7d158794e8ba',0,3,19,N'<p>During the internship Joanne</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A. Sau kỳ thực tập, Joanne quyết định thay đổi định hướng nghề nghiệp và theo đuổi một lĩnh vực khác sau khi hoàn thành bằng Thạc sĩ. Key words: change career: thay đổi nghề nghiệp, different line of work: hướng đi khác. Scripts: “after that, I’ve decided to pursue a different line of work.”

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1646),
(N'91dda857-fd71-503e-9781-4ec34f219001',N'32d7116e-0cff-5752-8802-d8f6474babf0',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below. </p><p>Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,2,NULL,0,1646),
(N'e2e9875e-8ce8-5d2b-9159-9e71f266853c',N'91dda857-fd71-503e-9781-4ec34f219001',0,3,19,N'<p>How to apply for an internship</p> }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{ ',N'<p>Đáp án là documents. Bước đầu tiên khi xin thực tập là chuẩn bị đầy đủ hồ sơ như thư giới thiệu và bản sao bảng điểm đã được xác nhận. Key words: documents: giấy tờ, transcripts: bảng điểm. Scripts: “students need to get their documents sorted.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1646),
(N'ca355854-ad96-57d2-adaa-82b5e16664bc',N'91dda857-fd71-503e-9781-4ec34f219001',0,3,19,N'Question number 27',N'<p>Đáp án là research companies. Sinh viên cần tìm hiểu nhiều công ty khác nhau và các vị trí thực tập họ cung cấp trước khi nộp đơn. Key words: research: tìm hiểu, companies: công ty. Scripts: “students should begin researching companies.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1646),
(N'de24d412-260d-56f9-b080-863e6c8a0041',N'91dda857-fd71-503e-9781-4ec34f219001',0,3,19,N'Question number 28',N'<p>Đáp án là tailor applications. Hồ sơ xin thực tập cần được điều chỉnh cho phù hợp với từng vị trí thay vì dùng chung một CV và thư xin việc. Key words: tailor: điều chỉnh, individual position: từng vị trí riêng. Scripts: “they really need to alter their applications… refer to the individual needs of each position.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1646),
(N'db17064d-5fa1-533d-bdfd-33a99740627a',N'91dda857-fd71-503e-9781-4ec34f219001',0,3,19,N'Question number 29',N'<p>Đáp án là call companies. Sau khi nộp hồ sơ, sinh viên nên gọi điện cho công ty để theo dõi kết quả vì email dễ bị bỏ qua. Key words: call: gọi điện, follow up: theo dõi. Scripts: “it’s best to call each company.”

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1646),
(N'40ddaf49-8f4d-5550-8597-8c042377b6e5',N'91dda857-fd71-503e-9781-4ec34f219001',0,3,19,N'Question number 30',N'<p>Đáp án là ask questions. Khi phỏng vấn, việc đặt câu hỏi cho nhà tuyển dụng thể hiện sự quan tâm thực sự đến công ty và vị trí ứng tuyển. Key words: ask questions: đặt câu hỏi, genuine interest: quan tâm thật sự. Scripts: “the most important thing is actually to ask questions.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1646),
(N'e44f1ce0-0c9d-5944-9347-e1ee9773b444',N'3ac15c67-ae56-540f-b03a-2f455a0530f4',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Research on A Chain Store in the UK</h2><p>Welcome, ladies and gentlemen, to the second lecture in our series on chain stores in the UK. Today, I will be focusing on a very successful global petrol company called Telsa, paying particular attention to their expansion and future development. There will be time for questions at the end, so please refrain from interrupting during the talk. When it was founded, the company was a family-run organisation with only one shop open in a small town in southern England. To pay their accounts for returning customers, which resulted in a huge expansion of their client base. This strategy was so successful that they were able to open petrol stations across the country, subsequently increasing their market share in England. Within a decade, Telsa were running establishments throughout. They were able to collate data that highlighted which areas were the most profitable and receptive to their business. They discovered that by closing the less profitable stations in the south of Scotland, they could increase the income from their other more profitable stations. &nbsp;</p><p>  To run the company had exceeded the abilities of the family, they needed to hire professional consultants. After assessing the company''s financial statements, the consultants advised that Telsa closed the head office of the convenience stores in Oxford. They deemed the head office. In order to avoid firing any employees, they transferred over 200 staff to other offices and retrained them to carry out their new roles. Now that Telsa had conquered the petrol market in the UK, they needed of convenience stores that had been grown by local farmers. This strategy would not only broaden the scope of the company, but also help to strengthen its relationships with local communities. Every year, as feedback is heard from all levels of the organization, from the sales staff to the CEOs, they did this by assigning every manager the task of holding staff discussions at which ideas and concerns would be passed on for evaluation at the meeting. &nbsp;   Tesla decided that by increasing the flexibility of the profit-making strategies of each of the retail stores, they subsequently became more commercially successful. The level of staff satisfaction also increased as a result, as the individuals felt that they were not being constrained to one way of thinking. To avoid this, were introduced in stages in order to familiarize the staff with the new regulations. Now i' AS nvarchar(max))+N't''s time to conclude the lecture for today, so I will leave you with some food for thought before you leave. In order to run a successful business, an independent consultant who can offer an objective and unbiased view on the running of your company. Emotion will only ever cloud your ability to make business-related decisions. Research suitable case studies and look at how department stores, for example, were able to alter their organizational structure whilst minimizing the risk to making decisions for your own business, so don''t undervalue them. Well, that wraps up our lecture for today. If there are any questions...</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Welcome, ladies and gentlemen, to the second lecture in our series on chain stores in the UK. Today, I will be focusing on a very successful global petrol company called Telsa, paying particular attention to their expansion and future development. There will be time for questions at the end, so please refrain from interrupting during the talk. When it was founded, the company was a family-run organisation with only one shop open in a small town in southern England. To pay their accounts for returning customers, which resulted in a huge expansion of their client base. This strategy was so successful that they were able to open petrol stations across the country, subsequently increasing their market share in England. Within a decade, Telsa were running establishments throughout. They were able to collate data that highlighted which areas were the most profitable and receptive to their business. They discovered that by closing the less profitable stations in the south of Scotland, they could increase the income from their other more profitable stations. &nbsp;</p><p>  To run the company had exceeded the abilities of the family, they needed to hire professional consultants. After assessing the company''s financial statements, the consultants advised that Telsa closed the head office of the convenience stores in Oxford. They deemed the head office. In order to avoid firing any employees, they transferred over 200 staff to other offices and retrained them to carry out their new roles. Now that Telsa had conquered the petrol market in the UK, they needed of convenience stores that had been grown by local farmers. This strategy would not only broaden the scope of the company, but also help to strengthen its relationships with local communities. Every year, as feedback is heard from all levels of the organization, from the sales staff to the CEOs, they did this by assigning every manager the task of holding staff discussions at which ideas and concerns would be passed on for evaluation at the meeting. &nbsp;   Tesla decided that by increasing the flexibility of the profit-making strategies of each of the retail stores, they subsequently became more commercially successful. The level of staff satisfaction also increased as a result, as the individuals felt that they were not being constrained to one way of thinking. To avoid this, were introduced in stages in order to familiarize the staff with the new regulations. Now it''s time to conclude the lecture for today, ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'so I will leave you with some food for thought before you leave. In order to run a successful business, an independent consultant who can offer an objective and unbiased view on the running of your company. Emotion will only ever cloud your ability to make business-related decisions. Research suitable case studies and look at how department stores, for example, were able to alter their organizational structure whilst minimizing the risk to making decisions for your own business, so don''t undervalue them. Well, that wraps up our lecture for today. If there are any questions...</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-2/iL.VOL2.8.S1646.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1646),
(N'abb3ecef-aae4-56b1-8e89-4d4abeeaf846',N'e44f1ce0-0c9d-5944-9347-e1ee9773b444',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below
