(N'f84abdde-7581-5ff5-8195-6b4504fd8699',N'c98474e0-8666-5693-bcfe-5e0baf68048b',0,3,19,N'<p>Which <span>TWO</span> points were most frequently made by students about the pieces they select for assessed performances?</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Những điểm nào sinh viên thường nói về bản nhạc họ chọn cho buổi biểu diễn được chấm điểm? </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ thích chọn những bản ít người biết (B) và chơi thuộc lòng (C). </p><p>+Từ vựng: +little-known pieces = bản nhạc ít được biết tới; +learn by heart = học thuộc lòng</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1589),
(N'1983a021-0cfe-5268-b201-0d119f7f577c',N'c98474e0-8666-5693-bcfe-5e0baf68048b',0,3,19,N'<p>Which <span>TWO</span> points were most frequently made by students about the pieces they select for assessed performances?</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Những điểm nào sinh viên thường nói về bản nhạc họ chọn cho buổi biểu diễn được chấm điểm? </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ thích chọn những bản ít người biết (B) và chơi thuộc lòng (C). </p><p>+Từ vựng: +little-known pieces = bản nhạc ít được biết tới; +learn by heart = học thuộc lòng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1589),
(N'3c53b211-822c-5403-a205-1bcf7566eb1d',N'5b4bd884-27f2-507b-99ca-973a06687f1e',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose <span>FOUR </span>answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span>A-G</span>, next to questions 27-30.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Student comments',15,0,4,NULL,0,1589),
(N'8fa09d9f-2ef8-57fe-91e1-65041e7079e5',N'3c53b211-822c-5403-a205-1bcf7566eb1d',0,3,19,N'<p>Performing solo</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Nhận xét của sinh viên về biểu diễn solo là gì? </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ thấy khó nhưng thú vị, vì đó là thử thách kích thích bản thân =&gt; difficult but enjoyable. </p><p>+Từ vựng: -stimulating = kích thích, khơi gợi hứng thú; -appreciate the challenge = trân trọng thử thách.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1589),
(N'5434ce20-5475-5809-8cbd-375f6ffc0090',N'3c53b211-822c-5403-a205-1bcf7566eb1d',0,3,19,N'<p>Deciding which pieces to perform</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Nhận xét của sinh viên về việc quyết định chọn bản nhạc biểu diễn? </p><p>+Giải thích: Quá trình này tốn nhiều thời gian (E). </p><p>+Từ vựng: -time-consuming = tốn thời gian; make up one’s mind = quyết định.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1589),
(N'db1af240-7a4b-5af8-becb-1d91ebd5a668',N'3c53b211-822c-5403-a205-1bcf7566eb1d',0,3,19,N'<p>Performing with musicians from a different genre</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Nhận xét của sinh viên về việc biểu diễn với nhạc sĩ từ thể loại khác? </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ ít khi làm việc này =&gt; happens infrequently (G).</p><p> +Từ vựng: -infrequently = hiếm khi; genre = thể loại âm nhạc.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1589),
(N'10d415bd-985a-5224-bb8e-695225461711',N'3c53b211-822c-5403-a205-1bcf7566eb1d',0,3,19,N'<p>Performing with musicians who play the same instrument</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Nhận xét của sinh viên về việc biểu diễn với người chơi cùng nhạc cụ? </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ thấy rất có ích, mang lại lợi ích lớn =&gt; very beneficial (A). </p><p>+Từ vựng: -beneficial = có lợi; helpful = hữu ích.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1589),
(N'e422cadb-7457-5f59-9f50-60127915757d',N'9bdf3c34-cc78-5b9f-a28d-bb0805572394',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Stone Age man in Japan </h2><p>So far in these lectures, we''ve been looking at evidence of human migration across the African continent and into mainland Asia. Today, let''s see what evidence we have for Stone Age man, specifically in Japan. The particular evidence, as you will see, has had a considerable impact on the beliefs of both archaeologists and anthropologists. Let''s start by mentioning the man who first discovered this evidence. In 1877, an American zoologist, Edward S. Morse, went looking for shells and fossils in Japan at Omori, an area which had once been coastal. But he also ended up discovering fragments of pottery. On the fragments, there was a pattern that was unlike anything archaeologists had seen before. At the time, it was thought that this pottery was no more than a few thousand years old. Then, in the 1960s, archaeologists were carrying out excavations in Fukui Cave in the Japanese island of Kyushu, and they found more examples of this pottery. They could now take advantage of carbon dating technology, and to their amazement, it showed that the pottery had been made around 12,000 years ago. That''s 10,000 B.C. This pottery has become known as Jomon pottery, and the period of time during which it was produced has been named the Jomon period. How did the people who made this pottery find their way to Japan in the first place? During the Ice Age, somewhere between 35,000 B.C. and 30,000 B.C., the Japanese islands as we know them today were connected to the eastern and southeastern Asian mainland. &nbsp;  </p><p> So you could cross from one large landmass to another over something that we call a land bridge. That''s how Stone Age humans crossed into Japan. But when temperatures finally rose, this led to some major environmental changes. Japan had been covered in frozen earth, but now, with the warmer climate, forests grew. With this new habitat, smaller animals began to appear, such as Sika deer, still found in Japan today, as well as wild boar and bear. Where once people had used spears to attack the elephants and giant deer that they depended upon for food, for hunting the new smaller animals, they replaced these weapons with arrows and developed skills in archery. The new habitat also meant that they had a new range of foodstuffs at their disposal, which they could collect—a greater variety of plants, nuts, and fish. Now, this way of life continued for thousands of years. But then we come to the early Jomon period, which was from abo' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ut 10,500 BC to 8,000 BC. Well, according to previous archaeological theory, the people in these very early societies didn''t make or use pottery, because they were hunters who were constantly on the move, following the animals, and they wouldn''t have been able to carry it around with them. But it now appears that the early Jomon people actually seemed to have invented pottery making while they were still getting their food in this way, long before they had begun to rely on agriculture as a source of food. Later, in the Jomon period 8,000 BC to 5,000 BC, we find larger pots that were used to boil vegetable-type food to make it edible, which suggests that the Jomon people had become at least semi-sedentary— in other words, were remaining in the same place for a while. For a long time they had lived in caves, but now we find evidence of villages. One of these has been found to the southwest of Mount Fuji. There is evidence of ten round buildings. &nbsp;   </p><p>The Jomon dug shallow pits in the ground and used wooden posts to hold up the roof. Each of these houses had its own two stones, a flat one and a grinding one, for breaking down the nuts and other hard vegetable-type foodstuffs the Jomon survived on at this time. This more sedentary culture led to important population increases. By the end of the Jomon period, we see the influence of migration from northern Asia and southern Pacific. For example, the newcomers brought with them technological developments in textiles and glassmaking, and they also introduced their marriage rituals. We also have evidence that the migrants influenced local architectural styles and methods of construction. &nbsp;   </p><p>At this time, the Jomon people also began to cultivate food, and in particular rice— at last, a real step towards the practice of agriculture. It is remarkable that this unique culture survived for so long without it. You have ten minutes to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet.  You have two minutes left. You have one minute left. Please stop writing and wait for your question booklet to be collected.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>So far in these lectures, we''ve been looking at evidence of human migration across the African continent and into mainland Asia. Today, let''s see what evidence we have for Stone Age man, specifically in Japan. The particular evidence, as you will see, has had a considerable impact on the beliefs of both archaeologists and anthropologists. Let''s start by mentioning the man who first discovered this evidence. In 1877, an American zoologist, Edward S. Morse, went looking for shells and fossils in Japan at Omori, an area which had once been coastal. But he also ended up discovering fragments of pottery. On the fragments, there was a pattern that was unlike anything archaeologists had seen before. At the time, it was thought that this pottery was no more than a few thousand years old. Then, in the 1960s, archaeologists were carrying out excavations in Fukui Cave in the Japanese island of Kyushu, and they found more examples of this pottery. They could now take advantage of carbon dating technology, and to their amazement, it showed that the pottery had been made around 12,000 years ago. That''s 10,000 B.C. This pottery has become known as Jomon pottery, and the period of time during which it was produced has been named the Jomon period. How did the people who made this pottery find their way to Japan in the first place? During the Ice Age, somewhere between 35,000 B.C. and 30,000 B.C., the Japanese islands as we know them today were connected to the eastern and southeastern Asian mainland. &nbsp;  </p><p> So you could cross from one large landmass to another over something that we call a land bridge. That''s how Stone Age humans crossed into Japan. But when temperatures finally rose, this led to some major environmental changes. Japan had been covered in frozen earth, but now, with the warmer climate, forests grew. With this new habitat, smaller animals began to appear, such as Sika deer, still found in Japan today, as well as wild boar and bear. Where once people had used spears to attack the elephants and giant deer that they depended upon for food, for hunting the new smaller animals, they replaced these weapons with arrows and developed skills in archery. The new habitat also meant that they had a new range of foodstuffs at their disposal, which they could collect—a greater variety of plants, nuts, and fish. Now, this way of life continued for thousands of years. But then we come to the early Jomon period, which was from about 10,500 BC to 8,000 BC. Well, ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'according to previous archaeological theory, the people in these very early societies didn''t make or use pottery, because they were hunters who were constantly on the move, following the animals, and they wouldn''t have been able to carry it around with them. But it now appears that the early Jomon people actually seemed to have invented pottery making while they were still getting their food in this way, long before they had begun to rely on agriculture as a source of food. Later, in the Jomon period 8,000 BC to 5,000 BC, we find larger pots that were used to boil vegetable-type food to make it edible, which suggests that the Jomon people had become at least semi-sedentary— in other words, were remaining in the same place for a while. For a long time they had lived in caves, but now we find evidence of villages. One of these has been found to the southwest of Mount Fuji. There is evidence of ten round buildings. &nbsp;   </p><p>The Jomon dug shallow pits in the ground and used wooden posts to hold up the roof. Each of these houses had its own two stones, a flat one and a grinding one, for breaking down the nuts and other hard vegetable-type foodstuffs the Jomon survived on at this time. This more sedentary culture led to important population increases. By the end of the Jomon period, we see the influence of migration from northern Asia and southern Pacific. For example, the newcomers brought with them technological developments in textiles and glassmaking, and they also introduced their marriage rituals. We also have evidence that the migrants influenced local architectural styles and methods of construction. &nbsp;   </p><p>At this time, the Jomon people also began to cultivate food, and in particular rice— at last, a real step towards the practice of agriculture. It is remarkable that this unique culture survived for so long without it. You have ten minutes to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet.  You have two minutes left. You have one minute left. Please stop writing and wait for your question booklet to be collected.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.8.S1589.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1589),
(N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',N'e422cadb-7457-5f59-9f50-60127915757d',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.

Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Stone Age Man in Japan',11,0,1,NULL,0,1589),
(N'6206cf00-dbf0-59b9-a7ca-213bfedd1a3d',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'<h3>Stone Age Man in Japan</h3><p><span>Evidence</span></p><p>1877 Omori</p><p>Zoologist found pieces of pottery with an unusual pattern</p><p>1960s Fukui cave</p><p>Same style of pottery dated from about }{SPACE}{ BC</p><p><span>35,000 to 30,000 BC</span></p><p>People used a  }{SPACE}{ to get into Japan</p><p>Higher temperatures resulted in:</p><ul><li><p>Growth of</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p><ul><li><p>New animal species</p></li></ul><ul><li><p>Use of</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ as weapons</p><ul><li><p>Availability of plants</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ and fish</p><p><span>10,500 to 8,000 BC (Early Jomon period)</span></p><ul><li><p>Old theory: people who were hunters didn''t make or use pottery</p></li></ul><ul><li><p>Present belief: pottery making was invented by Jomon hunters before the development of agriculture</p></li></ul><p><span>8,000 to 5,000 BC (Later Jomon period)</span></p><p>Jomon people were:</p><ul><li><p> Staying in one place for longer</p></li></ul><ul><li><p>Not living in caves but in </p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p><ul><li><p>Using pairs of </p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p><p>Sedentary lifestyle led to a rise in }{SPACE}{ </p><p><span>End of Jomon period</span></p><p>Migrants introduced:</p><ul><li><p>Developments in the manufacture of textiles and glass</p></li></ul><ul><li><p>New traditions for </p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ ceremonies</p><ul><li><p>New architecture</p></li></ul><ul><li><p>Main crop grown was </p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Cùng kiểu đồ gốm được xác định niên đại từ khoảng bao nhiêu năm trước Công nguyên? </p><p>+Dịch script: “Công nghệ định tuổi bằng carbon cho thấy đồ gốm được làm khoảng 12.000 năm trước, tức là 10.000 TCN.” +Giải thích: Dựa vào carbon dating, đồ gốm có niên đại khoảng 10,000 BC. </p><p>+Từ vựng: -carbon dating = định tuổi bằng carbon; -pottery = đồ gốm; BC = trước Công nguyên.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1589),
(N'8359e81e-a655-547a-a3ed-09cfd5384b4b',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Con người đã dùng gì để đến Nhật Bản? </p><p>+Dịch script: “Có thể băng qua các khối đất liền bằng thứ mà ta gọi là ‘cầu đất liền’.” </p><p>+Giải thích: Trong thời kỳ băng hà, các đảo Nhật Bản nối liền châu Á bằng land bridge. +Từ vựng: -land bridge = cầu đất liền; -cross over = băng qua.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1589),
(N'cf710045-5811-5530-b711-40bb47ac8465',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 33',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Nhiệt độ cao hơn dẫn đến sự phát triển của gì?</p><p>+Dịch script: “Khi khí hậu ấm hơn, rừng bắt đầu phát triển.” </p><p>+Giải thích: Sự nóng lên khiến rừng (forests) mọc lên, thay thế băng giá trước đó. +Từ vựng: -forests = rừng; -warmer climate = khí hậu ấm hơn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1589),
(N'90c01c7e-93e4-51c6-b784-ca05216ca7f6',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Người Nhật dùng gì làm vũ khí? </p><p>+Dịch script: “Họ thay giáo bằng mũi tên và phát triển kỹ năng bắn cung.” </p><p>+Giải thích: Để săn thú nhỏ, họ đổi sang dùng arrows. +Từ vựng: -arrows = mũi tên; -archery = bắn cung.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1589),
(N'c9aab990-a13c-5bfa-9790-40b6c63b2169',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Nhiệt độ cao giúp họ có thêm những loại cây gì (ngoài cá)? </p><p>+Dịch script: “Họ có thêm nhiều loại cây, hạt (nuts) và cá.” </p><p>+Giải thích: Nguồn thực phẩm đa dạng hơn, gồm nuts. +Từ vựng: -nuts = hạt; -foodstuffs = thực phẩm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1589),
(N'd951ea25-3ed4-5641-b1ee-08a4ce663c90',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Người Jomon không còn sống trong hang mà sống ở đâu? </p><p>+Dịch script: “Trước kia họ sống trong hang, nhưng giờ có bằng chứng về làng mạc.” </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ định cư trong villages thay vì hang động. </p><p>+Từ vựng: -villages = làng; -caves = hang động.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1589),
(N'31eab5af-d789-5e45-aa92-59dfb4ab88d4',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Mỗi ngôi nhà có đôi gì để nghiền thức ăn?
+Dịch script: “Mỗi nhà có hai viên đá - một phẳng, một để nghiền.”
+Giải thích: Dùng stones để nghiền hạt và rau củ.
+Từ vựng:
-stones = đá;
-grinding = nghiền.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1589),
(N'f71479fc-05ed-5a87-b5ab-47e709077e2c',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 38',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Lối sống định cư dẫn đến điều gì? </p><p>+Dịch script: “Lối sống ổn định hơn dẫn đến gia tăng dân số đáng kể.” </p><p>+Giải thích: Định cư =&gt; sinh sản nhiều hơn =&gt; population increases. </p><p>+Từ vựng: -sedentary lifestyle = lối sống định cư; -population = dân số; -increase = tăng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1589),
(N'f9f481ac-bb82-5ca1-8e77-a5feeb025d9d',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Người nhập cư mang đến những truyền thống mới cho các nghi lễ gì? </p><p>+Dịch sc ript: “Người nhập cư mang theo các tiến bộ về dệt, thủy tinh, và họ cũng du nhập các nghi lễ kết hôn.” </p><p>+Giải thích: Cụm marriage rituals nghĩa là nghi lễ cưới hỏi, nên truyền thống mới là về marriage ceremonies. </p><p>+Từ vựng: -migrants = người nhập cư; -rituals = nghi lễ; -ceremonies = lễ nghi.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1589),
(N'4b659719-01e7-53ed-93f6-afafab786700',N'f1329b29-b3c9-5353-a75c-8136183731b9',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Loại cây trồng chính được trồng là gì? </p><p>+Dịch script: “Người Jomon cũng bắt đầu trồng trọt, đặc biệt là lúa một bước quan trọng hướng đến nông nghiệp thực sự.” +Giải thích: Cây trồng chính được đề cập rõ ràng là rice. </p><p>+Từ vựng: -cultivate = canh tác, trồng trọt; -main crop = cây trồng chính; -rice = lúa, gạo.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1589),
(N'12766c47-362b-50fe-90ef-5649704a4190',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.7.S1588.mp3',N'Vol 3 LISTENING FULL TEST 7',0,7,1,NULL,1,1588),
(N'dd71d9af-c2e4-548b-9b8b-da66f5905295',N'12766c47-362b-50fe-90ef-5649704a4190',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL3] Post Office-Package Delivery Form</h2><p>You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear. There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work. All the recordings will be played once only. The test is in four parts, write all your answers in the Listening Question booklet at the end of the test. You will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet</p><p>Now turn to Part 1 on page 2 of your question booklet</p><p><span><span>Part 1. You will hear a conversation between an assistant in a post office and a customer who needs to send a package overseas</span></span></p><p><span><span>First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 8. Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 8</span></span></p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Hello can I help you</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> Yes I''d like to send this package to the USA urgently but I''m not sure what the best way of sending it is</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Well first of all when does it need to get there</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Well it''s Tuesday today it''s absolutely got to be there by Friday. Ideally I''d like it to be there by Thursday but I realize that might be difficult</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> OK well there''s Courier Post but that takes four working days so that would be next Monday, or slightly more expensive service called International Express which will get there in three working days</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Okay I''ll use that then and it''s really got to be there as quickly as possible</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Okay can I just take some details from you for the form. First of all what''s your name?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Peter White</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>and your address?</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> 14 Mountain Road</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>and where''s that?</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> that''s in Lake View</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Okay now who are you sending the package to</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Anna Hilman, that''s H I double L M A N</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> And what''s the address?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Apartment 2 2 8 North building</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Okay</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Upper Park Avenue</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> And what city is that in?</p><p><span><span>B:</sp' AS nvarchar(max))+N'an></span> New York</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Now I need a description of the contents of the package just so customs know what''s being sent</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> I''m sending books</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> How many are you sending I need to give the number</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>There are 6 in total</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Right and is there anything else in the package?</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> Yes some photographs</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Okay</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 9 and 10</span></span></p><p><span><span>Now listen and answer questions 9 and 10</span></span></p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Now would you like to insure the contents?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>hmm it might be a good idea</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Alright now can we just weigh the package and I''ll tell you how much it''s going to cost</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>ugh here you are that''s heavy</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> It''s just over four kilos so let''s see that''s going to be $98 altogether</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Will I be able to get a receipt with that?</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Yes of course. Now if the package gets lost you should contact us as soon as possible, it''s very unusual for anything to go missing but just in case, your receipt has an ID code on it which we will need to be able to trace the package</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> Right so will I also need to bring in the receipt and the delivery form to you if it does get lost</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Nope you can just give us a call with the ID code okay, now is there anything else I can help you with?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>No that''s all and thanks for your help</p><p><span><span>That is the end of part 1 you now have one minute to check your answers to part one</span></span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p>You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear. There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work. All the recordings will be played once only. The test is in four parts, write all your answers in the Listening Question booklet at the end of the test. You will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet</p><p>Now turn to Part 1 on page 2 of your question booklet</p><p><span><span>Part 1. You will hear a conversation between an assistant in a post office and a customer who needs to send a package overseas</span></span></p><p><span><span>First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 8. Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 8</span></span></p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Hello can I help you</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> Yes I''d like to send this package to the USA urgently but I''m not sure what the best way of sending it is</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Well first of all when does it need to get there</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Well it''s Tuesday today it''s absolutely got to be there by Friday. Ideally I''d like it to be there by Thursday but I realize that might be difficult</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> OK well there''s Courier Post but that takes four working days so that would be next Monday, or slightly more expensive service called International Express which will get there in three working days</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Okay I''ll use that then and it''s really got to be there as quickly as possible</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Okay can I just take some details from you for the form. First of all what''s your name?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Peter White</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>and your address?</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> 14 Mountain Road</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>and where''s that?</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> that''s in Lake View</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Okay now who are you sending the package to</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Anna Hilman, that''s H I double L M A N</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> And what''s the address?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Apartment 2 2 8 North building</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Okay</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Upper Park Avenue</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> And what city is that in?</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> New York</p><p><span><span>A:</span></' AS nvarchar(max))+N'span> Now I need a description of the contents of the package just so customs know what''s being sent</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> I''m sending books</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> How many are you sending I need to give the number</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>There are 6 in total</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Right and is there anything else in the package?</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> Yes some photographs</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Okay</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 9 and 10</span></span></p><p><span><span>Now listen and answer questions 9 and 10</span></span></p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Now would you like to insure the contents?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>hmm it might be a good idea</p><p><span><span>A: </span></span>Alright now can we just weigh the package and I''ll tell you how much it''s going to cost</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>ugh here you are that''s heavy</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> It''s just over four kilos so let''s see that''s going to be $98 altogether</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>Will I be able to get a receipt with that?</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Yes of course. Now if the package gets lost you should contact us as soon as possible, it''s very unusual for anything to go missing but just in case, your receipt has an ID code on it which we will need to be able to trace the package</p><p><span><span>B:</span></span> Right so will I also need to bring in the receipt and the delivery form to you if it does get lost</p><p><span><span>A:</span></span> Nope you can just give us a call with the ID code okay, now is there anything else I can help you with?</p><p><span><span>B: </span></span>No that''s all and thanks for your help</p><p><span><span>That is the end of part 1 you now have one minute to check your answers to part one</span></span></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.7.S1588.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1588),
(N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',N'dd71d9af-c2e4-548b-9b8b-da66f5905295',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the form below.
Write <span>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/ORA NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Post Office-Package Delivery Form',11,0,1,NULL,0,1588),
(N'cc46a25e-9799-5109-a3c7-46b5dbf573c6',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'<h3>Post Office-Package Delivery Form</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>Country of destination:</span></p></td><td></td><td><p>USA</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><span>Package to reach destination by:</span></p></td><td></td><td><p> }{SPACE}{ at latest</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><span>Service selected:</span></p></td><td></td><td> }{SPACE}{ Express</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><span>Sender:</span></p></td><td><p>Name:</p></td><td><p>Peter White</p></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><p><span>Address:</span></p></td><td> }{SPACE}{ </td></tr><tr><td></td><td><p><span>Suburb:</span></p></td><td><p>Lakeview</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Delivery to:</p></td><td><p><span>Name:</span></p></td><td><p>Anna }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><p><span>Address:</span></p></td><td><p>Apartment 228, }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td><p>Upper }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td><p>New York</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><span>Contents:</span></p></td><td></td><td> }{SPACE}{ </td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td> }{SPACE}{ </td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Package to reach destination by…at latest. </p><p>+Giải thích: Người gửi nói rõ gói hàng phải đến Mỹ muộn nhất là thứ Sáu. </p><p>+Scripts: “It’s absolutely got to be there by Friday.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - absolutely = chắc chắn - at latest = muộn nhất </p><p>+Đáp án: Friday</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1588),
(N'd4269262-8d10-5a49-936a-de023aa20bf7',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'Question number 2',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Service selected: _______ Express. </p><p>+Giải thích: Nhân viên gợi ý dịch vụ nhanh nhất là International Express, giao trong 3 ngày làm việc. </p><p>+Scripts: “A slightly more expensive service called International Express.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - express = chuyển phát nhanh - working days = ngày làm việc </p><p>+Đáp án: International</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1588),
(N'b2871948-0619-52dd-bf82-6e203de3d72d',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'Question number 3',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Address… </p><p>+Giải thích: Địa chỉ người gửi Peter White là 14 Mountain Road. </p><p>+Scripts: “And your address?” – “14 Mountain Road.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - address = địa chỉ - road = đường </p><p>+Đáp án: 14 Mountain Road</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1588),
(N'73bfca5e-7bd7-5c44-bdd9-1f25d9839d02',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'Question number 4',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Delivery to: Anna…. </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ người nhận là Hillman, được đánh vần rõ ràng. </p><p>+ Scripts: “Anna Hillman. That’s H-I-double-L-M-A-N.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - double-L = hai chữ L - spell = đánh vần  </p><p>+Đáp án: Hillman</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,4,NULL,0,1588),
(N'9e5037e1-852f-5553-8c38-75be11be9d2e',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'Question number 5',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Apartment 228, …Building. </p><p>+Giải thích: Người gửi nói rõ tên tòa nhà là North Building. </p><p>+Scripts: “Apartment 228, North Building, Upper Park Avenue.” </p><p>+Từ vựng:  - apartment = căn hộ - building = tòa nhà  </p><p>+Đáp án: North</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,5,NULL,0,1588),
(N'7ecdea57-f840-513b-b850-f98e2f9b7606',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'Question number 6',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Upper …. </p><p>+Giải thích: Đường người nhận sống là Park Avenue. </p><p>+Scripts: “Upper Park Avenue.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - avenue = đại lộ - upper = phía trên </p><p>+Đáp án: Park Avenue</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,6,NULL,0,1588),
(N'670567eb-8651-5e01-be50-502c29afb0ea',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'Question number 7',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Contents:…. </p><p>+Giải thích: Người gửi khai báo hàng hóa trong gói là books. </p><p>+Scripts: “I’m sending books.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - contents = nội dung - books = sách </p><p>+Đáp án: books</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,7,NULL,0,1588),
(N'374b1e97-d514-5000-8bc7-db943c89c108',N'db994099-8e26-5682-be7d-aac13a71d404',0,3,19,N'Question number 8',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: (Contents)…. </p><p>+Giải thích: Ngoài sách còn có photographs. </p><p>+Scripts: “And is there anything else in the package?” – “Yes, some photographs.” Từ vựng: - photographs = ảnh - anything else = còn gì khác </p><p>+Đáp án: photographs</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,8,NULL,0,1588),
(N'0011377f-497d-58b5-a10e-6979dc408aa7',N'dd71d9af-c2e4-548b-9b8b-da66f5905295',0,2,18,N'<p>Answer the questions below.</p><p>Write <span>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,2,NULL,0,1588),
(N'3721db33-ba3b-55a7-b955-acd2c31866a5',N'0011377f-497d-58b5-a10e-6979dc408aa7',0,3,19,N'<p>What is the total cost of sending the package?  £ }{SPACE}{ </p><p>What is required in order to find a lost package? }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: What is the total cost of sending the package? </p><p>+Giải thích: Nhân viên cân gói hàng và báo giá tổng cộng là $98. </p><p>+Scripts: “It’s just over four kilos... that’s going to be $98 altogether.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - altogether = tổng cộng - weigh = cân  </p><p>+Đáp án: $98</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,1588),
(N'd7cbc7b9-3263-5dda-8a35-62ae7e40609a',N'0011377f-497d-58b5-a10e-6979dc408aa7',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: What is required in order to find a lost package? </p><p>+Giải thích: Nếu gói hàng bị mất, cần cung cấp ID code trên hóa đơn để tra cứu. </p><p>+Scripts: “Your receipt has an ID code on it, which we will need to trace the package.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: - trace = truy tìm - receipt = biên lai  </p><p>+Đáp án: ID code</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1588),
(N'9f8434c3-5490-55fe-921f-132929bd28e3',N'12766c47-362b-50fe-90ef-5649704a4190',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL3] Regional Park</h2><p><span><span>Part 2 You will hear a guide talking to a group of tourists who are going to spend a day on an island. First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 14.</span></span></p><p>Well morning everyone, welcome to Palm Island. First, I''ll run through the plan for the day. So the idea is that in the morning you''ll go for a walk. There are actually a number of different walks on the island, so I''ll describe each one and then you can choose. So there''s the boundary walk. This is one of the longer walks and it will appeal especially to people who love the coastline. And in fact, the tide is out today so it''ll be perfect for exploring the very old rock formations behind the beach. A good option is the loop track. Now this is quite short, but don''t be misled by that because it''s very hilly and you need to be quite fit. The reward though is that you''ll be able to see right down the length of the island and even across to the mainland.</p><p>It''ll be pretty as a picture on a fine day like today. There''s the puriri walk, which follows one of the earliest trails that was built on the island. Unfortunately the track''s been damaged slightly by a storm, but it''s still passable. The nice thing is about halfway there''s a table and chairs. They''ve recently been restored and painted in fact, and it''s a great place to eat your packed lunch and have a rest. You could also walk up to North Head. This is an inland walk. You''ll hardly get a glimpse of the sea the whole way, but the attraction is you''ll pass through a large area of very old native bush. The number of birds is amazing, including several rare species. You''ll find some are quite tame, but please don''t give them any food.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 15 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 15 to 20.</span></span></p><p>Okay, well after your walk the bus will take you to the regional park where you can relax for a couple of hours before going home. Now if you look at your maps of the regional park, I''ll just describe a few things you can do. So the bus will drop you at the park entrance right at the bottom of the map. One interesting place is the bird colony. To get there from the park entrance, you go up the path to the lake. About halfway around the east side of the lake, you need to turn off and take the path all the way out to the coast. You''ll find t' AS nvarchar(max))+N'he colony there, overlooking a little island. </p><p>Now if you''d like a swim, remember that not all the beaches are really suitable in this wind. For the safest place, follow the path north from the park entrance, but before you get to the lake, turn right and go down there to the beach. Something that people find interesting is the old ship. It''s more than 100 years old. To get there, just north of the park entrance, you take the path which heads towards Okiwi Bay. Then walk along the coast a little way before turning right and following that path over to Rocky Bay. You''ll see the wharf, go past that, and the ship is just along there. A long walk, but one that''s well worth it, is up to Stoney Batter. You follow the path right around the lake to the far side and keep heading north. But if you reach the water tower, you''ve gone too far. </p><p>Before that, you want to turn off to your left and take the path to the coast. Now where else is there? Oh yes, the Settler''s Monument. That''s popular. So from the park entrance, take the first path on the left and follow it around behind Okiwi Bay. The monument is out at the end there on the headland. Finally, if all this exercise has made you thirsty, there''s a kiosk where you can get hot and cold drinks. It''s easy to find, beside the lake, right on the water''s edge. Well now, if anyone has any questions...</p><p><span><span>That is the end of Part 2. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 2.</span></span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span><span>Part 2 You will hear a guide talking to a group of tourists who are going to spend a day on an island. First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 14.</span></span></p><p>Well morning everyone, welcome to Palm Island. First, I''ll run through the plan for the day. So the idea is that in the morning you''ll go for a walk. There are actually a number of different walks on the island, so I''ll describe each one and then you can choose. So there''s the boundary walk. This is one of the longer walks and it will appeal especially to people who love the coastline. And in fact, the tide is out today so it''ll be perfect for exploring the very old rock formations behind the beach. A good option is the loop track. Now this is quite short, but don''t be misled by that because it''s very hilly and you need to be quite fit. The reward though is that you''ll be able to see right down the length of the island and even across to the mainland.</p><p>It''ll be pretty as a picture on a fine day like today. There''s the puriri walk, which follows one of the earliest trails that was built on the island. Unfortunately the track''s been damaged slightly by a storm, but it''s still passable. The nice thing is about halfway there''s a table and chairs. They''ve recently been restored and painted in fact, and it''s a great place to eat your packed lunch and have a rest. You could also walk up to North Head. This is an inland walk. You''ll hardly get a glimpse of the sea the whole way, but the attraction is you''ll pass through a large area of very old native bush. The number of birds is amazing, including several rare species. You''ll find some are quite tame, but please don''t give them any food.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 15 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 15 to 20.</span></span></p><p>Okay, well after your walk the bus will take you to the regional park where you can relax for a couple of hours before going home. Now if you look at your maps of the regional park, I''ll just describe a few things you can do. So the bus will drop you at the park entrance right at the bottom of the map. One interesting place is the bird colony. To get there from the park entrance, you go up the path to the lake. About halfway around the east side of the lake, you need to turn off and take the path all the way out to the coast. You''ll find the colony there, overlooking ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'a little island. </p><p>Now if you''d like a swim, remember that not all the beaches are really suitable in this wind. For the safest place, follow the path north from the park entrance, but before you get to the lake, turn right and go down there to the beach. Something that people find interesting is the old ship. It''s more than 100 years old. To get there, just north of the park entrance, you take the path which heads towards Okiwi Bay. Then walk along the coast a little way before turning right and following that path over to Rocky Bay. You''ll see the wharf, go past that, and the ship is just along there. A long walk, but one that''s well worth it, is up to Stoney Batter. You follow the path right around the lake to the far side and keep heading north. But if you reach the water tower, you''ve gone too far. </p><p>Before that, you want to turn off to your left and take the path to the coast. Now where else is there? Oh yes, the Settler''s Monument. That''s popular. So from the park entrance, take the first path on the left and follow it around behind Okiwi Bay. The monument is out at the end there on the headland. Finally, if all this exercise has made you thirsty, there''s a kiosk where you can get hot and cold drinks. It''s easy to find, beside the lake, right on the water''s edge. Well now, if anyone has any questions...</p><p><span><span>That is the end of Part 2. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 2.</span></span></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.7.S1588.PART2.mp3',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1588),
(N'c2c0fccf-5088-515b-b6ab-fd6a65d2ced9',N'9f8434c3-5490-55fe-921f-132929bd28e3',0,2,18,N'<p>What feature of each of the following walks does the speaker recommend? </p><p>Choose <span>FOUR</span> answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span>A-G</span>, next to questions 11-14.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,1,NULL,0,1588),
(N'50a40989-21ad-5d1f-91fb-fa47d110265a',N'c2c0fccf-5088-515b-b6ab-fd6a65d2ced9',0,3,19,N'<p>Boundary Walk</p><p></p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Boundary Walk </p><p>=&gt; có đặc điểm gì nổi bật?</p><p>+Giải thích: “Appeal to people who love the coastline” và “rock formations behind the beach”</p><p>=&gt; liên quan đến biển / bờ biển</p><p>=&gt;Seashore.</p><p>+Scripts: “It’ll appeal especially to people who love the coastline… perfect for exploring the very old rock formations behind the beach.”</p><p>+Từ vựng:</p><p>- coastline = đường bờ biển</p><p>- rock formations = cấu trúc đá</p><p>+Đáp án: D - Seashore</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1588),
(N'88118443-0bbc-50a8-b3d4-52a7f61d2f05',N'c2c0fccf-5088-515b-b6ab-fd6a65d2ced9',0,3,19,N'<p>Loop Track</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Loop Track  điểm nổi bật? </p><p>+Giải thích: Từ “you’ll be able to see right down the length of the island and even across to the mainland” </p><p>=&gt; mô tả tầm nhìn đẹp </p><p>=&gt;views. </p><p>+Scripts: “The reward though is that you’ll be able to see right down the length of the island and even across to the mainland.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- reward = phần thưởng </p><p>- view = khung cảnh, tầm nhìn  </p><p>+Đáp án: G - Views</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1588),
(N'331b5248-ff56-556c-9695-d605fa7fd058',N'c2c0fccf-5088-515b-b6ab-fd6a65d2ced9',0,3,19,N'<p>Puriri Walk</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Puriri Walk </p><p>=&gt; đặc điểm gì được nhắc đến?</p><p>+Giải thích: “a great place to eat your packed lunch” khu vực thích hợp để ăn trưa</p><p>=&gt; Picnic area.</p><p>+Scripts: “Halfway there’s a table and chairs… a great place to eat your packed lunch and have a rest.”</p><p>+Từ vựng:</p><p>- packed lunch = cơm hộp mang theo</p><p>- picnic area = khu dã ngoại / ăn trưa ngoài trời</p><p>+Đáp án: A – Picnic are a</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1588),
(N'5f85992d-8138-54f7-8938-8eb01331b53d',N'c2c0fccf-5088-515b-b6ab-fd6a65d2ced9',0,3,19,N'<p>North Head</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: North Head </p><p>=&gt; điểm đặc trưng? </p><p>+Giải thích: Mô tả có “many birds” và “rare species” </p><p>=&gt; liên quan đến động vật hoang dã </p><p>=&gt; Wildlife. </p><p>+Scripts: “You’ll pass through a large area of very old native bush. The number of birds is amazing, including several rare species.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- native bush = rừng tự nhiên bản địa </p><p>- rare species = loài hiếm  </p><p>+Đáp án: F - Wildlife</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1588),
(N'63994bf5-5540-5f64-9c26-bf0febbb8871',N'9f8434c3-5490-55fe-921f-132929bd28e3',0,2,18,N'<p>Label the map below.</p><p>Write the correct letter,<span> A-J</span>, next to questions 15-20.</p><p><img src="https://ieltsroom.com/upload/images/ieltsadvancedtest/91ce77d3c5f2247cc6e3.jpg" alt="Question diagram"></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Regional Park',12,0,2,NULL,0,1588),
(N'0571c0ae-dd20-57d2-bd63-6fa975d81ad0',N'63994bf5-5540-5f64-9c26-bf0febbb8871',0,3,19,N'<p>Bird colony</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Hướng dẫn mô tả đi ra bờ biển phía đông, nơi nhìn ra hòn đảo nhỏ =&gt; vị trí tương ứng ký hiệu A trên bản đồ.</p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- colony = bầy đàn (chim, động vật) </p><p>- overlooking = nhìn ra / hướng ra  </p><p>=&gt;Đáp án: A </p><p>+Đọc bản đồ: Đi từ PARK ENTRANCE lên tới LAKE, men theo phía đông của hồ, rẽ ra bờ biển. Ký hiệu đúng vị trí đó là A, và ngay ngoài khơi có một đảo nhỏ (khớp “overlooking a little island”).</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1588),
(N'4ff824eb-6f59-574b-914a-7b529b254092',N'63994bf5-5540-5f64-9c26-bf0febbb8871',0,3,19,N'<p>Safe swimming beach</p>',N'<p>Giải thích: Mô tả “north from the park entrance… before the lake, turn right”  =&gt;vị trí F.</p><p>+Scripts: “Follow the path north… before you get to the lake, turn right and go down there to the beach.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- safe swimming beach = bãi biển tắm an toàn </p><p>- turn right = rẽ phải  </p><p>+Đáp án: F   </p><p>+Ký hiệu F nằm ở vị trí rẽ phải trước khi tới hồ (đúng hướng dẫn của Câu 16 </p><p>– Safe swimming beach: “before you get to the lake, turn right… to the beach”).</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1588),
(N'5a6f077a-9b54-5e18-9f46-f5da9514ddfd',N'63994bf5-5540-5f64-9c26-bf0febbb8871',0,3,19,N'<p>Old ship</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Mô tả hướng tới Okiwi Bay =&gt; Rocky Bay =&gt; wharf =&gt; ship, tương ứng vị trí J. </p><p>+Scripts: “Một điều mà mọi người thấy thú vị là con tàu cũ... hãy đi theo con đường dẫn đến vịnh Okiwi. Sau đó đi bộ dọc theo bờ biển một đoạn ngắn trước khi rẽ phải và đi theo con đường đó đến vịnh Rocky. Bạn sẽ thấy bến tàu, đi qua đó, và con tàu nằm ngay phía bên kia.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- wharf = cầu tàu </p><p>- shipwreck = con tàu cũ  </p><p>+Đáp án: J</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1588),
(N'c44eed6a-a0f2-5308-9770-90aa08c22c15',N'63994bf5-5540-5f64-9c26-bf0febbb8871',0,3,19,N'<p>Stony Batter</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Đi quanh hồ tới phía bắc xa hơn, gần tháp nước =&gt; vị trí B. </p><p>+Scripts: “Đường đi đến Stoney Batter khá dài nhưng rất đáng giá. Bạn đi theo con đường vòng quanh hồ đến phía bên kia và tiếp tục đi về phía bắc. Nhưng nếu đến tháp nước thì bạn đã đi quá xa rồi.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- heading north = đi hướng bắc </p><p>- worth it = đáng công sức  </p><p>=&gt;Đáp án: B</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1588),
(N'1e890a84-51d2-594a-b903-cb13110d5654',N'63994bf5-5540-5f64-9c26-bf0febbb8871',0,3,19,N'<p>Settlers’ Monument</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: “Từ lối vào công viên, hãy rẽ vào con đường đầu tiên bên trái và đi vòng ra phía sau vịnh Okiwi. Đài tưởng niệm nằm ở cuối con đường đó, trên mũi đất.”” </p><p>=&gt; vị trí C. </p><p>+Scripts: “Follow it around behind Okiwi Bay. The monument is out at the end there on the headland.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- monument = đài tưởng niệm </p><p>- headland = mũi đất nhô ra biển  </p><p>+Đáp án: C </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1588),
(N'36e54520-8280-5faa-9cc7-0d105583b5ca',N'63994bf5-5540-5f64-9c26-bf0febbb8871',0,3,19,N'<p>Kiosk</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: “Beside the lake, right on the water’s edge” </p><p>=&gt; vị trí I trên bản đồ (G không sát hồ). Scripts: “Cuối cùng, nếu tất cả những bài tập này khiến bạn khát nước, có một quầy bán đồ uống nóng và lạnh. Rất dễ tìm, nó nằm ngay bên cạnh hồ, sát mép nước.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- kiosk = quầy bán hàng nhỏ </p><p>- water’s edge = ven hồ / mép nước  </p><p>+Đáp án: I</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1588),
(N'a5f05e87-d986-5d1b-a0da-e4211d519642',N'12766c47-362b-50fe-90ef-5649704a4190',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 3] Test 7 - Section 3</h2><p><span><span>Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.</span></span></p><p>Hi, come on in.</p><p>Thanks.</p><p>Thanks for agreeing to see us, Dr Harrison. We wanted to talk over our work on the Managing Visitor Attractions module.</p><p>Right. You''ve been on placement at Davenport Zoo, haven''t you?</p><p>Yeah.</p><p>So, what did you want to ask me about?</p><p>Basically, we''re very positive about what we''ve seen at the zoo as a tourist attraction. But there are things we''re more critical about.</p><p>What precisely?</p><p>Well, the numbers visiting the zoo are rising rapidly and the quality of the visitor experience is good for young and old.</p><p>Yes, and we''ll certainly emphasise that. But from a business point of view, we saw some problems. They don''t seem to employ their workers fully enough. - There really seem to be too many of them, which means they''re wasting money on wages. They should streamline.</p><p>Yeah, and they could do more to get larger visitor groups, school groups, for example.</p><p>They put on great educational activities for kids, but there isn''t much take-up by schools, probably because their advertising isn''t targeted enough.</p><p>Well, those are valid observations, but you should word them in such a way that they won''t cause offence. Any advice should be constructive.</p><p>Yeah, it''s a really interesting module and I think work placements like these are particularly valuable on a course like tourism.</p><p>It boosts your confidence to think that the management is interested in what a student has to say.</p><p>Absolutely. Though I worry slightly that we''re making business judgements based on so little experience. Companies might think we know more than we actually do.</p><p>I think you''ve got a point there. But don''t get me wrong, taken as a whole, it''s been a great module and I think it''s good to include such practical study alongside the more traditional, academically oriented ones.</p><p>It''s been completely absorbing. - Which sometimes means we haven''t devoted enough time to other parts of the course. You know?</p><p>Yeah.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.</span></span></p><p>How are you getting on with your preparation for the presentation?</p><p>Not too bad, but we''d like to talk over some of our plans with you.</p><p>Fine.</p><p>We''ve got several sections. W' AS nvarchar(max))+N'e''re going to start with historical development of the visitor attraction. - How it started as a very small zoo and grew, especially in the 1990s. - We''ve got some great pictures of the way it looked in 1970. - Lovely. But remember the emphasis of the presentation as a whole should be on the present. And the future. - Perhaps we should shorten this? - I think we should.</p><p>Yep.</p><p>We want to explain the management organisation. - I''m worried this section''s going to be so long and boring for the audience. - Well, it''d be more interesting if you have a slide with a graphic showing the organisational structure. It won''t seem so long if the audience have something to look at.</p><p>That''s easy because I got hold of a copy of one from the zoo last week.</p><p>Well done.</p><p>For the section on finances, we''ve only got basic information. And I wondered whether we need to interview the accounts director. - She''s very helpful. - To be honest, I''d aim to keep this part to the minimum. It''s not a major focus of the module. - Are you sure? I''m happy to talk to her. - Let''s not use up time we could spend on more useful things.</p><p>OK. We''ll go with what we''ve got then.</p><p>Now, marketing''s really important. We encourage you to give that a lot of emphasis. - That''s our next section. And we''ll distribute flyers from the zoo and say what''s good and bad about them. - We could get the audience to give a couple of suggestions as well. - Nice idea.</p><p>Yeah, I like that. Keeps them engaged.</p><p>Yep.</p><p>And then we''ve got a section called key problems. (5:00) We''re not altogether happy with the name. I was wondering about key challenges. - I think that automatically gives it a more positive perspective.</p><p>OK then. And the concluding part is further research needed. We''re planning to mention one other tourist attraction, a local theme park. - Will that be enough? - Maybe two different attractions then?</p><p>OK.</p><p>OK. Well, why don''t you get back to work?</p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span><span>Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.</span></span></p><p>Hi, come on in.</p><p>Thanks.</p><p>Thanks for agreeing to see us, Dr Harrison. We wanted to talk over our work on the Managing Visitor Attractions module.</p><p>Right. You''ve been on placement at Davenport Zoo, haven''t you?</p><p>Yeah.</p><p>So, what did you want to ask me about?</p><p>Basically, we''re very positive about what we''ve seen at the zoo as a tourist attraction. But there are things we''re more critical about.</p><p>What precisely?</p><p>Well, the numbers visiting the zoo are rising rapidly and the quality of the visitor experience is good for young and old.</p><p>Yes, and we''ll certainly emphasise that. But from a business point of view, we saw some problems. They don''t seem to employ their workers fully enough. - There really seem to be too many of them, which means they''re wasting money on wages. They should streamline.</p><p>Yeah, and they could do more to get larger visitor groups, school groups, for example.</p><p>They put on great educational activities for kids, but there isn''t much take-up by schools, probably because their advertising isn''t targeted enough.</p><p>Well, those are valid observations, but you should word them in such a way that they won''t cause offence. Any advice should be constructive.</p><p>Yeah, it''s a really interesting module and I think work placements like these are particularly valuable on a course like tourism.</p><p>It boosts your confidence to think that the management is interested in what a student has to say.</p><p>Absolutely. Though I worry slightly that we''re making business judgements based on so little experience. Companies might think we know more than we actually do.</p><p>I think you''ve got a point there. But don''t get me wrong, taken as a whole, it''s been a great module and I think it''s good to include such practical study alongside the more traditional, academically oriented ones.</p><p>It''s been completely absorbing. - Which sometimes means we haven''t devoted enough time to other parts of the course. You know?</p><p>Yeah.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.</span></span></p><p>How are you getting on with your preparation for the presentation?</p><p>Not too bad, but we''d like to talk over some of our plans with you.</p><p>Fine.</p><p>We''ve got several sections. We''re going to start with historical' AS nvarchar(max))+N' development of the visitor attraction. - How it started as a very small zoo and grew, especially in the 1990s. - We''ve got some great pictures of the way it looked in 1970. - Lovely. But remember the emphasis of the presentation as a whole should be on the present. And the future. - Perhaps we should shorten this? - I think we should.</p><p>Yep.</p><p>We want to explain the management organisation. - I''m worried this section''s going to be so long and boring for the audience. - Well, it''d be more interesting if you have a slide with a graphic showing the organisational structure. It won''t seem so long if the audience have something to look at.</p><p>That''s easy because I got hold of a copy of one from the zoo last week.</p><p>Well done.</p><p>For the section on finances, we''ve only got basic information. And I wondered whether we need to interview the accounts director. - She''s very helpful. - To be honest, I''d aim to keep this part to the minimum. It''s not a major focus of the module. - Are you sure? I''m happy to talk to her. - Let''s not use up time we could spend on more useful things.</p><p>OK. We''ll go with what we''ve got then.</p><p>Now, marketing''s really important. We encourage you to give that a lot of emphasis. - That''s our next section. And we''ll distribute flyers from the zoo and say what''s good and bad about them. - We could get the audience to give a couple of suggestions as well. - Nice idea.</p><p>Yeah, I like that. Keeps them engaged.</p><p>Yep.</p><p>And then we''ve got a section called key problems. (5:00) We''re not altogether happy with the name. I was wondering about key challenges. - I think that automatically gives it a more positive perspective.</p><p>OK then. And the concluding part is further research needed. We''re planning to mention one other tourist attraction, a local theme park. - Will that be enough? - Maybe two different attractions then?</p><p>OK.</p><p>OK. Well, why don''t you get back to work?</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-3/iL.VOL3.7.S1588.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1588),
(N'a1ddeb9e-61f6-5fc7-a8b4-2533139d001a',N'a5f05e87-d986-5d1b-a0da-e4211d519642',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose <span>TWO</span> letters, <span>A-E.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',5,0,1,NULL,0,1588),
(N'74f5dffa-4e62-568c-b2a1-fc5efaea79db',N'a1ddeb9e-61f6-5fc7-a8b4-2533139d001a',0,3,19,N'<p>Which <span>TWO</span> things do Gemma and Simon criticize about the zoo?</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Which TWO things do Gemma and Simon criticize about the zoo? </p><p>+Giải thích: </p><p>- “Too many workers” =&gt; họ phê bình việc thừa nhân viên </p><p>=&gt; B - high level of staffing. </p><p>- “Advertising isn’t targeted enough to schools” =&gt; E - marketing to schools. </p><p>+Scripts: </p><p>-“They don’t seem to employ their workers fully enough… too many of them.”</p><p>-“There isn’t much take-up by schools, probably because their advertising isn’t targeted enough.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- staffing = nhân sự, biên chế </p><p>- advertising targeted = quảng cáo nhắm mục tiêu </p><p>+Đáp án: B, E</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1588),
(N'6d799a0f-6f12-534d-bf30-5839d058c2d6',N'a1ddeb9e-61f6-5fc7-a8b4-2533139d001a',0,3,19,N'<p>Which <span>TWO</span> things do Gemma and Simon criticize about the zoo?</p>',N'<p>+Câu hỏi: Which TWO things do Gemma and Simon criticize about the zoo? </p><p>+Giải thích: </p><p>- “Too many workers” =&gt; họ phê bình việc thừa nhân viên =&gt; B - high level of staffing. </p><p>- “Advertising isn’t targeted enough to schools” =&gt; E - marketing to schools.</p><p>+ Scripts: </p><p>-“They don’t seem to employ their workers fully enough… too many of them.” </p><p>-“There isn’t much take-up by schools, probably because their advertising isn’t targeted enough.” </p><p>+Từ vựng: </p><p>- staffing = nhân sự, biên chế </p><p>- advertising targeted = quảng cáo nhắm mục tiêu  </p><p>+Đáp án: B, E </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1588),
