(N'9af30604-2423-586f-a3c6-026ebf0542b8',N'702c6c15-b162-5318-9177-491fcd17b31d',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2> International Club</h2><p>Hello. Could I speak to Mr John Girdlestone, please? Speaking. Oh, hi My name is Julie Bloom. I am studying here in the UK for a year, and a friend told me you organise an international club where people of different nationalities can meet up? That is right. The club was founded over 50 years ago, and at present we have got members from approximately 30 countries. What is the name of your friend? Louise. She is back in the States now. Oh, Louise. I remember her. So, are you from the States too, Julie? Yes, from California. Great. Well, let se what I can tell you about the club. It does not cost a lot to become a member.</p><p>You can join for two months for £2.50, or it is £8.50 for 12 months. And is it only for university students? No, we are open to anyone. We have quite a few members from the language schools in town, and also local people who are interested in meeting people from other countries. OK. So, what sorts of things do you do? Well, one thing we organise on a regular basis is dancing. That is a great way to get to know people. Do you mean like disco? No, we do not do disco. We do folk dances, some from Europe, but some from further afield. Everyone really enjoys them. We meet on Monday evenings at 8.30. Do you know the University Centre? Yes, I think so. We meet there, in the main hall. Do not get confused with the old hall, where there is a ballroom dancing group. They are nothing to do with us. And can I just show up? Sure, everyone is welcome. And do I need to come along with a partner for the dancing? You do not have to, though you can if you want to. I am sure you will have a good time. We have live music for some sessions. I do not suppose you play a musical instrument, do you? I play the violin, but I am not very good. Well, we are looking for some people to join the band. If you are interested, why don''t you call Mike? His number is 01356 890674. OK, thanks. Then we also have a meal together regularly. That is once a month, and it is always on a Sunday, the second one of the month. Oh, so that will be this coming Sunday. And where is that held? In the university centre again, in the canteen. We used to have it in the restaurant on the ground floor, but that is closed at weekends now, unfortunately, so we are up on the top floor. They do very reasonably priced meals, things like baked potatoes and pizza. And can I just show up there? Absolutely. Then we have some special summer events too.</p><p>On July th' AS nvarchar(max))+N'e 2nd, we are going to a place called Great Hampton, about half an hour''s drive away, to take part in the carnival they hold there every year. It is always a good day out, especially if it is a sunny day. Oh, Louise said she went on that last year. She said it was really good, and it would be nice to go somewhere out of town. Well, in that case, you might be interested in our event on July the 16th. That is a walk in the Stannard Hills. We do about 15 kilometres, so it is a decent walk, and it is for charity. Sounds a pretty long way. Then on August the 7th, we are having a garden party at the home of one of our organisers. She has an outdoor pool, so if the weather is warm enough, you will be able to go swimming. OK. Hard to imagine that today. Well, the summer has not really started yet. Then our last summer events on September the 4th. Last year, we went to a castle, but this year, we are going to the theatre. We do ask members to let us know in advance for that, so we can book. Sure. Well, I would definitely like to join.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Hello. Could I speak to Mr John Girdlestone, please? Speaking. Oh, hi My name is Julie Bloom. I am studying here in the UK for a year, and a friend told me you organise an international club where people of different nationalities can meet up? That is right. The club was founded over 50 years ago, and at present we have got members from approximately 30 countries. What is the name of your friend? Louise. She is back in the States now. Oh, Louise. I remember her. So, are you from the States too, Julie? Yes, from California. Great. Well, let se what I can tell you about the club. It does not cost a lot to become a member.</p><p>You can join for two months for £2.50, or it is £8.50 for 12 months. And is it only for university students? No, we are open to anyone. We have quite a few members from the language schools in town, and also local people who are interested in meeting people from other countries. OK. So, what sorts of things do you do? Well, one thing we organise on a regular basis is dancing. That is a great way to get to know people. Do you mean like disco? No, we do not do disco. We do folk dances, some from Europe, but some from further afield. Everyone really enjoys them. We meet on Monday evenings at 8.30. Do you know the University Centre? Yes, I think so. We meet there, in the main hall. Do not get confused with the old hall, where there is a ballroom dancing group. They are nothing to do with us. And can I just show up? Sure, everyone is welcome. And do I need to come along with a partner for the dancing? You do not have to, though you can if you want to. I am sure you will have a good time. We have live music for some sessions. I do not suppose you play a musical instrument, do you? I play the violin, but I am not very good. Well, we are looking for some people to join the band. If you are interested, why don''t you call Mike? His number is 01356 890674. OK, thanks. Then we also have a meal together regularly. That is once a month, and it is always on a Sunday, the second one of the month. Oh, so that will be this coming Sunday. And where is that held? In the university centre again, in the canteen. We used to have it in the restaurant on the ground floor, but that is closed at weekends now, unfortunately, so we are up on the top floor. They do very reasonably priced meals, things like baked potatoes and pizza. And can I just show up there? Absolutely. Then we have some special summer events too.</p><p>On July the 2nd, we are going to a pla' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ce called Great Hampton, about half an hour''s drive away, to take part in the carnival they hold there every year. It is always a good day out, especially if it is a sunny day. Oh, Louise said she went on that last year. She said it was really good, and it would be nice to go somewhere out of town. Well, in that case, you might be interested in our event on July the 16th. That is a walk in the Stannard Hills. We do about 15 kilometres, so it is a decent walk, and it is for charity. Sounds a pretty long way. Then on August the 7th, we are having a garden party at the home of one of our organisers. She has an outdoor pool, so if the weather is warm enough, you will be able to go swimming. OK. Hard to imagine that today. Well, the summer has not really started yet. Then our last summer events on September the 4th. Last year, we went to a castle, but this year, we are going to the theatre. We do ask members to let us know in advance for that, so we can book. Sure. Well, I would definitely like to join.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-2/iL.VOL2.7.S1586.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1586),
