(N'a14162c0-3986-5b2f-9936-c6922ac2c435',N'0eeb56f5-08a3-50a8-97ad-98cceae08e08',0,3,19,N'<p>What was the main objection to Dominic''s training?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Phản đối chính đối với khóa đào tạo của Dominic không phải là chi phí hay quãng đường di chuyển mà là việc anh sẽ vắng mặt đúng vào thời điểm công ty có cuộc họp bán hàng quan trọng. Key words: main problem: vấn đề chính, away: vắng mặt. Scripts: “they didn''t want you away on the days of the training” – họ không muốn anh vắng mặt trong những ngày đào tạo.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1567),
(N'0d0fc8b8-b5c7-51cb-9485-3e2e66cd0612',N'0eeb56f5-08a3-50a8-97ad-98cceae08e08',0,3,19,N'<p>When do Grace and Dominic arrange to meet?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A. Sau khi các phương án khác không phù hợp, Grace và Dominic thống nhất gặp nhau trước giờ làm việc bình thường vào sáng thứ Hai. Key words: before work: trước giờ làm, Monday: thứ Hai. Scripts: “just before the normal start of work on Monday” – ngay trước giờ làm việc bình thường vào thứ Hai.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1567),
(N'f108b751-d1f2-54bc-9720-c63c67757c15',N'0eeb56f5-08a3-50a8-97ad-98cceae08e08',0,3,19,N'<p>Why will Grace need her computer at her meeting with Dominic?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Grace cần Dominic mang máy tính để xem và làm việc với mô hình đã được thiết kế nhằm trình bày cho khách hàng. Key words: model: mô hình, explain: giải thích. Scripts: “I''ll also explain the model that we''ve made” – tôi cũng sẽ giải thích mô hình mà chúng ta đã làm</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1567),
(N'02c236e3-e3d2-5dc5-b1f8-25af767b6a00',N'0eeb56f5-08a3-50a8-97ad-98cceae08e08',0,3,19,N'<p>How will Dominic pay for his hotel?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Grace nói rõ rằng cô sẽ đảm bảo Dominic được sử dụng thẻ tín dụng của công ty để thanh toán khách sạn, thay vì tự trả tiền trước. Key words: company card: thẻ công ty, pay: thanh toán. Scripts: “I''ll make sure you get that” – tôi sẽ đảm bảo anh nhận được thẻ đó.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1567),
(N'b5303716-f0af-5d65-8c5e-b145ba38576e',N'0eeb56f5-08a3-50a8-97ad-98cceae08e08',0,3,19,N'<p>How will Dominic travel to his training?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là C. Sau khi loại trừ xe buýt và máy bay, Grace quyết định đặt vé tàu hỏa cho Dominic vì tiện làm việc và đỡ mệt hơn so với lái xe. Key words: train: tàu hỏa, booking: đặt vé. Scripts: “I''ll make a train booking for you” – tôi sẽ đặt vé tàu cho anh.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1567),
(N'293fb4a6-4dcf-5c11-93bb-ea6469a234a7',N'b300f78a-01cd-5b73-b15b-4b91c425b34a',0,2,18,N'<p>Match the locations in <span>questions 26 - 28</span> with the correct locations on the map and write the correct letter <span>(A - 0)</span> next to questions</p><p><img src="https://ieltsroom.com/upload/images/ieltsadvancedtest/17c9317e6dc22c74fecd.jpg" alt="Question diagram"></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',12,0,2,NULL,0,1567),
(N'fb4a3cbc-c929-57eb-bcf4-7b295e5cec5a',N'293fb4a6-4dcf-5c11-93bb-ea6469a234a7',0,3,19,N'<p>The GPS Center</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là G. Grace hướng dẫn Dominic đi vào cổng chính, rẽ theo đường giữa và sẽ đi ngang qua trung tâm GPS ở bên trái. Key words: GPS centre: trung tâm GPS, left: bên trái. Scripts: “the GPS centre on the left” – trung tâm GPS ở bên trái.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1567),
(N'c5145374-fd1e-569c-9324-06a86dfe9005',N'293fb4a6-4dcf-5c11-93bb-ea6469a234a7',0,3,19,N'<p>Foward Thinking</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là F. Trung tâm Forward Thinking nằm bên trái đường và đối diện là đài phát thanh, đúng với vị trí F trên bản đồ. Key words: on the left: bên trái, opposite: đối diện. Scripts: “Forward Thinking will be on the left with the radio station opposite”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1567),
(N'537e38ce-3e1f-537b-83b5-4d01b9d0272e',N'293fb4a6-4dcf-5c11-93bb-ea6469a234a7',0,3,19,N'<p>Recycling Center</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là D. Trung tâm tái chế nằm ở cuối đường cụt phía sau Forward Thinking, phù hợp với vị trí D trên sơ đồ. Key words: recycling depot: trung tâm tái chế, dead end: đường cụt. Scripts: “there''s a dead end with the recycling depot”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1567),
(N'5e64eb8a-362a-565c-a3b1-1d224e0d7495',N'b300f78a-01cd-5b73-b15b-4b91c425b34a',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Use <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS </span>from the listening for each answer.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,3,NULL,0,1567),
(N'5a5068fc-e839-5066-93ea-4b656fccb545',N'5e64eb8a-362a-565c-a3b1-1d224e0d7495',0,3,19,N'<p>Dominic only needs to take the }{SPACE}{ with him to identify himself.</p><p>Forward Thinking will give Dominic a }{SPACE}{ so that he can use the internet during the training day.</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là confirmation letter. Dominic chỉ cần mang theo thư xác nhận để nhận dạng, không cần thẻ nhân viên. Key words: confirmation letter: thư xác nhận, identify: xác nhận danh tính. Scripts: “you only need to bring this confirmation letter”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1567),
(N'b7614fee-83fc-5f4d-b979-4f376e4a6c42',N'5e64eb8a-362a-565c-a3b1-1d224e0d7495',0,3,19,N'Question number 30',N'<p>Đáp án là password. Forward Thinking sẽ cung cấp cho Dominic mật khẩu để anh có thể sử dụng internet trong ngày đào tạo. Key words: password: mật khẩu, internet: mạng. Scripts: “They''ll also give you a password to get online there”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1567),
(N'42ce4921-9655-56a9-b8e7-9e851d7e116e',N'a9ed6ee5-445b-5966-8c87-c84a1f7d802a',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 2] Test 4 - Section 4</h2><p>Now turn to part 4. You will hear part of a lecture on cities of the future. You now have 30 seconds to read questions 31 to 37.  OK. We''ve been looking at how societies will develop in the future, and at the increase in the size of cities. So I want to talk to you today about the key considerations in these cities of the future. There are three key elements I want to look at, and these are the new features they will have, issues of size, and the main problems to be considered.  First of all, individual transportation will be a big factor in these new megacities, as public transport becomes unmanageable. There''ll be a huge rise in the use of segways, which are personal transporters, like motorised scooters. As a result, and partly also to reduce pollution, roads will be altered so that they are narrower and will take up less of a city''s space than they do currently. Naturally, this is a major change to the infrastructure, and something that may hinder it is the huge amount of investment it will require.  The next thing is what is going to happen to the commercial areas. We do not want these to become even larger concrete jungles than they are at present, so we have to look at design. And current designs for city development include building gardens on the roofs of these buildings to make a more pleasant environment for workers.  And you may think that these areas will expand to cope with increased commercial activity. In fact, the prediction is that they will cover one-fifth of the area that they do at present as we build upwards. The exception to this is shopping centres, which we predict will expand with more and more temperature-controlled malls. What may cause difficulties is that the superstores will be confined to the outer edges of the city, as they will be too big to fit into the new malls.  Then, of course, there are the residential areas, and these will undergo their own changes. One particular development will be houses which are built from glass, as innovations in this material allow it to provide light without causing problems with temperature inside a building. The residential areas will not be allowed to expand without limit, as happens in some areas at present, and their size will be restricted to a population of 15,000.  One issue which has yet to be resolved, and I''m not sure it ever will be, is how we manage to house older residents. They will be increasing in numbers as time goes on.  Finally, how w' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ill these cities live? We know we have limited energy sources, so what will we do? Well, something currently in development, which will be a feature, is that waste is going to become an energy source, for example, to provide gas in homes. Also, as new technology and systems are developed, we will find that energy plants will become smaller. Another energy source we could use, but one which raises issues of having enough space and too much noise, is wind farms. Because of the problems, I''m not convinced these will be the grand solution to our energy problems that we thought they were going to be.  You now have 15 seconds to read questions 38 to 40.  Now, moving on to looking at the social aspect of cities, we need to look at housing and how people will live. Cities currently have flats in the centre, populated by single people and wealthier residents, and families tend to move to the outskirts. In the future, the centre of cities will see a dramatic change.  We will see many more examples of cooperative buildings. This is where people join together to form a company that owns the building they live in. And, despite continuing shortages, there will also be a rise in the provision of retirement homes in city centres, so that the elderly can have easy access to hospitals and shops.  Recently, we have seen a levelling off in the growth of private housing, and I think that will not change. But we are likely to see more social housing, as far fewer people will be able to afford to own their own homes.  OK now, if anybody has any…  That is the end of part four.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Now turn to part 4. You will hear part of a lecture on cities of the future. You now have 30 seconds to read questions 31 to 37.  OK. We''ve been looking at how societies will develop in the future, and at the increase in the size of cities. So I want to talk to you today about the key considerations in these cities of the future. There are three key elements I want to look at, and these are the new features they will have, issues of size, and the main problems to be considered.  First of all, individual transportation will be a big factor in these new megacities, as public transport becomes unmanageable. There''ll be a huge rise in the use of segways, which are personal transporters, like motorised scooters. As a result, and partly also to reduce pollution, roads will be altered so that they are narrower and will take up less of a city''s space than they do currently. Naturally, this is a major change to the infrastructure, and something that may hinder it is the huge amount of investment it will require.  The next thing is what is going to happen to the commercial areas. We do not want these to become even larger concrete jungles than they are at present, so we have to look at design. And current designs for city development include building gardens on the roofs of these buildings to make a more pleasant environment for workers.  And you may think that these areas will expand to cope with increased commercial activity. In fact, the prediction is that they will cover one-fifth of the area that they do at present as we build upwards. The exception to this is shopping centres, which we predict will expand with more and more temperature-controlled malls. What may cause difficulties is that the superstores will be confined to the outer edges of the city, as they will be too big to fit into the new malls.  Then, of course, there are the residential areas, and these will undergo their own changes. One particular development will be houses which are built from glass, as innovations in this material allow it to provide light without causing problems with temperature inside a building. The residential areas will not be allowed to expand without limit, as happens in some areas at present, and their size will be restricted to a population of 15,000.  One issue which has yet to be resolved, and I''m not sure it ever will be, is how we manage to house older residents. They will be increasing in numbers as time goes on.  Finally, how will these cities live? We know we h' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ave limited energy sources, so what will we do? Well, something currently in development, which will be a feature, is that waste is going to become an energy source, for example, to provide gas in homes. Also, as new technology and systems are developed, we will find that energy plants will become smaller. Another energy source we could use, but one which raises issues of having enough space and too much noise, is wind farms. Because of the problems, I''m not convinced these will be the grand solution to our energy problems that we thought they were going to be.  You now have 15 seconds to read questions 38 to 40.  Now, moving on to looking at the social aspect of cities, we need to look at housing and how people will live. Cities currently have flats in the centre, populated by single people and wealthier residents, and families tend to move to the outskirts. In the future, the centre of cities will see a dramatic change.  We will see many more examples of cooperative buildings. This is where people join together to form a company that owns the building they live in. And, despite continuing shortages, there will also be a rise in the provision of retirement homes in city centres, so that the elderly can have easy access to hospitals and shops.  Recently, we have seen a levelling off in the growth of private housing, and I think that will not change. But we are likely to see more social housing, as far fewer people will be able to afford to own their own homes.  OK now, if anybody has any…  That is the end of part four.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-2/iL.VOL2.4.S1567.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1567),
(N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',N'42ce4921-9655-56a9-b8e7-9e851d7e116e',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</span> for each answer.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,1,NULL,0,1567),
(N'9d22a680-b20d-52b9-a1c6-d7ea73389323',N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',0,3,19,N'<table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>Category</span></p></td><td><p><span>New Features</span></p></td><td><p><span>Size</span></p></td><td><p><span>Problems</span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Transport</p></td><td><p>Individual transportation</p></td><td><p>Roads will be narrower</p></td><td><p>Levels of investment</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Commercial areas</p></td><td><p>Roofs will have }{SPACE}{ </p></td><td> }{SPACE}{ of current area</p></td><td> }{SPACE}{ will be limited to outskirts</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Residential areas</p></td><td><p>Homes made of }{SPACE}{ </p></td><td><p>Will be limited to 15,000</p></td><td><p>Providing enough housing }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Energy sources</p></td><td> }{SPACE}{ will be an energy source</p></td><td><p>Energy plants will be smaller</p></td><td><p>Noise and congestion caused by }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Đáp án là gardens. Người nói mô tả thiết kế mới của khu thương mại sẽ có các khu vườn trên mái nhằm tạo môi trường làm việc dễ chịu hơn cho người lao động. Key words: gardens: khu vườn, roofs: mái nhà. Scripts: “building gardens on the roofs of these buildings”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1567),
(N'80d78625-59df-528b-914b-123dfad948b2',N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>Đáp án là one fifth. Dù hoạt động thương mại tăng, diện tích khu thương mại được dự đoán sẽ giảm còn một phần năm so với hiện tại vì các tòa nhà được xây cao lên. Key words: one fifth: một phần năm, build upwards: xây theo chiều cao. Scripts: “they will cover one-fifth of the area that they do at present”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1567),
(N'897ea549-dbb1-51ed-9938-c456a1cf12d0',N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',0,3,19,N'Question number 33',N'<p>Đáp án là superstores. Các siêu cửa hàng lớn sẽ bị giới hạn ở vùng ngoại ô vì quá lớn để đặt trong các trung tâm thương mại mới. Key words: superstores: siêu cửa hàng lớn, outskirts: vùng ven. Scripts: “the superstores will be confined to the outer edges of the city”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1567),
(N'b699e6d7-ba13-5b8d-8265-955f1fb69f96',N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>Đáp án là glass. Khu dân cư tương lai sẽ có những ngôi nhà làm bằng kính nhờ công nghệ mới cho phép lấy sáng mà không gây vấn đề nhiệt độ. Key words: glass: kính, light: ánh sáng. Scripts: “houses which are built from glass”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1567),
(N'b8ff66ff-dd53-5515-8431-52b8b7fe327e',N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>Đáp án là elderly. Vấn đề lớn trong khu dân cư là việc cung cấp đủ chỗ ở cho người cao tuổi khi nhóm dân số này ngày càng tăng. Key words: elderly: người cao tuổi, housing: chỗ ở. Scripts: “how we manage to house older residents”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1567),
(N'08cb25cc-9af0-5dcf-ba13-791e01ceee78',N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>Đáp án là waste. Rác thải được dự đoán sẽ trở thành một nguồn năng lượng, ví dụ để sản xuất khí gas cho gia đình. Key words: waste: rác thải, energy source: nguồn năng lượng. Scripts: “waste is going to become an energy source”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1567),
(N'd4e6673f-abb8-52b5-a1a5-681aa007a6ed',N'd096be6e-c79a-5de9-8ce3-8b6e35b93633',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>Đáp án là wind farms. Trang trại điện gió gây ra vấn đề về không gian và tiếng ồn, dẫn đến sự nghi ngờ về hiệu quả lâu dài của chúng. Key words: wind farms: điện gió, noise: tiếng ồn. Scripts: “having enough space and too much noise is wind farms”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1567),
(N'f88e7502-3c3f-56b1-bb50-7ed3c9c5512f',N'42ce4921-9655-56a9-b8e7-9e851d7e116e',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS </span>for each answer.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Which three types of accommodation does the speaker say will increase in city centres?',11,0,2,NULL,0,1567),
(N'c2b61217-fc3e-5bb0-9378-7bf3ad3ecb12',N'f88e7502-3c3f-56b1-bb50-7ed3c9c5512f',0,3,19,N'<h3>Which three types of accommodation does the speaker say will increase in city centres?</h3> }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{  }{SPACE}{ ',N'<p>Đáp án là cooperative buildings. Trung tâm thành phố tương lai sẽ xuất hiện nhiều tòa nhà hợp tác, nơi cư dân cùng sở hữu và quản lý nơi ở của mình. Key words: cooperative: hợp tác, buildings: tòa nhà. Scripts: “many more examples of cooperative buildings”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1567),
(N'444de9d8-4e42-500f-9871-630fbc75c4e2',N'f88e7502-3c3f-56b1-bb50-7ed3c9c5512f',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>Đáp án là retirement homes. Nhà dưỡng lão sẽ tăng trong trung tâm thành phố để người cao tuổi dễ tiếp cận bệnh viện và dịch vụ. Key words: retirement homes: nhà dưỡng lão, elderly: người cao tuổi. Scripts: “a rise in the provision of retirement homes”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1567),
(N'374f304f-7bf6-5a3e-b7a7-b1548ed4aabd',N'f88e7502-3c3f-56b1-bb50-7ed3c9c5512f',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>Đáp án là social housing. Nhà ở xã hội sẽ tăng do ngày càng ít người đủ khả năng mua nhà riêng. Key words: social housing: nhà ở xã hội, afford: đủ khả năng chi trả. Scripts: “we are likely to see more social housing”.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1567),
(N'b397a748-2dd3-5a77-a7c2-015757762403',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-2/iL.VOL2.3.S1564.mp3',N'Vol 2 LISTENING FULL TEST 3',0,7,1,NULL,1,1564),
(N'9ea259c7-b4e9-519e-a866-104e2023f137',N'b397a748-2dd3-5a77-a7c2-015757762403',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 2] A conversation about a new school</h2><p>Hi, John. How''s it going?

Pretty good. What about you?

Terrific. So what do you think of our new school?

A one, man. Much better than the old one. In fact, I''m going to write an article for the London Times about it.

Really?

Yeah. Have you got half an hour? Maybe you should give me some ideas.

If you buy me a coffee.

No problem. How do you think I should start?

Introduce the headmaster. Why don''t you compare it with the old one? You know, the buildings, laboratories, language lab, computer equipment, fitness centre, pool, stuff like that.

Yeah, that sounds reasonable. I mean, the old one was pretty bad, and the new headmaster seems like a great guy. Actually, all the teachers I''ve met seem pretty decent.

Same here. The French teacher is really cool, and he''s actually French, not like Smith at the old place. He sounded less French than even you do.

Hey, my accent is perfect.

Dream on. But why don''t you start with what the headmaster told us school this morning? You know, his ideas on education and things.

Yeah, I liked that. None of that stuff about how we must study hard for our futures and the honor of the school. There''s no free lunch, that type of thing.

He sounds pretty progressive. What did he say?

If we teachers succeed in stimulating your minds, stimulating you to ask questions and think critically, then you will discover the joy of learning, enjoy your studies, and therefore work hard.

Hey, what a memory. Those are his exact words.

Do you remember what he said next?

Something about it''s good to keep fit.

Yeah. Do you remember him mentioning those experiments showing that after half an hour of exercise our brains are much more creative for hours and put you in a better mood?

Did you see the fitness center?

Look at Questions 6 to 10.

Now listen to more of the conversation between John and Joan and answer Questions 6 to 10.

I went early this morning for a workout. Fantastic. Olympic-size pool, not like that tiny 25-meter thing we had before. And the gym has absolutely everything. I counted 10 exercise bikes, some rowing machines, really good ones.

Are you gonna go?

I''ve been every other day for 2 weeks. Feel great.

Hey, maybe I should interview some of the teachers and students for my article.

Of course you should.

Wow, just thought of it. I''m doing a video project. Maybe I could film the interviews.

Makes sense.

And I could take a few photos. Maybe the newspaper would ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'use one or two of them.

The main hall is awesome. Really light, lots of windows, and a huge stage big enough for a 60- or 70-person orchestra.

Did you see Mr Clark, the old music teacher? I think he''s the only teacher here from the old school.

He is, and he was the only decent teacher.

I met him in the hall. He''s really happy, planning to double the size of the school orchestra.

It''s great.

Okay, let''s find what we''re going to do.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Hi, John. How''s it going?

Pretty good. What about you?

Terrific. So what do you think of our new school?

A one, man. Much better than the old one. In fact, I''m going to write an article for the London Times about it.

