(N'04428b50-64bf-578b-aff4-63e610b8d568',N'c0f7e0fe-a3f5-57b1-9a6b-65ec9de4d835',0,3,19,N'<p>Paying a visit to a rock concert as a teenager:</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Trong câu "The very first rock concert I went to when I was 13, your band was playing," chủ ngữ I chính là người đã đi xem buổi hòa nhạc rock đầu tiên lúc còn là thiếu niên (13 tuổi). Cụm "your band" cho thấy người nói đang hướng lời tới Hamish - chủ nhân ban nhạc đó. Vậy người đi xem concert năm 13 tuổi chính là Juliette (interviewer), không phải Hamish.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1563),
(N'4f52645d-a52d-5683-928d-49921989b950',N'c0f7e0fe-a3f5-57b1-9a6b-65ec9de4d835',0,3,19,N'<p>Crying on the radio:</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là C. Ray Rogers được nhắc đến là người đã khóc trực tiếp trên chương trình radio của mình khi ban nhạc tan rã, cho thấy hành động này gắn với Ray.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1563),
(N'5e4a4279-98ca-556a-a07a-50053ac6f180',N'c0f7e0fe-a3f5-57b1-9a6b-65ec9de4d835',0,3,19,N'<p>Very nervous about performing in public:</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A. Hamish thừa nhận anh rất lo lắng mỗi khi biểu diễn trước công chúng và khán giả trực tiếp khiến anh sợ hãi, phù hợp với mô tả rất căng thẳng khi biểu diễn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1563),
(N'144dfbc0-b88a-527b-8bfe-4c5483e69b9b',N'c0f7e0fe-a3f5-57b1-9a6b-65ec9de4d835',0,3,19,N'<p>Work adversely affected private life:</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A. Hamish cho biết cuộc sống riêng của anh bị ảnh hưởng nghiêm trọng bởi công việc lưu diễn, dẫn đến hôn nhân tan vỡ và mối quan hệ gia đình rạn nứt.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1563),
(N'abe505de-55bb-5f37-aaa7-691e799d2c19',N'9eb6a5c2-ea9a-5be3-b1e3-233bed0575dd',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</span> in each gap.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,2,NULL,0,1563),
(N'd5546486-6aa8-5827-aa92-86ee9f276e84',N'abe505de-55bb-5f37-aaa7-691e799d2c19',0,3,19,N'<table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><em>A process of making an acoustic guitar:</em></p> <p><span>Stage 1:</span></p> <ul><li><p>Mahogany is used for the neck as it can endure changes in</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ and humidity.</p> <p><span>Stage 2:</span></p> <ul><li><p>Braces are glued onto the top and back to improve</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ and reflect sound waves.</p> <p><span>Stage 3:</span></p> <ul><li><p>Wet wooden strips are placed into a curved mold.</p></li></ul> <p><span>Stage 4:</span></p> <ul><li><p>The neck is carved from a single piece of wood, and a metal rod is driven up it for</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Stage 5:</span></p> <ul><li><p>The back, sides, top, and neck are glued and clamped together for</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Đáp án là temperature. Hamish giải thích cần dùng gỗ mahogany cho cần đàn vì nó chịu được sự thay đổi về nhiệt độ và độ ẩm mà không bị biến dạng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1563),
(N'17eb3b2f-acf9-58f2-adcb-80541d4d6158',N'abe505de-55bb-5f37-aaa7-691e799d2c19',0,3,19,N'Question number 16',N'<p>Đáp án là tone. Phần giằng bên trong đàn được dùng để kiểm soát độ rung và cải thiện âm sắc của cây đàn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1563),
(N'f344b71c-4089-55d2-8738-199babe88b7b',N'abe505de-55bb-5f37-aaa7-691e799d2c19',0,3,19,N'Question number 17',N'<p>Đáp án là reinforcement. Hamish mô tả một thanh kim loại được gắn vào cần đàn nhằm gia cố và tăng độ bền cho cấu trúc.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1563),
(N'e56c0b3e-328c-524d-932c-5a0a27aff2b0',N'abe505de-55bb-5f37-aaa7-691e799d2c19',0,3,19,N'Question number 18',N'<p>Đáp án là several days. Sau khi các bộ phận được dán lại với nhau, cây đàn được kẹp cố định trong vài ngày để đảm bảo kết cấu chắc chắn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1563),
(N'34e71ac3-3a90-5fc1-9484-b6ab951226bc',N'9eb6a5c2-ea9a-5be3-b1e3-233bed0575dd',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Choose the correct letters, <span>A, B, </span>or <span>C.</span></em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,3,NULL,0,1563),
(N'98f234f9-a0e5-59ed-ade5-6b890ea459ff',N'34e71ac3-3a90-5fc1-9484-b6ab951226bc',0,3,19,N'<p>Why does Hamish enjoy making guitars?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Hamish nói rất rõ rằng việc làm đàn giúp cải thiện sức khỏe tinh thần của anh, vì quá trình này chậm rãi, chính xác và có tác dụng làm dịu. Đây là lý do trực tiếp khiến anh thích công việc này, không phải vì biểu diễn hay chơi đàn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1563),
(N'a973dde1-094c-52c7-958e-79199ff706e8',N'34e71ac3-3a90-5fc1-9484-b6ab951226bc',0,3,19,N'<p>How is the name of Hamish’s old band spelt?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là A. Hamish đùa rằng anh đã phải giải thích suốt nhiều năm rằng tên ban nhạc được viết là whole với chữ W, cho thấy tên ban nhạc là Sound Whole, không phải các cách viết khác.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1563),
(N'c47c42ba-a87f-58f7-aa89-3442c013d32e',N'78744c34-1803-50a6-8c6a-9ca5a9a187b9',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 2] Test 2 - Section 3</h2><p>Hi, Marina. How are you?

Fine, David, but this assignment''s taking longer than I hoped.

Yes, it''s quite detailed. Designing small vertical gardens doesn''t seem too hard, but there''s so much stuff online about large-scale projects that reads like propaganda.

I know what you mean. Vertical gardening is flavor of the month when all it amounts to is growing plants on surfaces not used in the past. Tricky surfaces at that.

Yes, walls do present challenges. Vertical gardening is popular in warm climates and where there''s cash to spare. I can''t see people in my country adopting it, or indeed anyone who doesn''t own their own home. All that construction effort, then watering your plants five times a day. How would you transport your wall if you moved?

We should certainly include your concerns at the end of our presentation when we assess whether vertical gardening will become a design fundamental or remain a passing fad.

Okay. How are we going to divide up the presentation?

I''m happy to do the second part, our domestic designs.

Yes. I''m afraid I can''t get interested in the large-scale works like the wall at the CaixaForum Museum in Madrid. They seem gratuitous to me.

Don''t worry, I''ll comment on them. However, I do think the vertical garden in the Rue Dulsarce in Paris is amazing. It''s like having a vast mossy forest floor stood up on its side amidst all the concrete and tarmac.

So we just need to work on the first part together — the history of the movement and the reasons for its growth.

I read that a French botanist, Patrick Blanc, invented vertical gardening in 2008.

And I read that he got the idea from tropical rainforests in Malaysia, where plants grow at any height and their superficial root systems don''t need any soil.

Still, I don''t understand why the movement took off and companies from Sydney to Tokyo commission him to cover walls with plants, especially when the walls need so much watering.

I suppose businesses want to show how green they are.

Before you listen to the rest of the conversation, you have 30 seconds to read Questions 26 to 30.

So how do you build a vertical garden?

The principle is quite simple. Firstly, you need a wall, either the wall of a building or a boundary wall. This is called a supporting wall. Vertical wooden battens and a PVC panel are secured to it. Currently, Japan is the only country where PVC is recyclable, meaning elsewhere vertical gardens are not environmentally frien' AS nvarchar(max))+N'dly inside.

What happens after the PVC panel is in place?

Irrigation piping and two layers of matting are attached. The matting is made from felt or woven plastic that lasts a long time outdoors, but it can''t be recycled either.

Speaking about recycling, unless it comes off a nearby roof, the great amount of water needed to keep the wall growing seems like the biggest drawback to me, especially when watering five times a day, as you mentioned. In a tropical rainforest there is plenty of water, but not so in downtown Paris. Also, Parisian water has few nutrients, so the amount of fertilizer for vertical gardens is high — 0.4 grams of micronutrients and 0.2 milliliters of trace elements per liter of water. If a designer can ensure the fertilizer is absorbed by the plants or drains into fish ponds, that''s okay. But if it goes into the main water supply, which I bet it does most of the time, it just adds to pollution.

Good point. How exactly do the plants grow?

They sprout from slots cut into the matting.

What are your designs?

One is a mosquito-repellent wall containing scented plants that drive mosquitoes away. The other is a culinary wall with herbs, salad plants, and strawberries.

Sounds delicious.

Personally, I would be happier planting my veges in pots on the balcony.

Perhaps, but I do find the giant vertical gardens in Madrid and Paris inspiring. Once obstacles like the plastic components and frequent irrigation are overcome, vertical gardening will be an aesthetic and environmental achievement.

I''m not sure the whole world will take it up, but richer countries could well do so.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Hi, Marina. How are you?

