(N'745af88e-85d4-52b0-9010-aa4dbafcb0a5',N'0e78a730-f427-5584-88b9-f561428eb147',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Choose the correct letters, <span>A, B</span>, or <span>C.</span></em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,1,NULL,0,1563),
(N'6e3f9344-90c6-5121-a1ad-50aa1536200d',N'745af88e-85d4-52b0-9010-aa4dbafcb0a5',0,3,19,N'<p>What is a concern of a growing number of the speaker’s clients?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là C. Người nói cho biết ngày càng nhiều khách hàng của ông lo lắng về tương lai của chính họ, dù cuộc sống hiện tại có vẻ lý tưởng, nên mối quan tâm chính là sự bất định trong tương lai. Key words: future: tương lai, concern: lo lắng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1563),
(N'66161eaa-c283-5c1e-8aec-870aea8e1808',N'745af88e-85d4-52b0-9010-aa4dbafcb0a5',0,3,19,N'<p>Why did the female client stop her sessions with the psychologist?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án là B. Người nói chỉ đề cập rằng người phụ nữ không tiếp tục buổi hẹn thứ tư, nhưng không nêu rõ lý do cụ thể vì sao cô ấy dừng việc trị liệu, nên nguyên nhân là không rõ ràng. Key words: didn’t keep: không tiếp tục, unclear: không rõ ràng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1563),
(N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',N'0e78a730-f427-5584-88b9-f561428eb147',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Write <span>ONE WORD ONLY</span> in each gap.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,2,NULL,0,1563),
(N'00621a17-ba6b-54f7-9214-7ffc69a2a90d',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'<table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><em>Self-fulfillment</em></p> <ul><li><p>Writers/philosophers/others who symbolize</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ rather than self-fulfillment</p> <p>     - People in  }{SPACE}{ regions, ghost towns, parts of the developing world</p> <p>- The speaker’s }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><em>King’s Dimensions</em></p> <p><span>Height:</span></p> <ul><li><p>Height = especially hard for the</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ to accept</p> <ul><li><p>For non-religious people, height = something</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ than oneself / an abstract idea,</p> <p>like equality or }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><em>Conclusion</em></p> <ul><li><p>The do-what-you-love principle may degrade mental</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p> <ul><li><p>True self-fulfillment is sometimes doing disagreeable things for the sake of</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Đáp án là sacrifice. Người nói nhấn mạnh rằng những nhà tư tưởng và lãnh đạo vĩ đại tượng trưng cho sự hy sinh bản thân, thay vì theo đuổi sự thỏa mãn cá nhân. Key words: self-sacrifice: sự hy sinh, meaning: ý nghĩa</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1563),
(N'23263f0e-1a58-53b6-b8d5-c4aa12da064c',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>Đáp án là rural. Người nói đề cập đến những người sống ở vùng nông thôn hoặc thị trấn gần như bị bỏ hoang như ví dụ cho việc làm điều cần làm hơn là tìm kiếm sự tự thỏa mãn. Key words: rural: nông thôn, ghost towns: thị trấn hoang.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1563),
(N'7439a263-8b0f-598d-89e2-66aa0704bd6c',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>Đáp án là mother. Người nói lấy chính mẹ mình làm ví dụ tiêu biểu cho lối sống đặt trách nhiệm và bổn phận lên trên mong muốn cá nhân. Key words: mother: mẹ, responsibility: trách nhiệm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1563),
(N'6d619c4c-2ad5-5883-8bf6-7491b6958e94',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>Đáp án là young. Chiều kích “height” theo Martin Luther King đặc biệt khó chấp nhận đối với người trẻ, vì họ thường tập trung vào bản thân. Key words: young: người trẻ, difficult: khó.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1563),
(N'd0e32fc0-7b46-517f-9639-61f1679e6cb1',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>Đáp án là greater. Với người không theo tôn giáo, khái niệm height có thể hiểu là trở thành một phần của điều gì đó lớn lao hơn bản thân. Key words: greater: lớn hơn, oneself: bản thân.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1563),
(N'b67da595-0b6c-5836-8234-dfaca7ea3276',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'Question number 38',N'<p>Đáp án là justice. Người nói đưa ra ví dụ cụ thể về những lý tưởng trừu tượng có thể thay thế niềm tin tôn giáo, như công bằng hoặc bình đẳng. Key words: equality: bình đẳng, justice: công lý.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1563),
(N'd871a2f7-b39f-5d30-854b-61051548413d',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>Đáp án là health. Người nói cho rằng nguyên tắc chỉ làm điều mình thích có thể làm suy giảm sức khỏe tinh thần nếu bị hiểu và áp dụng sai. Key words: mental: tinh thần, degrade: làm suy giảm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1563),
(N'7feff9e3-a83e-52ea-b2eb-15587bb46fe4',N'63d37508-a507-58e7-9277-d434d7cf080f',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>Đáp án là others. Người nói kết luận rằng đôi khi sự tự hoàn thiện bản thân đến từ việc làm những điều không dễ chịu vì lợi ích của người khác. Key words: benefit: lợi ích, others: người khác.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1563),
(N'c6f1b814-4f27-53b1-ae33-5d64f7d745fd',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-2/iL.VOL2.1.S1555.mp3',N'Vol 2 LISTENING FULL TEST 1',0,7,1,NULL,1,1555),
(N'd682ae9e-fa39-5e78-a28f-fd84678a2a14',N'c6f1b814-4f27-53b1-ae33-5d64f7d745fd',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2> [VOL 2] Joining Social Club Form</h2><p>Hello. Come in and take a seat.
Oh, thanks.
Good. And how can I help you?
Well, I''d quite like to join this international social club, and I was hoping you could help me.

Yes, no problem. Let me just get the form up on my screen and I''ll fill in your details. Let''s see.
Yes, here we are. Okay, the first thing we need is your name.
Jenny Fu.
That''s F-double-O.
Okay, great. And can you tell me how old you are, Jenny?
I''m 21.

Great. And how long have you been here in Australia, by the way?
I arrived just last month, two weeks before the start of the academic year, just to sort things out and settle in a bit.
Good idea. Where are you from originally?
I''m from Kuala Lumpur. That''s where I was born and brought up.

So, you''re Malaysian, are you?
That''s right, though I lived in the United States for a couple of years when I was a teenager. We went there for my father''s job.
Right. And can you tell me your current address, please?
Sure. Just at the moment I''m lodging with a family at 13 Anglesey Road in Bondi.

Okay, let me just type that in. How do you spell Anglesey, by the way?
It''s spelled A-N-G-L-E-S-E-A.
Thanks. That''s quite a long way from the city centre, isn''t it? Is it a problem getting into the city centre?
Not really, because the buses are good and it''s a nice, quiet area to live in.

That''s true. So, I guess you must have a cell phone number you can give me so we can keep you informed of events and so on?
Yes. Let me just have a look. It''s a new one, so I haven''t learned the number yet.
Ah, here it is. It''s 040-422-9160.
Okay, good.

And you like the family you''re living with?
Sure. They''ve got a little boy who is quite noisy, but he''s really no trouble.
Fine. Now, let''s see. What''s next?
Yep. Can you tell me what you do? I mean, are you working or studying?

Well, at the moment I''m doing a temporary job with a company here in Sydney. I''m an economist, in fact.
Okay. And how long do you think you''ll be here in Sydney?
At least a year. I may look for work here afterwards.

Great. Now, you want to join the International Social Club, and it will be good to know a bit about your free time interests as well. What do you like doing?
Well, I''m quite musical, and I really enjoy singing. Back home I sang with a band just, you know, for fun. But for me, what I like best is dancing.
You know, the modern sort? I really love it.

Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions fou' AS nvarchar(max))+N'r to ten on page three.

So, how are you getting on here? I mean, your level of English is better than most people who come from overseas to work, and you''ve got a really nice American accent, so I don''t suppose you have any communication problems in the office, though you might find some of our Australian slang more difficult to understand.
Well, a bit. But I haven''t met that many Australians yet, outside of work, I mean.

Right. But could you tell me a bit about the International Club, now I''ve joined?
Sure. We''ve got currently about fifty members, but people join all the time, so I should think that figure will go up. Last year, we had thirty members, and the year before, just eighteen, so we''re growing and getting better known. I reckon that at this rate, next year we''ll have about eighty.

And does the club hold regular meetings?
Yes. Every second Thursday evening, in fact. So, a couple of times a month. Though, of course, when you start making friends, you''ll be getting together with them more often than that, I guess. The next meeting will be next Thursday, if I''m not mistaken.

Yes, that''s right. And what happens when the club meets? I mean, what sort of things are organised?
The usual thing is for one of the members to give a little presentation about where they''re from, their customs, and so on. But from time to time, they do other things. Outings to places around Sydney, or meeting up to eat together in a restaurant, or go to a concert together, or something like that.

Okay, that sounds fun. And the members aren''t just people from other countries? Non-Australians, are they?
No, not at all. The main point of the club is to give people like you the chance to mix in more with people from this country. People of all ages. You''ll find us very friendly.

I think the contact has a positive effect on visitors to this country. And, in fact, it affects us locals positively as well. You know, it''s a sort of intercultural experience for everybody. And, of course, you should get the chance to do all sorts of activities with other members of the club if you want to. It''s not just for talking. And hopefully you''ll make friends with people who have similar interests.

It sounds great. I''m really looking forward to the first meeting.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Hello. Come in and take a seat.
