 C sai vì nhắc tới bus, cab, shuttle bus nhưng không phải lời khuyên cuối cùng.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1540),
(N'3dd6ffad-78e2-51c1-a935-6b19f64aede5',N'37b9d2e7-2476-5551-9f21-dd94ab381c0a',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Work experience programme</h2><p>Hi John, you wanted some advice about the work experience programme?

Yeah, I wanted to check I''ve understood what I''m supposed to do.

Sure! For a start, we have to work for a company during the holidays between the first and second year and again between the second and third year, right?

Yes, usually you do around 50 days with two different employers, like 100 days in total.

I see. You know there''s one aspect of the work experience programme I''m unhappy with. I mean, considering we have to attend a preparation course for it and that it''s a big investment in terms of time, it''s just the fact that no matter how well we perform on the programme, it makes no difference whatsoever to our final grade for our degree.

It goes unrecognised.

I know, but there are lots of advantages too. Anyway, when does your preparation course for the work experience start?

Soon.

We''re starting with a session on interview technique, how to respond to questions, that stuff.

That can be useful, but I remember we had to make up a list of things we wanted to achieve during the time with the company. That was a bit pointless.

My thoughts exactly. You can''t decide on your aims until you''ve started work for real.

The tutor will give you some good advice about presenting your résumé, though, so don''t miss that session.

OK. When the work experience starts for real, it would be great if I could actually do a bit of software design.

That''s highly unlikely on a work experience course.

You''ll be doing more basic things than that, but they might start you off on the IT help desk. That''s often where they need extra people.

But then you''ll move on to work in areas that suit your field of study. I mean, whatever you focused on this year at college, you need to be able to put it into practice during work experience.

There has to be a match.

