(N'0a6287af-9011-5133-a884-675bca122b2b',N'08293024-d1a5-52cb-816a-3977d4526f53',0,3,19,N'<p>John is unhappy because the work experience programme</p>',N'<p>Đúng: John khó chịu vì chương trình không được tính điểm (doesn’t earn credit). Sai A: Không có thông tin chương trình rất đắt tiền. Sai B: John có nói “a big investment in terms of time”, nhưng nhấn mạnh nguyên nhân không được ghi nhận, không phải “mất nhiều thời gian”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1540),
(N'9baf33ba-f45d-53dc-90ff-4520ee03d250',N'08293024-d1a5-52cb-816a-3977d4526f53',0,3,19,N'<p>What part of the preparation course do Emma and John agree is unnecessary?</p>',N'<p>Đúng: Cả hai cho rằng việc đặt mục tiêu cá nhân trước khi làm là vô ích. Sai A: Buổi résumé được khuyên “don’t miss that session” → cần thiết. Sai B: Buổi interview technique được nói là “can be useful” → không phải vô ích.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1540),
(N'1ccb7f5b-700d-5ec4-9000-019682c1ad60',N'08293024-d1a5-52cb-816a-3977d4526f53',0,3,19,N'<p>What does Emma say about the work experience role John might take on?</p>',N'<p>Đúng: Ban đầu John sẽ làm việc cơ bản, nhưng sau đó sẽ được giao việc đúng chuyên ngành. Sai A: Không đúng vì chỉ bắt đầu ở help desk, không phải toàn bộ thời gian. Sai B: Emma nói “highly unlikely” được làm software design.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1540),
(N'c45167c7-f30d-56cc-806d-4ef6d4c3b453',N'08293024-d1a5-52cb-816a-3977d4526f53',0,3,19,N'<p>According to John, when students are doing work experience after their second year ?</p>',N'<p>Đúng: John khẳng định nhà tuyển dụng không bắt buộc phải trả lương cho sinh viên thực tập. Sai A: Thực tế có thể nhận mức 20–25$/h, gần bằng cử nhân mới tốt nghiệp → không phải “không thể mong”. Sai C: Vẫn có thể làm cho volunteer organisation nếu muốn, tức không bị cấm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1540),
(N'40bc70ed-f271-542f-968a-54f8c73d1f16',N'3dd6ffad-78e2-51c1-a935-6b19f64aede5',0,2,18,N'<p><em>Choose <span>SIX</span> answers from the box and write the correct letter, A-I, next to Questions <span>25-30</span>.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'The process for getting and doing work experience',15,0,2,NULL,0,1540),
(N'3c2dbc16-e2b2-5c9b-ae47-21a1c64bce45',N'40bc70ed-f271-542f-968a-54f8c73d1f16',0,3,19,N'<p>The student gets a simple</p><p>form.</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi đề: “The student gets a simple … form.” → Ở đoạn nghe nói rõ sinh viên phải điền form nộp đơn ngay từ đầu ⇒ đáp án là application.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1540),
(N'1a56da66-cfdb-5648-bcd7-097ba8f46bc3',N'40bc70ed-f271-542f-968a-54f8c73d1f16',0,3,19,N'<p>The college looks at a short</p><p>and confirms whether the work is suitable.</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi đề: “The college looks at a short … and confirms whether the work is suitable.” → Trường yêu cầu một bản mô tả nhiệm vụ ngắn ⇒ chính là job description</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1540),
(N'97c92216-2302-50d4-b80f-3d5b959fac05',N'40bc70ed-f271-542f-968a-54f8c73d1f16',0,3,19,N'<p>The college obtains the relevant</p><p>from the employer.</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi đề: “The college obtains the relevant … from the employer.” → Thông tin lấy được là tên và email ⇒ đó là contact details.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1540),
(N'18fb78a2-f87b-5b63-94f2-4d5cc1d4ddf9',N'40bc70ed-f271-542f-968a-54f8c73d1f16',0,3,19,N'<p>A meeting is arranged to discuss</p><p>issues.</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi đề: “A meeting is arranged to discuss … issues.” → Cuộc họp nhằm bàn về an toàn, phòng ngừa tai nạn ⇒ đáp án health and safety.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1540),
(N'ae0d5c5b-a386-50ae-8437-168ccf757178',N'40bc70ed-f271-542f-968a-54f8c73d1f16',0,3,19,N'<p>The student must complete a</p><p>while doing the work experience.</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi đề: “The student must complete a … while doing the work experience.” → Sinh viên phải ghi lại công việc hằng ngày ⇒ chính là record of work.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1540),
(N'201e7306-7ef9-5e51-9681-1a966ed17c5d',N'40bc70ed-f271-542f-968a-54f8c73d1f16',0,3,19,N'<p>Employers are provided with a voluntary</p><p>form.</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi đề: “Employers are provided with a voluntary … form.” → Biểu mẫu cho nhà tuyển dụng là nhận xét/đánh giá ⇒ feedback.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1540),
(N'14061e38-b099-5ac6-b237-024c75646e6d',N'37b9d2e7-2476-5551-9f21-dd94ab381c0a',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Theories of intelligence</h2><p>I am going to consider three theories of intelligence and look at their implications for studying effectively while you are here at university.

Early psychologists, such as Spearman in the 1920s, believed that each individual has a single general level of intelligence known as the intelligence quotient or IQ.

According to that theory, we are born with a certain level of intelligence which is inherited from our parents, and our intelligence remains constant throughout our life.

It follows that any intelligence test we do at any time will give broadly similar results.

If this is true, it suggests that there isn''t much you can do to make yourself more intelligent since it''s a genetic feature. You wouldn''t be able to perform any better in your studies than you do now.

Most of the evidence for the genetic origin of intelligence has come from studies of twins, which show a considerable similarity in their intelligence.

However, other psychologists have interpreted the findings of this research differently, arguing that genetic influence plays only a small part in our intelligence or even no part at all.

Their theory said that the similarity in intelligence between the subjects used in such studies may have an environmental rather than genetic origin.

These psychologists point out that children brought up in the same family are likely to have experiences which are very similar. This, rather than their genetic inheritance, may be the main reason why they have similar levels of intelligence. Take the case of the Raven''s Test, an intelligence test used to measure abstract reasoning ability. The Raven''s Test was designed for use with people of any language, age, or country.

So in theory, a person''s result shouldn''t be affected by where they grow up.

Yet some research using Raven''s Test on children who moved home and lived in another culture showed that after a few years there was a significant improvement in their results.

In other words, our intelligence isn''t fixed.

An alternative view, which is significantly different, is that we have multiple intelligences, not one general one.

In the 1990s, Howard Gardner claimed that there are at least seven separate intelligences.

The list includes what you might expect, such as linguistic and logical skills, and also other less traditional ones such as a category of musical abilities.

Research in neuropsychology suggests that different cognitive abilities such as spee' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ches may be partially independent of each other and controlled by different circuits within the brain. Support for this theory comes from the fact that some people show a weakness in one particular area.

For instance, some find it very difficult to recognise someone''s face while they may have no problem remembering names.

This apparent variation in strengths and weaknesses within one person supports Gardner''s view that intelligence is multiple rather than general.

Gardner suggests that the various intelligences don''t operate in isolation from each other, and instead that interaction between them creates a far more complex picture and greatly increases what we can achieve.

If this is the case, and it seems likely, we can consciously use this knowledge in our own approach to studying and learning.

If you develop a sense of rhythm, this can improve not only music and dance as you would expect, but also areas that aren''t obviously linked, spelling for instance. If you need to learn a chemical formula or law of physics by heart, turn it into a song. It doesn''t matter at all if you''re out of key, but it''ll be easier for you to learn it.

That''s just one way of using multiple intelligences to make studying easier and much more fun.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>I am going to consider three theories of intelligence and look at their implications for studying effectively while you are here at university.

Early psychologists, such as Spearman in the 1920s, believed that each individual has a single general level of intelligence known as the intelligence quotient or IQ.

According to that theory, we are born with a certain level of intelligence which is inherited from our parents, and our intelligence remains constant throughout our life.

It follows that any intelligence test we do at any time will give broadly similar results.

If this is true, it suggests that there isn''t much you can do to make yourself more intelligent since it''s a genetic feature. You wouldn''t be able to perform any better in your studies than you do now.

Most of the evidence for the genetic origin of intelligence has come from studies of twins, which show a considerable similarity in their intelligence.

However, other psychologists have interpreted the findings of this research differently, arguing that genetic influence plays only a small part in our intelligence or even no part at all.

Their theory said that the similarity in intelligence between the subjects used in such studies may have an environmental rather than genetic origin.

These psychologists point out that children brought up in the same family are likely to have experiences which are very similar. This, rather than their genetic inheritance, may be the main reason why they have similar levels of intelligence. Take the case of the Raven''s Test, an intelligence test used to measure abstract reasoning ability. The Raven''s Test was designed for use with people of any language, age, or country.

So in theory, a person''s result shouldn''t be affected by where they grow up.

Yet some research using Raven''s Test on children who moved home and lived in another culture showed that after a few years there was a significant improvement in their results.

In other words, our intelligence isn''t fixed.

An alternative view, which is significantly different, is that we have multiple intelligences, not one general one.

In the 1990s, Howard Gardner claimed that there are at least seven separate intelligences.

The list includes what you might expect, such as linguistic and logical skills, and also other less traditional ones such as a category of musical abilities.

Research in neuropsychology suggests that different cognitive abilities such as speeches may be partially independent of each' AS nvarchar(max))+N' other and controlled by different circuits within the brain. Support for this theory comes from the fact that some people show a weakness in one particular area.

For instance, some find it very difficult to recognise someone''s face while they may have no problem remembering names.

This apparent variation in strengths and weaknesses within one person supports Gardner''s view that intelligence is multiple rather than general.

Gardner suggests that the various intelligences don''t operate in isolation from each other, and instead that interaction between them creates a far more complex picture and greatly increases what we can achieve.

If this is the case, and it seems likely, we can consciously use this knowledge in our own approach to studying and learning.

If you develop a sense of rhythm, this can improve not only music and dance as you would expect, but also areas that aren''t obviously linked, spelling for instance. If you need to learn a chemical formula or law of physics by heart, turn it into a song. It doesn''t matter at all if you''re out of key, but it''ll be easier for you to learn it.

That''s just one way of using multiple intelligences to make studying easier and much more fun.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.7.S1540.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1540),
