(N'32112440-6556-59f8-8021-43029bf29c30',N'8a630097-06b5-56cf-bd7d-21af331265a5',0,3,19,N'<p>Where is the suggestion box found at the health centre? }{SPACE}{ </p><p>Who will deal with any complaints made? }{SPACE}{ </p><p>What area is closed off during the open morning? }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là (at) reception</p><p>- Hộp góp ý được đặt “at reception”, vị trí này được nêu rõ ràng trong bài nghe</p><p>- Key word:+ suggestion box: (noun) hộp góp ý+ reception: (noun) quầy lễ tân</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1503),
(N'cc3a5719-ec88-55b0-a646-f08a275f89ce',N'8a630097-06b5-56cf-bd7d-21af331265a5',0,3,19,N'Question number 19',N'<p>- Đáp án là manager</p><p>- Người xử lý khiếu nại là “the practice manager”, được chỉ rõ trong script</p><p>- Key word:+ complaint: (noun) khiếu nại+ manager: (noun) quản lý</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1503),
(N'cbbdbe7f-b26f-5a88-bccc-b5f862ad96f4',N'8a630097-06b5-56cf-bd7d-21af331265a5',0,3,19,N'Question number 20',N'<p>- Đáp án là offices</p><p>- Trong buổi open morning, chỉ có “the practice offices” bị đóng vì chứa tài liệu nhạy cảm</p><p>- Key word:+ offices: (noun) văn phòng+ sensitive material: (noun phrase) tài liệu nhạy cảm</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1503),
(N'0fc6f910-95d8-5e8b-973c-6d98445c20d9',N'f24f244d-5fb2-522a-aa5f-64c5876eb899',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 1] Song-writing course for drama students</h2><p>Lorna: Hi Charles, I''m writing my reports on the songwriting course for drama students. You know, the one week residential course for drama students from different universities where you learn about writing songs. You did that course last year didn''t you? Could you give me some tips for the report? I''m having a few problems with it.</p><p>Charles: Yes, it was great. Well Lorna, you could start by thinking about what you got out of it. Evaluating how useful it was is simple. The challenge is not to go over length. Referring to published sources might help. That would make it more scholarly too.</p><p>Lorna: Okay, I''ll go with that. And saying why you did the songwriting course is important. Some people just do it as another string to their bow to help with auditions for work and things. I thought it was a good opportunity to try out new techniques for communicating feelings and emotions. I was already confident about singing before I did it. I thought it was the location of the course which made it special, being held in a country house rather than in a university. They were doing some building work on part of the house though, so it wasn''t always easy to concentrate. I enjoyed the workshop most. How about you?</p><p>Charles: Well, we were divided into six small groups for the afternoon, each with their own advisor. That was the best bit for me, having that discussion with an expert. When we got into pairs to start writing our songs, the idea was to combine people with different levels of knowledge and experience. But my partner preferred working on her own generally.</p><p>Lorna: And what did you think of the course content?</p><p>Charles: I hadn''t realised before how important the structure of a song is to its success.- Lorna: Yes, we learned it''s no use writing the most beautiful words and melodies if there''s no shape. And we learned about the importance of a chorus in a song. I knew about that stuff from my singing classes. It''s a bit obvious, isn''t it?</p><p>Charles: No, I suppose so. It was the input on lyrical styles and forms that I found tedious. You could always look them up on the web. Anyway, how did you get on with writing your song?</p><p>Lorna: It was tough and peggy, my partner, was hard work, which wasn''t funny. And writing was a slow process as a result of our lack of communication. By the time we finished, I was exhausted. Writing music from the heart is like that, especially by th' AS nvarchar(max))+N'e end of a course.</p><p>Lorna: Well, that''s got the report sorted. Thanks. Have you decided which teacher''s workshops you''re going to do next term?</p><p>Charles: I want to do Bob Lacey''s. He''s got a brilliant reputation for voice coaching, you know, getting us to use dialects and different pronunciation and stuff. He gets you to do things like weed out a newspaper article in character. It''s amazing.</p><p>Lorna: I''m thinking of going to Evelyn Chanse''s workshop.</p><p>Charles: Oh Evelyn, she''s quite a character. Is she running the stage fights course again?</p><p>Lorna: Yes, fencing and pretend punches and throwing techniques. She really knows how to help actors use the whole of the space available.</p><p>Charles: Lots of people like Ted Winters'' workshop. He knows how to deliver. He''s keen on actors conveying emotion through their bodies and faces alone. It''s a great way to reveal complex relationships and situations to an audience without making a speech of any kind.</p><p>Lorna: I''m definitely going to sign up for that one.</p><p>Charles: If you want to understand a director''s ideas, Kevin Gray''s workshop would be perfect for you. He likes telling people what to do and he''s always saying, you can''t have a company of actors reading a play anyway they want because they''d end up fighting amongst themselves. They must listen to the director.</p><p>Lorna: What about Dorothy Thomas? She''s good at activities for getting rid of stress and emptying your mind of all thoughts about the past and that can really help when acting.</p><p>Charles: Well, I think I''ll put my name down for...</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Lorna: Hi Charles, I''m writing my reports on the songwriting course for drama students. You know, the one week residential course for drama students from different universities where you learn about writing songs. You did that course last year didn''t you? Could you give me some tips for the report? I''m having a few problems with it.</p><p>Charles: Yes, it was great. Well Lorna, you could start by thinking about what you got out of it. Evaluating how useful it was is simple. The challenge is not to go over length. Referring to published sources might help. That would make it more scholarly too.</p><p>Lorna: Okay, I''ll go with that. And saying why you did the songwriting course is important. Some people just do it as another string to their bow to help with auditions for work and things. I thought it was a good opportunity to try out new techniques for communicating feelings and emotions. I was already confident about singing before I did it. I thought it was the location of the course which made it special, being held in a country house rather than in a university. They were doing some building work on part of the house though, so it wasn''t always easy to concentrate. I enjoyed the workshop most. How about you?</p><p>Charles: Well, we were divided into six small groups for the afternoon, each with their own advisor. That was the best bit for me, having that discussion with an expert. When we got into pairs to start writing our songs, the idea was to combine people with different levels of knowledge and experience. But my partner preferred working on her own generally.</p><p>Lorna: And what did you think of the course content?</p><p>Charles: I hadn''t realised before how important the structure of a song is to its success.- Lorna: Yes, we learned it''s no use writing the most beautiful words and melodies if there''s no shape. And we learned about the importance of a chorus in a song. I knew about that stuff from my singing classes. It''s a bit obvious, isn''t it?</p><p>Charles: No, I suppose so. It was the input on lyrical styles and forms that I found tedious. You could always look them up on the web. Anyway, how did you get on with writing your song?</p><p>Lorna: It was tough and peggy, my partner, was hard work, which wasn''t funny. And writing was a slow process as a result of our lack of communication. By the time we finished, I was exhausted. Writing music from the heart is like that, especially by the end of a course.</p><p>Lorna: Well, that''s got the re' AS nvarchar(max))+N'port sorted. Thanks. Have you decided which teacher''s workshops you''re going to do next term?</p><p>Charles: I want to do Bob Lacey''s. He''s got a brilliant reputation for voice coaching, you know, getting us to use dialects and different pronunciation and stuff. He gets you to do things like weed out a newspaper article in character. It''s amazing.</p><p>Lorna: I''m thinking of going to Evelyn Chanse''s workshop.</p><p>Charles: Oh Evelyn, she''s quite a character. Is she running the stage fights course again?</p><p>Lorna: Yes, fencing and pretend punches and throwing techniques. She really knows how to help actors use the whole of the space available.</p><p>Charles: Lots of people like Ted Winters'' workshop. He knows how to deliver. He''s keen on actors conveying emotion through their bodies and faces alone. It''s a great way to reveal complex relationships and situations to an audience without making a speech of any kind.</p><p>Lorna: I''m definitely going to sign up for that one.</p><p>Charles: If you want to understand a director''s ideas, Kevin Gray''s workshop would be perfect for you. He likes telling people what to do and he''s always saying, you can''t have a company of actors reading a play anyway they want because they''d end up fighting amongst themselves. They must listen to the director.</p><p>Lorna: What about Dorothy Thomas? She''s good at activities for getting rid of stress and emptying your mind of all thoughts about the past and that can really help when acting.</p><p>Charles: Well, I think I''ll put my name down for...</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-1/iL.VOL1.10.S1503.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1503),
(N'e9c65fe3-6708-54ed-84e2-95ee63b5615b',N'0fc6f910-95d8-5e8b-973c-6d98445c20d9',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A,B </span>or<span> C.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Song-writing course for drama students',1,0,1,NULL,0,1503),
(N'74cc2877-0747-5ee6-a70a-8e3b194e0de5',N'e9c65fe3-6708-54ed-84e2-95ee63b5615b',0,3,19,N'<p>What problem is Lorna having with her report?</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là B</p><p>- Charles nói rõ vấn đề chính của Lorna không phải là nội dung mà là “not to go over length”, tức khó giữ bài trong giới hạn số từ</p><p>- Key word:+ go over length: (verb phrase) vượt quá độ dài quy định+ report: (noun) báo cáo</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1503),
(N'74a59e2c-842e-5bed-b162-dc04a742f635',N'e9c65fe3-6708-54ed-84e2-95ee63b5615b',0,3,19,N'<p>Why did Charles take the song-writing course?</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là C</p><p>- Charles cho biết anh tham gia khóa học để thử các cách mới thể hiện cảm xúc và cảm xúc cá nhân, tức phát triển khả năng tự biểu đạt, không phải vì việc làm hay sự tự tin</p><p>- Key word:+ communicating: (verb) truyền đạt+ emotions: (noun) cảm xúc</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1503),
(N'a480f8af-21fc-52c1-8492-003b9f226170',N'e9c65fe3-6708-54ed-84e2-95ee63b5615b',0,3,19,N'<p>Which type of work did Lorna most enjoy?</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là C</p><p>- Charles đánh giá cao nhất hoạt động làm việc theo nhóm nhỏ có cố vấn, cho thấy làm việc nhóm là hình thức anh thích nhất</p><p>- Key word:+ groups: (noun) nhóm+ discussion: (noun) thảo luận</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1503),
(N'06f180ed-fceb-51ad-80b2-e762da4ae26a',N'e9c65fe3-6708-54ed-84e2-95ee63b5615b',0,3,19,N'<p>Which aspect of the course content did both students find interesting?</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là A</p><p>- Cả hai cùng đề cập và đồng thuận rằng cấu trúc bài hát là điểm quan trọng và đáng chú ý nhất trong nội dung khóa học</p><p>- Key word:+ structure: (noun) cấu trúc+ song: (noun) bài hát</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1503),
(N'3ebcae4b-ae35-59e0-9ca9-a0ef491cf861',N'e9c65fe3-6708-54ed-84e2-95ee63b5615b',0,3,19,N'<p>Lorna found writing a song</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là B</p><p>- Lorna mô tả quá trình viết bài hát là mệt mỏi, kiệt sức, cho thấy cô cảm thấy việc viết bài là “tiring” chứ không phải thú vị hay bất khả thi</p><p>- Key word:+ exhausted: (adjective) kiệt sức+ hard work: (noun phrase) vất v</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1503),
(N'2e3ca8e8-c32b-5ecd-8827-4b87dd7753f6',N'0fc6f910-95d8-5e8b-973c-6d98445c20d9',0,2,18,N'<p>What is the focus of the drama workshop run by each of the following teachers?</p><p>Choose FIVE answers from the box and write the correct letter, A-H, next to questions 26-30.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Focus of drama workshop',15,0,2,NULL,0,1503),
(N'86163bab-d941-5558-95e6-dfa7a33f2850',N'2e3ca8e8-c32b-5ecd-8827-4b87dd7753f6',0,3,19,N'<p>Bob Lacey</p>',N'<p> Đáp án là G</p><p>- Bob Lacey tập trung vào việc sử dụng các phương ngữ và cách phát âm khác nhau, đúng với “using a range of accents”</p><p>- Key word:+ dialects: (noun) phương ngữ+ pronunciation: (noun) phát âm</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1503),
(N'4c261827-92c8-5ee6-b681-ef80b2be8d0c',N'2e3ca8e8-c32b-5ecd-8827-4b87dd7753f6',0,3,19,N'<p>Evelyn Chance</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là B</p><p>- Evelyn Chance giúp diễn viên sử dụng toàn bộ không gian sân khấu thông qua các hoạt động đấu kiếm và chuyển động</p><p>- Key word:+ space: (noun) không gian+ stage: (noun) sân khấu</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1503),
(N'a134fee5-b475-5cb5-8dce-9e645898f7e8',N'2e3ca8e8-c32b-5ecd-8827-4b87dd7753f6',0,3,19,N'<p>Ted Winter</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là D</p><p>- Ted Winter tập trung vào việc thể hiện nhân vật thông qua biểu đạt cơ thể và nét mặt</p><p>- Key word:+ bodies: (noun) cơ thể+ expression: (noun) biểu đạt</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1503),
(N'77bccd44-761e-5199-a54a-bbcea00181bb',N'2e3ca8e8-c32b-5ecd-8827-4b87dd7753f6',0,3,19,N'<p>Kevin Gray</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là H</p><p>- Kevin Gray nhấn mạnh việc diễn viên phải làm theo chỉ dẫn của đạo diễn, tức “following instructions”</p><p>- Key word:+ director: (noun) đạo diễn+ instructions: (noun) chỉ dẫn</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1503),
(N'46d5c78d-724b-55d7-bab4-badcfa0ded63',N'2e3ca8e8-c32b-5ecd-8827-4b87dd7753f6',0,3,19,N'<p>Dorothy Thomas</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là C</p><p>- Dorothy Thomas tập trung vào các kỹ thuật thư giãn và giải tỏa căng thẳng, phù hợp với “relaxation techniques”</p><p>- Key word:+ stress: (noun) căng thẳng+ relaxation: (noun) thư giãn</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1503),
(N'0968f553-273b-59cb-9033-a3fec83741c3',N'f24f244d-5fb2-522a-aa5f-64c5876eb899',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2> [VOL 1] The Influence of Children on Adult Diet Background</h2><p>Today, we continue with our series of lectures on diet and society in the 21st century.</p><p>We are all aware of the obesity epidemic facing our society. The reasons for this are well documented and are mainly due to poor eating habits coupled with too little exercise.</p><p>In fact, a national health and nutrition survey here in the U.S. concluded that the average total food intake increased considerably between the years 1971 and 2000. This appears to be a general trend across most age groups.</p><p>In fact, apart from the over 60s, all areas of American society are consistently above the dietary guidelines for caloric intake and saturated fat. However, total fat consumption is highest for teenagers and there is a clear correlation with fast food consumption. </p><p>Among the wide range of factors influencing and individuals dietary choices, one of the strongest influences is the home.</p><p>It will come as no surprise that within a family the fat intakes of husbands and wives and parents and children who cohabit are remarkably similar.</p><p>Many people assume that this relationship indicates the influence of parents on their children, but we wanted to ask if children influence poor diets in adults. This hypothesis had not been tested in a national sample until now.</p><p>In our study, we focused only on adults and we set our lower age limit at 17 and the upper one at 65.</p><p>The key variable was the presence or absence of children, so we identified only those adults whose children were under 17.</p><p>Other variables included the age of the adult, level of education they had reached, their ethnic background, their income, and foreign-born status.</p><p>To obtain our data, first we conducted interviews in the respondents'' own homes, and all in all we interviewed 6,600 adults aged over 17.</p><p>Then we invited them to mobile examination centers where surveys were conducted.</p><p>From this information, we were able to focus not only on the total fat intake, but also on the person''s total caloric consumption.</p><p>Our results show that the presence of children led to significantly higher levels of both fat consumption and saturated fat in adults.</p><p>We found on average that adults with children in the home ate 4.9 grams per day of fat more than adults without children.</p><p>This result is consistent irrespective of race, gender, or age.</p><p>The foods more commonly were conve' AS nvarchar(max))+N'nience items high in fat or sugar, including pizza, salty snacks, and ice cream.</p><p>Interestingly, there is a significantly higher percentage of adults in this group who drink milk.</p><p>There are many explanations for this increased fat intake.</p><p>Hectic schedules where parents try to balance work, family, and leisure put time at a premium.</p><p>Another issue is children''s preference for fatty foods or foods with a high sugar content.</p><p>Fast foods and ready to eat foods are convenient choices.</p><p>It is inconvenient to prepare different meals for both the children and the parents.</p><p>So it is likely that adults consume the same as their children.</p><p>Of course, there were limitations to our study.</p><p>One of these is that we did not take into account the number of minors in each household, which could have an effect on the adult who feeds the children.</p><p>In addition, this study does not take into account the relationship of the adult to the minor.</p><p>We could also postulate that the older a child gets, the more independent he or she becomes, and so the influence a child has on an adult may lessen depending on the age of the child.</p><p>In terms of recommendations as a result of this study, we would like to see more research conducted to overcome the limitations outlined earlier.</p><p>We also feel there is a need for even further research into the influence that friends can have on our fat intake given the clear link we''ve established between family members.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Today, we continue with our series of lectures on diet and society in the 21st century.</p><p>We are all aware of the obesity epidemic facing our society. The reasons for this are well documented and are mainly due to poor eating habits coupled with too little exercise.</p><p>In fact, a national health and nutrition survey here in the U.S. concluded that the average total food intake increased considerably between the years 1971 and 2000. This appears to be a general trend across most age groups.</p><p>In fact, apart from the over 60s, all areas of American society are consistently above the dietary guidelines for caloric intake and saturated fat. However, total fat consumption is highest for teenagers and there is a clear correlation with fast food consumption. </p><p>Among the wide range of factors influencing and individuals dietary choices, one of the strongest influences is the home.</p><p>It will come as no surprise that within a family the fat intakes of husbands and wives and parents and children who cohabit are remarkably similar.</p><p>Many people assume that this relationship indicates the influence of parents on their children, but we wanted to ask if children influence poor diets in adults. This hypothesis had not been tested in a national sample until now.</p><p>In our study, we focused only on adults and we set our lower age limit at 17 and the upper one at 65.</p><p>The key variable was the presence or absence of children, so we identified only those adults whose children were under 17.</p><p>Other variables included the age of the adult, level of education they had reached, their ethnic background, their income, and foreign-born status.</p><p>To obtain our data, first we conducted interviews in the respondents'' own homes, and all in all we interviewed 6,600 adults aged over 17.</p><p>Then we invited them to mobile examination centers where surveys were conducted.</p><p>From this information, we were able to focus not only on the total fat intake, but also on the person''s total caloric consumption.</p><p>Our results show that the presence of children led to significantly higher levels of both fat consumption and saturated fat in adults.</p><p>We found on average that adults with children in the home ate 4.9 grams per day of fat more than adults without children.</p><p>This result is consistent irrespective of race, gender, or age.</p><p>The foods more commonly were convenience items high in fat or sugar, including pizza, salty snacks, an' AS nvarchar(max))+N'd ice cream.</p><p>Interestingly, there is a significantly higher percentage of adults in this group who drink milk.</p><p>There are many explanations for this increased fat intake.</p><p>Hectic schedules where parents try to balance work, family, and leisure put time at a premium.</p><p>Another issue is children''s preference for fatty foods or foods with a high sugar content.</p><p>Fast foods and ready to eat foods are convenient choices.</p><p>It is inconvenient to prepare different meals for both the children and the parents.</p><p>So it is likely that adults consume the same as their children.</p><p>Of course, there were limitations to our study.</p><p>One of these is that we did not take into account the number of minors in each household, which could have an effect on the adult who feeds the children.</p><p>In addition, this study does not take into account the relationship of the adult to the minor.</p><p>We could also postulate that the older a child gets, the more independent he or she becomes, and so the influence a child has on an adult may lessen depending on the age of the child.</p><p>In terms of recommendations as a result of this study, we would like to see more research conducted to overcome the limitations outlined earlier.</p><p>We also feel there is a need for even further research into the influence that friends can have on our fat intake given the clear link we''ve established between family members.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-1/iL.VOL1.10.S1503.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1503),
(N'00c1df32-d05d-51a6-aea7-3a5dcf4009cc',N'0968f553-273b-59cb-9033-a3fec83741c3',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.</p><p>Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'The Influence of Children on Adult Diet',11,0,1,NULL,0,1503),
