axi to take them to the forest hotel after the party. Is that something you can sort out?</p><p>Absolutely.</p><p>Anything else?</p><p>I think that''s all for now.</p><p>Okay. Well, just get in touch if you think of anything else. Now, if I can just take some contact details, I just need your name and phone number.</p><p>I''m Maria Nelson and my numbers 07749132760.</p><p>Excellent. We look forward to seeing you soon.</p><p><span><span>That is the end of Section 1. You now have half a minute to check your answers</span></span></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-1/iL.VOL1.8.S1502.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1502),
(N'7fdee7d1-c2e7-5a0d-8402-5c9dfc0f38aa',N'cc5dffc0-45e6-5b1e-827f-3b264c37afbc',0,2,18,N'<p>Write <span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Party Reservation',11,0,1,NULL,0,1502),
(N'68d431cf-7e88-5d16-860b-11178589703a',N'7fdee7d1-c2e7-5a0d-8402-5c9dfc0f38aa',0,3,19,N'<h3>Party Reservation</h3><p>Example</p><p>Number of guests for the <span>party</span> - 60 people</p><p>Number of guests for the <span>party</span> - 60 people</p><p>Starting time of the party: }{SPACE}{ pm.</p><p><span>Menu options:</span></p><p>the standard ($25)</p><ul><li><p>the</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ ($35)</p><p>the gold ($50)</p><p>each course has some }{SPACE}{ option</p><p>one guest is allergic to }{SPACE}{ </p><p>other requests: desserts and }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là 7.45</p><p>- Trong đoạn hội thoại, khi được hỏi về thời gian bắt đầu buổi tiệc, người đặt phòng trực tiếp nói “7.45?”, đây là thông tin xác định rõ thời gian bắt đầu và không có phương án nào khác được đưa ra sau đó</p><p>- Key word:+ time: (noun) thời gian+ start: (verb) bắt đầu</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1502),
(N'14ea4d63-4548-53ef-a520-e2a24188cc67',N'7fdee7d1-c2e7-5a0d-8402-5c9dfc0f38aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 2',N'<p>- Đáp án là 3 courses</p><p>- Người phục vụ giới thiệu rõ “the celebration menu which is three courses” và người đặt tiệc đồng ý chọn phương án này, do đó số lượng món ăn là ba món</p><p>- Key word:+ menu: (noun) thực đơn+ course: (noun) món ăn trong bữa</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1502),
(N'699f05fc-4341-5db8-8d68-29086924d7ee',N'7fdee7d1-c2e7-5a0d-8402-5c9dfc0f38aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 3',N'<p>- Đáp án là vegetarian</p><p>- Khi được hỏi về khách không ăn thịt, nhà hàng xác nhận sẽ chuẩn bị “a vegetarian option for each course”, cho thấy lựa chọn ăn chay được cung cấp</p><p>- Key word:+ vegetarian: (adjective) ăn chay+ option: (noun) lựa chọn</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1502),
(N'43556556-dcd4-5fd5-a67b-6a9b591d6246',N'7fdee7d1-c2e7-5a0d-8402-5c9dfc0f38aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 4',N'<p>- Đáp án là strawberries</p><p>- Người nói nhấn mạnh một vị khách “very allergic to strawberries”, vì vậy nhà hàng cần tránh hoàn toàn loại thực phẩm này</p><p>- Key word:+ allergic: (adjective) dị ứng+ strawberries: (noun) dâu tây</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,4,NULL,0,1502),
(N'b7f52ea6-5e44-561f-b444-e0a84643fee1',N'7fdee7d1-c2e7-5a0d-8402-5c9dfc0f38aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 5',N'<p>- Đáp án là cheese</p><p>- Trong phần yêu cầu về món tráng miệng, người đặt tiệc hỏi trực tiếp “could we have cheese as well?”, thể hiện rõ mong muốn có thêm phô mai</p><p>- Key word:+ dessert: (noun) món tráng miệng+ cheese: (noun) phô mai</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,5,NULL,0,1502),
(N'd00ccbe8-80b2-55ff-9a20-16be47c0f066',N'cc5dffc0-45e6-5b1e-827f-3b264c37afbc',0,2,18,N'<p>Write<span> ONLY ONE WORD</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,2,NULL,0,1502),
(N'798d48ab-f0eb-59f1-9973-7fcafbe54b7d',N'd00ccbe8-80b2-55ff-9a20-16be47c0f066',0,3,19,N'<p>entertainment: book a }{SPACE}{ after dinner</p><p>room decoration: pink }{SPACE}{ </p><p>Special request: write '' }{SPACE}{ Susie'' on the birthday cake</p><p>Book a }{SPACE}{ to take guests to the Forest Hotel</p><p>Phone number }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là Disco</p><p>- Khi bàn về giải trí sau bữa tối, người đặt tiệc lựa chọn “a disco” thay vì ban nhạc, và nhà hàng đồng ý sắp xếp</p><p>- Key word:+ disco: (noun) nhạc disco / tiệc nhảy+ entertainment: (noun) giải trí</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,6,NULL,0,1502),
(N'd2574730-91e4-58d9-be89-bebb5699b447',N'd00ccbe8-80b2-55ff-9a20-16be47c0f066',0,3,19,N'Question number 7',N'<p>- Đáp án là flowers</p><p>- Dù nhà hàng đề xuất bóng bay, người đặt tiệc nói rõ sẽ chọn “flowers to balloons”, nên trang trí phòng bằng hoa</p><p>- Key word:+ decorate: (verb) trang trí+ flowers: (noun) hoa</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,7,NULL,0,1502),
(N'b9ffcbcc-01d4-55d6-899a-cefa154753d2',N'd00ccbe8-80b2-55ff-9a20-16be47c0f066',0,3,19,N'Question number 8',N'<p>- Đáp án là congratulations</p><p>- Khi được hỏi về thông điệp trên bánh sinh nhật, người nói nêu trực tiếp từ “Congratulations” trước khi thêm tên, nên đây là nội dung cần điền</p><p>- Key word:+ message: (noun) thông điệp+ congratulations: (noun) lời chúc mừng</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,8,NULL,0,1502),
(N'b486eacb-1082-5d54-9473-633a5420cead',N'd00ccbe8-80b2-55ff-9a20-16be47c0f066',0,3,19,N'Question number 9',N'<p>- Đáp án là taxi</p><p>- Đoạn hội thoại cho biết một số khách cần “a taxi” để di chuyển sau bữa tiệc, và nhà hàng xác nhận có thể sắp xếp</p><p>- Key word:+ taxi: (noun) xe taxi+ transport: (noun) phương tiện di chuyển</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,1502),
(N'ed9dee6a-1794-53b2-bb99-3936eb63f6e3',N'd00ccbe8-80b2-55ff-9a20-16be47c0f066',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>- Đáp án là 07749 132760</p><p>- Ở cuối cuộc gọi, người đặt tiệc cung cấp số điện thoại liên hệ, đây là thông tin được ghi lại làm chi tiết liên lạc</p><p>- Key word:+ phone number: (noun) số điện thoại+ contact: (noun) liên hệ</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1502),
(N'f09f7f79-77a1-5e6f-97d9-f52ff0fadeb1',N'45fb961b-2e39-555a-8180-2b2ea6af1309',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 1] extrahands.com</h2><p><span><span>You will hear a radio program in which a man called Steven talks about an organization called extrahands.com. First you have some time to look at questions 11 to 15. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 15</span></span></p><p>Speaker A: Stephen Jennings has been involved with an intriguing organization called extrahands.com. I''ll let him explain.</p><p>Stephen: Thanks. Yes, extrahands.com was started 15 years ago by two New Zealanders who had bought an old farm building in some land. They wanted to go organic, but they realized they''d need help clearing the fields for cultivation, and they just didn''t have the money to pay for it. But they knew lots of people wanted to visit New Zealand, so they set up a website and advertised for volunteers to come and help on the farm there. Anyway, they got lots of volunteers, and they got their farm going, and extrahands.com was born.</p><p>Stephen: Today the organization matches people who need help with people who are willing to work as a way of seeing the world. You can either be a host if you need assistance or a volunteer if you''re offering to work, in either case, to become a member you just fill out a form on the extrahands.com website and pay the $25 subscription. You only have to do that once, but for your membership to stay active, every 12 months you have to go back to the website and check the details on your form there, adding information about any help you requested or work you did over that period.</p><p>Stephen: So how does it work? Well, if a host has some work that needs to be done, he or she goes to the extrahands.com website and posts a description of the work. So the way most volunteers access these details on the website is by specifying the country or region they''re interested in working in, but a few search according to how long they want to work for. Not many people have strong feelings about what sort of work they want to do, they''re mostly fairly open about it.</p><p>Stephen: If you''re interested in volunteering for a particular job, you can email your host to arrange the dates and establish things like accommodation and hours of work. It''s all fairly informal. There are no legal documents or anything like that, but you do both need to have some sort of written record of what''s been agreed before you actually arrive.</p><p>Stephen: What about problems? Well, there are too many members for the organization to be able to investigate them ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'all, but there is an online message board where members can post details of any difficulties they''ve had, things like unsatisfactory accommodation or volunteers who haven''t done the work agreed, and this works very well.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the program you have some time to look at questions 16 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 16 to 20</span></span></p><p>Speaker A: I know you''ve personally had lots of extra hands.com placements, Stephen, tell us about them. Stephen Well, my first placement was in Portugal where I helped a local guy with a guidebook he''d written in English. I read what he''d written and corrected the English and made some suggestions for improving it. I was very pleased with it when I finished and I learned lots about the country.</p><p>Stephen: The next placement was on the other side of the world in Australia. I was on a farm in the middle of nowhere, and since I''m pretty good in the kitchen, they had me fixing meals for 30 sheep''s shears. I stayed two months, and it was a lot of fun, but I can tell you I was glad to get back home to California and just lie on a beach and relax. Stephen OK, then, after a well-deserved rest, I did a job in South Korea for a small company that wanted an English speaker to show visiting Americans around. They gave me some books to read, and I learned as I worked. I loved it, and I developed a real taste for Korean food.</p><p>Stephen: My most difficult job was for an older lady in the USA. She had a gigantic house and grounds, but she couldn''t maintain them all herself, so I worked for her digging and planting flowers and stuff around her swimming pool. And she grew tons of vegetables too. She was nice, but she fed me hot dogs morning, noon, and night.</p><p>Stephen: Now my most recent placement was last summer in Italy. I helped a family add a room onto their house. I didn''t speak Italian or know the first thing about building, but the husband had an English Italian phrasebook, and together with sign language showed me exactly what to do. And the wife''s cooking was out of this world.</p><p>Speaker A: thanks Steven and if you want more information about</p><p><span><span>That is the end of part 2 you. Now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part two now turn to Section 3</span></span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span><span>You will hear a radio program in which a man called Steven talks about an organization called extrahands.com. First you have some time to look at questions 11 to 15. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 15</span></span></p><p>Speaker A: Stephen Jennings has been involved with an intriguing organization called extrahands.com. I''ll let him explain.</p><p>Stephen: Thanks. Yes, extrahands.com was started 15 years ago by two New Zealanders who had bought an old farm building in some land. They wanted to go organic, but they realized they''d need help clearing the fields for cultivation, and they just didn''t have the money to pay for it. But they knew lots of people wanted to visit New Zealand, so they set up a website and advertised for volunteers to come and help on the farm there. Anyway, they got lots of volunteers, and they got their farm going, and extrahands.com was born.</p><p>Stephen: Today the organization matches people who need help with people who are willing to work as a way of seeing the world. You can either be a host if you need assistance or a volunteer if you''re offering to work, in either case, to become a member you just fill out a form on the extrahands.com website and pay the $25 subscription. You only have to do that once, but for your membership to stay active, every 12 months you have to go back to the website and check the details on your form there, adding information about any help you requested or work you did over that period.</p><p>Stephen: So how does it work? Well, if a host has some work that needs to be done, he or she goes to the extrahands.com website and posts a description of the work. So the way most volunteers access these details on the website is by specifying the country or region they''re interested in working in, but a few search according to how long they want to work for. Not many people have strong feelings about what sort of work they want to do, they''re mostly fairly open about it.</p><p>Stephen: If you''re interested in volunteering for a particular job, you can email your host to arrange the dates and establish things like accommodation and hours of work. It''s all fairly informal. There are no legal documents or anything like that, but you do both need to have some sort of written record of what''s been agreed before you actually arrive.</p><p>Stephen: What about problems? Well, there are too many members for the organization to be able to investigate them all, but there is an online mes' AS nvarchar(max))+N'sage board where members can post details of any difficulties they''ve had, things like unsatisfactory accommodation or volunteers who haven''t done the work agreed, and this works very well.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the program you have some time to look at questions 16 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 16 to 20</span></span></p><p>Speaker A: I know you''ve personally had lots of extra hands.com placements, Stephen, tell us about them. Stephen Well, my first placement was in Portugal where I helped a local guy with a guidebook he''d written in English. I read what he''d written and corrected the English and made some suggestions for improving it. I was very pleased with it when I finished and I learned lots about the country.</p><p>Stephen: The next placement was on the other side of the world in Australia. I was on a farm in the middle of nowhere, and since I''m pretty good in the kitchen, they had me fixing meals for 30 sheep''s shears. I stayed two months, and it was a lot of fun, but I can tell you I was glad to get back home to California and just lie on a beach and relax. Stephen OK, then, after a well-deserved rest, I did a job in South Korea for a small company that wanted an English speaker to show visiting Americans around. They gave me some books to read, and I learned as I worked. I loved it, and I developed a real taste for Korean food.</p><p>Stephen: My most difficult job was for an older lady in the USA. She had a gigantic house and grounds, but she couldn''t maintain them all herself, so I worked for her digging and planting flowers and stuff around her swimming pool. And she grew tons of vegetables too. She was nice, but she fed me hot dogs morning, noon, and night.</p><p>Stephen: Now my most recent placement was last summer in Italy. I helped a family add a room onto their house. I didn''t speak Italian or know the first thing about building, but the husband had an English Italian phrasebook, and together with sign language showed me exactly what to do. And the wife''s cooking was out of this world.</p><p>Speaker A: thanks Steven and if you want more information about</p><p><span><span>That is the end of part 2 you. Now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part two now turn to Section 3</span></span></p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-1/iL.VOL1.8.S1502.PART2.mp3',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1502),
