(N'7c683eff-766a-53d5-9832-1a6427f3bc58',N'52cf6ded-8567-5555-8c69-0fb91a56d7df',0,3,19,N'Question number 20',N'<p>- Đáp án là lockers. </p><p>- Bảo tàng cung cấp tủ gửi đồ miễn phí cho khách. </p><p>- Key word: </p><p>+ free (adj) miễn phí </p><p>+ lockers (n) tủ gửi đồ</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1445),
(N'6ab33037-6310-57df-a174-c10ca30f2324',N'19ee5f3d-e88c-5f0a-9c2c-f78db1a0162a',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Archaeology Course</h2><p>Good morning. Come in. Your Paul are you? Yes, I spoke to you on the phone. Yes, have a seat. You wanted to talk to me about the archaeology course. Yes, I have read the handbook, but I would like to find out a few more details before I decide whether to do it. Right, yes. What would you like to know? Well, first of all, can I combine the archaeology course with one in anthropology? Yes, you can combine it with any other subject apart from classical history. That is simply because there is some overlap in the lecture times for those two courses. We were not able to coordinate them. Okay, fine. And could you tell me about the modules? Well, in the first semester there are three. All of them are compulsory. We do not offer optional modules till next year. Right. &nbsp;   </p><p>The first one focuses on what can be learned from specific artifacts, such as pottery and stone tools. It is called Object Matters, and it is taught by Dr Morris. How is the module presented? I mean, is it lectures? We refer to the means of presentation as the learning method. And in this case, it is lectures integrated with practical sessions, so it is a mixture. What about the content? I suppose we will be looking at different kinds of archaeological remains, and how to date them and so on. To some extent, but the module is basically about processes. First of all, recording material, then classification, then interpretation of the data. That is how archaeologists draw conclusions about their findings. And finally, display. Is that okay? I think so. Yes, thanks. One other thing, how is the module assessed? Is there an exam? No, it is all based on coursework. &nbsp;  </p><p>Okay. And the second module, that is the one called Towns and Cities, is taught by our department head Professor Elliott. And as the name suggests, the modules about the origins of built environments and how they developed. That is mainly factual, then I suppose. It is really. And for that reason, the assessments by examination. But you may be pleased to know it is an oral rather than a written exam. And it is a bit scary. Most of our students find they actually enjoy it, so do not worry too much. Okay.  And then the title of the third module is Method and Science. And in that, Dr Thompson will be introducing you to the standard techniques used in archaeological fieldwork and analysis. Things like excavating and dating. What about the learning method for this module? Are there any lect' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ures or is it all laboratory work? Oh, it is half lab work and half seminars. There are not any lectures. Then right at the end of the module, you will take part in a site survey. The date for that is week beginning the 10th of March. But I cannot tell you the location yet. That will be announced later. But I think you will find it very useful. Yes, I know someone who went on that one.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Good morning. Come in. Your Paul are you? Yes, I spoke to you on the phone. Yes, have a seat. You wanted to talk to me about the archaeology course. Yes, I have read the handbook, but I would like to find out a few more details before I decide whether to do it. Right, yes. What would you like to know? Well, first of all, can I combine the archaeology course with one in anthropology? Yes, you can combine it with any other subject apart from classical history. That is simply because there is some overlap in the lecture times for those two courses. We were not able to coordinate them. Okay, fine. And could you tell me about the modules? Well, in the first semester there are three. All of them are compulsory. We do not offer optional modules till next year. Right. &nbsp;   </p><p>The first one focuses on what can be learned from specific artifacts, such as pottery and stone tools. It is called Object Matters, and it is taught by Dr Morris. How is the module presented? I mean, is it lectures? We refer to the means of presentation as the learning method. And in this case, it is lectures integrated with practical sessions, so it is a mixture. What about the content? I suppose we will be looking at different kinds of archaeological remains, and how to date them and so on. To some extent, but the module is basically about processes. First of all, recording material, then classification, then interpretation of the data. That is how archaeologists draw conclusions about their findings. And finally, display. Is that okay? I think so. Yes, thanks. One other thing, how is the module assessed? Is there an exam? No, it is all based on coursework. &nbsp;  </p><p>Okay. And the second module, that is the one called Towns and Cities, is taught by our department head Professor Elliott. And as the name suggests, the modules about the origins of built environments and how they developed. That is mainly factual, then I suppose. It is really. And for that reason, the assessments by examination. But you may be pleased to know it is an oral rather than a written exam. And it is a bit scary. Most of our students find they actually enjoy it, so do not worry too much. Okay.  And then the title of the third module is Method and Science. And in that, Dr Thompson will be introducing you to the standard techniques used in archaeological fieldwork and analysis. Things like excavating and dating. What about the learning method for this module? Are there any lectures or is it all laborator' AS nvarchar(max))+N'y work? Oh, it is half lab work and half seminars. There are not any lectures. Then right at the end of the module, you will take part in a site survey. The date for that is week beginning the 10th of March. But I cannot tell you the location yet. That will be announced later. But I think you will find it very useful. Yes, I know someone who went on that one.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-1/iL.VOL1.4.S1445.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1445),
