(N'dfdd82fd-d9a2-502d-9328-fd70ad17cf23',N'69d92cbe-ea3c-5366-b6d0-541c8c3b5c9c',0,3,19,N'<p>What is the new activity in the zoo in November?</p>',N'<p>+Phân tích câu hỏi: Câu hỏi hỏi hoạt động mới trong tháng 11, yêu cầu xác định chính xác cái nào mới được giới thiệu.
+Transcript (0:59–1:33): “…we are offering a two hour mini tour after 7pm. Seeing the animals at a different time of day will be a fascinating experience…”
+Giải thích: Người nói nhấn mạnh mini tour buổi tối sau 7h, đây là hoạt động mới trong tháng 11. Các đáp án khác được nhắc tới (bonfire nights, parties) nhưng bị loại: bonfire bị hủy do cháy, parties để tháng 12.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1056),
(N'3eaae4b0-2bdd-5eef-9d87-87ca1b32d300',N'69d92cbe-ea3c-5366-b6d0-541c8c3b5c9c',0,3,19,N'<p>What is the most popular species in the zoo?</p>',N'<p>+Phân tích câu hỏi: Câu hỏi yêu cầu xác định loài được yêu thích nhất trong sở thú.
+Transcript (1:42–1:54): “…the most popular animal around here is not the kangaroo or even the sheepdog. No, our star animal here is, without doubt, the koala.”
 +Giải thích: Người nói phủ định kangaroo và sheepdog, khẳng định rõ koala là loài nổi bật nhất.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1056),
(N'e6e96417-bf35-57e7-94f8-5d4524c0f7b3',N'69d92cbe-ea3c-5366-b6d0-541c8c3b5c9c',0,3,19,N'<p>What is the key to feeding kangaroos?</p>',N'<p>+Phân tích câu hỏi: Câu hỏi muốn biết điều quan trọng nhất khi cho kangaroo ăn.
+Transcript (2:24–2:38): “…we always tell our guests that it’s important to stand with an upright posture when giving them food. The kangaroos may interpret you bending over as a sign of aggression.”
+Giải thích: Chìa khóa là phải đứng thẳng khi cho ăn, nếu cúi xuống thì kangaroo có thể hiểu nhầm thành hành vi đe dọa
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1056),
(N'26c32963-35b1-5039-a4e1-7a414f3c5c78',N'69d92cbe-ea3c-5366-b6d0-541c8c3b5c9c',0,3,19,N'<p>Why is the wild dog area closed?</p>',N'<p>+Phân tích câu hỏi: Câu hỏi yêu cầu tìm nguyên nhân khu vực chó sói hoang đóng cửa.
+Transcript (3:02–3:28): “…our wild dog pen is temporarily closed… Right now, we are fixing the fences. They are wild animals after all and we need to consider public safety…”
 +Giải thích: Chuồng chó sói hoang tạm thời đóng cửa vì đang sửa chữa hàng rào, không phải do chó bệnh hay chưa nhập về.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1056),
(N'00f52814-322d-5362-95d4-7dbaf93c04ff',N'69d92cbe-ea3c-5366-b6d0-541c8c3b5c9c',0,3,19,N'<p>Where can visitors get discount tickets this year?</p>',N'<p>+Phân tích câu hỏi: Câu hỏi hỏi về nơi duy nhất khách có thể mua vé giảm giá năm nay.
+Transcript (3:28–3:50): “…the website is currently under maintenance, so the coupons are available only at the cafe near the gift shop for this year.”
+Giải thích: Trước đây bán ở nhiều nơi, nhưng năm nay chỉ còn quán café gần gift shop mới có bán vé giảm giá.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1056),
(N'0a22b165-e120-5f5d-b534-00a6ff843b8b',N'65bf275a-849a-5303-9617-bcee622b3a66',0,2,18,N'<p>Label the map below.
Write the correct letter,<span> A–G</span>, next to Question 16–20.</p><p><img src="https://ieltsroom.com/upload/images/ieltsadvancedtest/33e5a0edbb75fe30f390.jpg" alt="Question diagram"></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',12,0,2,NULL,0,1056),
(N'6c1811b6-f22e-5721-ba74-091134f45e72',N'0a22b165-e120-5f5d-b534-00a6ff843b8b',0,3,19,N'<p>Arena</p>',N'<p>+Transcript: Người hướng dẫn nói: nếu muốn xem động vật rrõ hơn thì nên đến Arena, nơi có các buổi biểu diễn mỗi nửa tiếng. Để đến đó, hãy băng qua cầu, đi thẳng tới cuối đường mòn và rẽ phải.</p><p>+Giải thích: Như vậy, vị trí cuối đường mòn bên phải tương ứng với vị trí C trên bản đồ.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1056),
(N'd8b8e20b-c5df-5a6f-b552-5f0c810c4f15',N'0a22b165-e120-5f5d-b534-00a6ff843b8b',0,3,19,N'<p>Educational hall</p>',N'<p>+Transcript: Người hướng dẫn nói: Với ai muốn tìm nơi yên tĩnh để học ở sở thú, ở phía Bắc có Educational Hall, rất dễ tìm, nằm ngay phía Tây.
 +Giải thích: Phía Bắc, gần toilet, hướng Tây trùng với ô E.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1056),
(N'03db24c1-3a46-52b1-9692-61740b71e675',N'0a22b165-e120-5f5d-b534-00a6ff843b8b',0,3,19,N'<p>Picnic spot</p>',N'<p>+Transcript: Thời tiết đẹp, rất lý tưởng để picnic và có một vị trí picnic quanh Botanic Garden (vườn thực vật), hướng ra sông, nơi có khung cảnh tuyệt đẹp.
+Giải thích: Nơi picnic vừa cạnh vườn thực vật, nhìn ra sông trùng đúng với ô A.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1056),
(N'10cf7e54-424a-5c35-b07e-5c3a174f3c90',N'0a22b165-e120-5f5d-b534-00a6ff843b8b',0,3,19,N'<p>Photo printing shop</p>',N'<p>+Transcript: Trước kia, “photo printing site” (nơi in ảnh) nằm ở góc Đông Bắc sở thú, giờ đã chuyển sang tòa nhà cạnh cây cầu.
+Giải thích: Bản đồ cho thấy tòa nhà gần cầu chính là D.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1056),
(N'e2c35de5-f813-5c1e-a4a2-b02454a1c3b6',N'0a22b165-e120-5f5d-b534-00a6ff843b8b',0,3,19,N'<p>Gift shop</p>',N'<p>+Transcript: Có nhiều trạm xe đạp miễn phí, chỉ cần đi xe 5 phút là đến gift shop (cửa hàng quà lưu niệm), nằm ngay cạnh lối ra.
+Giải thích: Gift shop sát cổng ra, cạnh toilet nên đúng với ô B.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1056),
(N'0bef8d86-b441-5e0d-a032-15b97f0174ce',N'4a2b4448-f61e-5c15-b3c1-52e8af7f8422',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Maori Carving</h2><p>Hi Mike. Aren''t these New Zealand carvings we''ve been studying in class fascinating? Oh hi Amy. Oh, the Maori greenstone tikis! They''re all a bit organic for my taste. You know, all curves and twisting shapes. I like modern stuff, geometric shapes, but I can see the skill involved in making them. But the stories behind the tikis are what make them interesting. Besides, I imagine it would be difficult to create geometric patterns. The shape of each tiki. Seems to follow the natural contours of the stone. It''s extremely hard, and it requires expertise and time to. That''s true, I suppose that''s why modern greenstone carvings are so expensive. But there aren''t that many genuine old ones around. Archaeologists rarely discover such treasures, which is odd given they are such a huge part of Maori culture. I wonder if archaeological dig sites were raided and the tikis were taken to sell. I believe it''s because the owners valued them and so preserved them. Many of them would have been passed down through the generations and remain in the possession of the families today, like heirlooms. Do you know what they were used for? I thought they were just an art form or a means of decoration. But Professor Matiu says that the Maori believed tikis were sacred and could be used as a pathway to their ancestors. They believed the dead could speak to them through these talismans. So they don''t represent tribes or anything. Interesting. How many different kinds are there? There must be hundreds of variation. There are other greenstone carvings beside tikis, although I can''t remember all their names. Traditional tikis themselves can be divided into two distinct groups, though. Can they? By the materials, I''ve seen some with metal features. Or is it to do with size or where they were made? Actually, it is the ways that their feet, heads, and hands are placed that make the major difference. Is it easy to discern the difference between the ancient stones and more modern reproductions? Are the styles very different? Modern reproductions usually use a different type of stone, although they may be of the same design. They may also be of a smaller size. But it''s actually quite easy to tell if you are buying a genuine carving or a mass-produced souvenir. How? Nowadays, they are produced by machine using lasers even. So, they have no blemishes. In genuine carvings, they aren''t always perfect. because the Maori used simple hand tools. For example, the hole ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'that the cord is fed through isn''t perfectly round. That makes sense. Actually, it''s amazing that they produce such detailed tikis with such simple tools and materials. Talk me through the process. I know the basic shape is carved from New Zealand greenstone. Yes, but first they had to get a blank of the right size. You mean a piece of stone of the right size and basic shape? That''s right. They used a hard stone tool, something rough, to wear a groove. into a piece of greenstone. After they''d scored it with that, they could just snap it in two. I guess they could have used some kind of hammer, though. They repeated the process until they had a piece of stone suitable for what they wanted to carved: usually flat pieces for tikis. Right, because they''re fairly small and quite thin. Then they started carving? Not right away. Before they could start carving, they needed an absolutely smooth, flat surface. Nowadays, when we want to do something like that, we''d clamp the stone in a vice and use a sander. But they used a heavy block of sandstone and rubbed the piece of greenstone on it over and over to prepare it for carving. It must have been very time-consuming. So how did they cut out the little details like hands and feet? They had carving tools. They used small chips of stone, glued and tied onto a wooden handle. Then they also used a piece of sharpened bone made into a point. Is that what they used to make the holes? Actually, they had a special tool to make holes. It was a stick tied between two heavy pebbles with a point at the end. They would wrap string around it, pull it back and forth to wear a hole into the tiki. They used the same technique to make round shapes too, but didn''t make the hole go all the way through. And I''ve seen some tikis with coloured Eyes or decorations. Are they painted? Mostly, they are inlaid with different coloured stone or mother of pearl from shells, or sometimes dyed pieces of bones. They were fixed in place using teratagum, a sticky plant resin. They used plants to make the natural strings too. That''s right. The Maori were very resourceful, weren''t they? They certainly were.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Hi Mike. Aren''t these New Zealand carvings we''ve been studying in class fascinating? Oh hi Amy. Oh, the Maori greenstone tikis! They''re all a bit organic for my taste. You know, all curves and twisting shapes. I like modern stuff, geometric shapes, but I can see the skill involved in making them. But the stories behind the tikis are what make them interesting. Besides, I imagine it would be difficult to create geometric patterns. The shape of each tiki. Seems to follow the natural contours of the stone. It''s extremely hard, and it requires expertise and time to. That''s true, I suppose that''s why modern greenstone carvings are so expensive. But there aren''t that many genuine old ones around. Archaeologists rarely discover such treasures, which is odd given they are such a huge part of Maori culture. I wonder if archaeological dig sites were raided and the tikis were taken to sell. I believe it''s because the owners valued them and so preserved them. Many of them would have been passed down through the generations and remain in the possession of the families today, like heirlooms. Do you know what they were used for? I thought they were just an art form or a means of decoration. But Professor Matiu says that the Maori believed tikis were sacred and could be used as a pathway to their ancestors. They believed the dead could speak to them through these talismans. So they don''t represent tribes or anything. Interesting. How many different kinds are there? There must be hundreds of variation. There are other greenstone carvings beside tikis, although I can''t remember all their names. Traditional tikis themselves can be divided into two distinct groups, though. Can they? By the materials, I''ve seen some with metal features. Or is it to do with size or where they were made? Actually, it is the ways that their feet, heads, and hands are placed that make the major difference. Is it easy to discern the difference between the ancient stones and more modern reproductions? Are the styles very different? Modern reproductions usually use a different type of stone, although they may be of the same design. They may also be of a smaller size. But it''s actually quite easy to tell if you are buying a genuine carving or a mass-produced souvenir. How? Nowadays, they are produced by machine using lasers even. So, they have no blemishes. In genuine carvings, they aren''t always perfect. because the Maori used simple hand tools. For example, the hole that the cord is fed through i' AS nvarchar(max))+N'sn''t perfectly round. That makes sense. Actually, it''s amazing that they produce such detailed tikis with such simple tools and materials. Talk me through the process. I know the basic shape is carved from New Zealand greenstone. Yes, but first they had to get a blank of the right size. You mean a piece of stone of the right size and basic shape? That''s right. They used a hard stone tool, something rough, to wear a groove. into a piece of greenstone. After they''d scored it with that, they could just snap it in two. I guess they could have used some kind of hammer, though. They repeated the process until they had a piece of stone suitable for what they wanted to carved: usually flat pieces for tikis. Right, because they''re fairly small and quite thin. Then they started carving? Not right away. Before they could start carving, they needed an absolutely smooth, flat surface. Nowadays, when we want to do something like that, we''d clamp the stone in a vice and use a sander. But they used a heavy block of sandstone and rubbed the piece of greenstone on it over and over to prepare it for carving. It must have been very time-consuming. So how did they cut out the little details like hands and feet? They had carving tools. They used small chips of stone, glued and tied onto a wooden handle. Then they also used a piece of sharpened bone made into a point. Is that what they used to make the holes? Actually, they had a special tool to make holes. It was a stick tied between two heavy pebbles with a point at the end. They would wrap string around it, pull it back and forth to wear a hole into the tiki. They used the same technique to make round shapes too, but didn''t make the hole go all the way through. And I''ve seen some tikis with coloured Eyes or decorations. Are they painted? Mostly, they are inlaid with different coloured stone or mother of pearl from shells, or sometimes dyed pieces of bones. They were fixed in place using teratagum, a sticky plant resin. They used plants to make the natural strings too. That''s right. The Maori were very resourceful, weren''t they? They certainly were.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.4.S1056.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1056),
(N'd7114fb3-1903-538a-88ba-cfab4c063036',N'0bef8d86-b441-5e0d-a032-15b97f0174ce',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A</span>, <span>B</span> or <span>C</span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Maori Carving',1,0,1,NULL,0,1056),
(N'1c9f858c-8003-59bc-b04c-364a82812940',N'd7114fb3-1903-538a-88ba-cfab4c063036',0,3,19,N'<p>Amy and Mike agree that the greenstone tikis</p>',N'<p>+Ques: Amy and Mike agree that the greenstone tikis... (Amy và Mike đồng ý rằng những chiếc tiki làm bằng đá greenstone thì...) </p><p>A. take great skill to produce. (cần kỹ thuật chế tác cao.) </p><p>B. are fascinating curved shapes. (có hình dạng cong thú vị.) </p><p>C. have interesting stories behind them. (có những câu chuyện thú vị đằng sau.) </p><p>+Giải thích: Họ nói rằng "it''s extremely hard" (nó rất khó thực hiện) và "requires expertise and time" (cần kĩ thuật phức tạp và nhiều thời gian) → có nghĩa là để chế tác cần kỹ năng cao. Không có đoạn nào đề cập rõ ràng rằng cả hai đề cập đến câu chuyện của tikis (C) hay hình dạng cong của tikis (B). </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1056),
(N'63ad616c-585b-5f36-9a2f-092c1240ff3f',N'd7114fb3-1903-538a-88ba-cfab4c063036',0,3,19,N'<p>According to Amy, why are so few genuine old tikis found on archaeological dig sites?</p>',N'<p>+Ques: According to Amy, why are so few genuine old tikis found on archaeological dig sites? (Theo Amy, tại sao có rất ít tiki cổ được tìm thấy tại các địa điểm khảo cổ?) </p><p>A. Not many people know about them. (Ít người biết đến chúng.) </p><p>B. They tend to be stolen by treasure hunters. (Chúng thường bị những kẻ săn kho báu đánh cắp.) </p><p>C. The majority become inherited items. (Phần lớn trở thành đồ thừa kế.) </p><p>+Giải thích: Amy nói rõ rằng những chiếc tiki này được truyền từ thế hệ này sang thế hệ khác. Không có đề cập gì đến bị đánh cắp (B) hay ít người biết (A). </p><p>+Key vocabs: heirloom (n) = vật gia truyền. passed down through generations = được truyền lại qua các thế hệ.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1056),
(N'fb5e15ad-ed8f-5ace-bf4a-6f39ef8c37de',N'd7114fb3-1903-538a-88ba-cfab4c063036',0,3,19,N'<p>The Maori people considered tikis to be</p>',N'<p>+Ques: The Maori people considered tikis to be... (Người Maori xem tiki là...) </p><p>A. decorative items. (đồ trang trí.) </p><p>B. religious objects. (đồ vật tôn giáo.) </p><p>C. tribal symbols. (biểu tượng bộ tộc.) </p><p>+Giải thích: Tiki được xem là vật linh thiêng, giúp liên hệ với tổ tiên đã mất, mang ý nghĩa tâm linh nên nó là đồ vật tôn giáo (religious objects). Họ không coi là biểu tượng bộ tộc → loại C. Và chúng không đơn thuần là đồ trang trí </p><p>+Key vocabs: sacred (a) = linh thiêng. pathway to ancestors = con đường kết nối với tổ tiên.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1056),
(N'd403171a-eded-50bd-b0f6-6442cc129750',N'd7114fb3-1903-538a-88ba-cfab4c063036',0,3,19,N'<p>Tikis are classified into one type or the other by</p>',N'<p>+Ques: Tikis are classified into one type or the other by... (Tiki được phân loại thành từng loại dựa trên...) </p><p>A. where they originated. (nơi chúng được tạo ra.) </p><p>B. the materials used. (vật liệu sử dụng.) </p><p>C. the position of the body. (vị trí các bộ phận.) </p><p>+Giải thích: Nội dung chỉ rõ: sự khác biệt chính nằm ở cách sắp xếp vị trí chân, đầu, tay (position of the body). Mặc dù có nhắc đến làm từ vật liệu kim loại, nhưng ngay lập tức được bác bỏ trong câu sau: “Actually, it is the ways that...” </p><p>+Key vocabs: placement (n) = vị trí sắp xếp. make the major difference = tạo nên sự khác biệt chính.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1056),
(N'e6a169e8-b724-5102-b0a7-184cb0b5f1ba',N'd7114fb3-1903-538a-88ba-cfab4c063036',0,3,19,N'<p>How can modern reproductions be easily distinguished from genuine Maori carvings?</p>',N'<p>+Ques: How can modern reproductions be easily distinguished from genuine Maori carvings? (Làm sao để phân biệt dễ dàng tiki hiện đại sản xuất hàng loạt và tiki của người Maori thật?) </p><p>A. The materials differ. (Khác nhau về vật liệu.) </p><p>B. They are too regular in shape. (Chúng có hình dạng quá đều đặn.) </p><p>C. They are of different sizes. (Chúng có kích thước khác nhau.) </p><p>+Giải thích: Tiki hiện đại dùng máy sản xuất nên ngoại hình rất đều đặn, không có khuyết điểm. Tiki thật thì không hoàn hảo, do được làm thủ công bằng tay. Không nói gì về vật liệu khác nhau (A) hay kích thước khác nhau (C). </p><p>+Key vocabs: +blemishes (n) = vết xước, khuyết điểm. +mass-produced souvenir = đồ lưu niệm sản xuất hàng loạt.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1056),
(N'490aa70e-d867-52d2-a3ff-d8b1790f8cd7',N'0bef8d86-b441-5e0d-a032-15b97f0174ce',0,2,18,N'<p><em>What tool did Maoris use to carry out each of the following tasks?</em>
Choose <span>FIVE</span> answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span>A–E</span>, next to Question 27–30.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,2,NULL,0,1056),
(N'11153e73-1ffe-5454-a282-86d7d6ffdbbd',N'490aa70e-d867-52d2-a3ff-d8b1790f8cd7',0,3,19,N'<p>creating a blank</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: “Blank” là khối đá thô ban đầu (phôi). Câu "used a hard stone tool... to wear a groove" → họ dùng công cụ từ đá cứng để tạo rãnh, nghĩa là dùng để tạo hình thô ban đầu, tương ứng với stone scoring tool (dụng cụ rạch đá). </p><p>+Từ vựng cần nhớ: blank (n) = phôi ban đầu. wear a groove into = mài mòn tạo rãnh.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1056),
(N'7ce978d1-40e9-5254-95e9-d8b84dfcd60b',N'490aa70e-d867-52d2-a3ff-d8b1790f8cd7',0,3,19,N'<p>smoothing the surface</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Trước khi mài, họ cần một mặt đá phẳng. Vì ngày xưa chưa có giấy mài như ngày nay, nên người Maori dùng khối đá sa thạch (heavy block of sandstone) để mài nhẵn bề mặt. "rubbed the piece... over and over" = chà xát liên tục để làm mịn, đúng với “smoothing the surface”. +Từ vựng cần nhớ: sandstone (n) = đá sa thạch. rub over and over = chà liên tục.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1056),
(N'b80d5c11-317c-5980-bd8d-1612be555c60',N'490aa70e-d867-52d2-a3ff-d8b1790f8cd7',0,3,19,N'<p>carving details</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Họ dùng xương được mài nhọn (small chips of stone) để khắc các chi tiết (carving details). Trong đó, một phần xương được mài nhọn để làm đầu vật mài (bone point) "sharpened bone... made into a point" +Từ vựng cần nhớ: sharpened (a) = được mài sắc. carving (v) = khắc, chạm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1056),
(N'9b997cd1-30c9-5436-be64-363c3faabeb3',N'490aa70e-d867-52d2-a3ff-d8b1790f8cd7',0,3,19,N'<p>making holes</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Họ dùng que gỗ buộc đá để khoan lỗ (stick drill). Họ kéo qua lại que gỗ để tạo lỗ giống thao tác khoan. </p><p>+Từ vựng cần nhớ: drill (v) = khoan. pull back and forth = kéo qua lại.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1056),
(N'57fa3865-01af-5a9e-9c77-0477401c7589',N'490aa70e-d867-52d2-a3ff-d8b1790f8cd7',0,3,19,N'<p>fixing coloured decorations</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Từ “teratagum” là một loại nhựa cây dính, dùng để gắn các chi tiết trang trí màu sắc. "plant resin" tương đương với plant glue (keo thực vật). </p><p>+Từ vựng cần nhớ:</p><p>resin (n) = nhựa cây.
inlaid with = được khảm bằng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1056),
(N'80bb4011-cf46-508f-9992-b11a39cbc179',N'4a2b4448-f61e-5c15-b3c1-52e8af7f8422',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] SUBURBS</h2><p>In the beginning of human civilization, there were no cities. There were just small settlements. They grew with time and became villages. And then at a certain stage, when the size became big enough, we call it a suburb. So, what about suburbs? Generally speaking, a suburb is a mixed-use or residential area existing as either part of a city or as a separate residential community within commuting distance of a Some small towns near a big city were absorbed by the city and became. its Many labor workers, such as carpenters, construction, and factory workers, live in the suburbs of the city; usually, they have a lower income than the middle class and can''t afford the expensive houses or apartments in downtown area. They live in the suburbs where the infrastructure is poor and the rent is low. Interestingly, you may also see wealthy residents, most of whom are highly paid engineers, lawyers, and doctors, enjoy their life in the live in the expensive neighborhoods which are located in the nicer areas. They usually have a very big house and a garden. Traditionally, many people think that living in the suburbs is less convenient than living downtown, where there were supermarkets, shopping malls, and other facilities. These facilities are not concentrated or even available in the Sometimes people there have to travel for half an hour to get to the nearest market or grocery store. Actually, there are many good things about living in the For instance, people pay relatively lower taxes than people in the city, so they can have a more disposable income, and food is cheaper than that in the cities. Many people have noticed that vegetables and some kinds of fruits are usually produced in the suburbs or transported to the suburbs first. before they reach the city. Because of lower taxes and other beneficial policies, it''s much easier to start one''s own business in the suburbs; for instance, open a cafe or set up a shop. That''s why we can see a lot of startups in the suburban region. It could also save a lot of money, and of the ever-increasing population. Now you can see many large-scale housing areas constructed in almost all city suburbs, as there is no room for new apartment buildings or houses in the cities. Outside of many big cities, such as London, there are big residential areas. Also, the roads are designed and built much later, so they are usually much wider than. The roads downtown will provide greater mobility for suburban resident' AS nvarchar(max))+N's. They can travel faster from one place to another since there are fewer traffic jams, despite the growing number of automobiles on the This is one of the reasons why more people are moving out of the city these years. Yet the suburbs are also facing some troubles in their development. Many suburbs are usually occupied by houses and residential areas; as a result, there is not enough room for trade activities. Even some supermarkets and small restaurants are suffering from this. People usually have to drive a car to the nearest grocery store. This warns. the urban planning department to improve their work and have the vision to think ahead of. I think you might be interested in an interview that we did with some residents in the suburban area of London. Their opinions can give us a picture of what suburban life is like compared to life downtown. Jack Simmons is an office worker who lives in the western suburbs. Through his observation, he thinks that although there are few public gyms or clubs and it''s not convenient to travel to the town for fitness classes, people in the suburbs actually do more exercise such as jogging and swimming. So he concluded that the suburban folks. enjoy a much healthier lifestyle compared with their city counterparts. When we interviewed Ellen Simpson, a housewife who lives in the northern suburbs, she told us that living in the city center is a nightmare. She said the city center is always congested with both cars and people, and she could not stand it anymore. Though many of her friends live in the city and people are generally friendly there, she still prefers to live in suburban areas and has adjusted to living here now. We talked to an engineer named Robert Gregory, who works downtown and lives in the suburbs. In his opinion, the architectural style in the suburbs is quite the same as that in the city nowadays. Besides, people in the suburban area also pursue goals that are similar to the ones pursued by the people in the city. Also, people in the suburbs don''t seem to be more satisfied with their life. Sarah Godfrey is an English teacher at a school in the city. According to her, people in cities are not necessarily less friendly than their counterparts in the suburbs, but the latter always smile. They go to the parks and have gatherings more often. These activities are important in improving life quality and the level of happiness.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>In the beginning of human civilization, there were no cities. There were just small settlements. They grew with time and became villages. And then at a certain stage, when the size became big enough, we call it a suburb. So, what about suburbs? Generally speaking, a suburb is a mixed-use or residential area existing as either part of a city or as a separate residential community within commuting distance of a Some small towns near a big city were absorbed by the city and became. its Many labor workers, such as carpenters, construction, and factory workers, live in the suburbs of the city; usually, they have a lower income than the middle class and can''t afford the expensive houses or apartments in downtown area. They live in the suburbs where the infrastructure is poor and the rent is low. Interestingly, you may also see wealthy residents, most of whom are highly paid engineers, lawyers, and doctors, enjoy their life in the live in the expensive neighborhoods which are located in the nicer areas. They usually have a very big house and a garden. Traditionally, many people think that living in the suburbs is less convenient than living downtown, where there were supermarkets, shopping malls, and other facilities. These facilities are not concentrated or even available in the Sometimes people there have to travel for half an hour to get to the nearest market or grocery store. Actually, there are many good things about living in the For instance, people pay relatively lower taxes than people in the city, so they can have a more disposable income, and food is cheaper than that in the cities. Many people have noticed that vegetables and some kinds of fruits are usually produced in the suburbs or transported to the suburbs first. before they reach the city. Because of lower taxes and other beneficial policies, it''s much easier to start one''s own business in the suburbs; for instance, open a cafe or set up a shop. That''s why we can see a lot of startups in the suburban region. It could also save a lot of money, and of the ever-increasing population. Now you can see many large-scale housing areas constructed in almost all city suburbs, as there is no room for new apartment buildings or houses in the cities. Outside of many big cities, such as London, there are big residential areas. Also, the roads are designed and built much later, so they are usually much wider than. The roads downtown will provide greater mobility for suburban residents. They can travel faste' AS nvarchar(max))+N'r from one place to another since there are fewer traffic jams, despite the growing number of automobiles on the This is one of the reasons why more people are moving out of the city these years. Yet the suburbs are also facing some troubles in their development. Many suburbs are usually occupied by houses and residential areas; as a result, there is not enough room for trade activities. Even some supermarkets and small restaurants are suffering from this. People usually have to drive a car to the nearest grocery store. This warns. the urban planning department to improve their work and have the vision to think ahead of. I think you might be interested in an interview that we did with some residents in the suburban area of London. Their opinions can give us a picture of what suburban life is like compared to life downtown. Jack Simmons is an office worker who lives in the western suburbs. Through his observation, he thinks that although there are few public gyms or clubs and it''s not convenient to travel to the town for fitness classes, people in the suburbs actually do more exercise such as jogging and swimming. So he concluded that the suburban folks. enjoy a much healthier lifestyle compared with their city counterparts. When we interviewed Ellen Simpson, a housewife who lives in the northern suburbs, she told us that living in the city center is a nightmare. She said the city center is always congested with both cars and people, and she could not stand it anymore. Though many of her friends live in the city and people are generally friendly there, she still prefers to live in suburban areas and has adjusted to living here now. We talked to an engineer named Robert Gregory, who works downtown and lives in the suburbs. In his opinion, the architectural style in the suburbs is quite the same as that in the city nowadays. Besides, people in the suburban area also pursue goals that are similar to the ones pursued by the people in the city. Also, people in the suburbs don''t seem to be more satisfied with their life. Sarah Godfrey is an English teacher at a school in the city. According to her, people in cities are not necessarily less friendly than their counterparts in the suburbs, but the latter always smile. They go to the parks and have gatherings more often. These activities are important in improving life quality and the level of happiness.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.4.S1056.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1056),
(N'7a43d132-73b9-5167-94c1-1ca6450ca88a',N'80bb4011-cf46-508f-9992-b11a39cbc179',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.
Write <span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'SUBURBS',11,0,1,NULL,0,1056),
(N'96c9974b-0cc2-5479-acec-08c32bf96952',N'7a43d132-73b9-5167-94c1-1ca6450ca88a',0,3,19,N'<h3>SUBURBS</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>People living in the suburbs:</span></p> <p>• labor workers }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Advantages of living in the suburbs:</span></p> <p>• Tax and the prices of }{SPACE}{ are low.</p> <p>• It is easy to start a }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Building }{SPACE}{ housing area to meet the demand of growing population.</p> <p>• People have more }{SPACE}{ because of wider roads.</p> <p><span>Problem:</span></p> <p>• lack space for }{SPACE}{ in the suburbs.</p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>+Trans: Thật thú vị khi thấy những cư dân giàu có, phần lớn là kỹ sư, luật sư và bác sĩ có thu nhập cao, sống rất thoải mái ở vùng ngoại ô. </p><p>+Giải thích: Câu hỏi gợi ý rằng ngoài "công nhân lao động" (labor workers), còn ai sống ở vùng ngoại ô? Trong audio nói rõ là wealthy residents (cư dân giàu có). </p><p>+Key vocabs: residents (n) = inhabitants = people who live in a place: dân cư wealthy (a) = rich = affluent: giàu có</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1056),
(N'bbc5980e-3827-5123-a0f9-17ecfe5893cc',N'7a43d132-73b9-5167-94c1-1ca6450ca88a',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>+Trans: Người dân được trả thuế thấp hơn so với trong thành phố và thực phẩm cũng rẻ hơn so với thành phố. </p><p>+Giải Thích: Câu hỏi hỏi về thứ gì có giá thấp và câu trả lời trong audio là food (thực phẩm). </p><p>+Key vocabs: cheap (a) = low-priced = inexpensive: rẻ disposable income = thu nhập khả dụng (thu nhập còn lại sau khi trừ chi phí thiết yếu)</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1056),
(N'd7fb7769-f476-500d-8eb6-62cbe7429247',N'7a43d132-73b9-5167-94c1-1ca6450ca88a',0,3,19,N'Question number 33',N'<p>+Trans: Nhờ vào mức thuế thấp và các chính sách ưu đãi, việc bắt đầu một doanh nghiệp riêng ở vùng ngoại ô trở nên dễ dàng hơn ở nơi khác.</p><p>+Giải thích: Câu hỏi hỏi về thứ gì dễ khởi đầu ở vùng ngoại ô → transcript nói rõ là “start one''s own business”. </p><p>+Key vocabs: start a business = open a company/shop: khởi beneficial policies = chính sách có lợi</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1056),
(N'c3afd783-7d54-5470-9d28-2322f417a367',N'7a43d132-73b9-5167-94c1-1ca6450ca88a',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>+Trans: Bây giờ, bạn có thể thấy rất nhiều khu nhà ở quy mô lớn được xây dựng ở hầu hết các vùng ngoại ô. </p><p>+Giải thích: Từ khóa “building...housing area” → trong audio nói rõ là “large-scale housing areas”. </p><p>+Key vocabs: large-scale = massive = extensive: quy mô lớn constructed = built:</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1056),
(N'6f4b8e37-fd48-56e8-bcff-3806907b0521',N'7a43d132-73b9-5167-94c1-1ca6450ca88a',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>+Trans: Đường ở ngoại ô thường rộng hơn nhiều, người dân di chuyển dễ dàng hơn. </p><p>+Giải thích: Câu hỏi về tiện ích khi có đường rộng vậy nên đáp án là “mobility”. </p><p>+Key vocabs: mobility (n) = the ability to move easily: dễ di chuyển residents (n) = người dân</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1056),
(N'b40036ee-ac6f-5cce-802b-3cb43761c7d8',N'7a43d132-73b9-5167-94c1-1ca6450ca88a',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>+Trans: Ngoại ô đáng đối mặt với một số vấn đề trong quá trình phát triển, ví dụ như hông có đủ không gian cho các hoạt động thương mại.</p><p>+Giải thích: Câu hỏi yêu cầu điền danh từ chỉ hoạt động hiện đang bị thiếu không gian và hoạt động đó là thương mại: “trade”. </p><p>+Key vocabs: +trade (n) = commerce = business activities: Hoạt động thương mại +lack of space: không đủ không gian</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1056),
(N'099649ce-c7b8-5e61-92ac-9317a5dd2a07',N'80bb4011-cf46-508f-9992-b11a39cbc179',0,2,18,N'<p><em>What does each of the interviewees think of the life in the suburbs compared with that in the cities? </em></p><p>Choose four answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span>A–F</span>, next to Questions 37–40.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,2,NULL,0,1056),
(N'9fa66972-b2f8-5b52-b20c-9825923b4603',N'099649ce-c7b8-5e61-92ac-9317a5dd2a07',0,3,19,N'<p>Jack Simons</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Jack Simons nói rằng mặc dù không có nhiều phòng gym hay lớp thể dục ở vùng ngoại ô, nhưng người dân lại tập thể dục nhiều hơn như chạy bộ, bơi lội... → Người dân ở vùng ngoại ô có lối sống lành mạnh hơn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1056),
(N'505b1841-8b4e-5a41-9065-b639796b34d6',N'099649ce-c7b8-5e61-92ac-9317a5dd2a07',0,3,19,N'<p>Ellen Simpson</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Ellen nói rằng trung tâm thành phố quá đông đúc (congested with both cars and people), và bà không thể chịu nổi nữa. → Ngoại ô ít đông đúc hơn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1056),
(N'dc59dacf-fee7-5afc-b28c-977ce4dd7f8e',N'099649ce-c7b8-5e61-92ac-9317a5dd2a07',0,3,19,N'<p>Robert Gregory</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Robert cho rằng kiến trúc ở ngoại ô khá giống với thành phố, và người dân theo đuổi mục tiêu sống tương tự. → Có những điểm tương đồng giữa ngoại ô và thành phố.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1056),
(N'52769a93-f284-502d-90a6-1c90e68eaaf1',N'099649ce-c7b8-5e61-92ac-9317a5dd2a07',0,3,19,N'<p>Sarah Godfrey</p>',N'<p>+Giải thích: Sarah nói rằng người dân ở vùng ngoại ô hay cười, hay đi công viên, tụ họp với nhau – điều đó nâng cao chất lượng cuộc sống và mức độ hạnh phúc. → Người dân ở ngoại ô hạnh phúc hơn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1056),
(N'7906ab58-f259-5b8f-a61a-c8d59a28f2ca',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'',N'Vol 5 READING FULL TEST 2',0,7,1,NULL,1,1054),
(N'1a0b3a50-463c-5fb2-b2be-189a19cdaca0',N'7906ab58-f259-5b8f-a61a-c8d59a28f2ca',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<p><span><span><span>A Brief History of Ballet</span></span></span></p><p>Ballet originated in the Italian Renaissance of the 15th century. The nobility were treated to extravagant events, especially wedding celebrations, where dancing and music created a detailed and fascinating spectacle. Dancing masters taught the dance steps to the nobility, who participated in the performances. In the 16th century Catherine de Medici, an Italian noblewoman, wife of the king of France and a great patron of the arts, began to fund ballet in the French court. Her elaborate festivals encouraged the growth of ballet de cour, a programme that included dance, costume, song, music and poetry. A century later, King Louis XIV helped to popularise and standardise the ballet. A passionate dancer, he performed many roles himself, including that of the Sun King in Ballet de la Nuit. His love of ballet raised it from a hobby for amateurs to a skill requiring professional training</p><p></p><p>By 1661, a dance academy had opened in Paris, and in 1681 ballet moved to the stage. The French opera Le Triomphe de L’Amour incorporated ballet elements in its performance, creating a long-standing opera-ballet tradition in France. ln the mid-18th century, however, French ballet master Jean Georges Noverre rebelled against the artifice of opera-ballet, believing that ballet could stand on its own as an art form. His idea-that ballet should contain expressive dramatic movement, and this should reveal relationships between characters-introduced the ballet d''action, a dramatic style of ballet that features a narrative. Noverre''s work is considered the forerunner to the narrative ballets of the 19th century.</p><p></p><p>Early classical ballets such as Giselle explored the world of spirits and magic, and often showed women as passive and fragile. These themes are reflected in the ballets of the time which are called romantic ballets. This is the period which dancing on tiptoe, known as pointe work, became the normal style for the ballerina. The romantic tutu, a calf-length, full skirt, was also introduced at this time.</p><p></p><p>By the 19th century the popularity of ballet had soared in Russia and by the end of that century its composers and choreographers - who designed the movements and steps the dancers took -had taken the art form to new levels, The Nutcracker and The Sleeping Beauty, as choreographed by Marius Petipa, represent classical ballet in its grandest form. The primary purpose of ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'Russian ballet in this period was to display classical technique such as pointe work, high leg lifts and precision of movement. Complicated sequences that drew attention to demanding steps, leaps and turns were choreographed into the story. The classical tutu, much shorter and stiffer than the romantic tutu, was introduced in the second half of the century to reveal a ballerina''s legs and emphasise the skill of her footwork. George Balanchine is considered by many to be the greatest innovator of the contemporary "plotless" ballet, with no definite story line. Its purpose is to use movement to express the music and to depict human emotions. Today, the ballet takes many forms. Classical approaches, traditional stories and contemporary choreographic innovations blend together to produce the character of modern ballet.</p><p></p><p>Despite all the changes which ballet has undergone, pointe shoes, which were first worn in the 1830s, have remained the key ballet equipment. Specially made to allow ballerinas to dance on tiptoe, they create an illusion of lightness and even give a sense that the ballerina is floating on air. While they look very delicate, pointe shoes are, in fact, quite the opposite. The tip of the shoe is a rigid box made of densely packed layers of fabric, cardboard or paper which has been hardened by glue. The box needs to be extremely sturdy since the entire weight of the dancer''s body is balanced on it. The rest of the shoe is made of leather, cotton and satin, and each shoe is hand-made to fit a dancer''s specifications, which means that you''ll never see two pairs that are identical. Sometimes, men will also wear pointe shoes, usually for comedic effect, but male dancers usually wear a leather or canvas slipper with a soft sole, which provides flexibility when jumping.</p><p></p><p>Each time a ballerina gets a new pair of pointe shoes, she has to break them in. This is done by pounding the box of the shoe with a hammer to soften it. Dancers also sew on ribbons and elastic to hold their shoes in place. Despite this time-consuming preparation, the shoes may only last for one performance, or even part of a performance, depending on the difficulty of the ballet. In the course of a single season, a professional ballerina can go through 100-120 pairs of pointe shoes.</p>'),NULL,NULL,N'',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1054),
(N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',N'1a0b3a50-463c-5fb2-b2be-189a19cdaca0',0,2,18,N'<p>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1-7 on your answer sheet, write <span><span>TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN</span></span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,1,NULL,0,1054),
(N'210590ff-42a5-59e5-8415-b4c27b6947cc',N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',0,3,19,N'<p>The first form of ballet was designed as entertainment for Italian society''s upper classes</p>',N'<p>+ Synonym: nobility (giới quý tộc) = upper classes (tầng lớp thượng lưu)</p><p>+ Interpretation: Hình thức ballet đầu tiên được thiết kế để giải trí cho tầng lớp thượng lưu của xã hội Ý.</p><p>+ Answer: Dựa vào synonyms và thông tin trong bài ballet bắt nguồn từ thời Phục hưng Ý và được trình diễn cho giới thượng lưu </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là True</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1054),
(N'29b47c85-d4bf-5bb2-8798-ac5d5c513366',N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',0,3,19,N'<p>The Ballet De la Nuit was Louis XIV''s favourite ballet</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Ballet De la Nuit là vở ballet yêu thích của Louis XIV.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn chỉ nói ông biểu diễn trong vở đó, nhưng không đề cập đó có phải là vở yêu thích của ông hay không. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là Not given</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1054),
(N'98a60782-6a05-5dce-a558-7b2a6ba54186',N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',0,3,19,N'<p>The strong link between ballet and opera was challenged in mid-18th century France.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Mối liên hệ chặt chẽ giữa ballet và opera đã bị thách thức ở Pháp vào giữa thế kỷ 18.</p><p>+ Answer: Noverre vào giữa thế kỷ 18 tin rằng ballet có thể là một môn nghệ thuật riêng biệt khỏi opera </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là True</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1054),
(N'1e04f653-4b65-52be-ac0d-c84e0f1c7e6d',N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',0,3,19,N'<p>In the first half of the 19th century the majority of ballet dancers were women. </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Trong nửa đầu thế kỷ 19, đa số vũ công ballet là phụ nữ.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn chỉ nói về sự nổi tiếng của ballet vào thế kỉ 19 chứ không nói gì đến việc đa số vũ công ballet là phụ nữ </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là Not given</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,4,NULL,0,1054),
(N'9b03a6a9-2fdb-577a-b2e1-135e26f42757',N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',0,3,19,N'<p>Choreographers in 19th-century Russia were mostly interested in telling a story.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Các biên đạo múa ở Nga thế kỷ 19 chủ yếu quan tâm đến việc kể một câu chuyện.</p><p>+ Answer: Mục đích chính được nêu là "trình diễn kỹ thuật cổ điển" (display classical technique), không phải kể chuyện </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là False</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,5,NULL,0,1054),
(N'5f16b6cf-9f33-5c66-8d2d-84f7ab205700',N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',0,3,19,N'<p>The classical tutu was very popular with ballerinas when it was first introduced. </p>',N'<ul><li><p>Interpretation: Váy tutu cổ điển rất được các nữ diễn viên ballet ưa chuộng khi nó lần đầu được giới thiệu.</p></li><li><p>Answer: Đoạn văn nói lý do váy tutu được giới thiệu, nhưng không đề cập đến việc nó có được ưa chuộng bởi các vũ công hay không -&gt; Đáp án là Not given</p></li></ul>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,6,NULL,0,1054),
(N'f4448af9-4345-5009-b421-96f4bf63b027',N'87f74c56-b467-5878-877e-8bba9e5b7309',0,3,19,N'<p>George Balanchine began his ballet career in New York. </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: George Balanchine bắt đầu sự nghiệp ballet của mình ở New York.</p><p>+ Answer: Bài đọc không có thông tin nào về nơi Balanchine bắt đầu sự nghiệp </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là Not given</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,7,NULL,0,1054),
(N'fd3b2970-0a38-52e6-9dcf-85c3a2be4b7f',N'1a0b3a50-463c-5fb2-b2be-189a19cdaca0',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose <span><span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</span></span> from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 8-13 on your answer sheet.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Pointe shoes',11,0,2,NULL,0,1054),
(N'58605a27-9527-55eb-8d9e-74aad7d3b579',N'fd3b2970-0a38-52e6-9dcf-85c3a2be4b7f',0,3,19,N'<h3>Pointe shoes</h3><p>• first worn in 1830s </p><p>• make it seem like }{SPACE}{ rather than dancing</p><p>• are }{SPACE}{ in appearance, but this is misleading</p><p>• in the tip, layers of fabric, cardboard or paper form a  }{SPACE}{ </p> }{SPACE}{ is used to make the materials tougher</p><p>• no two pairs are identical </p><p>• a }{SPACE}{ is used to prepare shoes for dancing </p><p>• dancers attach ribbons or elastic to their shoes </p><p>• ballerina can wear up to  }{SPACE}{ pairs in one season</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: ..làm việc khiêu vũ giống như… hơn đang nhảy</p><p>+ Answer: Giày múa tạo ra một cảm giác rằng vũ công như đang lơ lửng trong không khí </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là floating</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,8,NULL,0,1054),
(N'756ddb33-4b05-54c6-a263-77642480fcae',N'fd3b2970-0a38-52e6-9dcf-85c3a2be4b7f',0,3,19,N'Question number 9',N'<p>+ Interpretation: ...có vẻ ngoài..., nhưng điều này là gây hiểu lầm.</p><p>+ Answer: Giày múa trông có vẻ "tinh tế, mỏng manh" (delicate) nhưng thực tế lại trái ngược </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là delicate</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,1054),
(N'31e063f4-3f7c-5009-91ff-140af54a05ae',N'fd3b2970-0a38-52e6-9dcf-85c3a2be4b7f',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>+ Interpretation: ...ở phần mũi, các lớp vải, bìa cứng hoặc giấy tạo thành một...</p><p>+ Answer: Phần mũi cứng của giày được gọi là một "cái hộp" (box). </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là box</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1054),
(N'a5ff905b-9142-583b-8d4b-31e7d70ef5bb',N'fd3b2970-0a38-52e6-9dcf-85c3a2be4b7f',0,3,19,N'Question number 11',N'<p>+ Interpretation: ...được sử dụng để làm cho vật liệu cứng hơn.</p><p>+ Answer: "Keo" (glue) được dùng để làm cứng các lớp vải, bìa và giấy </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là glue</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1054),
(N'633c79f0-4d0e-58e0-8c14-e792e75a13b5',N'fd3b2970-0a38-52e6-9dcf-85c3a2be4b7f',0,3,19,N'Question number 12',N'<p>+ Interpretation: ...được sử dụng để chuẩn bị giày cho việc nhảy.</p><p>+ Answer: Giày được làm mềm bằng cách đập phần “hộp” của giày bằng búa </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là hammer</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1054),
(N'a6584f22-933b-59a1-bf8f-5af1f3fd2d32',N'fd3b2970-0a38-52e6-9dcf-85c3a2be4b7f',0,3,19,N'Question number 13',N'<p>+ Interpretation: ...một nữ diễn viên ballet có thể sử dụng hết... đôi trong một mùa diễn.</p><p>+ Answer: Một vũ công chuyên nghiệp có thể dùng hết "100 đến 120" đôi giày trong một mùa. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là 120</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1054),
(N'87a967c5-0daa-54ca-9abf-8893fc4ed5ed',N'7906ab58-f259-5b8f-a61a-c8d59a28f2ca',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<p><span><span><span>Creative Problem-Solving</span></span></span></p><p><em>Puzzle-solving is an ancient, universal practice, scholars say, and it depends on creative insight, or a primitive spark. Now, modern neuroscientists are beginning to tap its source.</em></p><p></p><p><span><span>A. </span></span>In a recent study, researchers at Northwestern University in the United States found that people were more likely to solve word puzzles with sudden insight when they were amused, having just seen a short comedy routine. ‘What we think is happening,’ said Dr Mark Beeman, a neuroscientist who worked on the study, ''is that the humor, this positive mood, is lowering the brain''s threshold for detecting weaker or more remote connections,'' which enable people to solve puzzles.</p><p></p><p><span><span>B.</span></span> This suggests that the appeal of puzzles goes far deeper than the pleasant rush of finding a solution. The very act of doing a puzzle typically shifts the brain into an open, playful state that is itself a pleasing escape. Unlike the social and professional mysteries in the real world, puzzles are reassuringly solvable; but like any serious problem, they require more than mere intellect to crack. ‘It’s imagination, it''s inference, it''s guessing; and much of it is happening subconsciously,’ said Dr Marcel Danesi, a professor of anthropology at the University of Toronto, Canada. ‘It’s all about you, using your own mind, without any method or schema, to restore order from chaos,’ Danesi said. ‘And once you have, you can sit back and say, “Hey, the rest of my life may be a disaster, but at least I have a solution”.’</p><p></p><p><span><span>C.</span></span> For almost a century scientists have used puzzles to study what they call ‘insight thinking’, the leaps of understanding that seem to come out of the blue. In one experiment, the German psychologist Karl Duncker presented people with a candle, a box of pins, and the task of attaching the candle to a wall. About a quarter of the subjects thought to use the pins to tack the box to the wall as a support - some immediately, and others after failed efforts to tack wax to the wall. According to Duncker, the creative leap seems to have been informed by subconscious cues. In another well-known experiment, psychologists H.G. Birch and H.S. Rabinowitz challenged people to tie together two cords; the cords were hanging from the ceiling of a large room, too far apart to be grabbed at the same time. A small perc' AS nvarchar(max))+N'entage of people solved it without any help, by tying something else to one cord and swinging it like a pendulum so that it could be caught while they held the other cord. In some experiments researchers gave clues to those who were stumped - for instance, by bumping into one of the strings so that it swung. Many of those who then solved the problem said they had no recollection of the clue, though it very likely registered subconsciously.</p><p></p><p><span><span>D.</span></span> All along, researchers have debated the definitions of insight and analysis, and some have concluded that the two are merely different sides of the same coin. Yet in an authoritative discussion of the research carried out so far, the psychologists Jonathan W. Schooler and Joseph Melcher concluded that the abilities most strongly correlated with insight problem-solving ‘were not significantly correlated’ with solving analytical problems. Either way, creative problem-solving usually requires both analysis and insight. Adam Anderson, a psychologist at the University of Toronto, Canada, argues that although when people are solving problems they may move back and forth between these abilities, they are truly different brain states.</p><p></p><p><span><span>E.</span></span> At first, studies did little more than confirm that brain areas that register reward spiked in activity when people came up with a solution, that is to say once they had completed a puzzle. However, in a series of recent studies, John Kounios, a psychologist at Drexel University in the United States, has imaged people’s brains as they prepare to tackle a puzzle, but before they’ve seen it. Those whose brains show a particular signature of preparatory activity, one that is strongly correlated with positive moods, turn out to be more likely to solve the puzzles with sudden insight than with trial and error (the clues can be solved either way). Previous research has also found activation of cells in a certain area of the brain when people widen or narrow their attention - say, when they filter out distractions to focus on a difficult task, like concentrating on someone’s voice in a noisy room. In the case of insight puzzle-solving, the brain seems to widen its attention, in effect making itself more susceptible to distraction.</p><p></p><p><span><span>F.</span></span> In the humor study, Beeman had college students solve word-association puzzles after watching a short video showing a stand-up comedian. Beeman found tha'+N't these students solved more of the puzzles overall, and significantly more by sudden insight, compared with when they’d seen a scary or boring video beforehand. This ‘open’ state of mind does not only apply to intellectual puzzles. In a study published last year, researchers at the University of Toronto found that people in positive moods picked up more background detail, even when they were told to block out distracting information during a computer task. The findings fit with dozens of experiments linking positive moods to better creative problem solving. The implication is that positive mood engages this broad,... attentional state that is both perceptual and visual,’ said Anderson. He explains that not only are people in a positive mood able to think more broadly, they are able to notice more visually.</p>'),NULL,NULL,N'',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1054),
(N'2e01ad8c-c323-5191-b5e8-83470838b74d',N'87a967c5-0daa-54ca-9abf-8893fc4ed5ed',0,2,18,N'<p>Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, <span><span>A-F</span></span>.</p><p>Which paragraph contains the following information?</p><p>Write the correct letter, <span><span>A-F</span></span>, in boxes 14-19 on your answer sheet.</p><p><span><span>NB </span></span>You may use any letter more than once.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',12,0,1,NULL,0,1054),
(N'dd3afd08-7b67-51a5-b293-af24872155c3',N'2e01ad8c-c323-5191-b5e8-83470838b74d',0,3,19,N'<p>a claim that people enjoy the process of doing puzzles as well as finding the answers.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Một tuyên bố rằng mọi người thích quá trình giải câu đố cũng như tìm ra câu trả lời.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn B mô tả việc giải câu đố chuyển não bộ sang trạng thái mở, vui chơi, điều này tự nó là một sự thoát ly thú vị. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1054),
(N'5c600e6f-9b43-584a-bf86-019927bb63a4',N'2e01ad8c-c323-5191-b5e8-83470838b74d',0,3,19,N'<p>a review of studies that looked at the relationship between insight and analysis.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Một đánh giá về các nghiên cứu đã xem xét mối quan hệ giữa sự thấu hiểu và phân tích.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn D thảo luận về các cuộc tranh luận và kết luận của các nhà nghiên cứu về thấu hiểu và phân tích. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là D</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1054),
(N'a8fde5ca-edab-5404-8366-84cef7cb159e',N'2e01ad8c-c323-5191-b5e8-83470838b74d',0,3,19,N'<p>the finding that people were less likely to solve puzzles after viewing uninteresting or disturbing material. </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Phát hiện rằng mọi người ít có khả năng giải câu đố sau khi xem tài liệu không thú vị hoặc gây khó chịu.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn F mô tả nghiên cứu cho thấy học sinh giải thêm được nhiều câu đố bằng sự thấu hiểu đột ngột hơn so với khi việc giải sau khi xem video đáng sợ hoặc nhàm chán </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là F</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1054),
(N'b63678a8-6341-53ff-a5b1-65c3fa8d8a07',N'2e01ad8c-c323-5191-b5e8-83470838b74d',0,3,19,N'<p>a comparison between doing puzzles and dealing with life challenges.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: So sánh giữa việc giải câu đố và đối mặt với thử thách cuộc sống.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn B so sánh câu đố với các vấn đề thực tế, chỉ ra rằng câu đố có thể giải được so với những bí ẩn xã hội và chuyên ngành </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1054),
(N'6bb49913-8b77-52e9-9056-0d8cc10a872d',N'2e01ad8c-c323-5191-b5e8-83470838b74d',0,3,19,N'<p>a description of a study where the subjects were given hints by those conducting the research.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Mô tả một nghiên cứu trong đó các đối tượng được đưa ra gợi ý bởi những người tiến hành nghiên cứu.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn C mô tả thí nghiệm của Birch và Rabinowitz, nơi các nhà nghiên cứu cung cấp manh mối (như làm dao động dây) để giúp đối tượng giải câu đố. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là C</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1054),
(N'82ce3e74-f384-5a6a-8778-fece19e1b11e',N'2e01ad8c-c323-5191-b5e8-83470838b74d',0,3,19,N'<p>details of a study in which the focus shifted to mental activity before a puzzle is  attempted.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Chi tiết về một nghiên cứu trong đó trọng tâm chuyển sang hoạtđộng tinh thần trước khi cố gắng giải câu đố.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn E mô tả nghiên cứu của Kounios, nơi ông quan sát hoạt động não khi mọi người chuẩn bị giải câu đố trước khi họ nhìn thấy nó </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là E</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1054),
(N'd68b4ace-b55d-5214-8f98-75ab49acfdb1',N'87a967c5-0daa-54ca-9abf-8893fc4ed5ed',0,2,18,N'<p>Look at the following statements (Questions 20-23) and the list of researchers below.</p><p>Match each statement with the correct researcher, <span><span>A-E.</span></span> <br>Write the correct letter,<span><span> A-E</span></span>, in boxes 20-23 on your answer sheet.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',10,0,2,NULL,0,1054),
(N'cbaf8572-cefa-5d9e-a2da-b3e77c16513d',N'd68b4ace-b55d-5214-8f98-75ab49acfdb1',0,3,19,N'<p>Solving a puzzle may help people facing difficulties feel better.</p><p></p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Giải một câu đố có thể giúp người đối mặt với khó khăn cảm thấy tốt hơn.</p><p>+ Answer: Danesi giải thích rằng việc giải câu đố có thể khôi phục trật tự từ sự hỗn loạn, có thể an ủi khi phần còn lại của cuộc sống là “thảm họa.” </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1054),
(N'42c43301-5067-5251-acee-2357d5b7738e',N'd68b4ace-b55d-5214-8f98-75ab49acfdb1',0,3,19,N'<p>Two distinctly separate functions of the brain are used when solving puzzles.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Hai chức năng riêng biệt của não được sử dụng khi giải câu đố.</p><p>+ Answer: Anderson lập luận rằng khi con người giải quyết vấn đề thì sẽ di chuyển giữa các khả năng phân tích và thấu hiểu, và chúng là các trạng thái não khác nhau </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là D</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1054),
(N'9a0a02cb-eea5-506b-a222-ae23fb80af05',N'd68b4ace-b55d-5214-8f98-75ab49acfdb1',0,3,19,N'<p>Some subjects were able to find a solution to the puzzle they were given without knowing how they had done it.</p>',N'<p>+ Synonyms: "without knowing how they had done it" (không biết rằng mình đã làm như vậy) ↔ "informed by subconscious cues" (được thông báo bởi những gợi ý tiềm thức)</p><p>+ Interpretation: Một số đối tượng có thể tìm ra giải pháp cho câu đố mà không biết làm thế nào họ đã làm điều đó.</p><p>+ Answer: Dựa vào phần trong bài và synonyms trong thí nghiệm của Duncker, sự nhảy vọt sáng tạo được thông báo bởi các tín hiệu tiềm thức</p><p> -&gt; Đáp án là C</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1054),
(N'9b7dba43-a080-5a26-970e-5c3eeae6883e',N'd68b4ace-b55d-5214-8f98-75ab49acfdb1',0,3,19,N'<p>Seeing something funny helps people make links that may not be obvious at first.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Nhìn thấy điều gì đó hài hước giúp mọi người tạo ra các kết nối có thể không rõ ràng ngay lúc đầu.</p><p>+ Answer: Beeman phát hiện rằng tâm trạng tích cực từ hài kịch làm giảm ngưỡng phát hiện kết nối yếu hoặc xa, giúp dễ dàng giải câu đố </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1054),
(N'5b6bcddf-3c9d-50c4-91cb-2b9af159577a',N'87a967c5-0daa-54ca-9abf-8893fc4ed5ed',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the summary below. Choose <span><span>ONE WORD ONLY</span></span> from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 24-26 on your answer sheet.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Kounios builds on studies of puzzle-solvers’ brain activity',11,0,3,NULL,0,1054),
(N'92bf8083-c3e5-55c0-98da-34ab2f92ad67',N'5b6bcddf-3c9d-50c4-91cb-2b9af159577a',0,3,19,N'<h3>Kounios builds on studies of puzzle-solvers’ brain activity</h3><p>Early studies showed that when people solved a puzzle, the parts of the brain linked to reward were more active. Studies by Kounios reveal that when people are feeling  }{SPACE}{ during the preparatory stage, it is more probable that they will use insight to solve puzzles. The  part of the brain that is affected is connected with the adjustment of people''s attention.</p><p>When someone is trying to listen to a }{SPACE}{ when the general sound level is high, the focus narrows.</p><p>When people solve puzzles using insight, their focus becomes wider, and they  are more open to }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Các nghiên cứu của Kounios tiết lộ rằng khi mọi người cảm thấy …… trong giai đoạn chuẩn bị, họ có nhiều khả năng sử dụng sự thấu hiểu để giải câu đố.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn E nói rằng hoạt động chuẩn bị của não tương quan với tâm trạng tích cực, dẫn đến khả năng giải quyết bằng thâu hiểu lớn hơn </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là positive</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1054),
(N'a193dbb9-6458-545a-a661-f92b19d78825',N'5b6bcddf-3c9d-50c4-91cb-2b9af159577a',0,3,19,N'Question number 25',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Khi ai đó cố gắng lắng nghe một ….. khi mức độ âm thanh chung cao, sự tập trung thu hẹp.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn E đưa ra ví dụ về việc tập trung vào giọng nói trong phòng ồn để minh họa sự thu hẹp chú ý. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là voice</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1054),
(N'3b46d21a-b003-56a7-b198-ade3a43d435e',N'5b6bcddf-3c9d-50c4-91cb-2b9af159577a',0,3,19,N'Question number 26',N'<p>+ Synonyms: "more open to" ↔ "more susceptible to" Interpretation: Khi mọi người giải câu đố bằng insight, sự tập trung trở nên rộng hơn, và họ dễ …. hơn.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn E mô tả rằng khi giải bằng sự thấu hiểu, bộ não sẽ mở rộng sự chú ý cho nên dễ bị phân tâm hơn. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là distraction</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1054),
(N'9942765b-547f-57a1-abac-f8089296c7a8',N'7906ab58-f259-5b8f-a61a-c8d59a28f2ca',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<p><span><span>The contradictions of tourism</span></span></p><p><em><span>Tourism may bring benefits to a society but its effects are not always straightforward. </span></em></p><p><span>International tourism presents itself as straightforward, passive and benign, yet it is complex, interesting and important, full of contradictions and depths. It has enormous growth potential; almost every country desires an expansion in this sector. Yet whether and how to grow is not nearly as straightforward as one might hope.   </span></p><p></p><p><span>Tourism can provide the ideal export for developing countries, which can capitalize on their  unique cultures and natural environments. However, to do this they need to have features such as airports, hotels, sanitation facilities, roads and medical facilities. A government can build  these itself, using revenue from local taxes or getting finance from foreign lenders. Alternatively, the government may attract foreign investment, allowing others to pay for the building, thus  relieving itself of providing capital but forgoing profits.   </span></p><p></p><p><span>Tourism is, however, a risky investment. It can gradually decline as a resort becomes seen as  outdated, or it can be destroyed overnight by a single hurricane, or a political emergency. Even when successful, countries and localities are faced with a dilemma: keep it within limits, or  extend its benefits but also deepen dependence on a single, fickle industry.   </span></p><p></p><p><span>By definition, tourists travel to see and knowingly to encounter the different, the original, the authentic. In this increasingly global world, what they want is regional and unique. But only through artifice can the tourist demand for authenticity be met. An event such as a festival or ceremony needs to be brought to their attention and its importance and unique nature elaborated to them. Natural sites, too, need to be presented if they are to be regarded as  noteworthy, gates, permits, and notices are required to set them apart from the mundane.  Complicated framing and staging allows tourists to recognize, by sight, ‘the actual thing’.   </span></p><p></p><p><span>Tourism places a premium on the parts of a culture that are visible, such as handicrafts and costume, and disregards those that are not, like the rules governing seating on a bus, attitudes to the elderly, or appropriate ways of addressing family members; in this way, tourism favors  what can be commodified. Yet' AS nvarchar(max))+N' commodities can be produced most efficiently using Industrial machinery to take advantage of economies of scale, an option closed to village artisans. This alienates the producers from what is being produced, and means the product no longer has any  personal meaning to them.   </span></p><p></p><p><span>In addition, cultural idiosyncrasies and natural spectacles change over time. Tourists can''t be  attracted, however, to an unstable and ambiguous culture, or to a natural site that is in the  process of transforming itself. As a consequence, efforts are made to fix and standardize: folk  dances, tortilla-making, peace pipes, bird habitats and the course of the Amazon have all been bound and defined. Had they not been fixed, however, they would not be accessible.   </span></p><p></p><p><span>Tourism also promotes an external orientation. Like other exports, the ''tourism experience’ is  produced by local people, and consumed by foreigners. To obtain loans and ultimately create  revenue, a government (of whatever size) has to please outside investors and potential  travelers. In these ways, tourism can result in a political system becoming more authoritarian  and giving less consideration to the needs of individuals within the country.  </span></p><p></p><p><span>In recent years, with increased awareness of some of these dangers, the phenomenon of  ecotourism has developed. This has been seen as a way to prevent and reverse the  environmental degradation resulting from tourism as well as from general human activity.  Ecotourists distance themselves from mainstream hotel tourists, and also from adventure tourists like those who have, for example, left rubbish at the base of Mount Everest in Nepal.  They want to do things like sleep in tents on platforms in tropical rainforests, swim among the fish on coral reefs, or walk over a tree canopy on a net bridge. They value the ecosystem as it is  and are willing to pay for its preservation. In this way, they create an Incentive for local people to refrain from using natural resources for short-term gain.  </span></p><p></p><p><span>However, what is fortunate for the birds is not always so fortunate for the local people. In order to be environmentally sustainable, ecotourists have to be few in number. The economies of  scale that allow tourism to be profitable cannot operate in a small bush camp. Camping doesn''t  generate much revenue. Ecotourism proves that tourism does not have to be in conflict with  environmental'+N' preservation. Unfortunately, it also proves that the realm in which the  environment and the economy can be mutually supporting is tiny.   </span></p><p></p><p><span>Furthermore, Westerners'' interest in biodiversity often means that the tourist wants to listen to a parrot, not a local elder, and that dollars bypass beggars on their way to support trees. Thus ecotourists'' environmental awareness can stand in contrast to their indifference to the local  human community. Tourism always contains a message: this is what we value, this is what we don''t. </span></p>'),NULL,NULL,N'',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1054),
(N'a4d37fd3-1e00-565d-8de9-9535e058a90c',N'9942765b-547f-57a1-abac-f8089296c7a8',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the summary using the list of words below.</p><p>Drag the correct word or phrase from the list into each gap.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Tourism - an ideal export?',13,0,1,NULL,0,1054),
(N'1da01df4-c54d-5180-ad67-1a25db858041',N'a4d37fd3-1e00-565d-8de9-9535e058a90c',0,3,19,N'<h3>Tourism - an ideal export?</h3><p>A developing country needs money to build things like roads, which provide the necessary }{SPACE}{ for tourism.</p><p>The country can use taxes from residents or }{SPACE}{ from other countries to provide capital.</p><p>Or, money can be found by using }{SPACE}{ from abroad, which means the country will not receive any financial gain from what is built.</p><p>Tourism may be affected by natural disasters, political events and people''s }{SPACE}{ of how modern the resort is.</p><p>Even if the development of tourism is successful, a country has to  decide whether the industry''s }{SPACE}{ is desirable.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Một nước đang phát triển cần tiền để xây dựng những … như đường xá, cung cấp điều kiện cần thiết cho du lịch.</p><p>+ Answer: Danh sách các "features" như đường xá, sân bay là cơ sở hạ tầng cần thiết để phát triển du lịch. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là I</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1054),
(N'944398e5-ea22-5fcc-9c45-f026b3c5a61a',N'a4d37fd3-1e00-565d-8de9-9535e058a90c',0,3,19,N'Question number 28',N'<p>+ Synonym: loans from other countries (khoản vay từ các quốc gia khác) = foreign lenders (những nhà cho vay nước ngoài)</p><p>+ Interpretation: Đất nước có thể sử dụng thuế từ cư dân hoặc … từ các nước khác để cung cấp vốn.</p><p>+ Answer: Dựa vào thông tin trong bài và synonym, đất nước có thể vay từ các nước khác để cung cấp vốn </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là G</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1054),
(N'99108b6f-129b-591d-b57b-be2fbefb549a',N'a4d37fd3-1e00-565d-8de9-9535e058a90c',0,3,19,N'Question number 29',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Hoặc, tiền có thể có được bằng cách sử dụng …. từ nước ngoài, có nghĩa là đất nước sẽ không nhận được lợi nhuận từ những gì được xây dựng.</p><p>+ Answer: Việc thu hút đầu tư nước ngoài sẽ miễn trách nhiệm cung cấp vốn nhưng sẽ phải từ bỏ lợi nhuận </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1054),
(N'74415226-b88a-5459-83da-7dd7617885b1',N'a4d37fd3-1e00-565d-8de9-9535e058a90c',0,3,19,N'Question number 30',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Du lịch có thể bị ảnh hưởng bởi thiên tai, sự kiện chính trị và …. của mọi người về mức độ hiện đại của khu nghỉ dưỡng.</p><p>+ Answer: Sự suy giảm xảy ra khi một khu resort được coi là lỗi thời, đồng nghĩa với “perception” (sự nhận thức) của mọi người về khu nghỉ dưỡng </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là D</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1054),
(N'1e6a147d-fc26-5a44-aa10-7b0ae673ae73',N'a4d37fd3-1e00-565d-8de9-9535e058a90c',0,3,19,N'Question number 31',N'<p>+ Synonyms: extend its benefits (mở rộng lợi ích của nó) = expansion (sự mở rộng)</p><p>+ Interpretation: Ngay cả khi phát triển du lịch thành công, một quốc gia phải quyết định xem việc …. của ngành công nghiệp đó có đáng mong muốn không.</p><p>+ Answer: Ngay cả khi thành công, các quốc gia và địa phương phải đối mặt với vấn đề giữ ngành du lịch trong giới hạn hoặc mở rộng lợi ích của nó </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là F</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1054),
(N'dbbc076f-abdc-575f-a86e-0c5c52ebdcab',N'9942765b-547f-57a1-abac-f8089296c7a8',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, A, B, C, or D. Write the correct letter in boxes <span><span>32-35</span></span> on your answer sheet.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,2,NULL,0,1054),
(N'ad9cc0d1-ba5a-5317-ad1e-97ca91d5ecc4',N'dbbc076f-abdc-575f-a86e-0c5c52ebdcab',0,3,19,N'<p>The writer says that for tourists, an authentic experience </p>',N'<p>+ Synonym: created in an unnatural manner (tạo ra 1 cách thiếu tự nhiên) = through artifice (qua sự sắp đặt)</p><p>+ Interpretation: Tác giả nói rằng đối với khách du lịch, một trải nghiệm đích thực…</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng nhu cầu về sự đích thực chỉ có thể được đáp ứng thông qua sự sắp đặt </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là C: được tạo ra một cách không tự nhiên.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1054),
(N'c28a1efc-923e-5534-989a-cf9e35f0b658',N'dbbc076f-abdc-575f-a86e-0c5c52ebdcab',0,3,19,N'<p>The writer refers to the rules governing seating on a bus as an example of </p>',N'<p>+ Synonyms: tends to ignore (thường được lờ đi) = disregards</p><p>+ Interpretation: Tác giả đề cập đến các quy tắc về chỗ ngồi trên xe buýt như một ví dụ về …</p><p>+ Answer: Quy tắc ngồi xe buýt được đưa ra như một ví dụ cụ thể cho những thứ du khách không nhìn thấy được </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là D: một khía cạnh văn hóa mà du lịch có xu hướng bỏ qua.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1054),
(N'e48106ea-a51f-5ce9-b4a2-e1423761ca97',N'dbbc076f-abdc-575f-a86e-0c5c52ebdcab',0,3,19,N'<p>Tourists'' interest in traditionally-made objects may lead to changes in </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Sự quan tâm của khách du lịch vào các vật thể làm thủ công có thể dẫn đến thay đổi ở….</p><p>+ Answer: Để đáp ứng nhu cầu và sản xuất hiệu quả, hàng thủ công có thể được sản xuất bằng máy móc công nghiệp, một lựa chọn mà những người làm thủ công trong làng ko có </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A: cách chúng được làm ra</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1054),
(N'8345cbeb-ef2d-5ad4-93a7-a2d5b71a44cd',N'dbbc076f-abdc-575f-a86e-0c5c52ebdcab',0,3,19,N'<p>The writer says that in order to promote tourism, features such as folk dances </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Tác giả nói rằng để quảng bá du lịch, các đặc điểm như điệu múa dân gian có thể…..</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng du khách ko bị thu hút bởi những văn hóa ko ổn định, cho nên các điệu múa dân gian được cố định và chuẩn hóa (fix and standardize), ràng buộc và định nghĩa (bound and defined) </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là D: bị ngăn ko cho phát triển tự nhiên</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1054),
(N'79f116a0-12ab-5407-aea4-7d9ff77e802f',N'9942765b-547f-57a1-abac-f8089296c7a8',0,2,18,N'<p>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 3? In boxes 36-40 on your answer sheet, write <span><span>TRUE/ FALSE/ NOT GIVEN.</span></span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,3,NULL,0,1054),
(N'b2b9b756-ef4b-51e4-81f2-54877c7e48b0',N'79f116a0-12ab-5407-aea4-7d9ff77e802f',0,3,19,N'<p>Ecotourism can be more profitable than mass tourism because it requires fewer  facilities.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Du lịch sinh thái có thể sinh lời nhiều hơn du lịch đại chúng vì nó đòi hỏi ít cơ sở vật chất hơn.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng du lịch sinh thái quy mô nhỏ không thể tận dụng lợi thế kinh tế theo quy mô và không tạo ra nhiều doanh thu. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là False</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1054),
(N'8512bc31-e0d6-541a-8c96-3c602c9e2923',N'79f116a0-12ab-5407-aea4-7d9ff77e802f',0,3,19,N'<p>Limiting the number of ecotourists is necessary to protect the environment.   </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Việc hạn chế số lượng khách du lịch sinh thái là cần thiết để bảo vệ môi trường.</p><p>+ Answer: Thông tin trong bài nói rằng để bền vững về môi trường, số lượng khách du lịch sinh thái phải ít. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là True</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1054),
(N'f75a20e3-1b02-5f7f-abf0-d540a38a0db1',N'79f116a0-12ab-5407-aea4-7d9ff77e802f',0,3,19,N'<p>Some ecotourists prefer interacting with wildlife over engaging with local residents.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Một số khách du lịch sinh thái thích tương tác với động vật hoang dã hơn là giao tiếp với cư dân địa phương.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng khách du lịch muốn nghe tiếng vẹt (động vật hoang dã) hơn là nghe một bô lão địa phương (cư dân) </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là True</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1054),
(N'6043cef3-eab8-5a84-98a9-283575677056',N'79f116a0-12ab-5407-aea4-7d9ff77e802f',0,3,19,N'<p>Ecotourism always benefits both the local community and the environment equally. </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Du lịch sinh thái luôn mang lại lợi ích như nhau cho cả cộng đồng địa phương và môi trường.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn chỉ ra rằng sự có ý thức môi trường của khách du lịch sinh thái có thể tương phản với sự thờ ơ với cộng đồng địa phương </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là False</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1054),
(N'ece598e6-1388-5cff-98e1-0708b7f8d9e1',N'79f116a0-12ab-5407-aea4-7d9ff77e802f',0,3,19,N'<p>Tourism can influence a country’s political priorities. </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Du lịch có thể ảnh hưởng đến các ưu tiên chính trị của một quốc gia.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng để có được lợi nhuận thì một chính phủ sẽ phải làm hài lòng cả các nhà đầu tư và khách du lịch tiềm năng, cho nên du lịch có thể dẫn đến hệ thống chính trị trở nên chuyên quyền hơn và ít quan tâm đến nhu cầu của người dân </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là True</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1054),
(N'aea84bff-16a8-5a9c-ba40-bdd0d4ccae20',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'',N'Vol 5 READING FULL TEST 9',0,7,1,NULL,1,1049),
(N'05438092-8301-523b-a878-7fa7399e64ba',N'aea84bff-16a8-5a9c-ba40-bdd0d4ccae20',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<p><span><span><span>Australia''s cane toad problem</span></span><span><br></span></span><em>How a toad introduced into Australia to control pests has itself become a pest</em></p><p><br>In the north of Australia there are many sugar cane plantations, which early in the 20th century were being damaged by a particular pest. This was a species of beetle whose larvae, the infant form of the beetle, live underground in the soil in the sugar cane fields. The sugar cane plants were weakened or died because their roots were eaten by the larvae. This had serious economic consequences for sugar cane farmers. Modern pesticides were not developed until the 1940s, so farmers had to use what was available at the time. Chemicals like arsenic and copper were used, but these were not only expensive but also stayed in the environment and were poisonous to people, plants and animals. It was generally acknowledged by government, farmers and scientists that cheaper and safer methods of pest control had to be found.</p><p><br>A promising replacement for copper and arsenic was the use of biological control. Farmers already used some forms of biological pest control in the form of predatory and parasitic wasps and flies, insect-eating birds, and plants from different regions or countries to control pests. Common practice was to release these introduced agents into new environments, the expectation being that they would destroy resident pests. Some species of toad already had successful records as agents of biological control in gardens. For example, in 19th-century France toads were sold to gardeners at markets in Paris to eat insect pests in their gardens. In the early 20th century French sugar cane farmers first took giant toads from South America to control pests in their Caribbean sugar cane plantations. Although there is no evidence that these toads did help to control pests, sugar cane scientists then carried some of these toads from Jamaica and Barbados to Puerto Rico and from there to Hawaii.<br>The idea of biological control of pests was not new to Australia. For example, in 1926 there had been a highly successful prevention of the increase of the exotic prickly-pear cactus by the introduction of a moth from Argentina. This success added strength to the argument that biological control was the answer to the sugar cane industry''s pest problems. Accordingly, in the early 1930s a decision was taken to introduce the giant South American toads, which in Australia are now comm' AS nvarchar(max))+N'only called cane toads, into Australian sugar cane plantations.</p><p><br>In 1935, an Australian entomologist brought 101 cane toads from Hawaii and released them in sugar cane plantations in the north of Australia. However, over the following years it became clear that the cane toads were a failure. There was a fatal flaw in the plan to use them as a form of biological control. This was that earthbound cane toads were expected to eat the mostly flying adult beetles in order to eliminate the soil-dwelling beetle larvae that ate the roots of the cane sugar plants. This, of course, cane toads could not do.</p><p><br>Prior to their introduction in Australia, there had been very few opponents and only one made his views public. He was a retired former Chief Entomologist from the state government of New South Wales named Walter Froggatt. He forecast that cane toads might become as great a pest in Australia as rabbits. However, Froggatt''s peers rebuked him and eminent scientists branded his views ''decidedly pessimistic''. It is estimated that today as many as a hundred million cane toads form a toxic infestation which is slowly spreading throughout the land. <br><br>Cane toads are large, heavily built amphibians. Average-sized adults are 10-15 cm long and weigh more than a kilo. They have large swellings on each shoulder from which they squirt poison when they are threatened. This venom contains 14 different chemicals, but they do not appear to be harmful to humans as no-one has died in Australia from cane toad poison. Until recently there was no understanding of the toxicity of cane toad poison, but it is now dear that freshwater crocodiles, goannas (large lizards) and dingoes (wild dogs) have died after eating cane toads . Cane toads compete with native Australian fauna for food, and eat the eggs and young of ground-nesting birds. As their numbers increase, they are taking over more and more of the land where native Australian fauna live.<br>The lesson that can be learned from the introduction of cane toads is important. It is wrong to think that such an awful biological event could not be repeated. In this instance, the catalyst was the overwhelming consensus of support for introducing cane toads to Australia. The error was that there was little or no testing of these biological agents before they were introduced to see what unplanned effects they might have on the environment.<br></p>'),NULL,NULL,N'',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1049),
