(N'7b9b2701-3ead-579e-90a3-997f686cbb54',N'9290e829-1255-5340-aed6-9587ffb1c9c9',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>Cần chú ý cụm quyết định lựa chọn (I’ll take / I’ll go for / I prefer). Đây là câu chứa đáp án.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1026),
(N'd156ea47-d807-5050-a355-172d3dcf165d',N'34ad76a5-f386-5197-9798-1e617b4a17e1',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] What Darren did with his photographs</h2><p>Thanks for inviting me here today. I''ll start by showing you some of my photographs and explaining a bit of background about the red squirrel, and then give some general advice for those of you who might be interested in doing some nature photography of your own. So let''s look at my first slide: a young red squirrel. Its Latin name, Sciurus vulgaris, derives from the Greek word skia meaning shadow and oura meaning tail. I thought this was a bit funny at first and couldn''t make any sense of this phase until I realized that it translated as an animal sitting in the shadow of its tail. And then it all fell into place because that''s what the red squirrel does. This photo was taken right outside my back door. Before I started, I set myself the goal of taking all these photographs in my garden, but using different kinds of camera and incorporating a variety of different contextual features, squirrels amongst birds for example. But why such a fascination with this animal? Well, the red squirrel was once common throughout much of the country.</p><p>In the late 19th century, the American grey squirrel was introduced into this country. Some people believe that they brought a disease with them that reduced the red squirrel population. But the decline of the red species had already started long before the greys came to the country. It’s to do with fewer nuts, which reds love to eat, being available. The only place reds seem to thrive now is in one or two ancient forests. How did I go about capturing them on film? Well, a wildlife photographer friend of mine advised me to use lure or bait of hazelnuts. He told me that you have to attract them over several months by leaving out, of course, lots of nuts. Other animals love to eat hazelnuts, but evidence of red squirrels is clear. Mice, for example, gnaw a neat hole through the shell, whereas a red squirrel picks up the nut, spins it until it finds a fault line, and splits it in two with one bite, removing the nut from within. I soon began to see signs of red squirrel visits to my patch, and I knew that I''d eventually see the animals themselves. I was amazed by how soon they started arriving, jumping delightfully around the trees and clotheslines. At first, they were very reluctant to come within a ten meter radius of me, but soon they began to recognize the hand that fed them, and they boldly approached me and I began to get some pretty remarkable photographs. It''s ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'curious, but the actual photography side of things didn''t bother me too much. Although I had had only a little practice and my cameras were largely secondhand, I''d had a very good mentor and picked up a lot of tips from her. It was really the attracting of the animals and capturing them in the most natural situations that I was most concerned about. I exhibited a selection of them in the local museum when friends advised me to show them to a wider audience.</p><p>Also, much to my surprise, I won third prize in a competition organized by a national newspaper. But what advice can I give to any of you who might be thinking of embarking on something similar? Well, Tracy, my photography expert friend and I, spent a bit of time thinking about this, and we came up with the following five points specifically related to nature photography. First of all, although a good camera is always of great importance, the crucial requirement for this kind of work must be a tripod. The level of detail needed, not to mention the weight of a good zoom lens, means that simply bracing your arm won''t do. Next, our review of a large number of outstanding photographs tells us that just having the animal in shot can be misleading. Make sure you set up the shot in such a way that what is named a secondary object is in view. It can be a branch, flowers, masonry, whatever you want. This helps portray the prime subject. Next, and this might seem bizarre, take care to look behind you when you''re setting up a shot. The number of times I''ve found that the most original new shot is in the very place I''ve just passed!</p><p>Now, it''s a well-known dictum in photography that midday or early afternoon light has the effect of bleaching true color. Late afternoon light is my favorite, with its warm red colors. Morning light can be beautiful but has a starker yellow glow. And finally, if you''re at all serious about your nature or wildlife photography, take the trouble to make detailed notes on all your shots. Mention information on time, equipment settings, etc., and this will work like a recipe book to help you recognize what contributed to successful shots.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Thanks for inviting me here today. I''ll start by showing you some of my photographs and explaining a bit of background about the red squirrel, and then give some general advice for those of you who might be interested in doing some nature photography of your own. So let''s look at my first slide: a young red squirrel. Its Latin name, Sciurus vulgaris, derives from the Greek word skia meaning shadow and oura meaning tail. I thought this was a bit funny at first and couldn''t make any sense of this phase until I realized that it translated as an animal sitting in the shadow of its tail. And then it all fell into place because that''s what the red squirrel does. This photo was taken right outside my back door. Before I started, I set myself the goal of taking all these photographs in my garden, but using different kinds of camera and incorporating a variety of different contextual features, squirrels amongst birds for example. But why such a fascination with this animal? Well, the red squirrel was once common throughout much of the country.</p><p>In the late 19th century, the American grey squirrel was introduced into this country. Some people believe that they brought a disease with them that reduced the red squirrel population. But the decline of the red species had already started long before the greys came to the country. It’s to do with fewer nuts, which reds love to eat, being available. The only place reds seem to thrive now is in one or two ancient forests. How did I go about capturing them on film? Well, a wildlife photographer friend of mine advised me to use lure or bait of hazelnuts. He told me that you have to attract them over several months by leaving out, of course, lots of nuts. Other animals love to eat hazelnuts, but evidence of red squirrels is clear. Mice, for example, gnaw a neat hole through the shell, whereas a red squirrel picks up the nut, spins it until it finds a fault line, and splits it in two with one bite, removing the nut from within. I soon began to see signs of red squirrel visits to my patch, and I knew that I''d eventually see the animals themselves. I was amazed by how soon they started arriving, jumping delightfully around the trees and clotheslines. At first, they were very reluctant to come within a ten meter radius of me, but soon they began to recognize the hand that fed them, and they boldly approached me and I began to get some pretty remarkable photographs. It''s curious, but the actual photography side of things di' AS nvarchar(max))+N'dn''t bother me too much. Although I had had only a little practice and my cameras were largely secondhand, I''d had a very good mentor and picked up a lot of tips from her. It was really the attracting of the animals and capturing them in the most natural situations that I was most concerned about. I exhibited a selection of them in the local museum when friends advised me to show them to a wider audience.</p><p>Also, much to my surprise, I won third prize in a competition organized by a national newspaper. But what advice can I give to any of you who might be thinking of embarking on something similar? Well, Tracy, my photography expert friend and I, spent a bit of time thinking about this, and we came up with the following five points specifically related to nature photography. First of all, although a good camera is always of great importance, the crucial requirement for this kind of work must be a tripod. The level of detail needed, not to mention the weight of a good zoom lens, means that simply bracing your arm won''t do. Next, our review of a large number of outstanding photographs tells us that just having the animal in shot can be misleading. Make sure you set up the shot in such a way that what is named a secondary object is in view. It can be a branch, flowers, masonry, whatever you want. This helps portray the prime subject. Next, and this might seem bizarre, take care to look behind you when you''re setting up a shot. The number of times I''ve found that the most original new shot is in the very place I''ve just passed!</p><p>Now, it''s a well-known dictum in photography that midday or early afternoon light has the effect of bleaching true color. Late afternoon light is my favorite, with its warm red colors. Morning light can be beautiful but has a starker yellow glow. And finally, if you''re at all serious about your nature or wildlife photography, take the trouble to make detailed notes on all your shots. Mention information on time, equipment settings, etc., and this will work like a recipe book to help you recognize what contributed to successful shots.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.9.S1026.PART2.mp3',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1026),
(N'd6f0d469-c984-59c2-a77b-d19c7f1b841c',N'd156ea47-d807-5050-a355-172d3dcf165d',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A, B or C.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,1,NULL,0,1026),
(N'c2fd148a-d626-542d-96d6-5bd2342dae0a',N'd6f0d469-c984-59c2-a77b-d19c7f1b841c',0,3,19,N'<p>Darren now finds the Latin name for the red squirrel</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là C vì Darren thấy cái tên khá hài hước và đúng với hành vi con sóc.
A sai vì không nói nó “cực kỳ phù hợp”, mà chỉ “buồn cười”.
B sai vì không hề “vô nghĩa”.

</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1026),
(N'ff9dc3d2-429a-5122-8ac2-9e92a5208fd3',N'd6f0d469-c984-59c2-a77b-d19c7f1b841c',0,3,19,N'<p>Which restriction did Darren set himself for this project?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là C vì Darren chỉ chụp ở vườn.
B sai vì ông dùng nhiều loại máy ảnh, không phải một.
A sai vì ông không giới hạn chỉ chụp sóc.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1026),
(N'2d57cbb9-a49b-5fc1-b219-c236c1109611',N'd6f0d469-c984-59c2-a77b-d19c7f1b841c',0,3,19,N'<p>Darren thinks the red squirrel’s decline was caused by</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là C vì nguyên nhân chính là thiếu thức ăn (hạt dẻ).
A sai vì sóc xám chỉ đến sau khi số lượng sóc đỏ đã giảm.
B sai vì Darren phủ nhận “bệnh dịch” là nguyên nhân chính.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1026),
(N'b2b1f582-40fe-56b3-aaa6-97c21920fe9a',N'd6f0d469-c984-59c2-a77b-d19c7f1b841c',0,3,19,N'<p>Darren knew when red squirrels rather than mice had taken the bait because of</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là B vì Darren phân biệt dựa trên cách hạt được ăn.
A sai vì loại thức ăn giống nhau (đều là hạt dẻ). C sai vì không liên quan đến lượng thức ăn còn lại.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1026),
(N'17bd6e54-ea67-5096-9f46-3f76f1cf9c3d',N'd6f0d469-c984-59c2-a77b-d19c7f1b841c',0,3,19,N'<p>Darren was confident about the quality of his photographs because</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là B vì Darren nhấn mạnh vai trò người thầy. A sai vì ông nói ít luyện tập.
C sai vì máy ảnh chỉ là đồ cũ, không phải chất lượng cao.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1026),
(N'35e67e3d-891f-5c19-8fe7-615cb82e12aa',N'd156ea47-d807-5050-a355-172d3dcf165d',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below. Write <span>NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'What Darren did with his photographs',11,0,2,NULL,0,1026),
(N'6d6afa9d-4305-59b4-a67d-dca28a182d8d',N'35e67e3d-891f-5c19-8fe7-615cb82e12aa',0,3,19,N'<h3>What Darren did with his photographs</h3><ul><li><p>exhibited them in a</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p><ul><li><p>submitted them for a competition in a</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p><p><span>Darren’s advice for beginner nature photographers</span></p><ul><li><p>Your most important piece of equipment is a tripod.</p></li></ul><ul><li><p>Include a so-called</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ to give a sense of scale.</p><ul><li><p>Check you are taking your shot from the best position.</p></li></ul><ul><li><p>The quality of light is best late in the</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ </p><ul><li><p>You should make</p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ for each photo.</p>',N'<p>Đáp án: Ông trưng bày ảnh tại một bảo tàng địa phương.
 Giải thích:
 Cần lưu ý khi nghe cụm “local museum”, vì dễ nhầm với “gallery” hoặc “art center”. Từ “exhibited” cho biết hành động trưng bày triển lãm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1026),
(N'7f5396bc-91b0-5256-99ed-f70887775a6b',N'35e67e3d-891f-5c19-8fe7-615cb82e12aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 17',N'<p>Giải thích:
 Nghe cụm “competition organized by…” → xác định ngay người hoặc tổ chức chịu trách nhiệm (ở đây là national newspaper).</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1026),
(N'ad7e8977-9d74-5c61-9453-1d67840bee1b',N'35e67e3d-891f-5c19-8fe7-615cb82e12aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 18',N'<p>Giải thích:
 Cần chú ý cụm “in view”, nghĩa là đối tượng phụ phải nằm trong khung hình khi chụp.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1026),
(N'43dd6260-40d7-50e2-8a1e-21788fabf212',N'35e67e3d-891f-5c19-8fe7-615cb82e12aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 19',N'<p>Giải thích:
 Từ khóa “late afternoon light” thể hiện thời điểm và đặc điểm ánh sáng (“warm red colors”).
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1026),
(N'e078335e-b66d-5b73-91bf-13534ff67b32',N'35e67e3d-891f-5c19-8fe7-615cb82e12aa',0,3,19,N'Question number 20',N'<p>Giải thích:
 Cụm “take the trouble to…” mang nghĩa “chịu khó làm điều gì đó”. “Detailed notes” là phần ghi chép tỉ mỉ giúp rút kinh nghiệm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1026),
(N'fe689b3a-0ba3-5cee-974c-1fc5872df450',N'34ad76a5-f386-5197-9798-1e617b4a17e1',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Choosing electives</h2><p>Hi, Anita. Did you see that the information about the electives we can choose from this year has just gone up on the class website? Really? Great! Do you want to look at it together? We''d better sign up as soon as possible. I guess so, but it says on the site that we can''t register any choices until 3 o''clock. Oh, OK. But we need to get a move on. Some people told me last year that they got turned away from a couple of the choices they wanted because they were too slow to decide. Let''s have a look at it all on my laptop then. It''s pretty complicated working out which classes are compulsory and what restrictions there are on your choices.  Yes, we all have to do a language class of some kind, don''t we? I think that''s a really good idea. That''s not quite right. We can do a business class, or a philosophy or economics one instead if we want. The required classes are Thermodynamics, Machine System Dynamics, and Mathematics. Oh no, I was sure we had finished with that last year. Well, I suppose it''s important for us. Hmm, guess you must be right. So let''s look at the descriptions of the topics covered by the classes we can choose from. </p><p>Can you get them up on the screen? Sure. The information also tells you how the classes are graded. Some of them have exams, but others just have classwork. Yes, but that shouldn''t influence our choices, should it? I''d say it''d be more important to try to find out how good the professor is. True. That can make or break any class. Well, we''ll have to ask the seniors to get that kind of information.  True. I know several people have recommended the Statistics class, but it would mean a very early start on Monday morning. Well, that''s not a bad thing, is it? What puts me off more is that everyone says it''s very hard work. Oh, I don''t know. Not if you''ve done lots of stats before. And I did a special elective on it in high school. And it''s very relevant, isn''t it? We''re bound to need it whatever work we end up doing. Fair enough. Then there''s an Art and Design class. My advisor said he wouldn''t recommend doing that one. Really? Well, he said lots of people think it''s an easy elective, but that actually it''s very time-consuming. I wouldn''t have expected that. Well, I thought it might be, but it''s useful to know for sure, isn''t it? Sometimes people''s advice can make decisions even harder, but I found that really helpful. Hmm, what I don''t know is the amount of practical work each class involves. I''d' AS nvarchar(max))+N' like to find that out. No, it''s there. Look, if you click on that link, you''ll get all that sort of information. Great. And the class times are there too. So, how do we tell them when we''ve made our choices? Maybe we need to go and see our advisors, or is there something we have to do online? Okay, let''s go and talk to the secretary and find out. Good idea.  </p><p>How about thinking about the electives we did last year? Maybe that would help us decide. Okay, we both did Metallurgy, didn''t we? It was pretty good, I think. It involved a lot of library work, but I quite enjoyed that. Yes, it was definitely useful and well-structured, but it was the most difficult class I chose. I didn''t think it would be that bad. Neither did I. But I didn''t mind because some of my other electives were fairly straightforward. Yes, even Fluid Mechanics wasn''t too bad. No, the online tutorials weren''t much help, but the professor made some hard stuff seem pretty easy. Yes, he was great at simplifying concepts that were actually really hard to understand. Mmm. You did Computing too, didn''t you? What was that like? It was okay. The later classes were much better than the earlier ones. Initially, I didn''t like it much, but I liked it once we started getting more hands-on stuff. And what did you think about the Energy Economics course we both did? Well, it was useful except for the notes on the Internet. They were pretty disappointing. Mmm, I liked the way it wasn''t too theoretical, though, and the fact that much of it was based on solving real problems. It really helped me get better at working through the questions we''d have to answer for our coursework. Yes, but more input from our advisors might have been helpful, don''t you think?</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Hi, Anita. Did you see that the information about the electives we can choose from this year has just gone up on the class website? Really? Great! Do you want to look at it together? We''d better sign up as soon as possible. I guess so, but it says on the site that we can''t register any choices until 3 o''clock. Oh, OK. But we need to get a move on. Some people told me last year that they got turned away from a couple of the choices they wanted because they were too slow to decide. Let''s have a look at it all on my laptop then. It''s pretty complicated working out which classes are compulsory and what restrictions there are on your choices.  Yes, we all have to do a language class of some kind, don''t we? I think that''s a really good idea. That''s not quite right. We can do a business class, or a philosophy or economics one instead if we want. The required classes are Thermodynamics, Machine System Dynamics, and Mathematics. Oh no, I was sure we had finished with that last year. Well, I suppose it''s important for us. Hmm, guess you must be right. So let''s look at the descriptions of the topics covered by the classes we can choose from. </p><p>Can you get them up on the screen? Sure. The information also tells you how the classes are graded. Some of them have exams, but others just have classwork. Yes, but that shouldn''t influence our choices, should it? I''d say it''d be more important to try to find out how good the professor is. True. That can make or break any class. Well, we''ll have to ask the seniors to get that kind of information.  True. I know several people have recommended the Statistics class, but it would mean a very early start on Monday morning. Well, that''s not a bad thing, is it? What puts me off more is that everyone says it''s very hard work. Oh, I don''t know. Not if you''ve done lots of stats before. And I did a special elective on it in high school. And it''s very relevant, isn''t it? We''re bound to need it whatever work we end up doing. Fair enough. Then there''s an Art and Design class. My advisor said he wouldn''t recommend doing that one. Really? Well, he said lots of people think it''s an easy elective, but that actually it''s very time-consuming. I wouldn''t have expected that. Well, I thought it might be, but it''s useful to know for sure, isn''t it? Sometimes people''s advice can make decisions even harder, but I found that really helpful. Hmm, what I don''t know is the amount of practical work each class involves. I''d like to find that out. No,' AS nvarchar(max))+N' it''s there. Look, if you click on that link, you''ll get all that sort of information. Great. And the class times are there too. So, how do we tell them when we''ve made our choices? Maybe we need to go and see our advisors, or is there something we have to do online? Okay, let''s go and talk to the secretary and find out. Good idea.  </p><p>How about thinking about the electives we did last year? Maybe that would help us decide. Okay, we both did Metallurgy, didn''t we? It was pretty good, I think. It involved a lot of library work, but I quite enjoyed that. Yes, it was definitely useful and well-structured, but it was the most difficult class I chose. I didn''t think it would be that bad. Neither did I. But I didn''t mind because some of my other electives were fairly straightforward. Yes, even Fluid Mechanics wasn''t too bad. No, the online tutorials weren''t much help, but the professor made some hard stuff seem pretty easy. Yes, he was great at simplifying concepts that were actually really hard to understand. Mmm. You did Computing too, didn''t you? What was that like? It was okay. The later classes were much better than the earlier ones. Initially, I didn''t like it much, but I liked it once we started getting more hands-on stuff. And what did you think about the Energy Economics course we both did? Well, it was useful except for the notes on the Internet. They were pretty disappointing. Mmm, I liked the way it wasn''t too theoretical, though, and the fact that much of it was based on solving real problems. It really helped me get better at working through the questions we''d have to answer for our coursework. Yes, but more input from our advisors might have been helpful, don''t you think?</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.9.S1026.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1026),
(N'7e75b912-ec34-5f55-bd9c-851ca3e19940',N'fe689b3a-0ba3-5cee-974c-1fc5872df450',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A, B or C.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Choosing electives',1,0,1,NULL,0,1026),
(N'23a08802-b5e1-5aae-9c38-bee575c79501',N'7e75b912-ec34-5f55-bd9c-851ca3e19940',0,3,19,N'<p>Why do Rick and Anita decide to discuss their electives immediately?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là B vì họ sợ lớp sẽ hết chỗ nhanh.
A sai vì script nói rõ “we can’t register any choices until 3 o’clock”, không phải bắt buộc chọn ngay.
C sai vì không đề cập đến việc họ bận sau này.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,1026),
(N'7d5117a5-e0a4-559d-b6b7-0da4745c3917',N'7e75b912-ec34-5f55-bd9c-851ca3e19940',0,3,19,N'<p>What is included in the compulsory courses for all students?</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là C vì Mathematics là bắt buộc.
A sai vì language class chỉ là lựa chọn, không bắt buộc.
B sai vì business class cũng chỉ là lựa chọn thay thế cho philosophy/economics.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,1026),
(N'96a8c087-1edb-5b91-8f46-1331e52448b6',N'7e75b912-ec34-5f55-bd9c-851ca3e19940',0,3,19,N'<p>In order to choose a new class Rick and Anita will</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là A vì họ quyết định hỏi sinh viên năm trên.
B sai vì không hề nhắc đến hỏi giáo sư.
C sai vì dù có nói “Some of them have exams…”, nhưng họ bảo điều này không nên ảnh hưởng đến quyết định.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,1026),
(N'9bb28515-5a73-5679-8f51-a5e7e846d326',N'7e75b912-ec34-5f55-bd9c-851ca3e19940',0,3,19,N'<p>Rick and Anita agree that the Statistics class</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là C vì cả hai đều đồng ý rằng lớp này nhiều việc.
A sai vì họ chỉ nói “useful” nhưng chưa thống nhất.
B sai vì tuy đề cập đến “very early start”, nhưng họ không xem đó là vấn đề chính.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,1026),
(N'd81d7999-b619-5667-ba57-e1acf4c3733f',N'7e75b912-ec34-5f55-bd9c-851ca3e19940',0,3,19,N'<p>Anita found her adviser’s comment about the Art and Design course</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là C vì mặc dù lúc đầu bạn nam thấy thấy bất ngờ về lời khuyên của giảng viên, bạn nữ vẫn thấy nó “helpful”
B sai vì không hề nhầm lẫn; thông tin rõ ràng. A sai vì bạn nam bất ngờ
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1026),
(N'8b6263c0-e2b9-5b57-b246-6e755079880e',N'7e75b912-ec34-5f55-bd9c-851ca3e19940',0,3,19,N'<p>Rick and Anita are both unsure about</p>',N'<p>Đáp án đúng là A vì họ cùng băn khoăn cách đăng kí
C sai vì chỉ một người thắc mắc về số lượng
B sai vì lịch học đã có sẵn trong website.
</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1026),
(N'156d4e02-14e9-50c0-87a5-ed19dc40bffa',N'fe689b3a-0ba3-5cee-974c-1fc5872df450',0,2,18,N'<p>Which opinion is expressed about each of the following electives the students did last
year?
Choose <span>FOUR</span> answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span>A–F</span>, next to questions
27–30.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,2,NULL,0,1026),
(N'1c9390fc-a610-5366-a4e2-89797b7844e1',N'156d4e02-14e9-50c0-87a5-ed19dc40bffa',0,3,19,N'<p>Metallurgy</p>',N'<p>Giải thích: Họ đều thấy môn này khó hơn mong đợi. Không chọn các đáp án khác vì đoạn hội thoại không nhắc đến tài liệu online, giảng viên, hay thực hành.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1026),
(N'0447fc0b-5321-510b-8789-db469704838f',N'156d4e02-14e9-50c0-87a5-ed19dc40bffa',0,3,19,N'<p>Fluid Mechanics</p>',N'<p>Giải thích: Giáo sư giảng dễ hiểu, giúp sinh viên nắm vững kiến thức. Đáp án khác sai vì không nói tới tài liệu online tốt (ngược lại còn chê), cũng không nhắc “harder than expected.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1026),
(N'c5432c93-0066-577f-9c6f-0c47aa924781',N'156d4e02-14e9-50c0-87a5-ed19dc40bffa',0,3,19,N'<p>Computing</p>',N'<p>Giải thích: Giáo sư giảng dễ hiểu, giúp sinh viên nắm vững kiến thức. Đáp án khác sai vì không nói tới tài liệu online tốt (ngược lại còn chê), cũng không nhắc “harder than expected.”</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1026),
(N'0f6d4752-45c5-5d3a-92c2-09bd159cd274',N'156d4e02-14e9-50c0-87a5-ed19dc40bffa',0,3,19,N'<p>Energy Economics</p>',N'<p>Giải thích: Người nói chê “notes on the Internet” là “disappointing” → tài liệu online không hữu ích, nên không chọn E. Thay vào đó, họ khen khóa học “wasn’t too theoretical” và “based on solving real problems” → nghĩa là có nhiều thực hành → chọn C.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1026),
(N'5398be61-d5b5-54f7-aab4-2875a57cc5db',N'34ad76a5-f386-5197-9798-1e617b4a17e1',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Plastic exhibition</h2><p>Good morning, my name''s Susan Mossman and as well as being a part-time lecturer on the University''s Museum Science degree, I''m also curator of a new exhibition opening at the Bidmouth Museum called Plasticity. This traces the history of plastics and ways they are being developed and used today. The first type of plastic was originally developed in response to a call from the makers of sports equipment. Animal-derived materials were becoming extremely expensive, so manufacturers launched a competition to find an alternative to ivory for making pool and billiard balls. Although various forms of plastic were developed, none of them were good enough to win the competition. &nbsp;  </p><p>But the inventors knew they were on to a good thing and started researching a variety of different substances, partly based on organic material, what we call semi-synthetics. One of the early semi-synthetic materials produced was the first commercially produced celluloid, or plastic film, which was made by dissolving cotton in a strong acid, such as nitric acid. Unfortunately, this had a tendency to explode, so technicians had to be very careful with it. They also experimented with making plastics out of products derived from milk, but this didn''t really work and efforts were therefore concentrated on other organic material. One of the first uses of plastic was in the production of artificial fabric, and the earliest successful one was a substitute for silk. This was obviously a very expensive fiber, so artificial alternatives were thought to be worth investing in. The next significant breakthrough came in 1907 with the invention of Bakelite. This was the first entirely synthetic plastic made in a laboratory. It was invented by Hendrik Bakeland, a chemist who had made a fortune from producing paper used in photographic processes. Bakelite is a pretty unusual type of plastic because it actually gets hard when it''s heated, whereas most other plastic becomes more malleable when hot. That is, they become more plastic, which is of course where the name for the category of materials comes from. So, strictly speaking, Bakelite isn''t a plastic at all. </p><p>But now let''s look at the exhibition itself. In it we show a variety of semi-synthetic and totally synthetic plastics, some of them dating back a hundred years. Some of the older plastics on display are unstable. This means that when they are in the process of degrading, they may produce gases which a' AS nvarchar(max))+N're acidic and which can cause neighboring objects in the display to disintegrate. We had to be very careful with these displays because there is one thing which causes plastic objects to degrade or break down, and that is light. So we''ve had to think of ways to get around this problem when setting up the objects on display. &nbsp;   </p><p>We have about 850 objects in the exhibition. One of our most interesting is a magnificent chandelier, a lamp designed to hang in the center of the ceiling of a big hall. It''s only when you look carefully that you can see this is a wonderful example of recycling. It''s made entirely of pens, ones which we use and throw away every day. A second exhibit which is drawing a lot of attention is a car made entirely from plastic, which is now in small-scale production in Japan.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Good morning, my name''s Susan Mossman and as well as being a part-time lecturer on the University''s Museum Science degree, I''m also curator of a new exhibition opening at the Bidmouth Museum called Plasticity. This traces the history of plastics and ways they are being developed and used today. The first type of plastic was originally developed in response to a call from the makers of sports equipment. Animal-derived materials were becoming extremely expensive, so manufacturers launched a competition to find an alternative to ivory for making pool and billiard balls. Although various forms of plastic were developed, none of them were good enough to win the competition. &nbsp;  </p><p>But the inventors knew they were on to a good thing and started researching a variety of different substances, partly based on organic material, what we call semi-synthetics. One of the early semi-synthetic materials produced was the first commercially produced celluloid, or plastic film, which was made by dissolving cotton in a strong acid, such as nitric acid. Unfortunately, this had a tendency to explode, so technicians had to be very careful with it. They also experimented with making plastics out of products derived from milk, but this didn''t really work and efforts were therefore concentrated on other organic material. One of the first uses of plastic was in the production of artificial fabric, and the earliest successful one was a substitute for silk. This was obviously a very expensive fiber, so artificial alternatives were thought to be worth investing in. The next significant breakthrough came in 1907 with the invention of Bakelite. This was the first entirely synthetic plastic made in a laboratory. It was invented by Hendrik Bakeland, a chemist who had made a fortune from producing paper used in photographic processes. Bakelite is a pretty unusual type of plastic because it actually gets hard when it''s heated, whereas most other plastic becomes more malleable when hot. That is, they become more plastic, which is of course where the name for the category of materials comes from. So, strictly speaking, Bakelite isn''t a plastic at all. </p><p>But now let''s look at the exhibition itself. In it we show a variety of semi-synthetic and totally synthetic plastics, some of them dating back a hundred years. Some of the older plastics on display are unstable. This means that when they are in the process of degrading, they may produce gases which are acidic and which can cau' AS nvarchar(max))+N'se neighboring objects in the display to disintegrate. We had to be very careful with these displays because there is one thing which causes plastic objects to degrade or break down, and that is light. So we''ve had to think of ways to get around this problem when setting up the objects on display. &nbsp;   </p><p>We have about 850 objects in the exhibition. One of our most interesting is a magnificent chandelier, a lamp designed to hang in the center of the ceiling of a big hall. It''s only when you look carefully that you can see this is a wonderful example of recycling. It''s made entirely of pens, ones which we use and throw away every day. A second exhibit which is drawing a lot of attention is a car made entirely from plastic, which is now in small-scale production in Japan.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.9.S1026.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1026),
(N'9bc34503-8c1f-51c8-8346-a08b39d2a485',N'5398be61-d5b5-54f7-aab4-2875a57cc5db',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.
Write <span>ONE WORD ONLY</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Plastic exhibition',11,0,1,NULL,0,1026),
(N'7d89f7b1-78e9-5f74-8b13-2cedb80f1b18',N'9bc34503-8c1f-51c8-8346-a08b39d2a485',0,3,19,N'<h3>Plastic exhibition</h3><p><span>History</span></p><p>• First plastic developed to replace ivory in sports equipment.</p><p>• Semi-synthetics, e.g., commercial celluloid used }{SPACE}{ and acid.</p><p>• Experiments making plastics from }{SPACE}{ products were unsuccessful.</p><p>• Earliest fabric to be produced was artificial }{SPACE}{ </p><p>• First plastic produced in a }{SPACE}{ </p><p>• Invented by a }{SPACE}{ called Hendrik Baekeland.</p><p>• Unusual because it becomes }{SPACE}{ when heated.</p><p><span>Exhibition</span></p><p><span>Problems with display:</span></p><p>• Older plastic objects may release acidic }{SPACE}{ </p><p>• Plastic objects degrade in }{SPACE}{ </p><p><span>Popular exhibits:</span></p><p>• Chandelier made using recycled }{SPACE}{ </p><p>• }{SPACE}{ , made in Japan from semi-synthetic materials based on substances found in plantations.</p>',N'<p>Giải thích: Câu hỏi: “Semi-synthetics, e.g. commercial celluloid used ___ and acid.” → cotton kết hợp với acid.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1026),
(N'f7ef5633-3496-5f45-af7a-2fe53a2c5c6c',N'9bc34503-8c1f-51c8-8346-a08b39d2a485',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>Giải thích: Câu hỏi: “Experiments making plastics from ___ products were unsuccessful.” → milk.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1026),
