(N'576313a0-5b7a-5a21-a0ea-8c50a1c651fc',N'fbfb6e9e-24ac-5ffe-acb0-c926dc6f99ee',0,3,19,N'<h3>Procedure to Reduce Copying Waste</h3> }{SPACE}{ that the staff do double-sided copying</p><p>↓</p><p>Unwanted copies are }{SPACE}{ into a special recycling tray</p><p>↓</p> }{SPACE}{ these clearly, for easy reuse</p><p>↓</p><p>Stack them in a special copying tray each morning</p><p>↓</p> }{SPACE}{ codes which allow each user to access this tray</p><p>↓</p><p>Give highest users a }{SPACE}{ (e.g. cinema tickets)</p>',N'<p>Question: “___ that the staff do double-sided copying?” → Cần động từ thể hiện yêu cầu/bắt buộc. </p><p>Answer: Người nói nhấn mạnh ensure → ensure. </p><p>Key vocab: ensure = đảm bảo, double-sided = hai mặt.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1025),
(N'965f702c-3ec1-525a-b3f5-347fd063cf50',N'fbfb6e9e-24ac-5ffe-acb0-c926dc6f99ee',0,3,19,N'Question number 27',N'<p>Question: “Unwanted copies are ___ into a special recycling tray?” → Cần động từ. </p><p>Answer: Được nêu rõ là deposited. </p><p>Key vocab: deposited = bỏ vào, recycling tray = khay tái chế.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1025),
(N'7ce5fe8b-73e5-513b-8030-03d76d5db713',N'fbfb6e9e-24ac-5ffe-acb0-c926dc6f99ee',0,3,19,N'Question number 28',N'<p>Question: “___ these clearly, for easy reuse?” → Cần động từ. </p><p>Answer: Người nói dùng từ display → đáp án là display. </p><p>Key vocab: display = trưng bày, clearly = rõ ràng.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1025),
(N'01f4caa9-70e1-57bc-91f7-c4247b10d944',N'fbfb6e9e-24ac-5ffe-acb0-c926dc6f99ee',0,3,19,N'Question number 29',N'<p>Question: “___ codes which allow each user to access this tray?” → Cần động từ. </p><p>Answer: Người nói distribute numbers or codes → đáp án là distribute. Key vocab: distribute = phân phát, codes = mã.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1025),
(N'd16b3fe5-c596-578f-9a52-67bf9db9f390',N'fbfb6e9e-24ac-5ffe-acb0-c926dc6f99ee',0,3,19,N'Question number 30',N'<p>Question: “Give highest users a ___ (e.g., cinema tickets)?” → Cần danh từ. </p><p>Answer: Câu nêu rõ reward → đáp án là reward. </p><p>Key vocab: reward = phần thưởng, cinema pass = ví dụ minh họa.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1025),
(N'5a7cb9af-b1ba-5488-a755-ffce5b7b5a49',N'b63bf087-958e-5c61-9f20-6e8cea65909e',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Transhumance</h2><p>Good afternoon everyone. Today I''m going to talk about what may be a new concept to you: the movement of people with their herds of animals from place to place. This is known as transhumance. These peoples are pastoralists, and their animals include cows, sheep, goats, camels, and reindeer. Now, researchers sometimes make a distinction between two types of transhumance: fixed and nomadic, and talk about various factors which differentiate between them. The first and most important distinction they make is to do with the climate. Fixed transhumance occurs in countries such as Switzerland, where seasons are predictable in terms of weather and timing, nomadic transhumans, on the other hand, are generally practiced in areas where climatic conditions are difficult to predict. An example of such a location is Somalia, as the anticipated rains often just don''t appear.</p><p>Secondly, researchers sometimes look at the type of journey that pastoralists make: their movement. For fixed transhumans, the pattern is sometimes known as vertical, because it typically consists of a journey from lower ground in the winter season to higher ground in the summer, and vice versa. In the wild, of course, animals frequently follow these kinds of migration patterns themselves. Nomadic transhumans generally take place over a longer distance, and it''s far more complex. The actual pattern varies from year to year, and it''s determined primarily by the animals'' needs for grass and also for water. Another distinction is sometimes made in terms of homes. In the case of fixed transhumans, the group will typically have permanent homes in the valley and a shelter which is semi permanent in a mountain, such as a cabin, to offer some protection from the weather. 00:02:07 In nomadic transhumans, however, the pastoralists always live in temporary shelters which are transported by their livestock from place to place. And are usually light and portable. In northern Kenya, for example the Gabra pastoralist, he carries the frame of his home on his camel and then covers it with branches when he stops for the night. A fourth factor often mentioned is the number of people taking part. In the case of fixed transhumans, only the people who actually look after the herds travel. These are normally the young ones because they can withstand adverse physical conditions more easily. By contrast, in nomadic transhumans, the whole group moves with their livestock. The last points' AS nvarchar(max))+N' of comparison often made by researchers are diet and goods. </p><p>In fixed transhumans, those who do not move from the valleys often start working as farmers, cultivating the build-up, contacts with other communities, and in some cases, they may also trade animals for grain. In nomadic transhumans, there are fewer contacts. The group is generally regarded as providers of all their own needs, and they obtain food such as cheese and meat, and also their clothing from their herds. Another example is found in Algeria, in North Africa. To see how far this model applies to transhumans, to obtain food such as cheese and meat, and also their clothing from their herds. Another example is found in Algeria, in North Africa. To see how far this model applies to transhumans, there. Fixed transhumans from valley to mountain pastures are common in the area, but some recent pastoralists also take their herds on longer journeys. At the end of spring, from the Sahara in the south to the plains in the north, where there is more abundant rainfall, they have traditionally assisted local farmers with the harvest, which takes place while they are temporarily a resident there. We have to decide whether their move would fall within the category of fixed or nomadic transhumans. It is true they live in tents, and these are movable and temporary right through the year, whether they are in the Sahara or in the north of Algeria. So, in considering their homes, they appear nomadic. On the other hand, in relation to their patterns of movement, they do not. They drive their livestock through the same passes in mountains each year, which suggests fixed transhumance. Here, in fact, we have a case showing some features of both types of transhumance, which we might more precisely refer to as intermediate. This all goes to show that you should treat every theory with great care and not force your case history into a model if it doesn''t fit.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Good afternoon everyone. Today I''m going to talk about what may be a new concept to you: the movement of people with their herds of animals from place to place. This is known as transhumance. These peoples are pastoralists, and their animals include cows, sheep, goats, camels, and reindeer. Now, researchers sometimes make a distinction between two types of transhumance: fixed and nomadic, and talk about various factors which differentiate between them. The first and most important distinction they make is to do with the climate. Fixed transhumance occurs in countries such as Switzerland, where seasons are predictable in terms of weather and timing, nomadic transhumans, on the other hand, are generally practiced in areas where climatic conditions are difficult to predict. An example of such a location is Somalia, as the anticipated rains often just don''t appear.</p><p>Secondly, researchers sometimes look at the type of journey that pastoralists make: their movement. For fixed transhumans, the pattern is sometimes known as vertical, because it typically consists of a journey from lower ground in the winter season to higher ground in the summer, and vice versa. In the wild, of course, animals frequently follow these kinds of migration patterns themselves. Nomadic transhumans generally take place over a longer distance, and it''s far more complex. The actual pattern varies from year to year, and it''s determined primarily by the animals'' needs for grass and also for water. Another distinction is sometimes made in terms of homes. In the case of fixed transhumans, the group will typically have permanent homes in the valley and a shelter which is semi permanent in a mountain, such as a cabin, to offer some protection from the weather. 00:02:07 In nomadic transhumans, however, the pastoralists always live in temporary shelters which are transported by their livestock from place to place. And are usually light and portable. In northern Kenya, for example the Gabra pastoralist, he carries the frame of his home on his camel and then covers it with branches when he stops for the night. A fourth factor often mentioned is the number of people taking part. In the case of fixed transhumans, only the people who actually look after the herds travel. These are normally the young ones because they can withstand adverse physical conditions more easily. By contrast, in nomadic transhumans, the whole group moves with their livestock. The last points of comparison often made by ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'researchers are diet and goods. </p><p>In fixed transhumans, those who do not move from the valleys often start working as farmers, cultivating the build-up, contacts with other communities, and in some cases, they may also trade animals for grain. In nomadic transhumans, there are fewer contacts. The group is generally regarded as providers of all their own needs, and they obtain food such as cheese and meat, and also their clothing from their herds. Another example is found in Algeria, in North Africa. To see how far this model applies to transhumans, to obtain food such as cheese and meat, and also their clothing from their herds. Another example is found in Algeria, in North Africa. To see how far this model applies to transhumans, there. Fixed transhumans from valley to mountain pastures are common in the area, but some recent pastoralists also take their herds on longer journeys. At the end of spring, from the Sahara in the south to the plains in the north, where there is more abundant rainfall, they have traditionally assisted local farmers with the harvest, which takes place while they are temporarily a resident there. We have to decide whether their move would fall within the category of fixed or nomadic transhumans. It is true they live in tents, and these are movable and temporary right through the year, whether they are in the Sahara or in the north of Algeria. So, in considering their homes, they appear nomadic. On the other hand, in relation to their patterns of movement, they do not. They drive their livestock through the same passes in mountains each year, which suggests fixed transhumance. Here, in fact, we have a case showing some features of both types of transhumance, which we might more precisely refer to as intermediate. This all goes to show that you should treat every theory with great care and not force your case history into a model if it doesn''t fit.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.5.S1025.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1025),
(N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',N'5a7cb9af-b1ba-5488-a755-ffce5b7b5a49',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.
Write <span>ONE WORD ONLY</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Transhumance',11,0,1,NULL,0,1025),
(N'46e418ce-40b8-5d5d-9e6c-f1268c48de25',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'<h3>Transhumance</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>Two Types of Transhumance</span></p> <p>• fixed: mainly in countries with predictable seasons, e.g. Switzerland</p> <p>• nomadic: in areas with unpredictable climatic conditions, e.g. Somalia</p> <p><span>Study on the Movement of Pastoralists</span></p> <p>• for fixed transhumance, the pattern of movement is described as }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• while for nomadic transhumance, the pattern is more complex and varied</p> <p><span>Differences in terms of Homes</span></p> <p>• fixed transhumance live in a semi-permanent shelter on high ground such as }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• nomadic transhumance always stay in portable shelters, e g. homes of the Gabra people carried on }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Number of People Involved</span></p> <p>• for fixed transhumance, only }{SPACE}{ pastoralists make the journey</p> <p>• however, all nomadic transhumance migrate with their livestock</p> <p><span>Diet and Goods</span></p> <p>• people who do not leave the valleys may become }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• exchange animals with other communities for }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• herds provide all needs-food and }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Research in Algeria in North Africa</span></p> <p>• they traditionally arrive in time to help local residents with the }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• their homes are }{SPACE}{ which are temporary shelters</p> <p>• a better term to describe this type of transhumance might be }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Question: “For fixed transhumance, the pattern of movement is described as ___?” → Cần tính từ mô tả loại hình di chuyển. </p><p>Answer: Người nói khẳng định từ “vertical” → đáp án là vertical. </p><p>Key vocab: pattern = kiểu mẫu, vertical = thẳng đứng/từ thấp lên cao.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1025),
(N'595119a3-7349-502f-b67e-27426b877273',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>Question: “Fixed transhumance live in a semi-permanent shelter on high ground such as ___?” →  Cần danh từ chỉ nơi ở. </p><p>Answer: Người nói ví dụ rõ  ràng là “cabin” → cabin. </p><p>Key vocab: semi- permanent = bán cố định, shelter = nơi trú.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1025),
(N'8643067e-93a0-5749-b7af-2d38afeaba4d',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 33',N'<p>Question: “Nomadic transhumance... homes of the Gabra people carried on ___?” → Cần danh từ chỉ động vật. </p><p>Answer: Người nói rõ là nhà được chở trên camels. </p><p>Key vocab: carried = mang theo, frame = khung.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1025),
(N'bd1aec51-3392-5a42-a6f6-d50c9aecd405',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>Question: “For fixed transhumance, only ___ pastoralists make the journey?” → Cần tính từ mô tả nhóm người. </p><p>Answer: Chỉ những người trẻ tuổi tham gia → young. </p><p>Key vocab: withstand = chịu đựng, adverse = khắc nghiệt.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1025),
(N'759b4574-4825-5260-a862-0507d966748c',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>Question: “People who do not leave the valleys may become ___?” → Cần nghề nghiệp. </p><p>Answer: Người nói rõ ràng: họ trở thành farmers. </p><p>Key vocab: valleys = thung lũng, working as = làm việc như.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1025),
(N'65e90fb9-253e-525a-bfc4-92dcecc875fd',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>Question: “Exchange animals with other communities for ___?” → Cần danh từ chỉ sản phẩm. </p><p>Answer: Người nói: đổi động vật lấy grain (ngũ cốc). </p><p>Key vocab: trade = trao đổi, grain = lúa/ngũ cốc.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1025),
(N'594d551a-311f-514d-b308-d442724b1fcf',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>Question: “Herds provide all needs – food and ___?” → Cần danh từ chỉ nhu cầu thiết yếu. </p><p>Answer: Herds cung cấp cả clothing. </p><p>Key vocab: obtain = lấy được, clothing = quần áo.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1025),
(N'8e83849e-4c54-56b9-aefb-cb54ca4901dc',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 38',N'<p>Question: “They traditionally arrive in time to help local residents with the ___?” → Cần danh từ chỉ mùa vụ. </p><p>Answer: Người nói rõ: giúp cư dân thu hoạch → harvest. </p><p>Key vocab: assisted = giúp, harvest = mùa gặt.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1025),
(N'e4953289-9451-5d70-a992-13184cdb5a42',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>Question: “Their homes are ___, which are temporary shelters?” → Cần danh từ chỉ nhà ở. </p><p>Answer: Homes là tents. </p><p>Key vocab: temporary = tạm thời, tents = lều.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1025),
(N'8fbded9b-22a8-5b0b-a169-f8cbeb06d4b9',N'86ba9d0d-c5cf-5bc4-a2cc-369de1d9a372',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>Question: “A better term to describe this type of transhumance might be ___?” → Cần tính từ/thuật ngữ thay thế. </p><p>Answer: Người nói kết luận rõ: intermediate. </p><p>Key vocab: refer to = gọi là, intermediate = trung gian.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1025),
(N'6e3fb186-c29c-5dbb-b888-512eb1cb6485',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.3.S1024.mp3',N'Vol 4 LISTENING FULL TEST 3',0,7,1,NULL,1,1024),
(N'882413ed-d6e8-5c42-b5f2-01d484881732',N'6e3fb186-c29c-5dbb-b888-512eb1cb6485',0,1,13,N'<h2>[VOL 4] Booking a residental workshop</h2><p>(1-5) Hello, Creative Centre. Hello there. I''m phoning to book a place on your next two-day drama workshop. OK, let me just check. Yes, we''ve still got room on that course. Great! Can I have your full name? Yes, it''s Jan Creswick. That''s C R E S W I C K. Thanks, Jan. And are you coming on your own? Yes. And have you done any of our courses before? I did one a couple of years ago. Great! So you may be on our computer records. Which course did you do? The Writing for the Theatre course. OK, right. Yes, here we are. Oh, so that''s 20 Gregory Avenue. Gregory Place, actually. I''ll correct that on the computer. Uh huh. And can I ask if you work? Yes, I want to be an actor, but I''m working as a waitress at the moment. After this course, who knows? Right, back to the paperwork. So, we''re enrolling you on the two-day course that starts tomorrow, the 30th of May. Oh, uh, sorry. No, on the 13th of June, I meant the one starting in two weeks. Is that still alright? Oh, that''s fine. (6-10) Can you tell me something about the accomodation? Yes... Is 5:30 too early to arrive? No, that''s fine ... no later than 5 o''clock on the last day. Right. Okay. Right. Just a few other things. Any dietary information we should know? All the food is provided, and we use organic produce. as often as we can. Oh, that sounds great. But I don''t eat anything that''s made from milk. I know that can be difficult. Oh, it''s not a problem. What about meat? Do you eat that? I don''t eat much of it, but that''s fine. You don''t need to note that. Yes. Hey, I am really looking forward to this. Great! I don''t know if you know this, but we may divide into two groups in the afternoon if we have more than 16 people on the course. If we do that, there''ll be a choice of doing comic or what we call classic work. Oh, I''d always go with comedy. I''m much more interested in that. Now, how did you find out about this course? Was it the adverts in the Sunday newspaper? No. In fact, I looked. for drama courses on the Internet and I saw that you were doing one. When I read about it, I just thought, hey, why not? Okay, I saw that you send out some pre-course material. Yes, I don''t have much time to read any of the books you''re recommending, but I''d be grateful for your leaflet, the one you mentioned in your publicity. Sure, I''ll put a copy in the post to you. Thanks a lot! I can''t wait. Wait till you see what we have in.</p>',N'<p>(1-5) Hello, Creative Centre. Hello there. I''m phoning to book a place on your next two-day drama workshop. OK, let me just check. Yes, we''ve still got room on that course. Great! Can I have your full name? Yes, it''s Jan Creswick. That''s C R E S W I C K. Thanks, Jan. And are you coming on your own? Yes. And have you done any of our courses before? I did one a couple of years ago. Great! So you may be on our computer records. Which course did you do? The Writing for the Theatre course. OK, right. Yes, here we are. Oh, so that''s 20 Gregory Avenue. Gregory Place, actually. I''ll correct that on the computer. Uh huh. And can I ask if you work? Yes, I want to be an actor, but I''m working as a waitress at the moment. After this course, who knows? Right, back to the paperwork. So, we''re enrolling you on the two-day course that starts tomorrow, the 30th of May. Oh, uh, sorry. No, on the 13th of June, I meant the one starting in two weeks. Is that still alright? Oh, that''s fine. (6-10) Can you tell me something about the accomodation? Yes... Is 5:30 too early to arrive? No, that''s fine ... no later than 5 o''clock on the last day. Right. Okay. Right. Just a few other things. Any dietary information we should know? All the food is provided, and we use organic produce. as often as we can. Oh, that sounds great. But I don''t eat anything that''s made from milk. I know that can be difficult. Oh, it''s not a problem. What about meat? Do you eat that? I don''t eat much of it, but that''s fine. You don''t need to note that. Yes. Hey, I am really looking forward to this. Great! I don''t know if you know this, but we may divide into two groups in the afternoon if we have more than 16 people on the course. If we do that, there''ll be a choice of doing comic or what we call classic work. Oh, I''d always go with comedy. I''m much more interested in that. Now, how did you find out about this course? Was it the adverts in the Sunday newspaper? No. In fact, I looked. for drama courses on the Internet and I saw that you were doing one. When I read about it, I just thought, hey, why not? Okay, I saw that you send out some pre-course material. Yes, I don''t have much time to read any of the books you''re recommending, but I''d be grateful for your leaflet, the one you mentioned in your publicity. Sure, I''ll put a copy in the post to you. Thanks a lot! I can''t wait. Wait till you see what we have in.</p>',NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.3.S1024.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1024),
(N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',N'882413ed-d6e8-5c42-b5f2-01d484881732',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the form below.
Write <span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Booking a residential workshop',11,0,1,NULL,0,1024),
(N'dfe05c20-63dd-5253-8764-0a45b3318bc2',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'<h3>Booking a residential workshop</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p>Course required: drama workshop</p> <p>Full name of participant: Jan }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>Previous course attended: Writing for the }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>Address: 20, Gregory }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>Current occupation: }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>Date chosen course begins: }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>Estimated arrival time: }{SPACE}{ pm</p> <p>Special dietary requests: no  }{SPACE}{ products</p> <p>Preferred afternoon course: }{SPACE}{ work</p> <p>First source of course information: }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>Pre-course material requested: the }{SPACE}{ mentioned in the publicity</p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Tên đầy đủ của học viên là gì?” → Cần một danh từ riêng (tên họ). Trả lời: Người nói khẳng định “it’s Jan Creswick” và còn đánh vần, nên đáp án là Creswick. </p><p>Key vocab: full name = họ tên đầy đủ; spell = đánh vần.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1024),
(N'0119e398-fa22-5176-bc40-f484b0f5047f',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 2',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Bạn đã từng tham gia khóa học nào trước đây (Writing for the ...)?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ lĩnh vực. </p><p>Trả lời: Người tham gia trả lời rõ ràng: “The Writing for the Theatre course”. Vì đề đã cho “Writing for the ...”, đáp án là Theatre. </p><p>Key vocab: previous course = khóa học trước; attended = tham gia.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1024),
(N'bd66230f-5151-5fea-80ef-ac60b2201dec',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 3',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Địa chỉ của học viên là gì (20 Gregory...)?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ địa điểm. </p><p>Trả lời: Ban đầu nhân viên nhầm là “Avenue”, nhưng học viên sửa lại: “Place”. Đáp án là Place. </p><p>Key vocab: address = địa chỉ; correct = sửa lại.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1024),
(N'5b6279f6-f586-56cf-a862-b688a35a0fe0',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 4',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Nghề nghiệp hiện tại của học viên là gì?” → Cần một danh từ chỉ nghề nghiệp. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói trực tiếp: “I’m working as a waitress”. Đáp án là waitress. </p><p>Key vocab: current occupation = nghề nghiệp hiện tại; working as = làm việc như</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,4,NULL,0,1024),
(N'7b2fb73a-685f-5618-a34d-562b98b921e5',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 5',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Khóa học sẽ bắt đầu vào ngày nào?” → Cần một cụm ngày/tháng. </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói chỉnh lại: “No, on the 13th of June”. Đáp án là 13 June. </p><p>Key vocab: course begins = khóa học bắt đầu; date = ngày.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,5,NULL,0,1024),
(N'1e03eb9c-2cde-5e52-b522-c2dfcf0fcc78',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 6',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Giờ đến dự kiến là mấy giờ?”</p><p>Trả lời: Người nói hỏi: “Liệu đến lúc 5 rưỡi có sớm không”-&gt; Cô dự định (estimate) đến lúc 5 rưỡi. </p><p>Đáp án là 5:30.</p><p>Key vocab: arrival time = thời gian đến; no later than = không muộn hơn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,6,NULL,0,1024),
(N'd27ddf6a-c908-5444-8cc8-c235bbcb202f',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 7',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Học viên không ăn sản phẩm nào?”
Trả lời: Người nói khẳng định: “don’t eat
anything made from milk”. </p><p>Đáp án là milk.
Key vocab: dietary requests = yêu cầu ăn kiêng;
made from = làm từ.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,7,NULL,0,1024),
(N'd981fb46-427c-5f12-869b-44277330eecd',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 8',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Khóa học buổi chiều mà học viên chọn là gì?” </p><p>Trả lời: Người tham gia chọn: “I’d always go with comedy”, tương ứng với lựa chọn “comic work”. </p><p>Đáp án là comic. </p><p>Key vocab: preferred = ưa thích; comedy = hài.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,8,NULL,0,1024),
(N'31de1be4-bb1e-544c-b575-6c8fc4d392f7',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 9',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Nguồn thông tin đầu tiên mà học viên biết đến khóa học là gì?” </p><p>Trả lời: Người tham gia nói rõ: “I looked ... on the Internet”. </p><p>Đáp án là Internet. </p><p>Key vocab: source of information = nguồn thông tin; looked for = tìm kiếm.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,1024),
(N'5ed36c3b-8d6f-5dee-8c41-6c85ad618550',N'8b6a5f18-d819-5984-9cce-3a8f6cb4db0d',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>Câu hỏi: “Tài liệu trước khóa học mà học viên yêu cầu là gì? </p><p>Trả lời: Người nói muốn “your leaflet”. </p><p>Đáp án là leaflet. </p><p>Key vocab: material = tài liệu; publicity = quảng bá.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,1024),
(N'674df49e-0996-5808-8a5a-51dab492287a',N'6e3fb186-c29c-5dbb-b888-512eb1cb6485',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Working at Parson''s Fruit Farm</h2><p>(11-15) Hello everyone and welcome to Parsons Fruit Farm. It''s Good to see a mix of so many different nationalities who have all come here on their working holidays. I''m sure you''re going to enjoy working on our farm. First Of all, just a few words about accommodation. I know that some of you have pitched your tents down at the campground but are looking for something more comfortable. So, one option is the motel. It''s located very conveniently. just a few minutes away, and they often do reduce rates if you''re staying long term. And then the Backpackers Hostel is always very popular, though it can be busy at this time of year. Okay, so the work itself. This summer, the weather''s been pretty mixed; we''ve had a bit of everything, and the forecasts have always been wrong. So, some of the apples and pears aren''t in great condition. That said, this season''s been better than some we''ve had recently, and the autumn looks like it''s going to be great, so we should have ideal conditions for the harvest. So, there are a number of different jobs to do. Most of you will be fruit picking. We''ll give all the fruit pickers'' gloves to wear to protect your fingers so you won''t hurt yourselves. We''ll also be looking for some tractor drivers. Their job is to collect the full baskets of fruit and take them to the shed. I should say in this country anyone is allowed to drive a tractor on farmland once they''re 13, rather than 18, as in some countries. However, we do have certain rules that are specific to our farm. You don''t need to have done farm work before, but here you must have a relevant document or permit from your country that allows you to operate this type of vehicle. Then there are also lots of jobs working inside the shed. This involves sorting the fruit. and cleaning it if necessary. There''s a lot of heavy machinery in the shed, so when that''s operating, you must wear your ear protection. The big advantage is the shed''s fully air-conditioned, so it''s a pleasant environment regardless of what the weather''s doing outside. Alright, well, whatever job you do, I''ll just give you some final advice. When you''re picking the fruit, sometimes you can break off a small branch. That''s not something you need to be worried about; it''ll grow back. Also, remember to have snacks and use your water bottles often. People get exhausted quickly during harvest time. And if you''ve got a mobile or cell, keep it with you at all times in case' AS nvarchar(max))+N' there''s an emergency. (16-20) Alright, I''d like to quickly go over where everything is located. So look at your maps of Parsons Fruit Farm and find the main yard. It''s towards the bottom of your maps; That''s really, it is the centre of the farm. Okay, the first place I''ll mention is the workshop. This is close to the main yard, but you actually have to leave the yard to get into it. So, you need to take the road that goes out to the orchards, and you access the workshop from there. Then, the lunchroom on the north. side of the map there''s a road that curves sharply round to the left. Halfway round this bend you''ll find the entrance to the lunchroom. It''s a pretty place, next to the river and also beside a little wood. You also need to be able to find the farm office. So, if you look at the main yard, you''ll see there''s a number of different buildings located around it, and the farm office is the smallest one. It''s on the south side. Obviously, the first aid room is important. Now, this is close to the main yard, so it''s quick to get there, and it''s actually more or less in the middle of your maps. That little building that''s attached to the farmhouse. Then The store shed. From the main yard, take the road heading north. Go past the farmhouse and turn right. The store is down that road. If you come to the vegetable gardens, you''ve gone too far. Now, the other thing is the farmhouse is further than you''d expect.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>(11-15) Hello everyone and welcome to Parsons Fruit Farm. It''s Good to see a mix of so many different nationalities who have all come here on their working holidays. I''m sure you''re going to enjoy working on our farm. First Of all, just a few words about accommodation. I know that some of you have pitched your tents down at the campground but are looking for something more comfortable. So, one option is the motel. It''s located very conveniently. just a few minutes away, and they often do reduce rates if you''re staying long term. And then the Backpackers Hostel is always very popular, though it can be busy at this time of year. Okay, so the work itself. This summer, the weather''s been pretty mixed; we''ve had a bit of everything, and the forecasts have always been wrong. So, some of the apples and pears aren''t in great condition. That said, this season''s been better than some we''ve had recently, and the autumn looks like it''s going to be great, so we should have ideal conditions for the harvest. So, there are a number of different jobs to do. Most of you will be fruit picking. We''ll give all the fruit pickers'' gloves to wear to protect your fingers so you won''t hurt yourselves. We''ll also be looking for some tractor drivers. Their job is to collect the full baskets of fruit and take them to the shed. I should say in this country anyone is allowed to drive a tractor on farmland once they''re 13, rather than 18, as in some countries. However, we do have certain rules that are specific to our farm. You don''t need to have done farm work before, but here you must have a relevant document or permit from your country that allows you to operate this type of vehicle. Then there are also lots of jobs working inside the shed. This involves sorting the fruit. and cleaning it if necessary. There''s a lot of heavy machinery in the shed, so when that''s operating, you must wear your ear protection. The big advantage is the shed''s fully air-conditioned, so it''s a pleasant environment regardless of what the weather''s doing outside. Alright, well, whatever job you do, I''ll just give you some final advice. When you''re picking the fruit, sometimes you can break off a small branch. That''s not something you need to be worried about; it''ll grow back. Also, remember to have snacks and use your water bottles often. People get exhausted quickly during harvest time. And if you''ve got a mobile or cell, keep it with you at all times in case there''s an emergency. (16-20) Alright, I''d lik' AS nvarchar(max))+N'e to quickly go over where everything is located. So look at your maps of Parsons Fruit Farm and find the main yard. It''s towards the bottom of your maps; That''s really, it is the centre of the farm. Okay, the first place I''ll mention is the workshop. This is close to the main yard, but you actually have to leave the yard to get into it. So, you need to take the road that goes out to the orchards, and you access the workshop from there. Then, the lunchroom on the north. side of the map there''s a road that curves sharply round to the left. Halfway round this bend you''ll find the entrance to the lunchroom. It''s a pretty place, next to the river and also beside a little wood. You also need to be able to find the farm office. So, if you look at the main yard, you''ll see there''s a number of different buildings located around it, and the farm office is the smallest one. It''s on the south side. Obviously, the first aid room is important. Now, this is close to the main yard, so it''s quick to get there, and it''s actually more or less in the middle of your maps. That little building that''s attached to the farmhouse. Then The store shed. From the main yard, take the road heading north. Go past the farmhouse and turn right. The store is down that road. If you come to the vegetable gardens, you''ve gone too far. Now, the other thing is the farmhouse is further than you''d expect.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-4/iL.VOL4.3.S1024.PART2.mp3',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,1024),
(N'4573fc0a-d14b-5022-a908-5cd358adcac6',N'674df49e-0996-5808-8a5a-51dab492287a',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter, <span>A</span>, <span>B</span> or <span>C</span>.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Working at Parson’s Fruit Farm',1,0,1,NULL,0,1024),
(N'28bb6afc-852b-5b5c-906a-2b0d5224dc26',N'4573fc0a-d14b-5022-a908-5cd358adcac6',0,3,19,N'<p>What does the manager say about accommodation?</p>',N'<p>Question (dịch &amp; yêu cầu):“Người quản lý nói gì về chỗ ở?” </p><p>Answer (vì sao đúng): </p><p>Script nói “located very conveniently, just a few minutes away” → khẳng định motel gần nông trại. Tại sao A sai: “Backpackers Hostel is always very popular, though it can be busy” → đông/ bận rộn, không hề nói “full/ hết chỗ”. Tại sao C sai: Campground được nhắc là “some of you have pitched your tents... but are looking for something more comfortable” → chỉ nói ít tiện nghi, không khẳng định “cheap”. </p><p>Key vocab: convenient = thuận tiện; just a few minutes away = cách vài phút.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,1024),
(N'e5e499a3-a3a2-5c45-969c-0aa7fb3ba3a0',N'4573fc0a-d14b-5022-a908-5cd358adcac6',0,3,19,N'<p>The weather this summer has been</p>',N'<p>Question: “Thời tiết mùa hè này như thế nào?”</p><p>Answer: “mixed... forecasts have always been wrong” ⇒ khó dự đoán chính xác. Tại sao A sai: Không có “perfect for growing fruit”; ngược lại “some of the apples and pears aren''t in great condition”. Tại sao B sai: Không so sánh “worse than last year”; chỉ nói “better than some we''ve had recently”. </p><p>Key vocab: mixed = thất thường; forecasts have been wrong = dự báo sai.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,1024),
(N'31dc9e79-f82c-56b4-8dd3-6d38ae42d266',N'4573fc0a-d14b-5022-a908-5cd358adcac6',0,3,19,N'<p>The manager says that tractor drivers</p>',N'<p>Question: “Quản lý nói gì về lái xe máy kéo?” → yêu cầu/điều kiện. </p><p>Answer: Nói rõ phải có relevant document/permit ⇒giấy phép phù hợp. Tại sao B sai: “You don''t need to have done farm work before” → không cần kinh nghiệm vườn. Tại sao C sai: Câu trên cho biết từ 13 tuổi đã có thể lái trên đất nông nghiệp ⇒ không phải over 18. </p><p>Key vocab: permit/document = giấy phép; operate = vận hành.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,1024),
(N'ebd05bd2-ddfc-511c-846f-8c514b330586',N'4573fc0a-d14b-5022-a908-5cd358adcac6',0,3,19,N'<p>Working inside the shed</p>',N'<p>Question: “Làm việc trong nhà kho có đặc điểm gì?” </p><p>Answer: Có nhiều máy móc nặng, phải đeo bảo vệ tai ⇒ đôi lúc ồn. Tại sao A sai: Trái lại, “shed''s fully air-conditioned” ⇒ không “rather hot”. Tại sao C sai: Không nói “often be dirty”; công việc là sorting/cleaningnên không chứng cứ về “bẩn”.</p><p>Key vocab: heavy machinery = máy móc nặng; ear protection = bảo vệ tai.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,1024),
(N'751e28f8-d4b8-5181-aca7-1f8caefe2e96',N'4573fc0a-d14b-5022-a908-5cd358adcac6',0,3,19,N'<p>What final advice does the manager give?</p>',N'<p>Question: “Lời khuyên cuối cùng của người quản lý là gì?” </p><p>Answer: Nhấn mạnh ăn vặt + uống nước thường xuyên để tránh kiệt sức ⇒ eat and drink regularly. Tại sao B sai:“Sometimes you can break off a small branch... not something you need to be worried about” ⇒ không phải “try not to damage the trees”. Tại sao C sai: Có nói “keep your mobile with you at all times” (mang theo), không phải “leave your phones in a secure location”. </p><p>Key vocab: snacks = đồ ăn nhẹ; use your water bottles often = uống nước thường xuyên.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,1024),
(N'6c3d30c7-174d-55f0-8f28-6f740828eff1',N'674df49e-0996-5808-8a5a-51dab492287a',0,2,18,N'<p>Label the map below.
Write the correct letter, <span>A–I</span>, next to questions 16–20.</p><p><img src="https://ieltsroom.com/upload/images/ieltsadvancedtest/dd7f4af0f72118449f56.jpg" alt="Question diagram"></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',12,0,2,NULL,0,1024),
(N'648c48cc-5df4-5a66-bb3f-4985c93b0892',N'6c3d30c7-174d-55f0-8f28-6f740828eff1',0,3,19,N'<p>Workshop</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Workshop. → Xưởng nằm sát main yard, trên đường dẫn ra orchards → vị trí G.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,1024),
(N'b322104f-a074-5b94-a905-e35a9997775a',N'6c3d30c7-174d-55f0-8f28-6f740828eff1',0,3,19,N'<p>Lunchroom</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Lunchroom. → Trên đường cong về phía bắc, cạnh sông và rừng nhỏ → vị trí B.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,1024),
(N'f28ece4a-a26f-58db-8f16-9b6e0ca2ce23',N'6c3d30c7-174d-55f0-8f28-6f740828eff1',0,3,19,N'<p>Farm Office</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Farm Office. → Tòa nhỏ nhất, nằm phía nam của main yard → vị trí H.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,1024),
(N'35898f8a-5d70-5067-a39f-8f5fb57bdc2a',N'6c3d30c7-174d-55f0-8f28-6f740828eff1',0,3,19,N'<p>First Aid Room</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: First Aid Room. → Phòng y tế gắn liền với farmhouse, ở giữa bản đồ → vị trí F.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,1024),
(N'4335515b-fc02-573d-90c4-fab88b8dd566',N'6c3d30c7-174d-55f0-8f28-6f740828eff1',0,3,19,N'<p>Store Shed</p>',N'<p>Câu hỏi: Store Shed. → Đi qua farmhouse, rẽ phải trước vegetable gardens → vị trí C.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,1024),
(N'97c8d32d-36ad-59d7-8007-e9fbc7338e55',N'6e3fb186-c29c-5dbb-b888-512eb1cb6485',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>[VOL 4] Stone Point: A New Zealand historical site</h2><p>(21-24) Hi Scott. Here are my note. Let''s sort this presentation out then. The first thing I found out about Stone Point is it''s an extinct volcanic cone. You probably know that already, being a New Zealander. Yes, and there are two other cones nearby which also overlook the havour. Right. Yeah, the first settlers of New Zealand, the Māori, sailed to New Zealand thousands. of years after these volcanoes erupted I think the tutor allocated us this topic because of Stone Point''s significant historical role. It''s been used by both Māori and European settlers as a place to shelter from attack or launch an attack for centuries. Yeah, and the fact it was an ideal place for building a village, it would have been quite a sheltered spounge. Suppose the Māori settled there because they thought the volcanic soil would help their crops grow. Well, they would have realised the benefits of the soil after a few good harvests, but initially, the fishing opportunities would have been the intraction. You''re right. Actually, I also read that Māori regard Stone Point as taonga. I believe that means a treasured thing. Wow, your Māori vocabulary is improving, Scott. We do have to deal with Stone Point''s cultural value for Māori. That''s still part of its historical significance. But we need to keep most Māori terminology out of the presentation; otherwise, a lot of the international students won''t be able to follow us. That''s a shame. I''m sure they''d be interested. I mean, I am. But yeah, we wouldn''t be able to explain it in the time allowed, so I guess we have to stick to English. Anyway, European migrants turned Stone Point into a lookout point for ships. Then it became a fort, an army base in the late 1880s, and then during the World Wars, the fort was modernised again. It''s also been a public park sometimes. I''d prefer to devote more of the presentation to the military use of Stone Point. There are some great websites about the engines they installed. Sorry, I wouldn''t go into the technical side if I were you. If you start describing mechanical stuff, people will switch off. I think it''s more interesting to look at the impact Stone Point had on the people''s lives at the time. I mean, for example, there were about 25 years when they used local prisoners to build the tunnels and the underground rooms. They lived there too. Yeah, I guess if we can find some firsthand accounts. (25-30) OK, so let''s make a checklist of' AS nvarchar(max))+N' things to do. For the Early Maori Inhabitation section, we have to look at the material we''ve used again and make sure we know who the writers were and when they wrote it. Yes, that needs work. And for the part about Stone Point being used as a lookout point, I''m sure we can locate some drawings of how Stone Point looked then and the kind of ships coming in. Nice. And what else? We haven''t got a lot of detail for the reconstruction of the fouth. We. should add some more information about what it was the prisoners were adding to it. OK, I''ll do that bit too. I''ll deal with the section regarding the modernisation that happened in the 1930s and 40s. 00:05:06 I mean, that''s the bit where we have the most visible evidence of historic buildings. I''ll ring the Department of Conservation and see if they''ll agree to let me film the sight, maybe allow me access to some of the tunnels the public don''t see. OK, for the 1950s onwards, could you also arrange a meeting with someone from the Department of Conservation to ask them about their restoration work? I could try. Oh, and just about its use as a public. park today. Where is aboundary it exactly? Does it go all the way to the Not sure; I''ll check that out. Great! I suppose it would finish off the presentation nicely if we explain the future plans for the site.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>(21-24) Hi Sc