(N'51e41721-87ab-5f1f-ae1d-75c58b3891a7',N'dc91aa3c-75b0-5db2-a1c0-406b034dbcc1',0,3,19,N'<p>Which<span> TWO</span> features of his research in Nigeria will Terence discuss in his presentation? </p>',N'<p>- Đáp án thứ hai là E (the original discovery of the sculptures).</p><p> - Terrence nhắc tới các tài liệu mô tả việc phát hiện ban đầu các tác phẩm, cũng như câu chuyện về lần tìm thấy đầu tiên vào năm 1938. </p><p>- Key words: first discovered; found</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1017),
(N'e0a12a3e-0e47-5fd4-9e8d-6060a34bc04d',N'a1ac85b0-5f51-59f7-ba13-a6206dcc93f2',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Reasons for and against urban migration</h2><p>You''ll hear part of a lecture on the subject of why living in the city may be better for the environment than living in rural areas. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. </p><p>Good morning, everyone. Today we''re looking at urban migration, the movement of people migrating from outside cities into cities. I think it''s easy to jump to the conclusion that a rural lifestyle is greener. However, recent studies have suggested otherwise. First of all, if we take the land area of the earth, the proportion that cities take up is a mere 3%, and these same cities actually produce 30 to 40% of the carbon that is released into the atmosphere, a lower amount than the 60 to 70% from non-urban areas. Anyway, let''s imagine that most people decide to migrate to the city. What kind of impact would that have? For a start, this movement of people from the countryside should lift the pressure of intensive agriculture from the land, allowing forests to regenerate. Another point in favour of urban migration is that it can be far more energy efficient if services are concentrated in the city, for example. This is true for electricity and, as you can imagine, water. Then, once you remember that rural communities are often very spread out, you can see yet another green reason to live in the city. Rural citizens rely on the car to make their journeys far more than city dwellers, who have other more environmentally friendly forms of transport that they can use, such as buses and trains. The journeys tend to be shorter too. Yet another point in favour of urban migration is the kind of recycling schemes that can only take place where the population is concentrated. Take, for instance, Seattle in the USA, where methane gas is being put to good use and is generating energy for the city. It''s produced from the city''s rubbish, which could not be collected so efficiently from rural areas. Now, another factor that threatens the environment is the growing world population. It''s estimated that 75 million babies are now born every year, and in some countries the birth rate shows no signs of slowing down. Urban migration may provide an answer here. Now, in cities, one of the reasons that women tend to have fewer children is that they get married later and start their families later. And the reason for this is that it''s far easier to find a job, and once you have one, to get ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'a promotion. So yes, the focus isn''t on bearing children. Well, it would be foolish to pretend that there are no downsides to urban living when clearly there are. The thing that critics often mention is the idea that migrants may end up losing their culture, although some studies have found the opposite is true. Rather than give up their traditions, people actually work hard to maintain them. More evident is the fact that compared to the lower rate that is observed in rural areas, we can see that there is more crime occurring in cities. There won''t be many of us here today who can say we haven''t been directly or indirectly affected by it. There''s another issue that we have to deal with. So many cities, including our own, now suffer from high levels of pollution so that the air we breathe is far from pure. And another factor against the urban is greener idea is that urbanisation often drives a country''s economy. Put simply, when there is greater wealth in society, more energy is consumed. One of the direct effects of a stronger economy, something that many citizens will now experience every day and may well find stressful, is the amount of traffic on the road, despite the other options we mentioned earlier. This is a problem that is not about to go away, although there are some solutions that could be considered. That is the end of the listening test. You now have 10 minutes to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>You''ll hear part of a lecture on the subject of why living in the city may be better for the environment than living in rural areas. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40. Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40. </p><p>Good morning, everyone. Today we''re looking at urban migration, the movement of people migrating from outside cities into cities. I think it''s easy to jump to the conclusion that a rural lifestyle is greener. However, recent studies have suggested otherwise. First of all, if we take the land area of the earth, the proportion that cities take up is a mere 3%, and these same cities actually produce 30 to 40% of the carbon that is released into the atmosphere, a lower amount than the 60 to 70% from non-urban areas. Anyway, let''s imagine that most people decide to migrate to the city. What kind of impact would that have? For a start, this movement of people from the countryside should lift the pressure of intensive agriculture from the land, allowing forests to regenerate. Another point in favour of urban migration is that it can be far more energy efficient if services are concentrated in the city, for example. This is true for electricity and, as you can imagine, water. Then, once you remember that rural communities are often very spread out, you can see yet another green reason to live in the city. Rural citizens rely on the car to make their journeys far more than city dwellers, who have other more environmentally friendly forms of transport that they can use, such as buses and trains. The journeys tend to be shorter too. Yet another point in favour of urban migration is the kind of recycling schemes that can only take place where the population is concentrated. Take, for instance, Seattle in the USA, where methane gas is being put to good use and is generating energy for the city. It''s produced from the city''s rubbish, which could not be collected so efficiently from rural areas. Now, another factor that threatens the environment is the growing world population. It''s estimated that 75 million babies are now born every year, and in some countries the birth rate shows no signs of slowing down. Urban migration may provide an answer here. Now, in cities, one of the reasons that women tend to have fewer children is that they get married later and start their families later. And the reason for this is that it''s far easier to find a job, and once you have one, to get a promotion. So yes, the focus isn''t on bearing ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'children. Well, it would be foolish to pretend that there are no downsides to urban living when clearly there are. The thing that critics often mention is the idea that migrants may end up losing their culture, although some studies have found the opposite is true. Rather than give up their traditions, people actually work hard to maintain them. More evident is the fact that compared to the lower rate that is observed in rural areas, we can see that there is more crime occurring in cities. There won''t be many of us here today who can say we haven''t been directly or indirectly affected by it. There''s another issue that we have to deal with. So many cities, including our own, now suffer from high levels of pollution so that the air we breathe is far from pure. And another factor against the urban is greener idea is that urbanisation often drives a country''s economy. Put simply, when there is greater wealth in society, more energy is consumed. One of the direct effects of a stronger economy, something that many citizens will now experience every day and may well find stressful, is the amount of traffic on the road, despite the other options we mentioned earlier. This is a problem that is not about to go away, although there are some solutions that could be considered. That is the end of the listening test. You now have 10 minutes to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-5/iL.VOL5.6.S1017.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1017),
(N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',N'e0a12a3e-0e47-5fd4-9e8d-6060a34bc04d',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.
Write <span>ONE WORD ONLY</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Reasons for and against urban migration',11,0,1,NULL,0,1017),
(N'8255c17e-f30f-56f6-9bab-f5f54e60466c',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'<h3>Reasons for and against urban migration</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>Cities today</span></p> <p>• account for 3% of our planet''s land area.</p> <p>• produce less }{SPACE}{ than non-urban areas.</p> <p><span>The advantages of more people moving to the city</span></p> <p>• some }{SPACE}{ would recover</p> <p>• more energy efficient for services like electricity and water</p> <ul><li><p></p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ in the city is more environmentally friendly</p> <p>• Recycling energy could be generated from the methane gas from }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Population urban women</p> <p>- start a family much later</p> <p>- are more likely to gain }{SPACE}{ at work.</p> <p><span>The downsides of living in the city</span></p> <p>• possible loss of }{SPACE}{ amongst some migrants</p> <p>• the higher }{SPACE}{ rate among the population</p> <p>• the poor quality of the }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Economic factors increased }{SPACE}{ in a population leads to more energy consumption </p> <p>• citizens may find the quantity of daily }{SPACE}{ stressful</p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>- Đáp án là carbon. Người nói so
sánh lượng khí thải giữa khu vực
đô thị và phi đô thị, và khẳng định
các thành phố sản sinh ít carbon
hơn so với vùng không đô thị.

- Từ “produce less ... than non-
urban areas” trong đề khớp trực

tiếp với thông tin này.
- Key words: produce less;
carbon; non-urban areas</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1017),
(N'8d8d814c-0948-5eae-8c02-41bb7243a15e',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>- Đáp án là forests. Khi dân số rời
nông thôn lên thành phố, áp lực
canh tác nông nghiệp giảm xuống,
từ đó rừng có thể phục hồi.
- Cụm “allowing forests to
regenerate” chính là nội dung
được diễn đạt lại trong đề.
- Key words: recover; regenerate;
forests</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1017),
(N'e2f53129-7c23-551d-8de1-abc62072a736',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 33',N'<p>- Đáp án là transport.
- Người nói so sánh cách di
chuyển ở nông thôn và thành phố,
nhấn mạnh rằng giao thông trong
thành phố thân thiện với môi
trường hơn. Điều này khớp với
cấu trúc “___ in the city is more
environmentally friendly”.
- Key words: environmentally
friendly; buses; trains</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1017),
(N'e9979eb7-f9e6-54c1-bea8-d3e1cd401935',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>- Đáp án là rubbish. Người nói
đưa ví dụ về Seattle, nơi khí
methane được tạo ra từ rác thải
của thành phố và dùng để sản
xuất năng lượng.
- Từ này điền chính xác vào cấu
trúc “methane gas from ...”.
- Key words: methane gas;
produced from; rubbish</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1017),
(N'30dac354-7901-5f2d-a0c6-8eeb45d29488',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>- Đáp án là promotion. Phụ nữ ở
đô thị có xu hướng sinh con muộn
vì tập trung vào sự nghiệp. Người
nói nêu rõ việc dễ được thăng tiến
là một lý do quan trọng.
- Từ “gain ... at work” tương ứng
với promotion.
- Key words: gain; promotion;
work</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1017),
(N'86bc49be-9db3-5980-bbd1-a31bc5d571c3',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>- Đáp án là culture.
- Một trong những lo ngại lớn
nhất về đô thị hóa là người di cư
có thể mất đi bản sắc văn hóa. Dù
người nói phản biện lại ý kiến
này, đây vẫn là downsides được
nêu trong bài.
- Key words: losing; culture;
traditions</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1017),
(N'5d9120f9-3aa8-5015-b3fb-cf6ff3eb851a',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>- Đáp án là crime.
- Người nói nhấn mạnh rằng tỷ lệ
tội phạm ở thành phố cao hơn so
với nông thôn. Đây là một nhược
điểm rõ ràng và được nhấn mạnh
bằng trải nghiệm thực tế của
người dân.
- Key words: higher rate; crime</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1017),
(N'123ddcf1-68f3-5bb6-aa67-9b83eaa6abbe',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 38',N'<p>- Đáp án là air.
- Người nói đề cập trực tiếp đến
chất lượng không khí kém do ô
nhiễm ở các thành phố.
- Cụm “the air we breathe is far
from pure” chính là diễn giải cho
câu hỏi.
- Key words: poor quality; air;
pollution</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1017),
(N'c61f08bc-f8d0-5adb-a28e-c74e6bee5ccc',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>- Đáp án là wealth.
- Người nói giải thích rằng khi xã
hội trở nên giàu có hơn, mức tiêu
thụ năng lượng cũng tăng theo.
- Điều này làm suy yếu lập luận
rằng đô thị luôn xanh hơn.
- Key words: greater wealth;
energy consumption</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1017),
(N'1d12d2c2-7738-5466-8c54-afe6514bf6f5',N'c80c48e0-18ac-5c05-b181-c4110d063a32',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>- Đáp án là traffic.
- Người nói mô tả lượng giao
thông lớn là hệ quả trực tiếp của
nền kinh tế phát triển và nhấn
mạnh rằng người dân thấy điều
này căng thẳng hằng ngày.
- Cụm “quantity of daily ...
stressful” khớp hoàn toàn.</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1017),
(N'51fca3d8-fea2-5c25-8645-29802998b6cf',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-5/iL.VOL5.5.S1016.mp3',N'Vol 5 LISTENING FULL TEST 5',0,7,1,NULL,1,1016),
(N'a51d18d1-ce7b-5056-8b4a-0db9fbb91c73',N'51fca3d8-fea2-5c25-8645-29802998b6cf',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>Australian Overseas Relocation Agency</h2><p><span>Time to look at questions one to six. Now listen carefully and answer questions one to six. Jason: Hello Australian Overseas Relocation Agency, this is Jason here.</span></p><p><span>Helen:</span> Oh hello, yes, I''m calling from London. My husband has just got a new job in Australia and his company recommended you to help us.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Great, I''ll get some details from you. Let''s start with your name, shall we?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Oh yes, it''s Helen Green.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Thank you, and what''s your current address?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> It''s a London address, 42 Station Road, Kew. It''s just near the Botanical Gardens.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Oh, that sounds nice. And in case I need to contact you, could I have a telephone number?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Yes, it''s 0207 9460 385.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Got it. Now, Helen, whereabouts in Australia are you relocating to? </p><p><span>Helen:</span> Brisbane. Jason: And is your husband''s company paying for the move? </p><p><span>Helen:</span> Yes, it''s a company called Kirkby Technology. </p><p><span>Jason:</span> I think we''ve worked with them before. And how soon do you need to move?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Well, they want my husband to start work in two weeks, at the start of the month. But school doesn''t finish here until the end of July, so we can''t possibly move until then. Shall we say the beginning of August then?</p><p><span>Jason:</span> That sounds more realistic. June is nearly over and I have so much to do before we move. Let''s start with the accommodation you need. Would you prefer a house or an apartment?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Well, we live in a big house with a garden now, which we love. But I think that initially we''d be happy with an apartment that''s fairly central, just until we get to know the city a little better and can find a house we love.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Sounds like a good idea. And what about location? There are some beautiful developments along the Brisbane River. It runs right through the city. The views are spectacular and you wouldn''t need to worry about transport.</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Well, actually, as we won''t have our own garden, I''d prefer something close to a park. That''s more important than a view for us.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> OK, I''ll make a note of that. Brisbane''s a lovely green city, so that won''t be hard. Is there anything else I can organise? <span>' AS nvarchar(max))+N'Helen:</span> Oh yes, furniture. We were planning to ship our own, but we''ve decided it isn''t really worth it. <span>Jason:</span> OK. Well, we can hire some for you for the first few months.</p><p><span>Helen:</span> That would be great.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 7 to 10. Now listen and answer questions 7 to 10. Jason: Now, Helen, what else would you like us to help you with?</span></span></p><p><span>Helen:</span> Well, we have a son, a teenager, who will be coming with us, so we''ll need to find a school for him.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Sure. What grade will he be in?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> He''s just turned 13, so he should be going into grade 9, but we''ve decided it would be better if he repeats grade 8. <span>Jason:</span> Right, so you''ll need a high school. Now, do you have any preferences regarding the school?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Well, he''s at a state school at the moment, but the company is prepared to pay his school fees, so we would probably go for a private school if possible. It doesn''t matter if it''s mixed or single sex.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Sure, I''ll make some enquiries and I''ll send you a short list of possibilities.</p><p><span>Helen:</span> That would be wonderful.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Oh, can you send me a copy of your son''s report? The latest one, if possible. The schools will want to know something about him as a student. You can fax or scan it to me.</p><p><span>Helen:</span> OK, fine. Jason: Now, is there anything else I can help you with today?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Yes, there is one other thing. We''d like some information to help us settle into the community and make friends. My son isn''t really into sport, but he''s a keen chess player. He''d like to continue playing when we move to Brisbane. Could you find out about a club for him?</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Certainly, I''ll make enquiries for you.</p><p><span>Helen:</span> And I play the flute, so I''d like to find a band that I could join, if possible.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Music is very popular here, so that should be easy. </p><p><span>Helen:</span> Thank you for all your help, Jason. </p><p><span>Jason:</span> My pleasure. I''ll be in touch soon.</p><p><span>That is the end of part one. You now have one minute to check your answers to part one.</span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span>Time to look at questions one to six. Now listen carefully and answer questions one to six. Jason: Hello Australian Overseas Relocation Agency, this is Jason here.</span></p><p><span>Helen:</span> Oh hello, yes, I''m calling from London. My husband has just got a new job in Australia and his company recommended you to help us.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Great, I''ll get some details from you. Let''s start with your name, shall we?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Oh yes, it''s Helen Green.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Thank you, and what''s your current address?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> It''s a London address, 42 Station Road, Kew. It''s just near the Botanical Gardens.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Oh, that sounds nice. And in case I need to contact you, could I have a telephone number?</p><p><span>Helen:</span> Yes, it''s 0207 9460 385.</p><p><span>Jason:</span> Got it. Now, Helen, whereabouts in Australia are you relocating to? </p><p><span