(N'b67de536-c07f-531c-8ccf-4f5254eac207',N'51fca3d8-fea2-5c25-8645-29802998b6cf',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Case study on one pupil''s learning strategies</h2><p><span>Part 3 You will hear a psychology undergraduate called Katrina discussing with her tutor her case study on the learning strategies used by one child at a school. First you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.</span></p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Ah, hello Katrina. Come in. You wanted to talk about your research project. I understand you''ll be doing it in Mountside Boys Secondary School?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yes, and I''ve decided to do a case study looking in detail at one pupil over the 12 days I''m in school, one day a week for three months, gathering data from a variety of different sources.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> OK. Now, have you had any ideas about your criteria for selecting your focus pupil?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Well, I figure it''s important to go for someone who''d be happy to participate. Otherwise it''d be very hard to gather rich data, sure. I don''t think it matters whether he''s the best in the class or the one who tries the hardest.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> OK. Well, it sounds as if you''ve thought it all through. Do you have any concerns at all?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> I''m meeting the teacher tomorrow, so I hope she''ll help on this. Tutor: I wouldn''t worry. I''m sure she''ll be very supportive.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yes. But I am concerned about something else. It''s that I''ll be gathering piles of verbal data and won''t know what''s going to be significant for my study.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> I tell you what, let me give you a very good article which should help you manage your data.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Great, thanks.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> So are you looking forward to starting?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Well, it''s the first time I''ll be researching on my own, and it''s over the whole term, both of which will be pretty challenging. But what I''m really wanting to do is experiment with the audio recording equipment we''ve been issued with. That should make a big difference to the quality of the data, you know.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> And you''ll be getting more tutorial support than last term.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yeah. What are the arrangements? Do I need to see you every week?</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, you have five hours allocated and you can judge how you use it yourself. So email me if you have any problems or queries and we can meet up. I have a lot of flexibility at the mo' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ment, so I should be able to see you without much notice.</p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.</span></p><p><span>Tutor:</span> So what sources of data do you intend to use for your case study?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> I was hoping to get your advice on that, but I do know I want to begin next week observing the pupil as he''s working in the classroom.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Yes, that sounds like the best starting point.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Would I need to prepare a detailed checklist in advance for this?</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> I think for this type of data gathering, it''s best to make field notes, but try to watch him in different lessons to see if his participation in maths is different from his participation in English, for example.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yeah. And of course, I want to do an interview with the pupil.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> You could base the questions on the structured survey we looked at last semester.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Well, I thought I''d go for something a bit less rigid, maybe start with some open questions and then follow up on his responses.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, usually when you''re new to interviewing, it can be good to plan ahead, but I''m happy for you to try something much less structured if you want.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Good, thanks.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Have you thought about trying out some think-aloud procedures? Get the pupil to explain what he''s doing while he''s working on a task, like his homework, for instance.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Actually, one of my classmates did that last term and I helped her analyse her data. It was very time-consuming, but really fascinating. But I was worried it might be a bit intrusive.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, you''d obviously have to talk to a teacher at the school about which task would be most appropriate, but I think it''ll be fine.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> OK, I''ll do that. And I was also wondering about whether I would be allowed to review the pupil''s marks to gauge his achievement in different subjects over the year?</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> You could, but I wouldn''t be too concerned if you can''t fit it in time-wise. Other data sources are probably more valuable.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Then I really think you should interview the teacher about the boy''s participation, etc.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Ye'+N's, I was hoping to do that, but I''d probably be a bit more nervous about it than doing the pupil interview.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, why not do a practice run with people in your group?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Good idea.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> And the last thing. I know we''re not allowed to do an IQ test with youngsters for ethical reasons, but what about a personality test?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> There''s no reason why not. I suggest you keep it short, though. - <span>Tutor:</span> Maximum one side of paper? Katrina: Yes, that''s a good rule of thumb to follow if you can.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p><span>Part 3 You will hear a psychology undergraduate called Katrina discussing with her tutor her case study on the learning strategies used by one child at a school. First you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.</span></p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Ah, hello Katrina. Come in. You wanted to talk about your research project. I understand you''ll be doing it in Mountside Boys Secondary School?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yes, and I''ve decided to do a case study looking in detail at one pupil over the 12 days I''m in school, one day a week for three months, gathering data from a variety of different sources.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> OK. Now, have you had any ideas about your criteria for selecting your focus pupil?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Well, I figure it''s important to go for someone who''d be happy to participate. Otherwise it''d be very hard to gather rich data, sure. I don''t think it matters whether he''s the best in the class or the one who tries the hardest.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> OK. Well, it sounds as if you''ve thought it all through. Do you have any concerns at all?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> I''m meeting the teacher tomorrow, so I hope she''ll help on this. Tutor: I wouldn''t worry. I''m sure she''ll be very supportive.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yes. But I am concerned about something else. It''s that I''ll be gathering piles of verbal data and won''t know what''s going to be significant for my study.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> I tell you what, let me give you a very good article which should help you manage your data.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Great, thanks.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> So are you looking forward to starting?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Well, it''s the first time I''ll be researching on my own, and it''s over the whole term, both of which will be pretty challenging. But what I''m really wanting to do is experiment with the audio recording equipment we''ve been issued with. That should make a big difference to the quality of the data, you know.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> And you''ll be getting more tutorial support than last term.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yeah. What are the arrangements? Do I need to see you every week?</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, you have five hours allocated and you can judge how you use it yourself. So email me if you have any problems or queries and we can meet up. I have a lot of flexibility at the moment, so I should be able to see you without much noti' AS nvarchar(max))+N'ce.</p><p><span>Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.</span></p><p><span>Tutor:</span> So what sources of data do you intend to use for your case study?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> I was hoping to get your advice on that, but I do know I want to begin next week observing the pupil as he''s working in the classroom.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Yes, that sounds like the best starting point.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Would I need to prepare a detailed checklist in advance for this?</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> I think for this type of data gathering, it''s best to make field notes, but try to watch him in different lessons to see if his participation in maths is different from his participation in English, for example.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yeah. And of course, I want to do an interview with the pupil.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> You could base the questions on the structured survey we looked at last semester.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Well, I thought I''d go for something a bit less rigid, maybe start with some open questions and then follow up on his responses.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, usually when you''re new to interviewing, it can be good to plan ahead, but I''m happy for you to try something much less structured if you want.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Good, thanks.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Have you thought about trying out some think-aloud procedures? Get the pupil to explain what he''s doing while he''s working on a task, like his homework, for instance.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Actually, one of my classmates did that last term and I helped her analyse her data. It was very time-consuming, but really fascinating. But I was worried it might be a bit intrusive.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, you''d obviously have to talk to a teacher at the school about which task would be most appropriate, but I think it''ll be fine.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> OK, I''ll do that. And I was also wondering about whether I would be allowed to review the pupil''s marks to gauge his achievement in different subjects over the year?</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> You could, but I wouldn''t be too concerned if you can''t fit it in time-wise. Other data sources are probably more valuable.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Then I really think you should interview the teacher about the boy''s participation, etc.</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Yes, I was hoping to do that, but I''d probably be a bit '+N'more nervous about it than doing the pupil interview.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> Well, why not do a practice run with people in your group?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> Good idea.</p><p><span>Tutor:</span> And the last thing. I know we''re not allowed to do an IQ test with youngsters for ethical reasons, but what about a personality test?</p><p><span>Katrina:</span> There''s no reason why not. I suggest you keep it short, though. - <span>Tutor:</span> Maximum one side of paper? Katrina: Yes, that''s a good rule of thumb to follow if you can.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-5/iL.VOL5.5.S1016.PART3.mp3',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,1016),
