(N'1c60d6ee-3404-5ce0-83a0-9719147812e2',N'b67de536-c07f-531c-8ccf-4f5254eac207',0,2,18,N'<p>What action do the speakers agree on for each of the following data gathering techniques? </p><p>Choose <span><span>SIX</span></span> answers from the box and write the correct letter, <span><span>A-G,</span></span> next to questions 25-30.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Answer choices',15,0,2,NULL,0,1016),
(N'500111dc-22d1-5a1d-8cec-12260984304b',N'1c60d6ee-3404-5ce0-83a0-9719147812e2',0,3,19,N'<p>Observation of the pupil in class</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là F.</p><p> - Khi quan sát trong lớp, Katrina được khuyên so sánh hành vi ở các môn học khác nhau. </p><p>- Đây là mục tiêu chính của hoạt động quan sát. </p><p>- Key words: + different lessons + participation</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,1016),
(N'3f60f02c-ac51-5108-a5df-052be8b6fc3f',N'1c60d6ee-3404-5ce0-83a0-9719147812e2',0,3,19,N'<p>Interview with the pupil</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là D. </p><p>- Phỏng vấn học sinh nên áp dụng cách tiếp cận linh hoạt, không cứng nhắc. </p><p>- Tutor cho phép Katrina tự điều chỉnh theo tình huống. </p><p>- Key words: + less rigid + flexible</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,1016),
(N'20d2e7e4-76ee-53b2-bd71-c72221faf53e',N'1c60d6ee-3404-5ce0-83a0-9719147812e2',0,3,19,N'<p>Think-aloud procedures</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là C. </p><p>- Với think-aloud procedures, Katrina cần hỏi giáo viên để xác định trọng tâm phù hợp.</p><p> - Việc này đảm bảo hoạt động không gây ảnh hưởng lớp học. </p><p>- Key words: + talk to a teacher + appropriate task</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,1016),
(N'b8bc2a25-657e-5dcf-af54-e7acacc91807',N'1c60d6ee-3404-5ce0-83a0-9719147812e2',0,3,19,N'<p>Review of the pupil''s marks</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là B. </p><p>- Việc xem lại điểm số có thể bỏ qua nếu không đủ thời gian. </p><p>- Tutor đánh giá đây không phải nguồn dữ liệu ưu tiên. </p><p>- Key words: + time-wise + not too concerned</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,1016),
(N'e6059408-bdca-59ca-b4ff-0251a4b3966c',N'1c60d6ee-3404-5ce0-83a0-9719147812e2',0,3,19,N'<p>Interview with class teacher</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là A. </p><p>- Trước khi phỏng vấn giáo viên, Katrina được khuyên thử trước với bạn cùng lớp. </p><p>- Điều này giúp giảm căng thẳng và chuẩn bị tốt hơn.</p><p>- Key words: + practice run + group</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,1016),
(N'01f9cc90-e4a6-5ed9-8175-7aab000c5bfe',N'1c60d6ee-3404-5ce0-83a0-9719147812e2',0,3,19,N'<p>Personality profile</p>',N'<p>- Đáp án là E.
- Với hồ sơ tính cách, hai bên
thống nhất giới hạn số mục
trong một trang.
- Đây là cách kiểm soát độ
dài và tính khả thi.
- Key words:
+ keep it short
+ one side of paper</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,1016),
(N'725f0f8a-3aa3-559b-a68e-7455fe2431ee',N'51fca3d8-fea2-5c25-8645-29802998b6cf',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Restoration Ecology Programme</h2><p>Good afternoon. I''d like to turn it over to Dr. Carey, who will talk about the program in Restoration Ecology. Thanks, Chris. A lot of people think that human beings can do whatever they want to the environment. But as Aldo Leopold taught, land is a system of interdependent parts which should be regarded as a community and not a commodity. Well, that idea has influenced what we teach here in our program, where students come from all over the world to learn about restoring native plant communities back into an ecologically natural state. This field is therefore a combination of formal science with practical applications, and that is quite attractive to many people.</p><p>We have students, for example, from many different nations who come just to take part in this unique program. Our location is also quite unique. We have the world''s oldest restored native plant community in Curtis Prairie at the Wisconsin Arboretum. Some say that this is proof that the science of restoration ecology was birthed in Wisconsin. Well, that may be oversimplified, but our reputation is strong. But students don''t have to study prairies only.</p><p>One student, Edmond Mukala from the Congo, came to study restoring ancient wetlands in the Congo using knowledge gained from historic samples of the soil seed bank. Not all the seeds survived, but some can remain dormant for many years. Mr. Mukala wanted to find out what type of community would be most similar to that ancient seed bank. He has recently returned to the Congo and is now cooperating with the government to implement his findings. So the only prerequisite for doing research here is that it is a native plant community.</p><p>That means not just prairies, but wetlands, woodlands, savannas, and other environments. We''re proud of the diversity of research topics in our program. And we continue to grow. This year we have 32 new students from eight different countries. When students first arrive, they begin rigid coursework in statistics, ecology, plant identification, and the theory of landscape change. Then they take part in internships at local conservation agencies such as the Arboretum, the Nature Conservancy, the Parks Department, and others. We find internships to be crucial in helping students apply the knowledge they have gained in the classroom. And we''re proud to say that, since the beginning, we have graduated 277 students with master degrees from our programs and 122 students wi' AS nvarchar(max))+N'th PhDs. Some have gone on to bigger and better things.</p><p>One graduate is now the Director of the Worldwide Fund for Nature in China. Another is the Director of Parks Development in California. And others now lead their own research departments in universities around the world.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Good afternoon. I''d like to turn it over to Dr. Carey, who will talk about the program in Restoration Ecology. Thanks, Chris. A lot of people think that human beings can do whatever they want to the environment. But as Aldo Leopold taught, land is a system of interdependent parts which should be regarded as a community and not a commodity. Well, that idea has influenced what we teach here in our program, where students come from all over the world to learn about restoring native plant communities back into an ecologically natural state. This field is therefore a combination of formal science with practical applications, and that is quite attractive to many people.</p><p>We have students, for example, from many different nations who come just to take part in this unique program. Our location is also quite unique. We have the world''s oldest restored native plant community in Curtis Prairie at the Wisconsin Arboretum. Some say that this is proof that the science of restoration ecology was birthed in Wisconsin. Well, that may be oversimplified, but our reputation is strong. But students don''t have to study prairies only.</p><p>One student, Edmond Mukala from the Congo, came to study restoring ancient wetlands in the Congo using knowledge gained from historic samples of the soil seed bank. Not all the seeds survived, but some can remain dormant for many years. Mr. Mukala wanted to find out what type of community would be most similar to that ancient seed bank. He has recently returned to the Congo and is now cooperating with the government to implement his findings. So the only prerequisite for doing research here is that it is a native plant community.</p><p>That means not just prairies, but wetlands, woodlands, savannas, and other environments. We''re proud of the diversity of research topics in our program. And we continue to grow. This year we have 32 new students from eight different countries. When students first arrive, they begin rigid coursework in statistics, ecology, plant identification, and the theory of landscape change. Then they take part in internships at local conservation agencies such as the Arboretum, the Nature Conservancy, the Parks Department, and others. We find internships to be crucial in helping students apply the knowledge they have gained in the classroom. And we''re proud to say that, since the beginning, we have graduated 277 students with master degrees from our programs and 122 students with PhDs. Some have gone on to bigger a' AS nvarchar(max))+N'nd better things.</p><p>One graduate is now the Director of the Worldwide Fund for Nature in China. Another is the Director of Parks Development in California. And others now lead their own research departments in universities around the world.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-5/iL.VOL5.5.S1016.PART4.mp3',NULL,1,0,4,NULL,0,1016),
(N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',N'725f0f8a-3aa3-559b-a68e-7455fe2431ee',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below.
Write <span>NO MORE THAN ONE WORD</span> for each answer.</p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Restoration Ecology Programme',11,0,1,NULL,0,1016),
(N'8d7526ac-b463-5629-8ea1-580e7e5858a3',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'<h3>Restoration Ecology Programme</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>Origins</span></p> <p>• Influence: Leopold''s concept that land should be viewed as }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• Practice: Restore native }{SPACE}{ communities to original condition.</p> <p>• A combination of }{SPACE}{ and applications.</p> <p><span>Types of communities</span></p> <p>• Curtis Prairie is the }{SPACE}{ restored prairie in America.</p> <p>• Edmond Moukala researched restoring }{SPACE}{ in the Congo.</p> <p><span>Academics</span></p> <p>• Courses in statistics, theory, ecology and plant }{SPACE}{ </p> <ul><li><p></p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ help students gain practical experience.</p> <p><span>Graduates</span></p> <p>• Granted }{SPACE}{ Master''s Degrees and 122 PhDs.</p> <p>• One former student is now the }{SPACE}{ of the World Wide Fund for Nature. </p> <p>• Many others continue to conduct }{SPACE}{ in their own university programmes. </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>- Đáp án là community. </p><p>- Leopold cho rằng đất đai phải được nhìn nhận như một cộng đồng, không phải hàng hóa. </p><p>- Đây là nền tảng tư tưởng của chương trình. </p><p>- Key words: + regarded as + community</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,1016),
(N'85cacc55-92af-5b76-8cbe-9c970485cdc0',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 32',N'<p>- Đáp án là plant. </p><p>- Chương trình tập trung vào cộng đồng thực vật bản địa. </p><p>- “Native plant communities” được nhắc trực tiếp. </p><p>- Key words: + native + plant communities</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,1016),
(N'850d6de7-ffab-5a1d-9ceb-65d462dac5bb',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 33',N'<p>- Đáp án là science. </p><p>- Restoration ecology kết hợp khoa học chính quy và ứng dụng thực tiễn. </p><p>- Đây là đặc điểm nổi bật của ngành. </p><p>- Key words: + combination of + formal science</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,1016),
(N'f24ba590-e06e-595e-b4d1-a7a1be60dfcc',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 34',N'<p>- Đáp án là oldest. </p><p>- Curtis Prairie được giới thiệu là thảo nguyên được phục hồi lâu đời nhất thế giới. </p><p>- Thông tin này nhấn mạnh uy tín học thuật của chương trình. </p><p>- Key words: + world’s + oldest</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,1016),
(N'71e47e4a-76e1-5075-8869-461fc6faea63',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 35',N'<p>- Đáp án là wetlands. </p><p>- Edmond Mukala nghiên cứu đầm lầy cổ ở Congo. </p><p>- Đây là ví dụ về phạm vi nghiên cứu đa dạng. </p><p>- Key words:</p><p>+ restoring + wetlands</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,1016),
(N'1150b222-b92c-56ca-b036-e4fd8becc31f',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 36',N'<p>- Đáp án là identification. </p><p>- Sinh viên học nhận dạng thực vật như một học phần cốt lõi. </p><p>- Từ này xuất hiện nguyên văn trong bài giảng. </p><p>- Key words: + coursework + plant identification</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,1016),
(N'd41c59a0-d670-527a-a0c0-8dd9b52185d1',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 37',N'<p>- Đáp án là internships. </p><p>- Thực tập giúp sinh viên tích lũy kinh nghiệm thực tiễn. </p><p>- Vai trò của internships được nhấn mạnh rõ ràng. </p><p>- Key words: + take part in + practical experience</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,1016),
(N'ade62170-6818-5569-9fd9-4adf27645d67',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 38',N'<p>- Đáp án là 277. </p><p>- Đây là số bằng Thạc sĩ đã được cấp từ trước đến nay. </p><p>- Con số được nêu trực tiếp.</p><p>- Key words: + graduated + master degrees</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,1016),
(N'9ad79c96-9872-5d56-832d-83064350e115',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 39',N'<p>- Đáp án là Director. </p><p>- Một cựu sinh viên hiện là Giám đốc của WWF tại Trung Quốc. </p><p>- Chức danh được nói rõ ràng. </p><p>- Key words: + now the + Director</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,1016),
(N'20a3d6ca-49cc-5b01-a73d-329dea4d22e4',N'0a7d15be-cea0-55e7-8aa5-c8434142f82d',0,3,19,N'Question number 40',N'<p>- Đáp án là research. </p><p>- Nhiều cựu sinh viên tiếp tục làm nghiên cứu trong các chương trình đại học. </p><p>- Điều này thể hiện tác động học thuật lâu dài của chương trình. </p><p>- Key words: + conduct + research</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,1016),
(N'5e51eb7f-7371-58e5-84eb-4925392eebd5',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-5/iL.VOL5.4.S1015.mp3',N'Vol 5 LISTENING FULL TEST 4',0,7,1,NULL,1,1015),
(N'5cbee37e-0ca9-55ca-9c8a-a4f70cc4077c',N'5e51eb7f-7371-58e5-84eb-4925392eebd5',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>Local fair</h2><p>You will hear a number of different recordings, and you will have to answer questions on what you hear. There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions, and you will have a chance to check your work. All the recordings will be played once only. The test is in four parts. Write all your answers in the listening question booklet. At the end of the test, you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet. Now turn to Part 1 on page 2 of your question booklet. Part 1 You will hear a reporter from a community newspaper phoning a woman who is organising a local fair. First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 6. Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 6.</p><p>Reporter: Good morning, is that Mrs. Flint?</p><p>Woman: Yes, speaking. Reporter: It''s Rick Graham here from the community newspaper. We want to print an article about the local fair to encourage more people to come. Could I ask you a few questions about the event?</p><p>Woman: Yes, of course.</p><p>Reporter: So the fair''s on Saturday, isn''t it? And I know that every year the fair raises money for something local.</p><p>Woman: Yes, that''s right. Last year we were able to help the school, and this time, after careful thought, we''ve decided to support the hospital, because that''s obviously important.</p><p>Reporter: OK, I''ll make a note of that. And then in terms of attendance, how many people do you think will come?</p><p>Woman: Well, it depends a bit on the weather. If it rains for 24 hours before the event, it does put some people off, but in the past we''ve had approximately 3,000 visitors, and I expect it''ll be roughly the same this time.</p><p>Reporter: So that''s why you need a big venue. It''s at Battersby Park, right?</p><p>Woman: Yes, it''s the same place every year. I''m actually new to the area. Could you spell that for me?</p><p>Reporter: Sure, it''s Battersby. B-A-T-T-E-R-S-B-Y.</p><p>Woman: Great, thanks.</p><p>Reporter: OK, so if my readers come to the fair, what sort of activities can they do?</p><p>Woman: Well, we''ve got lots of things. One example is what we''re calling the swap shop. Children can bring in a toy from home that they don''t want anymore and exchange it for something else.</p><p>Reporter: That''s a good idea. Then in the afternoon, there''s going to be a basketball competition. That''s mixed teams, boys and girls.</p><p>Woman: After that, we''ll be doing the same thing again, but this time it''ll be soccer. Unfor' AS nvarchar(max))+N'tunately, the cricket pitch is closed for maintenance at the moment.</p><p>Reporter: I''ll mention that in the article. And then anything else, what can I tell people that''ll really make them want to come to the fair?</p><p>Woman: Well, for the first time this year, we''ll have some classic vehicles for you to ride in. One of the highlights is you''ll be able to ride right around the park in an ambulance. It was built in the 1950s, so it''s a chance to experience how they did things in the past.</p><p>Reporter: That should be quite exciting, with the lights flashing and bells going.</p><p>Woman: Exactly. Then we''ve also got an international visitor coming, Kiko Mahara, who''s going to be running a workshop on making models.</p><p>Reporter: Oh, I''ve heard of her. She uses paper to create the most amazing little animals, planes, all sorts of things.</p><p>Woman: That''s right.</p><p>Reporter: I think it''ll be a lot of fun.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions seven to ten. Now listen and answer questions seven to ten.</span></span></p><p>Reporter: Then there are also things to buy, because of course we''re trying to raise money. So, for example?</p><p>Woman: Well, starting in the main tent. This is the place you want to come if you''re looking for previously owned clothing. There''ll be a good range, but no shoes this time. It''s just too difficult to keep them in pairs.</p><p>Reporter: OK.</p><p>Woman: Then, down by the playground, there''ll be coffee for sale all day. It makes a nice break from the shopping, and we always do really well with that. Sandwiches and hot food are by the duck pond.</p><p>Reporter: OK, got that. Is there anything else, Mrs Flint?</p><p>Woman: Well, we''re trying something new this year, and it''s going to be around the fountain. This one will interest the gardeners, because we''re going to sell a range of plants there. It''s a bit of an experiment.</p><p>Reporter: I''m sure it''ll be a success. Oh, just one more thing. We''re always looking for more volunteers to help run the fair, so could you ask people who want to volunteer to phone me?</p><p>Woman: Yeah, sure. What''s the number, Mrs Flint? Reporter: It''s 0274 548 9017. And you''re happy for that to go in the paper?</p><p>Woman: Yes, that''s right.</p><p>That is the end of Part 1. You now have one minute to check your answers to Part 1.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>You will hear a number of different recordings, and you will have to answer questions on what you hear. There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions, and you will have a chance to check your work. All the recordings will be played once only. The test is in four parts. Write all your answers in the listening question booklet. At the end of the test, you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet. Now turn to Part 1 on page 2 of your question booklet. Part 1 You will hear a reporter from a community newspaper phoning a woman who is organising a local fair. First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 6. Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 6.</p><p>Reporter: Good morning, is that Mrs. Flint?</p><p>Woman: Yes, speaking. Reporter: It''s Rick Graham here from the community newspaper. We want to print an article about the local fair to encourage more people to come. Could I ask you a few questions about the event?</p><p>Woman: Yes, of course.</p><p>Reporter: So the fair''s on Saturday, isn''t it? And I know that every year the fair raises money for something local.</p><p>Woman: Yes, that''s right. Last year we were able to help the school, and this time, after careful thought, we''ve decided to support the hospital, because that''s obviously important.</p><p>Reporter: OK, I''ll make a note of that. And then in terms of attendance, how many people do you think will come?</p><p>Woman: Well, it depends a bit on the weather. If it rains for 24 hours before the event, it does put some people off, but in the past we''ve had approximately 3,000 visitors, and I expect it''ll be roughly the same this time.</p><p>Reporter: So that''s why you need a big venue. It''s at Battersby Park, right?</p><p>Woman: Yes, it''s the same place every year. I''m actually new to the area. Could you spell that for me?</p><p>Reporter: Sure, it''s Battersby. B-A-T-T-E-R-S-B-Y.</p><p>Woman: Great, thanks.</p><p>Reporter: OK, so if my readers come to the fair, what sort of activities can they do?</p><p>Woman: Well, we''ve got lots of things. One example is what we''re calling the swap shop. Children can bring in a toy from home that they don''t want anymore and exchange it for something else.</p><p>Reporter: That''s a good idea. Then in the afternoon, there''s going to be a basketball competition. That''s mixed teams, boys and girls.</p><p>Woman: After that, we''ll be doing the same thing again, but this time it''ll be soccer. Unfortunately, the crick' AS nvarchar(max))+N'et pitch is closed for maintenance at the moment.</p><p>Reporter: I''ll mention that in the article. And then anything else, what can I tell people that''ll really make them want to come to the fair?</p><p>Woman: Well, for the first time this year, we''ll have some classic vehicles for you to ride in. One of the highlights is you''ll be able to ride right around the park in an ambulance. It was built in the 1950s, so it''s a chance to experience how they did things in the past.</p><p>Reporter: That should be quite exciting, with the lights flashing and bells going.</p><p>Woman: Exactly. Then we''ve also got an international visitor coming, Kiko Mahara, who''s going to be running a workshop on making models.</p><p>Reporter: Oh, I''ve heard of her. She uses paper to create the most amazing little animals, planes, all sorts of things.</p><p>Woman: That''s right.</p><p>Reporter: I think it''ll be a lot of fun.</p><p><span><span>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions seven to ten. Now listen and answer questions seven to ten.</span></span></p><p>Reporter: Then there are also things to buy, because of course we''re trying to raise money. So, for example?</p><p>Woman: Well, starting in the main tent. This is the place you want to come if you''re looking for previously owned clothing. There''ll be a good range, but no shoes this time. It''s just too difficult to keep them in pairs.</p><p>Reporter: OK.</p><p>Woman: Then, down by the playground, there''ll be coffee for sale all day. It makes a nice break from the shopping, and we always do really well with that. Sandwiches and hot food are by the duck pond.</p><p>Reporter: OK, got that. Is there anything else, Mrs Flint?</p><p>Woman: Well, we''re trying something new this year, and it''s going to be around the fountain. This one will interest the gardeners, because we''re going to sell a range of plants there. It''s a bit of an experiment.</p><p>Reporter: I''m sure it''ll be a success. Oh, just one more thing. We''re always looking for more volunteers to help run the fair, so could you ask people who want to volunteer to phone me?</p><p>Woman: Yeah, sure. What''s the number, Mrs Flint? Reporter: It''s 0274 548 9017. And you''re happy for that to go in the paper?</p><p>Woman: Yes, that''s right.</p><p>That is the end of Part 1. You now have one minute to check your answers to Part 1.</p>'),NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/vol-5/iL.VOL5.4.S1015.PART1.mp3',NULL,1,0,1,NULL,0,1015),
(N'62aa9d48-710d-51cc-9151-dffaf3daeed4',N'5cbee37e-0ca9-55ca-9c8a-a4f70cc4077c',0,2,18,N'<p>Complete the notes below. </p><p>Write <span>ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER</span> for each answer. </p>',N'',N'',N'',N'Local fair',11,0,1,NULL,0,1015),
(N'45264fb3-9780-5e19-a271-31a358a07c62',N'62aa9d48-710d-51cc-9151-dffaf3daeed4',0,3,19,N'<h3>Local fair</h3><table border="1"><tbody><tr><td><p><span>General information</span></p> <p>• The fair is on Saturday.</p> <p>• It is raising money for the }{SPACE}{ </p> <p>• It will be attended by about 3,000 people.</p> <p>• It will be held in }{SPACE}{ Park.</p> <p><span>Activities at the fair</span></p> <p>• swap a }{SPACE}{ for something else</p> <p>• play basketball and }{SPACE}{ in the afternoon</p> <p>• ride in an old }{SPACE}{ and other classic vehicles</p> <p>• take part in a workshop making models out of }{SPACE}{ </p> <p><span>Things to buy</span></p> <p>• Second-hand }{SPACE}{ will be in the main tent.</p> <ul><li><p></p></li></ul> }{SPACE}{ will be on sale by the playground.</p> <p>• Sandwiches and hot food will be beside the duck pond.</p> <p>• Different kinds of garden }{SPACE}{ will be sold around the fountain.</p> <p><span>People interested in volunteering</span></p> <p>• phone Mrs Flint on }{SPACE}{ </p></td></tr></tbody></table>',N'<p>- Đáp án là hospital.</p><p> - Người tổ chức nói rõ hội chợ năm nay gây quỹ cho bệnh viện, khác với năm trước là trường học. </p><p>- Thông tin được nói trực tiếp, không paraphrase phức tạp. </p><p>- Key words: </p><p>+ raise money </p><p>+ support the hospital</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,1,NULL,0,1015),
(N'3183c928-371d-5a83-984c-bbd4f88ebf84',N'62aa9d48-710d-51cc-9151-dffaf3daeed4',0,3,19,N'Question number 2',N'<p>- Đáp án là Battersby.</p><p>- Địa điểm tổ chức được xác nhận là Battersby Park. </p><p>- Câu hỏi chỉ cần tên công viên, nên điền “Battersby”. </p><p>- Key words: + Battersby Park</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,2,NULL,0,1015),
(N'48ec5865-da1d-59be-aa40-8ad7d20de3df',N'62aa9d48-710d-51cc-9151-dffaf3daeed4',0,3,19,N'Question number 3',N'<p>- Đáp án là toy. </p><p>- Hoạt động “swap shop” cho phép trẻ em mang đồ chơi đến để đổi. </p><p>- “Toy from home” là cụm thông tin trọng tâm. </p><p>- Key words: + swap shop + bring in a toy</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,3,NULL,0,1015),
(N'13bb6e59-69fc-59b4-a143-4df1d0c53109',N'62aa9d48-710d-51cc-9151-dffaf3daeed4',0,3,19,N'Question number 4',N'<p>- Đáp án là soccer.
