(N'9736fe54-c614-5075-9efa-c51c61e2e0b1',N'5e51eb7f-7371-58e5-84eb-4925392eebd5',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<h2>Peter De Wint</h2><p>Part 3. You will hear two art history students called Tina and Dave preparing for a seminar on a 19th century English artist called Peter DeWitt. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 26. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> Hi, Tina. Shall we prepare for our seminar tomorrow? </p><p><span>Tina:</span> You mean talk about the paintings of Peter DeWitt?</p><p><span>Dave:</span> Yeah. Let''s start with background information. I found a lot of sources about other English artists from the early 19th century, but not that much about DeWitt.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Yeah, I know. I''ve only just discovered him myself.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> I know. Most of the information about him came from his wife''s writings.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Right. </p><p><span>Dave:</span> One thing I''ve noticed about his paintings is that they never seem to be signed. </p><p><span>Tina:</span> Really? That''s interesting. </p><p><span>Dave:</span> I mean, it must have been a conscious decision, although signatures are really useful now for experts trying to distinguish forgeries from pictures that really are by famous artists.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Yes, even during their lifetimes, artists had to be wary of forgeries.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> But he had this idea that his paintings were signed all over, as he put it, because they were distinctly in his hand.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Right.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> So effectively there was no point in signing them. I was interested to read about what the professor said about the work DeWitt did for Oxford University.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Oh yeah? He did some engravings of various buildings at the university throughout the 1820s and 30s, but they weren''t well received. What the professor objected to was how inaccurately they represented the subject matter.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> I don''t really understand that because, to me, they look very accomplished and quite beautiful. He must have spent ages doing them.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> And DeWitt did a lot of pictures of the city of Lincoln and the region around it, didn''t he? Especially his more high-profile ones.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> It''s surprising in a way because it''s so flat. And other artists at that time would go and paint mountains, not flat, featureless landscapes. They wouldn''t have seen much potential there. </p><p><span>Tina:</span> But DeWitt managed to uncover all this great subjec' AS nvarchar(max))+N't matter in little villages. </p><p><span>Dave:</span> Sure. So do you see him as Lincoln''s artist then? </p><p><span>Tina:</span> No, not really. Admittedly, it''s hard to think of a greater painter who''s associated with the city. But if you look at a breakdown of the places featured in his pictures, Lincoln doesn''t feature as highly as you might think. He did more of places like the Lake District. Dave: And London.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Yes, where he lived too for a time. Oh, and did you know that DeWitt was friends with the poet Keats?</p><p><span>Dave:</span> Yes, what a small world. And there are lots of references in Keats'' poems to flowers and streams, exactly the kinds of thing that DeWitt was painting. So Keats and DeWitt must have influenced each other.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> What? I think that''s stretching it a bit.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> Well, that''s what the academic John Lord suggests in his essay.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Well, I see you''ve done your homework. </p><p><span>Dave:</span> I don''t know, maybe Lord does interpret some of DeWitt''s paintings as a response to Keats'' poetry. But it''s only his opinion. We still need to see if that theory stands up to scrutiny. </p><p><span>Tina:</span> I think you''re being excessively trusting of Lord.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> Perhaps. Maybe that''s true. </p><p>Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 27 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30. </p><p><span>Dave:</span> So, we need to consider four different paintings.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Right. Let''s start with Torquesy Castle. </p><p><span>Dave:</span> Yes, the interesting thing here is the way he painted the ruined castle and the nearby village, which was built much later. He seems to be emphasising the difference between them. </p><p><span>Tina:</span> Hard to say, but you might be right.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> And then there''s Still Life.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Right. Do you think that''s the kind of thing he''d have sold or exhibited? </p><p><span>Dave:</span> I don''t know. Maybe not. It was in all likelihood just done to showcase his technique to one of his pupils.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Ah, right. He worked as an art tutor, did he? </p><p><span>Dave:</span> Yeah. What about the cricketers? It makes living in a village seem really idyllic. - <span>Tina:</span> Right. I imagine things weren''t quite as perfect as they look there. </p><p><span>Dave:</span> No. One of my favourite de'+N' wints is The Burning of the Houses of Parliament.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Oh, yes. Because that fire actually happened, didn''t it, in 18... </p><p><span>Dave:</span> 34.</p><p><span>Tina:</span> Yes. But unlikely to have been done on site. I mean, it''s quite plausible that he actually witnessed the event and then went back and did it in his studio later.</p><p><span>Dave:</span> Yeah, I guess you''re right. So, what do you think to the... </p><p><span>That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.</span></p>'),(CAST(N'<p>Part 3. You will hear two art history students called Tina and Dave preparing for a seminar on a 19th century English artist called Peter DeWitt. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 26. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 26.</p><p><span>Dave:</span>