(N'3ce61111-d6aa-534d-89c6-55ba078d5de2',N'36b7603b-98e4-55b9-a8d7-6d0cbe938443',0,3,19,N'Question number 9',N'<p>+ Synonyms: "background in" (nền tảng) ↔ "long experience in that field" (kinh nghiệm lâu dài trong 1 lĩnh vực) </p><p>+ Interpretation: Nền tảng của ông ấy trong ngành …. cũng như kinh doanh đã cho ông kỹ năng điều hành Izon.</p><p>+ Answer: Kinh nghiệm lâu năm của ông trong lĩnh vực kỹ sư là yếu tố quan trọng cho thành công của công ty.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là engineering</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,9,NULL,0,977),
(N'4e0a9564-8d20-5fcf-817e-37caa26ffbc6',N'36b7603b-98e4-55b9-a8d7-6d0cbe938443',0,3,19,N'Question number 10',N'<p>+ Synonyms: "marketing relies on" (việc marketing dựa vào) ↔ "critical part of the company''s marketing is" (1 phần quan trọng của việc marketing của công ty là…) </p><p>+ Interpretation: Tiếp thị của công ty dựa vào các ….. về nghiên cứu của khách hàng. </p><p>+ Answer: Phần quan trọng trong khâu marketing là các bài báo mà khách hàng xuất bản, mô tả công việc họ thực hiện với qNano.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là articles</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,10,NULL,0,977),
(N'538dbd63-7d6a-5bdc-afc7-08293d8dc67e',N'36b7603b-98e4-55b9-a8d7-6d0cbe938443',0,3,19,N'Question number 11',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Yaniv Gaynor trước đây là một ….. không có kinh nghiệm bán hàng.</p><p>+ Answer: Gaynor chuyển từ vị trí giáo viên đại học sang vị trí đầu tiên ở khu vực tư nhân</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là teacher</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,11,NULL,0,977),
(N'c0deae58-6802-54f6-aaa7-04d8ccdae78f',N'36b7603b-98e4-55b9-a8d7-6d0cbe938443',0,3,19,N'Question number 12',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Để chẩn đoán huyết khối, một …. có thể được phát triển.</p><p>+ Answer: Dự án với Harvard nhằm tìm ra một loại hạt trong máu, và nếu trong tương lai họ có thể làm được thì có thể thiết kế một xét nghiệm đơn giản -</p><p>&gt; Đáp án là test</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,12,NULL,0,977),
(N'808181af-c8ae-5e13-b9e7-8657eb9bb4fc',N'36b7603b-98e4-55b9-a8d7-6d0cbe938443',0,3,19,N'Question number 13',N'<p>+ Interpretation: …. sẽ được thực hiện tại trụ sở Mỹ của Izon.</p><p>+ Answer: Kế hoạch tương lai là di chuyển đến văn phòng rộng hơn để khách hàng có thể đến tòa nhà Izon để đào tạo với sản phẩm</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là training</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,13,NULL,0,977),
(N'4f7a3b64-3316-5c03-9114-349cb0646fe8',N'4f84567c-3208-5b5d-976e-eb239fa1f1f6',0,1,14,(CAST(N'<p><span><span>Human-like culture in chimpanzees</span></span></p><p></p><p><span><span>A</span></span> The similarities between chimpanzees and humans have been studied for years, but in the past decade researchers have determined that these resemblances run much deeper than any one first thought. For instance, the nut cracking observed in the Tar Forest is far from a simple chimpanzee behavior; rather it is a singular adaptation found only in that particular part of Africa and a trait that biologists consider to be an expression of chimpanzee culture. Scientists frequently use the term “culture” to describe elementary animal behaviors—such as the regional dialects of different populations of songbirds—but as it turns out, the rich and varied cultural traditions found among chimpanzees are second in complexity only to human traditions.</p><p></p><p><span><span>B</span></span> During the past two years, an unprecedented scientific collaboration, involving every major research group studying chimpanzees, has documented a multitude of distinct cultural patterns extending across Africa, in actions ranging from the animals’ use of tools to their forms of communication and social customs. This emerging picture of chimpanzees not only affects how we think of these amazing creatures but also alters human beings’ conception of our own uniqueness and hints at ancient foundations for extraordinary capacity for culture.</p><p></p><p><span><span>C</span></span> Homo sapiens and Pan troglodytes have coexisted for hundreds of millennia and share more than 98 percent of their genetic material, yet only 40 years ago we still knew next to nothing about chimpanzee behavior in the wild. That began to change in the 1960s, when Toshisada Nishida of Kyoto University in Japan and Jane Goodall began their studies of wild chimpanzees at two field sites in Tanzania. (Goodall’s research station at Gombe—the first of its kind—is more famous, but Nishida’s site at Mahale is the second oldest chimpanzee research site in the world.)</p><p></p><p><span><span>D</span></span> In these initial studies, as the chimpanzees became accustomed to close observation, there remarkable discoveries began. Researchers witnessed a range of unexpected behaviors, including fashioning and using tools, hunting, meat eating, food sharing and lethal fights between members of neighboring communities. In the years that followed, other primatologists set up camp elsewhere, and, despite all the financial, politi' AS nvarchar(max))+N'cal and logistical problems that can beset African fieldwork, several of these out-posts became truly long-term projects. As a result, we live in an unprecedented time, when an intimate and comprehensive scientific record of chimpanzees’ lives at last exists not just for one but for several communities spread across Africa.</p><p></p><p><span><span>E</span></span> As early as 1973, Goodall recorded 13 forms of tool use as well as eight social activities that appeared to differ between the Gombe chimpanzees and chimpanzee populations elsewhere. She ventured that some variations had what she termed a cultural origin. But what exactly did Goodall mean by “culture”? According to the Oxford Encyclopedic English Dictionary, culture is defined as “the customs and achievements of a particular time or people.” The diversity of human cultures extends from technological variations to marriage rituals, from culinary habits to myths and legends. Animals do not have myths and legends, of course. But they do have the capacity to pass on behavioral traits from generation to generation, not through their genes but by learning. For biologists, this is the fundamental criterion for a cultural trait: it must be something that can be learned by observing the established skills of others and thus passed onto future generations.</p><p></p><p><span><span>F</span></span> What of the implications for chimpanzees themselves? We must highlight the tragic loss of chimpanzees, whose populations are being decimated just when we are at last coming to appreciate these astonishing animals more completely. Populations have plummeted in the past century and continue to fall as a result of illegal trapping, logging, and, most recently, the bushmeat trade. The latter is particularly alarming: logging has driven roadways into the forests that are now used to ship wild-animal meat—including chimpanzee meat—to consumers as far afield as Europe. Such destruction threatens not only the animals themselves but also a host of fascinatingly different ape cultures.</p><p></p><p><span><span>G</span></span> Perhaps the cultural richness of the ape may yet help in its salvation, however. Some conservation efforts have already altered the attitudes of some local people. A few organizations have begun to show videotapes illustrating the cognitive prowess of chimpanzees. One Zairian viewer was heard to exclaim, “Ah, this ape is so like me, I can no longer eat him.”</p><p></p><p><span><span>H</span></span> How'+N' an international team of chimpanzee experts conducted the most comprehensive survey of the animals ever attempted. Scientists have been investigating chimpanzee culture for several decades, but too often their studies contained a crucial flaw. Most attempts to document cultural diversity among chimpanzees have relied solely on officially published accounts of the behaviors recorded at each research site. But this approach probably overlooks a good deal of cultural variation for three reasons.</p><p></p><p><span><span>I</span></span> First, scientists typically don’t publish an extensive list of all the activities they do not see at a particular location. Yet this is exactly what we need to know—which behaviors were and were not observed at each site. Second, many reports describe chimpanzee behaviors without saying how common they are; without this information, we can’t determine whether a particular action was a once-in-a-lifetime aberration or a routine event that should be considered part of the animals’ culture. Finally, researchers’ descriptions of potentially significant chimpanzee behaviors frequently lack sufficient detail, making it difficult for scientists working at other spots to record the presence or absence of the activities.</p><p></p><p><span><span>J</span></span> To remedy these problems, the two of us decided to take a new approach. We asked field researchers at each site for a list of all the behaviors they suspected were local traditions. With this information in hand, we pulled together a comprehensive list of 65 candidates for cultural behaviors.</p><p></p><p><span><span>K</span></span> Then we distributed our list to the team leaders at each site. In consultation with their colleagues, they classified each behavior in terms of its occurrence or absence in the chimpanzee community studied. The key categories were customary behavior (occurs in most or all of the able-bodied members of at least one age or sex class, such as all adult males), habitual (less common than customary but occurs repeatedly in several individuals), present (seen at the site but not habitual), absent (never seen), and unknown.</p>'),NULL,NULL,N'',NULL,1,0,2,NULL,0,977),
(N'2e0b3e95-a44c-570a-8aeb-7db5f246be92',N'4f7a3b64-3316-5c03-9114-349cb0646fe8',0,2,18,N'<p>Reading Passage 2 has seven paragraphs. </p><p>Which paragraph contains the following information? </p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',12,0,1,NULL,0,977),
(N'36db7eb4-93ca-5d54-8481-bbf120e2eac7',N'2e0b3e95-a44c-570a-8aeb-7db5f246be92',0,3,19,N'<p>A problem of researchers on chimpanzee culture being only based on official sources.   </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Một vấn đề của các nhà nghiên cứu về văn hóa tinh tinh là chỉ dựa trên các nguồn chính thức.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn H chỉ ra rằng hầu hết các nỗ lực trước đây đều chỉ dựa vào các báo cáo đã xuất bản chính thức</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là H</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,14,NULL,0,977),
(N'6e70d34e-acd4-59cf-a7d4-ec3ed7d0eea0',N'2e0b3e95-a44c-570a-8aeb-7db5f246be92',0,3,19,N'<p>A new system designed by two scientists aims to solve the problem.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Một hệ thống mới được hai nhà khoa học thiết kế nhằm giải quyết vấn đề.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn J mô tả cách hai tác giả thực hiện một phương pháp tiếp cận mới (new approach)</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là J</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,15,NULL,0,977),
(N'11868e1e-0362-5e37-8951-b6b03132a0dc',N'2e0b3e95-a44c-570a-8aeb-7db5f246be92',0,3,19,N'<p>Reasons why previous research on ape culture is problematic.   </p>',N'<p>+ Synonyms: ape (khỉ không đuôi) = chimpanzee (tinh tinh) Interpretation: Lý do tại sao nghiên cứu trước đây về văn hóa của loài khỉ không đuôi là có vấn đề.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn I nói rằng có 3 lý do phương pháp dựa trên báo cáo chính thức về hành vi của tinh tinh được xuất bản đã bỏ qua nhiều sự khác biệt về văn hóa</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là I</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,16,NULL,0,977),
(N'74c630cf-b042-566d-93fa-75b4c31cc54c',N'2e0b3e95-a44c-570a-8aeb-7db5f246be92',0,3,19,N'<p>Classification of data observed or collected.   </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Phân loại dữ liệu được quan sát hoặc thu thập.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn K mô tả quy trình phân loại các hành vi vào các hạng mục cụ thể dựa trên hành vi, tần suất xuất hiện hoặc vắng mặt.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là K</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,17,NULL,0,977),
(N'c740d5d4-afef-5bb8-9a51-f0ce0ef0f664',N'2e0b3e95-a44c-570a-8aeb-7db5f246be92',0,3,19,N'<p>An example of how witnessing the mental abilities of an animal led to a change in local people’s attitude in preservation.   </p>',N'<p>+ Synonyms: mental abilities (khả năng về mặt tinh thần) = cognitive prowess (sức mạnh về mặt nhận thức) Interpretation: Một ví dụ về việc chứng kiến khả năng tinh thần của động vật dẫn đến thay đổi thái độ của người dân địa phương trong bảo tồn.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn G đưa ra ví dụ cụ thể về một người xem video về khả năng nhận thức của tinh tinh và thay đổi thái độ (không ăn thịt chúng nữa), minh họa cho nỗ lực bảo tồn</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là G</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,18,NULL,0,977),
(N'd7c75843-7e33-5b7f-86de-090aeed88846',N'4f7a3b64-3316-5c03-9114-349cb0646fe8',0,2,18,N'<p><span>Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 2? </span></p><p><span>In boxes 19–23 choose: </span></p><p><span><span>TRUE</span> if the statement is true </span></p><p><span><span>FALSE </span>if the statement is false </span></p><p><span><span>NOT GIVEN</span> if the information is not given in the passage </span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,2,NULL,0,977),
(N'87b36253-f24d-5f8d-83fa-7714b695672d',N'd7c75843-7e33-5b7f-86de-090aeed88846',0,3,19,N'<p>Research found that chimpanzees possess the same complex culture as humans.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Nghiên cứu phát hiện tinh tinh sở hữu nền văn hóa phức tạp giống hệt con người.</p><p>+ Answer: Bài đọc nói truyền thống văn hóa của tinh tinh chỉ đứng thứ hai về độ phức tạp chỉ sau con người, chứ không phải bằng với con người</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là False</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,19,NULL,0,977),
(N'b2868ce0-301c-57fd-9f43-5d7a793a5dbf',N'd7c75843-7e33-5b7f-86de-090aeed88846',0,3,19,N'<p>Human and apes have lived together since long ago and share most of their genetic material.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Con người và vượn đã sống cùng nhau từ lâu và chia sẻ phần lớn vật liệu di truyền.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn C xác nhận cả hai loài người (Homo sapiens) và vượn (Pan troglodytes) đã cùng tồn tại hàng trăm thiên niên kỷ và chia sẻ hơn 98% gen.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là True</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,20,NULL,0,977),
(N'78d5c83d-5591-5316-8eaa-c9f8078c819a',N'd7c75843-7e33-5b7f-86de-090aeed88846',0,3,19,N'<p>Toshisada Nishida and Jane Goodall’s beginning studies observed many surprising features of civilized behaviors among chimpanzees. </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Các nghiên cứu ban đầu của Toshisada Nishida và Jane Goodall đã quan sát thấy nhiều đặc điểm đáng ngạc nhiên về hành vi văn minh ở tinh tinh.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn D mô tả các khám phá đáng chú ý từ những nghiên cứu ban đầu này, bao gồm nhiều hành vi đáng ngạc nhiên như chế tạo công cụ, chia sẻ thức ăn.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là True</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,21,NULL,0,977),
(N'a15d6495-da2e-5aef-8f85-20fe74ae6f6b',N'd7c75843-7e33-5b7f-86de-090aeed88846',0,3,19,N'<p>Chimpanzees, like humans, have the ability to inherit cultural behaviors mostly from genetic. </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Tinh tinh, giống như con người, có khả năng kế thừa các hành vi văn hóa chủ yếu từ di truyền.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng động vật có khả năng truyền lại những đặc điểm hành vi ko phải qua di truyền mà là qua việc học tập</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là False</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,22,NULL,0,977),
(N'e763bee8-3abb-5c67-8921-65a798ae6fd6',N'd7c75843-7e33-5b7f-86de-090aeed88846',0,3,19,N'<p>For decades, researchers have investigated both unobserved and observed behaviors of chimpanzees.           </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Trong nhiều thập kỷ, các nhà nghiên cứu đã điều tra cả những hành vi không được quan sát và được quan sát của tinh tinh.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng các nhà khoa học thường không công bố danh sách các hành vi họ không thấy ở 1 địa điểm cụ thể</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là False</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,23,NULL,0,977),
(N'c62e9751-f23b-5d49-ba2f-421ababc6b0b',N'4f7a3b64-3316-5c03-9114-349cb0646fe8',0,2,18,N'<p>Answer the questions below. </p><p>Choose <span>NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER</span> from the passage for each answer. </p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',11,0,3,NULL,0,977),
(N'c5a89c5d-2b7a-59ce-be57-4b2ca185f8ea',N'c62e9751-f23b-5d49-ba2f-421ababc6b0b',0,3,19,N'<p>When did the unexpected discoveries of chimpanzee behavior start? }{SPACE}{ </p><p>e research site of Toshisada Nishida and Jane Goodall is in which country? }{SPACE}{ </p><p>  What did the chimpanzee get used to in the initial study?   }{SPACE}{ </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Những khám phá bất ngờ về hành vi tinh tinh bắt đầu khi nào?</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn C nói rằng sự thay đổi bắt đầu vào những năm 1960 với các nghiên cứu của Nishida và Goodall, cũng là thời điểm những khám phá bất ngờ bắt đầu</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là 1960s</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,24,NULL,0,977),
(N'bb10cabb-69ba-56bf-ab9b-f38d0ae6c788',N'c62e9751-f23b-5d49-ba2f-421ababc6b0b',0,3,19,N'Question number 25',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Địa điểm nghiên cứu của Toshisada Nishida và Jane Goodall ở quốc gia nào?</p><p>+ Answer: Câu trong bài xác định rõ hai địa điểm nghiên cứu thực địa đều nằm ở Tanzania.</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là Tanzania</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,25,NULL,0,977),
(N'3a6ff5fd-deef-5ae0-ad44-37607ce2f681',N'c62e9751-f23b-5d49-ba2f-421ababc6b0b',0,3,19,N'Question number 26',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Tinh tinh đã quen với điều gì trong nghiên cứu ban đầu?</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn D nói rằng khi tinh tinh trở nên quen thuộc với việc bị quan sát cận cảnh (close observation), thì các khám phá mới bắt đầu.  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,26,NULL,0,977),
(N'b59c11d1-2bc4-51d7-bb07-8c1e07604496',N'4f84567c-3208-5b5d-976e-eb239fa1f1f6',0,1,15,(CAST(N'<p><span><span>British Woodland</span></span></p><p></p><p>The climate in Britain has been arctic for the last several million years, punctuated k relatively warm timespans, or interglacials of thousands of years, one of which we are in as of now. Since the last glaciation, British woodland history is considered quite short in terms of geological time spans, and is also closely related to the human civilization developing.</p><p></p><p>At the peak of the last glaciation (100,000 - 12,000 BC), the majority of Britain would haw, had no trees. Birch and willow scrub may have grown along the lower reaches of the ice, with pine in some areas. It''s possible that remnants of pre-glacial flora were sheltered along 56 western bays of Great Britain and Ireland''s coasts, but as far as the southern parts of England the ice kept any land barren. Information regarding the development of Britain''s flora following glaciation can be found by studying the deposits of pollen and seed in peat, as by the use of radiocarbon dating. Tundra and moorland followed the retreating ice, which led to phases of different tree species spreading from the south. First came birch, aspen and sallow, followed by pine and hazel continuing to spread north as of 8500 BC, replacing birth to make it less commonly found for the next few thousand years. Oak and alder came after pine, then lime, elm, beech, and maple, all spreading northwards one after the other. From the moment lime arrived, in about 7300, to about 4500 BC the climate remained stable for a length of time known as the Atlantic Period, a time in which numerous species grew to form a series of wildwood or wilderness types.</p><p></p><p>What did the wilderness or wildwood look like, before man started interfering with it? One theory holds that Britain and Western Europe in Palaeolithic times was covered from coast to coast in a wildwood of continuous trees. However, a modern theory proposed by Francis Vera holds that Western Europe wilderness was a combination of grassland, scrub, and clusters or groves of trees. It was not a dense, impassable wildwood, but instead, an area similar to a park, kept up by wild herbivores eating the plants and grass. Throughout earlier interglacial periods, this may also have been the case in Britain, as creatures of the Palaeolitic era needed to roam large areas of grassland to survive. A variety of grassland plants continued to live there in the last interglacial, as according to pollen records.</p><p></' AS nvarchar(max))+N'p><p>However, since the last glaciation, the bison, elk and other large herbivores which persisted on mainland Europe were extinct in Britain, so Vera''s theory may not apply so well to Britain. Meanwhile, throughout the period since it''s spread northwards after the last glaciation, the sustained growth of oak in Britain demonstrates that the wildwood was not as continual as once believed. Oak is a pioneer species, which requires vacant space to generate more of itself. Grazing animals are also present to keep areas open, so Oak regenerates in the thorny brush as a protective measure from their grazing. Archaeological evidence indicates that red deer, who graze on grass as well as browse from trees, were essential to the economy in Mesolithic Britain, with people utilizing them for meat, skins, antlers and bones.</p><p></p><p>As the Mesolithic (10,000-3000 BC) era ended, evidence of the beginnings of agriculture emerges. Agricultural weeds, such as plantain and stinging nettle, were also increasing in number. Nearly all the wildwood was cut down as the population increased rapidly. However, the falling elm population around 4,000 BC all across Europe has been attributed not to the clearing of trees, but to what''s referred to as Elm disease.</p><p></p><p>Throughout the Bronze Age (2400-750 BC), people were cutting down trees more than ever before, until the prevalence of the practice "coppicing" peaked, likely at some point during the early Iron Age. Oliver Rackham (1990) theorizes that nearly 50% of land throughout England was no longer wildwood by 500 BC. The regrowth from a stump grows more readily than the original tree, and Neolithic man had discovered this practice, known as coppicing. Much of the remaining woods were maintained by way of this method during the Bronze Age.</p><p></p><p>The Celtic peoples living in the Iron Age were able to master woodworking as an artform. Today, Celtic woodworking can be seen in houses, boats, wheels and other artifacts of the time. Coppicing as a means to manage woodland was of massive importance throughout two millennia that followed. Buildings, roads, fences, carts, and the fuel for heating, cooking, metalworking and pottery were all made possible due to wood materials gained from the vital practice of coppicing.</p><p></p><p>A clear divide has existed between wooded and non-wooded regions of Britain since the time of the Romans. As evidenced by The Domesday Book (1086), all the wood in England had an economic valu'+N'e and was the property of either an individual or community owner. Woods were the territories, or ''exclaves'' of communities who lived some miles away. Even though it had to be transported over long distances, the materials which woodlands produced were of obvious value, and their ownershipwas long before established. Merely around 15% of land in England was represented by woodland or wood-pasture in the year 1086.</p><p></p><p>English woodlands produced mostly underwood used as fuel along with other things, with small oaks being used to construct buildings. The average wood-framed houses of the Medieval era mostly used oaks shorter than 18" in diameter. Longer pieces of timber were hard to come by, and kept only for elaborate buildings intended for the Church. Imported boards of thin oak or wainscot from Central Europe were brought in for the purpose of domestic building. Woodland cover was as low as 15% in 1086, and continued to decline from as a result of an ever-growing population to 10% by 1350. This stopped suddenly with the plague of the Black Death of 1349 wiping out some of the human population. Woods which had persisted up to 1350 prospered over the next 500 years.</p>'),NULL,NULL,N'',NULL,1,0,3,NULL,0,977),
(N'4b82387e-2bb8-50f7-a979-2e1f9f055781',N'b59c11d1-2bc4-51d7-bb07-8c1e07604496',0,2,18,N'<p><span>Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer in the Reading Passage? Write </span></p><p><span><span>YES</span> if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer </span></p><p><span><span>NO</span> if the statement contradicts with the claims of the writer </span></p><p><span><span>NOT GIVEN</span> if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this </span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,1,NULL,0,977),
(N'23b6a207-642d-5857-a1cb-f082ec9120f5',N'4b82387e-2bb8-50f7-a979-2e1f9f055781',0,3,19,N'<p>During the last glaciation, Britain had a large and diverse variety of trees.</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Trong thời kỳ băng hà cuối cùng, nước Anh có một sự đa dạng lớn và phong phú về cây cối.</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rõ phần lớn nước Anh trong thời kỳ băng hà cuối cùng không có cây cối</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là No</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,27,NULL,0,977),
(N'd31cc5b0-52d7-5b27-981e-2e8d4941b000',N'4b82387e-2bb8-50f7-a979-2e1f9f055781',0,3,19,N'<p>Pollen analysis has helped scientists understand how British woodland developed after the Ice Age.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Phân tích phấn hoa đã giúp các nhà khoa học hiểu rừng ở Anh phát triển thế nào sau Kỷ Băng hà.</p><p>+ Answer: Bài đọc chỉ ra rõ ràng rằng thông tin về sự phát triển của thực vật ở Anh được tìm thấy thông qua nghiên cứu phấn hoa</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là Yes</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,28,NULL,0,977),
(N'28eada82-1970-5450-8c20-850e90b4708c',N'4b82387e-2bb8-50f7-a979-2e1f9f055781',0,3,19,N'<p>Francis Vera believed that the wildwood of prehistoric Britain consisted entirely of thick, continuous forests.  </p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Francis Vera tin rằng khu rừng hoang dã của nước Anh thời tiền sử hoàn toàn là những khu rừng dày đặc, liên tục.</p><p>+ Answer: Lý thuyết của Vera mô tả cảnh quan là sự kết hợp giữa đồng cỏ, bụi cây và cụm cây, không phải là rừng rậm liên tục</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là No</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,29,NULL,0,977),
(N'f470e5ad-1c27-579b-b8d9-134a38f71258',N'4b82387e-2bb8-50f7-a979-2e1f9f055781',0,3,19,N'<p>The spread of oak trees contributed to keeping some open areas free from overgrowth.  </p>',N'<p>+ Synonym: vacant = open Interpretation: Sự lan rộng của cây sồi đã góp phần giữ cho một số khu vực mở không bị phát triển quá mức.</p><p>+ Explanation: Đoạn văn nói sự phát triển của cây sồi ở Anh cho thấy rằng rừng hoang dã không phải là liên tục, vì sồi là loài tiên phong cần không gian trống để sinh sản</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là Yes</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,30,NULL,0,977),
(N'4da5ecfa-c0d3-5316-ba9f-664df006e844',N'b59c11d1-2bc4-51d7-bb07-8c1e07604496',0,2,18,N'<p>Choose the correct letter,<span> A, B, C or D.</span></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'',1,0,2,NULL,0,977),
(N'a96c39ea-572d-5838-95fd-21141e2cca58',N'4da5ecfa-c0d3-5316-ba9f-664df006e844',0,3,19,N'<p>What does the writer say about the Mesolithic period?</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Tác giả nói gì về thời kỳ Đồ đá giữa?</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng khi thời kỳ Đồ đá giữa kết thúc, bằng chứng về nông nghiệp và cỏ dại nông nghiệp đã lộ diện</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A: việc trồng trọt và sự phát triển của cỏ bắt đầu xuất hiện</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,31,NULL,0,977),
(N'aa9c176f-1512-50d1-bac3-812787ea84e0',N'4da5ecfa-c0d3-5316-ba9f-664df006e844',0,3,19,N'<p>What is Oliver Rackham’s main point about England’s woodland by 500 BC?</p>',N'<p>+ Synonyms: half = 50% Interpretation: Ý chính của Oliver Rackham về rừng ở Anh vào năm 500 trước Công nguyên là gì?</p><p>+ Answer: Rackham lý thuyết rằng gần 50% diện tích đất (tức một nửa) không còn là rừng hoang vào thời điểm 500 BC</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,32,NULL,0,977),
(N'e9ec4c1f-fd66-5033-ae98-17a522508c8d',N'4da5ecfa-c0d3-5316-ba9f-664df006e844',0,3,19,N'<p> According to the passage, coppicing was important because it:</p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Theo đoạn văn, việc tỉa cây con (coppicing) quan trọng vì nó: </p><p>- Answer: Đoạn văn giải thích rằng việc cho cây mọc lại từ gốc cây phát triển dễ dàng hơn so với cây nguyên bản </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A: khuyến khích việc cây mọc lại nhanh hơn từ gốc   </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,33,NULL,0,977),
(N'093ec2c5-52ff-5fdf-94d2-cffeae2a4ef5',N'4da5ecfa-c0d3-5316-ba9f-664df006e844',0,3,19,N'<p>During the Iron Age, what was the main development in woodworking?</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Trong Thời đại Đồ sắt, sự phát triển chính trong nghề mộc là gì?</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói người Celtic đã thành thạo nghề mộc như một nghệ thuật và ngày nay nghề mộc của người Celt có thể được thấy ở nhà, thuyền, bánh xe và các tạo vật thời bấy giờ</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là B: Sử dụng gỗ cho mục đích nghệ thuật và thực tiễn</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,34,NULL,0,977),
(N'fa30f1b0-9fb0-5099-95f3-301ea83cab03',N'4da5ecfa-c0d3-5316-ba9f-664df006e844',0,3,19,N'<p>What does the passage suggest about the Medieval era?</p>',N'<p>+ Interpretation: Đoạn văn gợi ý điều gì về thời kỳ Trung cổ?</p><p>+ Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng gỗ nhập khẩu được dùng cho việc xây dựng trong nước</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là C</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,35,NULL,0,977),
(N'ede2383f-4f21-53c4-a57c-b00bbfec2ffc',N'b59c11d1-2bc4-51d7-bb07-8c1e07604496',0,2,18,N'<p>Look at the following items and a list of periods of time below.</p><p>Match each item with the period of time it best corresponds with.</p><p>Write the correct number <span><span>A-F</span></span> in boxes Questions 36-40 on your answer sheet.</p><p><span><span>NB</span></span> <em>You may use any letter more than once.</em></p>',N'',N'',N'',N'A list of periods of time',10,0,3,NULL,0,977),
(N'4656c9e5-3cbd-5302-8833-dbe8244265f8',N'ede2383f-4f21-53c4-a57c-b00bbfec2ffc',0,3,19,N'<p>Every type of wood in England belonged to some person or some community.</p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Mọi loại gỗ ở Anh đều thuộc về một cá nhân hoặc cộng đồng nào đó. </p><p>- Answer: Đoạn văn nói rằng từ thời La Mã đã có phân chia giữa các vùng ko có rừng và ko rừng, và từ năm 1086 tất cả gỗ ở Anh là tài sản của 1 cá nhân hoặc cộng đồng </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là F: thời La Mã  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,36,NULL,0,977),
(N'1741d2fc-51b7-5efa-983c-3eb0afbd738a',N'ede2383f-4f21-53c4-a57c-b00bbfec2ffc',0,3,19,N'<p>People used woodworking to create elaborate boats, houses, and wheels.</p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Người ta sử dụng nghề mộc để tạo ra những chiếc thuyền, ngôi nhà và bánh xe phức tạp. </p><p>- Answer: Thời đại Đồ sắt (Iron Age) được mô tả là thời kỳ người Celtic thành thạo nghề mộc và tạo ra những đồ vật này. </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là C: thời đồ sắt  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,37,NULL,0,977),
(N'3378cb3e-1d6a-5535-9b7f-d292b59a07d0',N'ede2383f-4f21-53c4-a57c-b00bbfec2ffc',0,3,19,N'<p>Animals kept extensive areas of land clear without human interference.</p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Động vật giữ cho những khu vực đất rộng lớn được thông thoáng mà không có sự can thiệp của con người. -Answer: Lý thuyết của Vera (được đề cập trong đoạn 3) về việc động vật ăn cỏ duy trì cảnh quan đồng cỏ áp dụng cho các thời kỳ gian băng trước, như Thời đại Đồ đá cũ (Palaeolithic). </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là A: thời kỳ đồ đá cũ  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,38,NULL,0,977),
(N'1266bad7-a483-531c-9e4f-71dc3675ec9e',N'ede2383f-4f21-53c4-a57c-b00bbfec2ffc',0,3,19,N'<p>Coppicing was first used for woodland management.</p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Tỉa cây con lần đầu tiên được sử dụng để quản lý rừng. </p><p>- Answer: Đoạn văn nói người Neolithic (thời kỳ đồ đá mới) đã phát hiện ra phương pháp tỉa cây </p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là E: thời kỳ đồ đá mới  </p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,39,NULL,0,977),
(N'8708930d-c205-537e-80b4-32a8f77e2385',N'ede2383f-4f21-53c4-a57c-b00bbfec2ffc',0,3,19,N'<p>Houses were made with short pieces of wood, and longer pieces were used for religious buildings. </p>',N'<p>- Interpretation: Nhà được làm bằng những thanh gỗ ngắn, và những thanh dài hơn được sử dụng cho các tòa nhà tôn giáo. </p><p>- Answer: Vào thời Trung cổ, nhà ở trung bình thường được làm từ gỗ ngắn và gỗ dài chỉ được dùng để xây nhà thờ</p><p>-&gt; Đáp án là D: thời trung cổ</p>',NULL,NULL,NULL,0,0,40,NULL,0,977),
(N'1725fc1d-3025-5312-a83a-abb6e10a24dc',NULL,0,0,11,NULL,NULL,NULL,N'https://ieltsroom.com/upload/audio/iL-full-test/ieltsadvancedtest/real-tests/iL.REAL.2.S484.S484.mp3',N'Real Tests LISTENING FULL TEST 2',0,6,1,NULL,1,484),
(N'7b405f83-e83e-5a47-b6e2-8a3f92e67f42',N'1725fc1d-3025-5312-a83a-abb6e10a24dc',0,1,13,(CAST(N'<h2>Rental Property Application Form</h2><p>IELTS Listening Version 18134</p><p>You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear. There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work.</p><p>All the recordings will be played once only. The test is in four sections. Write all your answers in the Listening question booklet. At the end of the test, you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet.</p><p>Now turn to Section One on page two of your question booklet.</p><p><span>Section One</span><br>You will hear a telephone conversation between a woman called Susan, who is looking for accommodation to rent, and a real estate agent.</p><p>First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 3 on page two.</p><p>You will see that there is an example that has been done for you. On this occasion only, the conversation relating to this will be played first.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Good morning. McMurray''s Real Estate, Peter speaking. How may I help you?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Hi Peter, my name is Susan. I''m calling from Australia. I''ll be moving to New Zealand soon, and I was hoping you could help me find somewhere to live in Auckland.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Would be pleased to. I just need to get some information about what you want so we can find the perfect place.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Sounds good. Let''s start with some personal details. What''s your last name?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>It''s Smith.</p><p><span>👉</span> The woman’s last name is <em>Smith</em>, so <span>Smith</span> has been written in the space.</p><p>Now we shall begin. You should answer the questions as you listen because you will not hear the recording a second time.</p><p>Listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 3.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Good morning. McMurray''s Real Estate, Peter speaking. How may I help you?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Hi Peter, my name is Susan. I''m calling from Australia. I''ll be moving to New Zealand soon, and I was hoping you could help me find somewhere to live in Auckland.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Would be pleased to. I just need to get some information about what you want so we can find the perfect place.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Sounds good. Let''s start with some personal details. What''s your last name?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>It''s Smith.</p><p><span>Agent ' AS nvarchar(max))+N'(Peter):</span><br>And your phone number?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>I only have a mobile. Is that okay?</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>That''s fine.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Okay, it''s 61 438 199 7. But if you want to contact me, usually the best way is by email.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Okay, what''s your email address then?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>It''s <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" href="http://SusanSmithpost.com">SusanSmithpost.com</a>.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Thanks. Now you said you''re living in Australia?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Right, in Brisbane.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Okay, what''s your address in Brisbane?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>234 Becketts — that''s B-E-C-K-E-T-T-S — Road, Brisbane 4054.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Right. Will you be working in New Zealand?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Yes, I have a job at Auckland Hospital.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Oh, are you a nurse?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Most people assume that, or think I''m a doctor. But actually, I''m a chemist.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Oh sorry.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>That''s okay.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>When will you need the accommodation from?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Well, I don''t start work until the 25th of February, but I''d like to give myself some time to settle in. A couple of weeks would be nice. Let''s say from the 10th of February.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Our leases start from a Monday, and the Monday closest to that is the 8th of February. Would that suit?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>That''s fine.</p><p>Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 4 to 10 on page two.</p><p>Now listen and answer questions 4 to 10.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Okay, let''s talk about what kind of place you''d like to rent.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>I like the outdoors, so my dream would be a house that has its own garden.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Is there anything else you''d consider? An apartment, for example?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>I don''t know. They usually don''t have an outdoor area. But I guess as an alternative, if you could find me one with a large balcony, I''d be happy.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>How many rooms do you want? Usually one-bedroom places are too small.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Two would be ideal.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br'+N'>Now, what would you like to have in the place when you move in?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>I''m bringing most of what I need with me. I have beds and sofas and a television, all that sort of stuff. So none of that is necessary.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Sounds like you want a partially furnished house with a washing machine.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Actually I''m bringing that too. All I really need in the place is a fridge. I don''t have one, so will definitely need one.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Okay. Can you find something like that for around $350 a week?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Well, I''ll certainly try. I''ll start looking for places that cost around $300 and see if there''s anything suitable. You definitely won''t need to pay more than $400 for something like that.</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Oh, that''s good. I couldn''t pay more than that.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>All right, I''ll see what I can find. You''re working at the hospital. Do you want to live somewhere near there?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>Most people think it''s strange, but I don''t like living close to work. But I''m a mad keen surfer, so it''s important for me to be close to the beach.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Okay. Do you need to be near public transport?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>No, I usually drive to work because I work a lot of shift work. So wherever I live will need to have parking close to the doors, so I won''t have to walk far at night.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Anything else?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>I''ve heard that power bills can get quite expensive, especially in the winter. It''d be great if electricity was part of the rent.</p><p><span>Agent (Peter):</span><br>Okay. Just one last question for our records. Where did you hear about us?</p><p><span>Susan:</span><br>You''re calling—</p><p>That is the end of Section One.</p><p>You now have half a minute to check your answers.</p><p>Now turn to Section Two on page three.</p>'),(CAST(N'<p>IELTS Listening Version 18134</p><p>You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear. There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work.</p><p>All the recordings will be played once only. The test is in four sections. Write all your answers in the Listening question booklet. At the end of the test, you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet.</p><p>Now turn to Section One on page two of your question booklet.</p><p><span>Section One</span><br>You will hear a telephone conversation between a woman called Susan, who is looking for accommodation to rent, and a real estate agent.</p><p>First you have some time to look at questions 1 to 3 on page two.</p><p>You w